window.DL_SEARCH=[{"p":"week-01.html","w":"Week 1","t":"Introduction, Mathematical Foundations, and Tooling","h":"Introduction, Mathematical Foundations, and Tooling","a":"","x":"Learning objectives By the end of this week you should be able to: - Explain what makes a model \"deep\", why depth helps, and how the field got from the perceptron to foundation models. - Formalize learning as risk minimization: define loss, empirical risk, generalization gap, and the supervised, unsupervised and self-supervised settings. - Use tensors fluently: shapes, matrix products, broadcasting, and the index notation that deep learning papers rely on. - Compute gradients of scalar functions with respect to vectors and matrices, and apply the chain rule through compositions. - Derive maximum likelihood estimators and show that squared error and cross-entropy are negative log-likelihoods. - Define entropy, cross-entropy and KL divergence, and explain why minimizing cross-entropy is the same as fitting a model distribution to the data. - Write reproducible PyTorch code: tensors, devices, autograd, and a training loop for linear regression. course is about one idea applied with enormous persistence: build a flexible parametric function out of many simple differentiable pieces, define a number that says how wrong it is, and use calculus to nudge the parameters so that the number goes down. Everything else (convolutions, attention, diffusion, large language models) is a way of choosing the pieces, the number, or the nudging procedure. That sounds simple, and in outline it is. The difficulty is that each of the three ingredients hides real mathematics, and the practical details (shapes, devices, random seeds, numerical stability) decide whether your experiment works at all. This first week therefore does two jobs. It gives you the big picture and the historical context, and it builds the mathematical and software foundation that every later week assumes. If you have a str"},{"p":"week-01.html","w":"Week 1","t":"Introduction, Mathematical Foundations, and Tooling","h":"What is deep learning?","a":"what-is-deep-learning","x":"Machine learning is the study of algorithms that improve at a task through data instead of through hand-written rules. Deep learning is the subfield in which the learned function is a composition of many parametrized, differentiable layers, and in which the intermediate representations are themselves learned from data. Consider recognizing a handwritten digit. A classical pipeline would have an engineer decide which features matter (stroke counts, edge histograms, loops), compute them, and train a simple classifier such as logistic regression or a support vector machine on top. The quality of the system was bounded by the quality of the hand-designed features. In a deep network, the input pixels go in at one end and class scores come out at the other; every stage in between is learned jointly, end to end, by gradient descent on a single objective. Classical machine learning separates feature engineering (hand-designed, fixed) from learning (a simple model on top). Deep learning learns a hierarchy of features and the classifier jointly, end to end. A deep network is a function written as a composition where each is a \"layer\" with its own parameters (weights and biases). The number is the depth . Typical layers apply a linear map followed by a simple nonlinearity, but the layers can be convolutions, attention blocks, normalizations, or anything else that is differentiable almost everywhere. The only structural requirement is that we can compute the derivative of the final loss with respect to every parameter, which is what backpropagation (Week 2) provides. Why \"representation learning\" is the real story The phrase to remember is representation learning . The first layers of a vision network respond to edges and color blobs; middle layers to textures and parts; late layer"},{"p":"week-01.html","w":"Week 1","t":"Introduction, Mathematical Foundations, and Tooling","h":"A short history, from the perceptron to foundation models","a":"a-short-history-from-the-perceptron-to-foundation-models","x":"History matters here for a practical reason: many \"new\" ideas are old ideas that finally had enough data and compute, and knowing which is which helps you judge claims. A condensed timeline of deep learning. Events are evenly spaced, not to scale in time. The long road - 1943. Warren McCulloch and Walter Pitts propose a logical model of a neuron as a threshold unit and show that networks of such units can compute logical functions. - 1958. Frank Rosenblatt introduces the perceptron and a learning rule for it, and builds the Mark I Perceptron hardware. Enthusiastic press coverage sets expectations the technology cannot meet. - 1969. Marvin Minsky and Seymour Papert's book Perceptrons proves that a single-layer perceptron cannot represent functions like XOR. Funding for neural networks shrinks; this is often counted as the start of the first \"AI winter\", although the book's effect on funding is debated by historians. - 1970s to 1980s. Backpropagation, the efficient application of the chain rule to layered networks, is discovered several times (Linnainmaa 1970 for reverse-mode automatic differentiation; Werbos 1974 for neural networks). Kunihiko Fukushima's Neocognitron (1980) anticipates convolutional networks. - 1986. Rumelhart, Hinton and Williams publish a clear and influential account of backpropagation and show that hidden layers learn useful internal representations. Multilayer networks become a serious research program again. - 1989 to 1998. Hornik, Stinchcombe and White (1989) and Cybenko (1989) prove universal approximation theorems. Yann LeCun and colleagues train convolutional networks with backpropagation to read zip codes, culminating in LeNet-5 (1998). - 1997. Hochreiter and Schmidhuber publish the LSTM, a recurrent architecture designed to fight vanishing g"},{"p":"week-01.html","w":"Week 1","t":"Introduction, Mathematical Foundations, and Tooling","h":"Machine learning recap","a":"machine-learning-recap","x":"The learning problem We observe data and want a function that works well on data we have not seen. Formally, assume pairs are drawn independently from an unknown joint distribution . We choose a hypothesis class (here, a neural architecture) and a loss function that measures the cost of predicting when the truth is . The quantity we really care about is the risk (also called the population risk or generalization error): We cannot compute , because we do not know . We have only a finite training set , so we minimize the empirical risk This is empirical risk minimization (ERM) . The difference at the minimizer is the generalization gap . It is almost always positive, because the parameters were chosen to fit exactly these samples: the training loss is an optimistically biased estimate of the risk. That is why we measure performance on a held-out validation set (used for model selection) and a final test set (used once). Training error vs. generalization - Underfitting: the model class or the optimization is too weak; both training and validation loss are high. - Overfitting: training loss is low but validation loss is much higher: the model has fit noise or idiosyncrasies of the training set. - Generalization gap: validation (or test) loss minus training loss. statistical learning theory said that overfitting is controlled by model capacity: too many parameters relative to means a large gap. Modern deep networks routinely have more parameters than training examples, fit the training set perfectly, and yet generalize. Understanding why (implicit regularization of gradient descent, the geometry of the loss landscape, \"double descent\") is an active research topic and we return to it in Week 3 and Week 4. For now, remember the protocol: never select hyperparameters on the tes"},{"p":"week-01.html","w":"Week 1","t":"Introduction, Mathematical Foundations, and Tooling","h":"Math refresher I: linear algebra for tensors","a":"math-refresher-i-linear-algebra-for-tensors","x":"Deep learning is, computationally, a long sequence of tensor operations. You do not need abstract vector-space theory; you need to be fluent in shapes and in what a matrix product does . Scalars, vectors, matrices, tensors A tensor is a multi-dimensional array of numbers. Its rank (or number of dimensions, \"ndim\") is the number of indices needed to address an element; its shape lists the size along each axis. Tensors of increasing rank, with the conventional meaning of each axis in deep learning: a batch of RGB images is a rank-4 tensor of shape (B, C, H, W) in PyTorch. Conventions used throughout the course: scalars are , vectors are bold lowercase and are column vectors in math, matrices are bold uppercase , and stacks examples as rows . This row-per-example convention matches how PyTorch stores a batch, so a linear layer reads in code with . (Math texts that treat as a column write ; transposing both sides gives the batch form. Keep track of which one a paper uses.) Matrix multiplication, three ways For and the product has entries Hold three pictures in your head, because different problems call for different ones. 1. Dot products. is the dot product of row of with column of . This is the view for \"similarity of every query with every key\", as in attention. 2. Linear combination of columns. Column of is applied to column of , i.e. a combination of the columns of weighted by column of . The map sends the -th basis vector to the -th column of . 3. Sum of outer products. , a sum of rank-one matrices. This is exactly the form that appears in weight gradients: is a sum over the batch of outer products of upstream gradient and input. The cost of the product is multiply-adds, which is why modern hardware is judged in FLOP/s: a transformer's cost is dominated by matrix multi"},{"p":"week-01.html","w":"Week 1","t":"Introduction, Mathematical Foundations, and Tooling","h":"Math refresher II: matrix calculus and the chain rule","a":"math-refresher-ii-matrix-calculus-and-the-chain-rule","x":"Training means computing derivatives of a scalar loss with respect to millions of parameters. This section fixes the conventions and derives the handful of gradient formulas that occur everywhere. Gradients, Jacobians and conventions For a scalar function , the gradient collects the partial derivatives, . It points in the direction of steepest ascent, and the first-order Taylor expansion is . Gradient descent moves along because that direction decreases fastest for a small step of fixed length. For a vector-valued the derivative is the Jacobian with . It is the best linear approximation: . For a scalar function, the Jacobian is the gradient written as a row vector. For a scalar loss and a matrix parameter, is the matrix of the same shape as with entries . That last convention ( gradient has the shape of the parameter ) is the one PyTorch uses: W.grad.shape == W.shape always. The Hessian , , collects second derivatives; it describes curvature and the second-order Taylor expansion . The chain rule If and , then the Jacobian of the composition is the product of Jacobians: For a scalar loss at the end of a chain, the row vector is multiplied from the left by successive Jacobians. Equivalently, in terms of gradient column vectors, the gradient is pulled back through the transpose: This product of a transposed Jacobian with a vector is called a vector-Jacobian product (VJP) . It is the one primitive that backpropagation needs, and it is cheap: we never form the full Jacobian (which could be ), only its action on a vector. We develop this in Week 2. Four gradients worth memorizing Here is a small toolkit. We derive each with index notation, which is the most reliable method when you are unsure: write the scalar as a sum, differentiate one component, and recognize the pattern. "},{"p":"week-01.html","w":"Week 1","t":"Introduction, Mathematical Foundations, and Tooling","h":"Math refresher III: probability and maximum likelihood","a":"math-refresher-iii-probability-and-maximum-likelihood","x":"Why does everyone minimize squared error for regression and cross-entropy for classification? Because these are what maximum likelihood estimation gives under simple probabilistic assumptions. Probability is the language that explains loss functions. The essentials A random variable takes values according to a distribution: a probability mass function for discrete variables, a density for continuous ones. The expectation is (or an integral), the variance is , and the covariance of two variables is . Expectation is linear regardless of dependence; variance of a sum adds only for uncorrelated variables. Conditional probability is , and Bayes' rule is Two distributions dominate deep learning: - The Bernoulli/categorical distribution over classes with probabilities , , . A classifier's softmax output is a categorical distribution over labels. - The Gaussian with density , the default noise model for real-valued targets, and (by the central limit theorem) a good description of sums of many small independent effects. Multivariate version: . Two facts underpin stochastic training. The law of large numbers says sample averages converge to expectations, which is why the empirical risk approximates the true risk. And a minibatch average of independent samples is an unbiased estimator of the full average with variance (where is the per-sample variance), so doubling the batch size cuts the gradient noise standard deviation only by . This one line of statistics explains many facts about batch size in Week 3. Maximum likelihood estimation Given i.i.d. data and a model , the likelihood is as a function of . The maximum likelihood estimator (MLE) picks the parameters under which the observed data are most probable: We take logs because products of many small numbers underflow and sums "},{"p":"week-01.html","w":"Week 1","t":"Introduction, Mathematical Foundations, and Tooling","h":"Math refresher IV: information theory","a":"math-refresher-iv-information-theory","x":"Information theory supplies the other half of the story: not just \"which loss\", but a quantitative way to compare distributions. Entropy, cross-entropy and KL divergence The information content (surprisal) of an outcome with probability is : rare events are more surprising. The entropy of a distribution is its average surprisal, With it is measured in bits (the minimum average number of binary questions, or code length, needed to describe a sample); with the natural log, in nats. Deep learning code uses nats because that is what torch.log returns. Entropy is maximal for the uniform distribution ( ) and zero for a deterministic one. The cross-entropy of a model relative to a true distribution is the average surprisal when the data come from but we score them with : The Kullback-Leibler divergence is the extra surprisal paid for using the wrong distribution: Cross-entropy decomposes into entropy (the irreducible part that depends only on the data) plus KL divergence (the penalty for the model q being different from p). KL is non-negative (Gibbs' inequality) Because is convex, Jensen's inequality ( for convex ) gives with equality iff . Properties to remember: KL is not symmetric and is not a distance; if assigns zero probability where does not; and the direction matters. Minimizing over (the \"forward\" direction) forces to cover everything covers (mass-covering); minimizing makes concentrate on one mode of (mode-seeking). Variational inference (Week 13) uses the second. Why minimizing cross-entropy is maximum likelihood Let be the empirical distribution of the training set. Then is exactly the average NLL. Since does not depend on , minimizing the cross-entropy, minimizing and maximizing the likelihood are the same optimization problem . Three viewpoints, one objective. Th"},{"p":"week-01.html","w":"Week 1","t":"Introduction, Mathematical Foundations, and Tooling","h":"Gradient descent: the algorithm you will use all semester","a":"gradient-descent-the-algorithm-you-will-use-all-semester","x":"We now have a loss and its gradient. Gradient descent repeats the update where is the learning rate (step size). The first-order Taylor expansion shows why this works: for a small step , , a decrease. \"Small\" is the catch: the approximation holds only if is small compared to the curvature scale. Convergence on a quadratic Take with curvature and minimizer . The update is , so . This converges iff , i.e. . At it lands on the minimum in one step; for it overshoots and oscillates while converging; above it diverges. For a multi-dimensional quadratic with Hessian eigenvalues , each eigen-direction contracts by , so stability needs , while progress along the flattest direction is governed by . The best rate is limited by the condition number : ill-conditioned problems are slow. (You will derive the optimal step in the exercises.) our linear regression loss the Hessian is . With standardized features its top eigenvalue is about 2, so we expect divergence for above about 1. The simulation below confirms this prediction. Use the demo to build the same intuition in two dimensions. Pick a loss surface (a well-conditioned bowl, an elongated ill-conditioned valley, or a non-convex surface), click to set the starting point, and run gradient descent with different step sizes. Watch the zig-zag in the narrow valley: it is the signature of high condition number and the motivation for momentum and adaptive methods in Week 3. Gradient descent on 2D loss surfaces 560"},{"p":"week-01.html","w":"Week 1","t":"Introduction, Mathematical Foundations, and Tooling","h":"Tensors, GPUs and the PyTorch programming model","a":"tensors-gpus-and-the-pytorch-programming-model","x":"Why PyTorch, and what it gives you PyTorch (Paszke et al., 2019) is the framework we use. It offers three things: (1) an n-dimensional array ( torch.Tensor ) with NumPy-like semantics that can live on a CPU or a GPU; (2) automatic differentiation ( autograd ), which records operations on tensors and computes gradients of any scalar with respect to any tensor that requires grad ; and (3) building blocks ( torch.nn modules, torch.optim optimizers, torch.utils.data loaders) for assembling and training models. Its defining design choice is define-by-run : the computation graph is built dynamically as ordinary Python code executes, so you can use if , for and the debugger freely. We unpack how this works in Week 2. Tensor attributes: shape, dtype, device Every tensor has a dtype ( float32 by default for floating point; float64 , float16 , bfloat16 , int64 , bool ...), a device ( cpu , cuda:0 , mps ), a shape , and strides describing how the multi-dimensional index maps to memory. Operations between tensors require compatible dtype (with some promotion rules) and identical devices . Mixed device operands raise an error, which is a feature. Precision matters. float32 has about 7 decimal digits of precision and a maximum near . float16 has a 5-bit exponent (maximum 65504) and 10 mantissa bits, so it overflows easily; bfloat16 keeps the 8-bit exponent of float32 with only 7 mantissa bits, trading precision for range, which is why it is the default for large-model training on modern accelerators. Training in reduced precision is covered in Week 17; for this course's small models and for gradient checking we sometimes use float64 . Views versus copies. reshape , view , transpose , permute and slicing usually return views that share memory with the original. Modifying one modifies "},{"p":"week-01.html","w":"Week 1","t":"Introduction, Mathematical Foundations, and Tooling","h":"Lab: linear regression with gradient descent in PyTorch","a":"lab-linear-regression-with-gradient-descent-in-pytorch","x":"Linear regression is the smallest model that has every part of a deep learning training loop: parameters, a forward pass, a loss, a backward pass, an update. The computation graph for one loss evaluation is shown below; autograd will traverse it backwards to produce and . The computation graph of the mean-squared-error loss of a linear model. Solid arrows are the forward pass; accent-colored arrows are the gradient flowing backward during backward(). We will fit with in three ways: closed form, manual gradient descent with autograd, and the idiomatic nn.Module plus optim loop. All three should agree. (Your digits will differ slightly; the point is that all methods recover roughly and the loss floors near the noise variance .) Now the same model written the way you will write every model in the course: Those four lines inside the loop (zero the gradients, forward, backward, step) are the skeleton of essentially all deep learning training code. Everything that follows in this course changes what model is, what criterion is, what optimizer is, or how the batches are produced. Experiments to run in the lab 1. Change lr to 0.9 and 1.5 in the manual loop and compare with the chart above. Which stays stable? Compute of with torch.linalg.eigvalsh to check the prediction. 2. Multiply the second feature by 100 ( X[:, 1] = 100 ) and rerun with the old learning rate. Explain what happens using the condition number. Then standardize features and repeat. 3. Remove w.grad.zero () and observe the effect on convergence. 4. Add label noise of scale 1.0 and see where the loss floors."},{"p":"week-01.html","w":"Week 1","t":"Introduction, Mathematical Foundations, and Tooling","h":"Putting it together: a map of what comes next","a":"putting-it-together-a-map-of-what-comes-next","x":"You now have the main ingredients of the course. The model is a differentiable composition of layers; the loss is a negative log-likelihood, equivalently a cross-entropy or KL to the data distribution; training is gradient descent, with gradients computed by reverse-mode autodiff on a graph. Week 2 builds the model (the multilayer perceptron), derives backpropagation in full, and implements it from scratch. Week 3 makes the descent work well (optimizers, schedules, initialization, normalization). Week 4 controls generalization (regularization, evaluation, experiment hygiene). Weeks 5 to 12 then specialize the architecture to images, sequences and attention."},{"p":"week-01.html","w":"Week 1","t":"Introduction, Mathematical Foundations, and Tooling","h":"Common mistakes and debugging tips","a":"common-mistakes-and-debugging-tips","x":"These are the errors that cost beginners the most time. Each has a recognizable symptom. Symptom Likely cause Fix --- --- --- Loss is nan after a few steps Learning rate too high, log(0) , division by zero, overflow in exp Lower the learning rate; use log softmax / cross entropy on logits; add eps inside logs and square roots; check inputs for nan Loss does not decrease at all Forgot optimizer.step() ; parameters not passed to the optimizer; requires grad=False ; learning rate far too small Print a parameter before and after one step; check p.grad is not None Loss decreases then explodes Learning rate above the stability threshold ( ) Reduce the rate, normalize inputs, clip gradients Gradients keep growing, odd convergence Forgot zero grad() , so gradients accumulate across steps Zero gradients each iteration (or set set to none=True ) Shape error or, worse, silent wrong results Broadcasting an (N,) against (N,1) producing (N,N) Assert shapes: assert pred.shape == target.shape ; use unsqueeze / squeeze deliberately RuntimeError: Expected all tensors to be on the same device Model on GPU, batch on CPU, or the reverse Move both with .to(device) RuntimeError: a leaf Variable that requires grad is being used in an in-place operation Updating a parameter in place outside torch.no grad() Wrap manual updates in with torch.no grad(): Validation numbers are better than they should be Test data leaked into training or normalization statistics; evaluating in train() mode Split first, compute statistics on the training set only; call model.eval() and use torch.no grad() Memory grows every iteration Storing loss (a tensor with a graph) in a list Store loss.item() or loss.detach() Results change from run to run Unseeded randomness, nondeterministic kernels Use the seeding recipe; com"},{"p":"week-01.html","w":"Week 1","t":"Introduction, Mathematical Foundations, and Tooling","h":"Exercises","a":"exercises","x":"Exercise 1: matrix calculus (derivation) Let with . Derive and the Hessian, and state when has a unique minimizer. Solution Write , so . Then , so . Differentiating again, , so is convex. The minimizer is unique iff is positive definite, i.e. has full column rank ( , which needs ). Then . If the columns are dependent, the minimizers form an affine subspace. Exercise 2: shapes and broadcasting (conceptual) Predict the shape of each result, then run it. (a) torch.randn(8,1,5) + torch.randn(3,5) ; (b) torch.randn(4) torch.randn(4,1) ; (c) torch.randn(2,3,4) @ torch.randn(4,5) ; (d) torch.randn(2,3,4).sum(dim=1, keepdim=True) ; (e) torch.randn(6,3)[:, None, :] - torch.randn(5,3)[None, :, :] . Explain what (e) computes and why it is useful. Solution (a) Align trailing axes: (8,1,5) with (1,3,5) gives (8,3,5) . (b) (1,4) with (4,1) gives (4,4) , an outer product. (c) matmul broadcasts the batch: (2,3,5) . (d) (2,1,4) . (e) (6,1,3) - (1,5,3) gives (6,5,3) : all pairwise difference vectors between 6 points and 5 points. Taking the squared norm over the last axis gives the (6,5) matrix of pairwise squared distances, the basis of nearest-neighbor and kernel computations without Python loops. Exercise 3: maximum likelihood of a Gaussian (derivation) Given i.i.d. samples , derive the MLE of and . Is unbiased? Solution . Setting gives . Setting gives . This estimator is biased: , because was fit to the same data and so the residuals are slightly too small. The unbiased version divides by (Bessel's correction). The same effect is why training loss underestimates test loss. Exercise 4: KL is asymmetric (computation) Let and . Compute and in nats, and . Then let and discuss versus . Solution . . , so . For : , because puts mass 0.5 on an outcome that says is impossible; but is finite. "},{"p":"week-01.html","w":"Week 1","t":"Introduction, Mathematical Foundations, and Tooling","h":"Key takeaways","a":"key-takeaways","x":"Key takeaways - Deep learning learns a composition of differentiable layers end to end; representation learning, scale and the tricks that make deep models trainable explain its success. - Learning is risk minimization: we minimize empirical risk on training data and care about risk on unseen data. Always separate training, validation and test data. - Squared error is Gaussian maximum likelihood; cross-entropy is categorical maximum likelihood; , so minimizing cross-entropy fits the model distribution to the data distribution. - Gradients have the shape of the parameter. For a linear layer, the weight gradient is an outer product (or its batched sum) of upstream gradient and input; the backward pass multiplies by the transposed Jacobian. - Gradient descent is stable only for , and its speed is limited by the condition number. - In PyTorch, keep tensors on one device, zero gradients, wrap manual updates in no grad , seed everything, and verify shapes. Use log-sum-exp and fused cross-entropy for stability."},{"p":"week-01.html","w":"Week 1","t":"Introduction, Mathematical Foundations, and Tooling","h":"Further reading","a":"further-reading","x":"- Essential. Goodfellow, Bengio and Courville, Deep Learning (MIT Press, 2016), Chapters 1 to 5 (introduction, linear algebra, probability and information theory, numerical computation, machine learning basics). Free at deeplearningbook.org. - Essential. Zhang, Lipton, Li and Smola, Dive into Deep Learning (d2l.ai), the \"Preliminaries\" chapter (tensors, calculus, autograd, probability). - Essential. The PyTorch documentation and tutorials (pytorch.org/tutorials), in particular \"Learn the Basics\" and \"Autograd: automatic differentiation\". Paszke et al., \"PyTorch: An Imperative Style, High-Performance Deep Learning Library\", NeurIPS 2019. - LeCun, Bengio and Hinton, \"Deep learning\", Nature 521, 2015: a short survey of the field's core ideas. - Krizhevsky, Sutskever and Hinton, \"ImageNet Classification with Deep Convolutional Neural Networks\", NIPS 2012. - Russakovsky et al., \"ImageNet Large Scale Visual Recognition Challenge\", International Journal of Computer Vision , 2015 (arXiv:1409.0575). - Telgarsky, \"Benefits of depth in neural networks\", COLT 2016 (arXiv:1602.04485): the sawtooth depth-separation result mentioned above. - Petersen and Pedersen, The Matrix Cookbook (2012): a reference table of matrix derivative identities. - Cover and Thomas, Elements of Information Theory , 2nd ed. (Wiley, 2006), Chapter 2: entropy, KL divergence and Gibbs' inequality. - Kaplan et al., \"Scaling Laws for Neural Language Models\", 2020 (arXiv:2001.08361)."},{"p":"week-02.html","w":"Week 2","t":"Neural Networks and Backpropagation","h":"Neural Networks and Backpropagation","a":"","x":"Learning objectives By the end of this week you will be able to: - Explain what a perceptron and a multilayer perceptron (MLP) compute, count their parameters, and track every tensor shape through a batched forward pass. - State the universal approximation theorem, explain why ReLU units can fit any 1D function, and say precisely what the theorem does not promise. - Compare sigmoid, tanh, ReLU, Leaky ReLU, GELU and SiLU by range, derivative, saturation and cost, and choose one for a given architecture. - Derive the gradient of softmax cross-entropy ( ), and use MSE, hinge and focal losses appropriately. - Draw the computational graph of an expression and run the forward and backward passes by hand. - Derive backpropagation for a scalar chain, then for a vectorized, batched layer, and implement it from scratch with numerical gradient checking. - Explain forward-mode versus reverse-mode automatic differentiation, how PyTorch's autograd records and replays a graph, and write a custom torch.autograd.Function . Week 1 gave you the language: tensors, gradients, losses, gradient descent. This week we build the object that language was designed to describe. A neural network is a parametrized function composed of simple layers , and training it means computing the gradient of a loss with respect to millions of parameters and following it downhill. The algorithm that makes that gradient affordable, backpropagation , is the single most important computation in deep learning. Everything in the next sixteen weeks (convolutions, attention, transformers, diffusion) is trained by it. We go in a deliberate order. First the neuron and its limits (why one is not enough), then layers and shapes, then the theory that justifies stacking them. Then the ingredients you choose when you design a"},{"p":"week-02.html","w":"Week 2","t":"Neural Networks and Backpropagation","h":"The perceptron","a":"the-perceptron","x":"A neuron as a weighted vote The simplest model of a neuron takes a vector of inputs , forms a weighted sum, adds a bias, and applies a nonlinearity: The numbers are weights (how strongly input pushes the output), is the bias (a threshold shift: the neuron fires more or less easily), and is the activation function . For Rosenblatt's original perceptron , is a hard threshold: if and otherwise. Geometrically, the equation describes a hyperplane. The vector is perpendicular to it, and sets its signed distance from the origin. The neuron answers one question: on which side of this hyperplane does the input lie? A perceptron is therefore a linear classifier , exactly the model you met as logistic regression in Week 1 but with a hard decision instead of a probability. A perceptron computes a weighted sum plus bias, then a threshold. It can represent linearly separable functions (left panel) but no single neuron can represent XOR (right panel). From McCulloch-Pitts to the Mark I Warren McCulloch and Walter Pitts (1943) proposed the first mathematical neuron: a binary threshold unit that, wired into networks, can compute logical functions. Frank Rosenblatt (1958) added what they lacked, a learning rule , and built the Mark I Perceptron, a room-sized machine that learned to classify simple images with motor-driven potentiometers as weights. The press was breathless. In 1969 Marvin Minsky and Seymour Papert published Perceptrons , a rigorous analysis of what single-layer perceptrons cannot do (parity, connectedness, XOR). Their book is often blamed for the first \"AI winter\" in neural network funding; the nuanced truth is that they also noted multilayer networks might be more powerful, but no one then had a practical way to train them. That way arrived with backpropagation (popular"},{"p":"week-02.html","w":"Week 2","t":"Neural Networks and Backpropagation","h":"Multilayer perceptrons","a":"multilayer-perceptrons","x":"Layers, notation and the forward pass A multilayer perceptron (MLP, also feedforward network or fully connected network ) stacks layers of neurons. Every neuron of layer receives the outputs of all neurons of layer (hence \"fully connected\" or \"dense\"), and nothing feeds back. We use the following notation throughout the course: Symbol Meaning Shape (one example) --- --- --- input weight matrix of layer bias of layer pre-activation (the \"logits\" of that layer) activation (output of layer ) number of weight layers The forward pass for one example is and the network's output is (often the last is the identity, and the loss handles the rest). A layer with inputs and outputs has weights and biases. For a batch of examples we stack them as rows: , and the forward pass becomes where the bias is broadcast across rows. This \"row convention\" matches how PyTorch and NumPy lay out data (first axis = batch), and all code in this course follows it. (PyTorch's nn.Linear stores its weight transposed, shape (out, in) , and computes x @ W.T + b ; the mathematics is identical.) A 784-128-64-10 MLP applied to a batch of B flattened MNIST images. Each arrow is one matrix multiply followed by a nonlinearity (except the last layer, whose softmax is usually folded into the loss). Counting parameters is a useful sanity habit. For the network in the figure: , then , then , for in total. Notice that the first layer holds 92% of them: the cost of a dense network is dominated by the widest matrices, which is one reason convolutions (Week 5) are so valuable for images. Why nonlinearity is essential Suppose we dropped every . Then , a product of matrices, which is itself just one matrix: the whole deep network collapses to a single affine map with no more expressive power than linear regression. Dept"},{"p":"week-02.html","w":"Week 2","t":"Neural Networks and Backpropagation","h":"The universal approximation theorem","a":"the-universal-approximation-theorem","x":"What the theorem says It is natural to ask: how much can one hidden layer compute? A remarkable family of results answers: essentially anything continuous , if the layer is wide enough. Universal approximation (informal statement) Let be a continuous, non-polynomial activation function (sigmoid, tanh, ReLU, GELU, ...). Let be compact and continuous. Then for every there exist a width , weights , biases , and output coefficients such that : George Cybenko (1989) proved it for sigmoidal activations using a functional-analysis argument (Hahn-Banach plus the Riesz representation theorem); Hornik, Stinchcombe and White (1989) proved a closely related result for general squashing functions; Hornik (1991) showed that what matters is the architecture , not the particular activation; and Leshno, Lin, Pinkus and Schocken (1993) showed that a continuous activation gives universality if and only if it is not a polynomial . That last condition makes sense: sums of polynomials of bounded degree stay polynomials of bounded degree, so they cannot approximate everything. Intuition: ReLU hinges build piecewise-linear functions The cleanest way to see universality is in one dimension with ReLU. A single hidden unit is a hinge : zero until the point (its kink ), then a ramp with slope . Adding a scaled hinge to a function changes its slope by a fixed amount at the kink and leaves everything to the left untouched. Left: each ReLU unit contributes a hinge. Right: a sum of hinges is a continuous piecewise-linear function, so hidden units can produce linear pieces and can follow a smooth target arbitrarily closely. Piecewise-linear interpolation as a one-hidden-layer ReLU network Take a target on and knots . Let be the slope of the chord on , for . Define On exactly the hinges are active, so t"},{"p":"week-02.html","w":"Week 2","t":"Neural Networks and Backpropagation","h":"Activation functions","a":"activation-functions","x":"The activation is applied elementwise after each linear map, and it determines three things at once: what the network can represent, how gradients flow backward through it, and how expensive and numerically stable it is. Because backpropagation multiplies by at every layer, the derivative matters as much as the function itself. What makes a good activation? Before the catalogue, here are the properties to look for. - Nonlinearity (and, per Leshno et al., non-polynomial). Otherwise the network collapses to a linear map. - Gradient flow. A derivative that is near zero over large input regions ( saturation ) starves earlier layers of gradient; a derivative that is exactly 1 over a large region passes gradient through unchanged. - Range and centering. Bounded outputs keep activations tame but saturate; outputs centered near zero make the next layer's inputs better conditioned. Unbounded outputs (ReLU) can grow, which normalization layers (Week 3) then control. - Smoothness. ReLU has a kink at 0; smooth functions (GELU, SiLU) have well-defined second derivatives, which matters for some optimizers, second-order analysis and double backpropagation. - Cost. ReLU is a comparison; sigmoid and tanh need exponentials; GELU needs an error function or an approximation. On large models, cheap elementwise ops are often memory-bandwidth-bound anyway, so the real cost difference is small on GPUs, but fused kernels help. Sigmoid The logistic sigmoid squashes the real line into and was the default activation of early networks, partly because it resembles a firing rate and partly because it is smooth and differentiable. Its drawbacks are severe for hidden layers. (1) Saturation: for the derivative is below , so units stuck at the extremes barely learn. (2) Maximum slope : even in the best c"},{"p":"week-02.html","w":"Week 2","t":"Neural Networks and Backpropagation","h":"Output layers and loss functions","a":"output-layers-and-loss-functions","x":"The last layer and the loss are a pair : the output layer produces numbers in a form that suits the task, and the loss measures their disagreement with the target. Choosing them is mostly a matter of matching the probabilistic model of your targets, and the right pairing produces gradients with a beautifully simple form. Regression: linear output and mean squared error For a real-valued target, use a linear output layer ( identity) and the squared error (The is a convention that cancels the 2 from differentiation; PyTorch's nn.MSELoss omits it, which only rescales the gradient by 2.) MSE is the maximum likelihood loss if the target is the model output plus Gaussian noise of fixed variance, : the negative log-likelihood is . That also tells you when it is wrong: for heavy-tailed noise or outliers, a few huge errors dominate the sum, and the Huber or L1 loss is more robust. Binary classification: sigmoid and binary cross-entropy For , output one logit and set . The negative log-likelihood of a Bernoulli variable is the binary cross-entropy (Check: and ; multiply and simplify.) Always compute this from the logit , not from : F.binary cross entropy with logits uses the identity and never evaluates . Why not MSE for classification? Suppose we put an MSE loss on a sigmoid output: . Then . When the model is confidently wrong ( but ), the factor kills the gradient, so the worst mistakes learn the slowest. Cross-entropy's gradient has no such factor: the more wrong the prediction, the larger the gradient. This is the cleanest argument for pairing each output nonlinearity with its matching log-likelihood loss. Multiclass classification: softmax and cross-entropy For classes the network outputs a vector of logits and the softmax converts them to a probability distribution: Softmax"},{"p":"week-02.html","w":"Week 2","t":"Neural Networks and Backpropagation","h":"Computational graphs","a":"computational-graphs","x":"Every differentiable program, however large, is a composition of small operations whose derivatives you already know: add, multiply, exp , log , matrix multiply, max(0, x) . A computational graph makes that composition explicit. Nodes are values (inputs, parameters, intermediate results, the final loss); edges say which values were used to compute which. The graph is a directed acyclic graph (DAG): you can order the nodes so that every node comes after its inputs, and that order is exactly the order of the forward pass. Why draw it? Because it turns the chain rule into bookkeeping. To get the derivative of the loss with respect to any node, you walk the graph backwards and, at each node, multiply the incoming gradient by the node's local derivative (how its output changes with each input). Where two paths from a node to the loss merge, you add their contributions. That is all backpropagation is. The graph of a single sigmoid neuron with squared loss, evaluated at , , , target . Black numbers are forward values, orange numbers are computed in the backward sweep. Addition passes the gradient through unchanged; multiplication swaps in the other operand. Three local rules cover a surprising amount of deep learning: Operation Forward Backward (given upstream gradient ) --- --- --- Sum (copy) Product (swap) Fan-out (one value used by several nodes) (add) Unary Step through the example yourself. Change the sliders and watch which cached values the backward pass needs; this is the reason frameworks store intermediate activations, and why training needs far more memory than inference. Step through the forward and backward pass of one neuron 560 Worked numbers With : , , , . Backward: ; so ; ; ; . A gradient step with moves to : the neuron's output rises toward the target, as it "},{"p":"week-02.html","w":"Week 2","t":"Neural Networks and Backpropagation","h":"Backpropagation derived","a":"backpropagation-derived","x":"We now derive backprop three times, at increasing generality: a scalar chain, a fully-connected layer for one example, and the batched matrix form you will implement in the lab. The scalar chain Consider a \"network\" with one weight per layer: , , for , and loss . The chain rule gives Two things to notice. First, the gradient for the weight in layer contains a product of factors . If these are typically smaller than one the gradient vanishes exponentially with depth; if larger, it explodes. This single formula is the source of the vanishing/exploding gradient problem treated in Week 3. Second, the products for different share their tails. Computing each from scratch would cost multiplications; defining reuses the running product, so all gradients cost in total, and . Backpropagation is dynamic programming on the chain rule : cache the forward values, sweep backward, reuse . One layer, one example For a layer , , with , suppose the upstream gradient is known. We want , , and (to pass on). Index-by-index derivation Through the activation. depends only on , so , i.e. (elementwise product; the Jacobian is diagonal). Weights. , so and for . Hence Bias. , so . Inputs. influences every with weight , so the contributions add (fan-out rule): useful memory aid: a gradient always has the shape of the thing it is the gradient of. is , so it must be built from ( ) and ( ); there is only one way to do that with an outer product. The batched, vectorized form With a batch the forward pass is , . The loss is the mean of per-example losses, . Each example contributes the outer product above, and parameter gradients sum over examples: The matrix product is the sum over examples of outer products, which is why one GEMM does all the work. The factor enters once, in the seed gradient at the o"},{"p":"week-02.html","w":"Week 2","t":"Neural Networks and Backpropagation","h":"Automatic differentiation","a":"automatic-differentiation","x":"Backprop as we derived it is a special case of a general technique: automatic differentiation (AD) computes exact derivatives of a program by applying the chain rule to its elementary operations. It is not numerical differentiation (finite differences, which are approximate and need one evaluation per parameter) and not symbolic differentiation (which manipulates formulas and can blow up in size). AD gives machine-precision derivatives at a small constant factor over the cost of the function itself. Forward mode versus reverse mode Let have Jacobian . Evaluating the chain rule for a composition means multiplying Jacobians, , and the only freedom is the order of multiplication . - Forward mode pushes a tangent vector along with the computation, multiplying right to left: it returns the Jacobian-vector product in one pass. A full Jacobian needs passes (one per input direction). - Reverse mode first runs the forward pass, then pulls a cotangent back, multiplying left to right: it returns the vector-Jacobian product in one pass. A full Jacobian needs passes. A loss has and the parameters have in the millions. One reverse pass with yields the entire gradient; forward mode would need passes. That asymmetry is why reverse mode (backprop) is the engine of deep learning. Forward mode wins when inputs are few and outputs many, and it needs no stored activations; it is used for Jacobian-vector products in some second-order and sensitivity methods. What each AD mode extracts from the Jacobian per pass. Forward mode: one column ( ). Reverse mode: one row ( ). For a scalar loss, reverse mode produces the full gradient in a single pass at the price of storing intermediates. To make forward mode concrete, here is a tiny dual number implementation: each value carries its derivative with"},{"p":"week-02.html","w":"Week 2","t":"Neural Networks and Backpropagation","h":"Lab: a neural network from scratch","a":"lab-a-neural-network-from-scratch","x":"Time to build it. The lab has three parts: (1) a NumPy MLP whose backward pass is the algorithm above, (2) a numerical gradient check that proves the backward pass is right, (3) training on MNIST and comparing with the same network in PyTorch. Do the NumPy part by hand at least once: nothing teaches backprop like debugging your own. Part 1: the network Read backward next to the boxed algorithm: dZ starts as ; each iteration forms gW = A[l].T @ dZ , gb = dZ.sum(0) , then moves the gradient down with dZ @ W.T and multiplies by the ReLU mask (Z 0) . Note that forward caches A and Z on the object; backward is only valid right after the forward it belongs to. Part 2: gradient checking If your backward is wrong, training will not crash; it will quietly do worse. The centered finite difference approximates with error (the one-sided version is only ). Compare it with your analytic gradient through a relative error , never an absolute one, because gradients can be tiny. A rough guide for relative error in float64: below is right, to deserves a look, above is a bug. On our run the worst error over 20 sampled entries per parameter array was about . Why not always use as small as possible? Because of floating-point cancellation, as the next chart shows. Check gradients on a tiny network and batch, with float64, and remove stochastic layers (dropout) while checking. Kinks matter too: if a pre-activation sits within of zero, the ReLU finite difference straddles the kink and disagrees with the (valid) subgradient. A single disagreeing coordinate in a ReLU net is not necessarily a bug; a consistent disagreement is. Part 3: train on MNIST MNIST has 60,000 training and 10,000 test images of 28x28 digits. We flatten, scale to and standardize with the training mean and standard deviation. "},{"p":"week-02.html","w":"Week 2","t":"Neural Networks and Backpropagation","h":"Common mistakes and debugging tips","a":"common-mistakes-and-debugging-tips","x":"Symptom Likely cause What to do --- --- --- Loss stuck at (about 2.303 for 10 classes) Network outputs near-uniform probabilities: learning rate too small, dead network, or labels shuffled relative to inputs Overfit 10 examples first; print gradient norms; check data/label alignment Loss becomes nan or inf Learning rate too high; log(0) ; exp overflow in a hand-written softmax Lower lr; use log softmax / cross entropy on logits; add gradient clipping Training accuracy at chance from the start Applied softmax before CrossEntropyLoss (double softmax) or forgot to remove sigmoid/softmax from the last layer Output raw logits to cross entropy and BCEWithLogitsLoss Gradients do not change between steps, or grow unboundedly Forgot opt.zero grad() Zero (or set to none ) every iteration before backward() Loss decreases on train, test accuracy random Forgot model.eval() / torch.no grad() (matters once dropout/BatchNorm appear), or different preprocessing at test time Reuse exactly the train-time normalization statistics Many units output exactly zero (ReLU) Dead ReLUs from a large learning rate or a large negative bias Lower lr, He init, try Leaky ReLU, monitor the fraction of zero activations Finite-difference check fails by a large factor Wrong backward, or float32, or too small, or a ReLU kink Use float64, , test a tiny net layer by layer RuntimeError: ... modified by an inplace operation In-place op overwrote a tensor needed by backward Replace x += y / relu () by out-of-place versions Trying to backward through the graph a second time Reused a graph whose buffers were freed (e.g. an RNN state kept across iterations) .detach() the carried state, or retain graph=True only if you truly need it Memory blows up over iterations Accumulating loss tensors (keeps graphs alive) Accumu"},{"p":"week-02.html","w":"Week 2","t":"Neural Networks and Backpropagation","h":"Exercises","a":"exercises","x":"Exercise 1: Parameter count and shapes A network maps 3x32x32 images (flattened) through hidden layers of width 512 and 256 to 10 classes. (a) How many parameters? (b) For a batch of 64, give the shape of every , and . Solution Input size 3072. Layer 1: . Layer 2: . Layer 3: . Total . Shapes: (64,512), (64,256), (64,10); : (3072,512), (512,256), (256,10); : (512,), (256,), (10,). Exercise 2: Softmax cross-entropy gradient Derive for , , one-hot . Solution where is the true class. Differentiate: . (Equivalently use the softmax Jacobian and .) Exercise 3: Backprop by hand Let , (row = input ), , , loss with . Compute . Solution , . ; ReLU mask so . . Exercise 4: Dead network Initialize all weights and biases of an MLP to zero (tanh hidden units, softmax output). What happens during gradient descent? What if only the biases are zero? Solution All hidden units in a layer compute the same function and receive the same gradient (by symmetry), so they stay identical forever: a layer of width behaves like one unit. In fact with zero weights the gradient into earlier layers is exactly zero (it is multiplied by ), so only the last layer's bias moves at first. Zero biases alone are fine: random weights already break symmetry, and the biases then learn like any other parameter. Exercise 5: Why not finite differences for training? For a network with parameters, estimate the cost of one gradient by centered finite differences versus backprop, in units of one forward pass. Solution Centered differences need two forward passes per parameter: forward passes. Backprop needs about 3 forward-pass equivalents in total (one forward, roughly two for the backward). The ratio is about . Finite differences are also inexact; they are used only to test backprop on small models. Exercise 6: Backwar"},{"p":"week-02.html","w":"Week 2","t":"Neural Networks and Backpropagation","h":"Key takeaways","a":"key-takeaways","x":"Key takeaways - A neuron is a weighted sum plus a nonlinearity; stacking layers without nonlinearity collapses to one linear map, and one hidden layer already suffices to approximate any continuous function on a compact set, but the theorem says nothing about learnability or width. - Activations matter through their derivatives: saturation (sigmoid, tanh) shrinks gradients, ReLU is cheap but can die, GELU/SiLU are smooth modern defaults. - Softmax cross-entropy has the clean gradient ; always feed logits to the loss, never probabilities. - Backprop is the chain rule plus dynamic programming on a computational graph: cache forward values, sweep backward, add where paths merge. A step costs about 3x a forward pass and memory proportional to depth. - Reverse-mode AD returns a vector-Jacobian product per pass, which is ideal for scalar losses with huge parameter counts; PyTorch builds the graph dynamically and accumulates into .grad . - Gradient-check every hand-written backward (float64, centered differences, relative error) and overfit a tiny batch before trusting a training run."},{"p":"week-02.html","w":"Week 2","t":"Neural Networks and Backpropagation","h":"Further reading","a":"further-reading","x":"- Essential. Rumelhart, Hinton & Williams (1986). Learning representations by back-propagating errors. Nature 323, 533-536. - Essential. Baydin, Pearlmutter, Radul & Siskind (2018). Automatic differentiation in machine learning: a survey. JMLR 18(153). arXiv:1502.05767. - Essential. Karpathy, A. The spelled-out intro to neural networks and backpropagation: building micrograd (video lecture, 2022) and the micrograd repository. - Hornik, Stinchcombe & White (1989). Multilayer feedforward networks are universal approximators. Neural Networks 2(5). - Cybenko (1989). Approximation by superpositions of a sigmoidal function. Mathematics of Control, Signals and Systems 2. - Leshno, Lin, Pinkus & Schocken (1993). Multilayer feedforward networks with a nonpolynomial activation function can approximate any function. Neural Networks 6(6). - Montufar, Pascanu, Cho & Bengio (2014). On the number of linear regions of deep neural networks. NeurIPS. arXiv:1402.1869. - Glorot & Bengio (2010). Understanding the difficulty of training deep feedforward neural networks. AISTATS. - Hendrycks & Gimpel (2016). Gaussian Error Linear Units (GELUs). arXiv:1606.08415. - Ramachandran, Zoph & Le (2017). Searching for activation functions. arXiv:1710.05941. - Lin, Goyal, Girshick, He & Dollar (2017). Focal loss for dense object detection. ICCV. arXiv:1708.02002. - Paszke et al. (2019). PyTorch: an imperative style, high-performance deep learning library. NeurIPS. arXiv:1912.01703. - Schmidhuber, J. (2015). Deep learning in neural networks: an overview. Neural Networks 61. arXiv:1404.7828. - Chen, Xu, Zhang & Guestrin (2016). Training deep nets with sublinear memory cost. arXiv:1604.06174."},{"p":"week-03.html","w":"Week 3","t":"Optimization for Deep Learning","h":"Optimization for Deep Learning","a":"","x":"Learning objectives By the end of this week you should be able to: - Describe what the loss surface of a neural network looks like (saddles, plateaus, flat and sharp minima) and what that implies for optimizers. - Explain the bias-variance structure of mini-batch gradients and use the linear scaling rule and the gradient noise scale to reason about batch size. - Derive and implement momentum, Nesterov momentum, AdaGrad, RMSProp, Adam (with bias correction) and AdamW (with decoupled weight decay) from scratch. - Choose and implement learning-rate schedules: step decay, cosine annealing, linear warmup, cyclical rates and the one-cycle policy. - Derive the Xavier/Glorot and He initialization variances, and diagnose vanishing and exploding gradients, including the use of gradient clipping. - Write the forward and backward pass of BatchNorm, explain train versus eval behavior, and compare BatchNorm, LayerNorm and GroupNorm. - Run a controlled experiment comparing optimizers, initializations and learning rates, and read the resulting loss curves. Week 2 gave you the machinery to compute gradients with backpropagation. This week is about what you do with them. Training a neural network means minimizing a function of millions or billions of parameters that is non-convex, noisy (because you only ever see a small batch of data per step), badly conditioned and full of strange geometry. Classical optimization theory says very little that is comforting about this setting. And yet a handful of simple, well-tuned ingredients make training reliably succeed: mini-batch stochastic gradient descent, momentum or Adam-style adaptive scaling, a learning-rate schedule with warmup, an initialization that preserves signal scale, gradient clipping for the cases where it does not, and normalizati"},{"p":"week-03.html","w":"Week 3","t":"Optimization for Deep Learning","h":"The geometry of neural network loss surfaces","a":"loss-landscapes","x":"Let be the training loss as a function of all parameters . For linear regression with squared error is a convex quadratic: one basin, one minimum, and gradient descent with a small enough step is guaranteed to find it. A neural network breaks this in two ways. First, the composition of weight matrices and nonlinearities makes non-convex. Second, the parameterization has enormous symmetries: permute the hidden units of a layer (together with the matching rows and columns of neighbouring matrices) and you get exactly the same function, so a minimum always comes with at least copies. Non-convexity is therefore not an accident but a structural feature. It is worth being precise about what \"non-convex\" implies and does not imply. It does not imply that gradient descent is hopeless. What matters is which kinds of critical points ( ) exist and how much of the surface is easy to descend. A cartoon of a non-convex loss along a single direction in weight space. The sharp minimum has slightly lower training loss but a large gap to the (shifted) test loss; the flat minimum is more tolerant of the shift between training and test distributions. Cartoon, not measured data. Critical points: minima, maxima and saddles { saddle-points} At a critical point the behavior is governed by the Hessian , which is symmetric and therefore has real eigenvalues . Writing a small displacement in the eigenbasis, the second-order Taylor expansion at a critical point is where is the coordinate of along eigenvector . If every you are at a local minimum, if every at a local maximum, and if the signs are mixed at a saddle point : the loss goes up in some directions and down in others. Now count. In one dimension a critical point is a minimum or a maximum. In dimensions, if you imagine the signs of the eige"},{"p":"week-03.html","w":"Week 3","t":"Optimization for Deep Learning","h":"Gradient descent variants and batch size","a":"gd-variants","x":"The training objective is an average over examples, , where is the loss on example . The three classical variants differ only in how many examples they use to estimate . Variant Examples per step Gradient Per-step cost Noise --- --- --- --- --- Batch (full-batch) GD all exact none Stochastic GD (SGD, ) 1 very high Mini-batch SGD (e.g. 32 to 4096) moderate, In modern usage \"SGD\" almost always means mini-batch SGD: sample a batch of size without replacement (one pass through a shuffled dataset is an epoch ), compute the average gradient, step. Mini-batches win for three reasons: hardware (a GPU computes 256 examples in barely more time than 8, so per-example cost falls), statistics (the gradient of a random subset is an unbiased estimate of the full gradient, so you get a good direction at a fraction of the price) and generalization (the noise is helpful, see above). Mini-batch gradients as noisy estimates { gradient-noise} Let be the true gradient and let be the covariance of the per-example gradients (over a random example). For a batch of examples drawn independently, (When sampling without replacement from a finite dataset there is an extra factor , which makes the noise vanish when .) The expected squared error falls like . The chart below shows this for a small network: the measured error follows the line and is much larger than the gradient itself for tiny batches. The code below measures this for any PyTorch model: it takes many random batches at a fixed parameter value and reports the mean squared deviation from the full-batch gradient. Running it you should see the error shrink roughly in proportion to (a little faster for close to , because of the finite-population correction). The effect of batch size { batch-size-effects} If you double the batch size, each st"},{"p":"week-03.html","w":"Week 3","t":"Optimization for Deep Learning","h":"Momentum and Nesterov accelerated gradient","a":"momentum","x":"On a ravine, plain SGD oscillates across the walls (large gradient components with alternating sign) and makes slow progress along the floor (small gradient components with a consistent sign). Momentum (Polyak, 1964, \"heavy ball\") keeps an exponentially decaying running sum of past gradients, the velocity . Components that alternate in sign cancel in that sum; components that keep their sign accumulate. with , typically . (PyTorch's SGD(momentum=...) uses exactly this form. Some texts write and ; the two are equivalent after rescaling by when is constant.) Unrolling the recursion shows that the update is : a weighted sum of past gradients with an effective memory of about steps. For a constant gradient the velocity saturates at , so momentum 0.9 gives an effective learning rate ten times larger along consistent directions. When you add momentum, you should usually lower accordingly. Gradient descent and heavy-ball momentum on the same ill-conditioned quadratic. GD zigzags across the steep axis; momentum damps the oscillation while retaining speed along the shallow axis. Why momentum accelerates: the quadratic case On (one eigendirection of a quadratic) the heavy-ball iteration in the form is the linear recurrence . Its characteristic polynomial is . When the roots are complex, which happens when , both roots have modulus exactly regardless of . So for every curvature in that range the error decays at the same rate per step (with oscillation). The range covers all if and are tuned as giving a per-step rate of . Compare that with for gradient descent: the number of iterations falls from to . With that is the difference between 10,000 and 100 steps. This is the best possible rate for any first-order method on quadratics. Real networks are not quadratics, and the tuned valu"},{"p":"week-03.html","w":"Week 3","t":"Optimization for Deep Learning","h":"Adaptive learning rates: AdaGrad, RMSProp, Adam, AdamW","a":"adaptive-methods","x":"Momentum fixes the direction but still uses one global learning rate for every parameter. In a network, different parameters live at wildly different gradient scales (embedding rows of rare words versus common words, early layers versus late layers, biases versus weights). Adaptive methods keep a per-parameter running estimate of the typical gradient magnitude and divide the step by it, so every coordinate moves at a comparable speed. AdaGrad { adagrad} Duchi, Hazan and Singer (2011) accumulate the sum of squared gradients for each coordinate: where is elementwise multiplication and all operations on vectors are elementwise. Coordinates with large past gradients get smaller steps; rare features whose gradients are mostly zero keep a large step on the few occasions they appear. That is a wonderful property for sparse problems (bag-of-words models, embeddings). The weakness is that only grows, so the effective learning rate decays monotonically, roughly like in the constant-gradient case, and in non-convex deep learning training can stall long before it converges. RMSProp { rmsprop} Geoffrey Hinton proposed, in a 2012 Coursera lecture (never formally published), replacing the sum by an exponential moving average so the denominator forgets old gradients: with (or 0.99). Now approximates the recent root-mean-square (RMS) gradient per coordinate, so the update has magnitude about per coordinate whatever the gradient scale. This is a diagonal approximation to a second-order method: dividing by the RMS gradient crudely rescales each coordinate toward unit curvature. Adam { adam} Adam (Kingma and Ba, 2014; ICLR 2015) combines momentum on the gradient (first moment) with RMSProp scaling (second moment), plus a bias correction: One Adam step for a parameter tensor: update the two"},{"p":"week-03.html","w":"Week 3","t":"Optimization for Deep Learning","h":"Learning-rate schedules","a":"lr-schedules","x":"The learning rate is the most important hyperparameter, and a constant value is almost never optimal. Early in training you want to move fast, late in training you want to settle into a minimum without bouncing around due to gradient noise. In the continuous-time picture, the stationary spread of the iterates around a minimum scales with , so decaying is a way to shrink the noise ball and sharpen the final answer, which is why a sudden improvement in loss appears every time the learning rate drops. Before looking at specific schedules, here is why a schedule helps at all. Near a minimum with SGD noise, the stationary spread of the iterates scales roughly with (see the noise argument in the batch-size section): a large constant rate keeps the loss floating above the floor set by the noise. Decaying shrinks the noise ball and lets the model settle. Early on the opposite problem holds: you want the largest stable rate to move quickly through the flat, high-loss regions. The standard schedules Write for the base (peak) rate, for the step and for the total number of steps. - Step decay. : multiply by (commonly ) every steps. The classic ImageNet recipe drops the rate by 10x at epochs 30, 60 and 90. Simple and still competitive, but the drop points are two extra hyperparameters. - Cosine annealing (Loshchilov and Hutter, 2017). . It decays slowly at first, fastest in the middle, and gently toward the end, and needs only . This is the dominant choice for modern vision and language training. (The original SGDR variant restarts the cosine periodically.) - Linear warmup. for , usually followed by one of the decays above. Warmup is needed because at initialization the loss surface is at its sharpest and Adam's second-moment estimate is built from very few samples; a full-size step"},{"p":"week-03.html","w":"Week 3","t":"Optimization for Deep Learning","h":"Weight initialization","a":"initialization","x":"A network's first gradient step is determined entirely by its initial weights, and so is whether the signal survives the trip through dozens of layers. Initialization is therefore not a detail. The principle is simple: choose the weight variance so that the scale of activations (forward) and of gradients (backward) stays roughly constant from layer to layer. If each layer multiplies the variance by 2 the signal grows as ; by , it shrinks as . At that is a factor of a billion either way. Deriving Xavier/Glorot initialization Consider a dense layer (ignore the bias, initialized at 0), with . Assume the weights are i.i.d. with mean 0 and variance , independent of the inputs, and that the inputs are i.i.d. Forward variance Because the terms are independent and zero-mean, Here we used and . For a symmetric activation that is approximately linear near zero (tanh, or no activation), , so to keep the variance constant from layer to layer we need Backward variance In the backward pass the gradient with respect to a layer's input is , a sum of terms. The same computation gives (taking ), so keeping gradient variance constant needs we cannot satisfy both. Glorot and Bengio (2010) split the difference with the harmonic compromise (The uniform bound follows from .) This is nn.init.xavier normal / xavier uniform . LeCun's earlier rule matches the forward condition only. Deriving He (Kaiming) initialization ReLU breaks the \"approximately linear\" assumption: it zeroes half the pre-activations. If is symmetric around zero, then . Redoing the forward computation: He variance Constant variance requires , that is The same argument applies backward (only half the units pass gradient), giving ; He et al. (2015) show that either choice keeps the product over layers bounded, and use (\"fan-in\" "},{"p":"week-03.html","w":"Week 3","t":"Optimization for Deep Learning","h":"Vanishing and exploding gradients","a":"vanishing-exploding","x":"Week 2 showed that the gradient reaching layer is a product of per-layer Jacobians . The norm of a product of matrices tends to grow or shrink exponentially in , governed by the typical singular value of each factor: the gradient scales like . If the early layers get no learning signal ( vanishing ); if the gradient blows up and a single step can destroy the model ( exploding ). Where each comes from: - Saturating activations. and in the tails give factors below 1 (Hochreiter 1991; Bengio, Simard and Frasconi 1994). - Bad initialization. Weight variance too small shrinks signals; too large inflates them and also saturates sigmoid/tanh units. - Depth and recurrence. In an RNN the same is applied at every time step, so the product is and its behavior is set by the largest eigenvalue: above 1 explodes, below 1 vanishes (see Week 7). How to diagnose it: log the gradient norm of each parameter group per step. A healthy network has comparable relative gradient magnitudes ( around ) across layers. Gradient norms that spike by orders of magnitude, or nan , indicate explosion; first-layer norms many orders below the last layer's indicate vanishing (compare the bar chart in Week 2). The toolbox, in order of impact: proper initialization; non-saturating activations (ReLU family, GELU); normalization layers ; residual connections (the identity path gives a Jacobian factor near 1, which is why ResNets train at depth 100+, Week 5); gated recurrent cells (LSTM/GRU); and, for explosions, gradient clipping . Gradient clipping Clipping bounds the size of an update without changing its direction. Two forms: - Clip by value: clamp each component to . Cheap but distorts the direction. - Clip by global norm (Pascanu, Mikolov and Bengio, 2013): treat all gradients as one vector and rescale If"},{"p":"week-03.html","w":"Week 3","t":"Optimization for Deep Learning","h":"Normalization layers","a":"normalization","x":"Initialization sets the scale of activations at step 0, but nothing keeps it there as the weights change. Normalization layers fix this by explicitly standardizing activations inside the network. They are among the most effective training aids ever found: they allow learning rates several times larger, reduce sensitivity to initialization and make very deep networks trainable. Batch normalization { batchnorm} Ioffe and Szegedy (2015) normalize each feature using the statistics of the current mini-batch. For a layer output (for images, with statistics taken over for each channel): The learnable let the network undo the normalization if that is best (so no expressive power is lost). Typically BN sits between the linear layer and the nonlinearity (original paper) or after the nonlinearity; both work, and with ReLU the former is the convention. Train versus eval. During training the statistics come from the current batch, which makes each output depend on the other examples in the batch (a mild source of noise that also regularizes). At test time you want a deterministic function of one input, so BN uses running averages of the training means and variances, updated as with momentum in PyTorch. Forgetting model.eval() is the classic BN bug. Which axes each normalizer averages over for an activation tensor of shape : BatchNorm over per channel; LayerNorm over per sample; GroupNorm over groups of channels (and ) per sample; InstanceNorm per sample and channel. Backward pass of BatchNorm Let denote for one channel, and . Then , and . Since every affects for all through and , three paths contribute: - directly: ; - through : and (plus a term through that vanishes because ), giving ; - through : and , giving . Adding the three terms, The gradient is the incoming gradient with its"},{"p":"week-03.html","w":"Week 3","t":"Optimization for Deep Learning","h":"Lab: compare SGD, momentum and Adam","a":"lab","x":"The lab turns every idea of the week into a controlled experiment, in pure NumPy so you can see every line. We train the same 2-64-64-3 ReLU network (the one from Week 2) on a three-arm spiral with mini-batches of 64, change one thing at a time , and read the loss curves. Everything below, including the numbers and charts, is produced by this code. Now the three experiments. Typical output (one seed, so exact digits will differ across machines only in the last digits): Reading the optimizer curves. Plain SGD needs the steepest-direction-limited learning rate, so progress along the shallow directions is slow; momentum at the same rate (an effective rate of ) is about 20x lower in loss by epoch 30. Adam starts slower here (its bias-corrected steps are small per coordinate with ) but catches up and matches momentum by epoch 60. Neither is universally better: SGD with momentum plus a tuned schedule remains the standard for many CNN recipes, Adam(W) for transformers. Compare optimizers at their own best learning rates, never at one shared value, or you are measuring your choice of rate. Reading the initialization curves. Initialization changes the first epochs, not the destination: Glorot lags He slightly on a ReLU net (its variance is half as large), and a weight std of 0.01 leaves the network on a plateau at the chance loss because the signal is too small to produce useful gradients, until it eventually breaks symmetry. The std-1 run is an honest surprise: it starts with a huge loss but trains fine in a 3-layer ReLU network, since ReLU is scale-equivariant and there is no saturation. The failure of bad initialization is a depth phenomenon (see the 30-layer chart above), which a shallow lab only hints at; try init params([2] + [64] 12 + [3], ...) to see it bite. Reading the"},{"p":"week-03.html","w":"Week 3","t":"Optimization for Deep Learning","h":"Common mistakes and debugging tips","a":"common-mistakes-and-debugging-tips","x":"Symptom Likely cause Fix --- --- --- Loss is nan after a few steps Learning rate too high; exploding gradient; log(0) ; fp16 overflow Lower lr by 3-10x, add warmup and clipping, use bf16 or a loss scaler Loss flat at from the start Learning rate too small, init too small, dead ReLUs, labels not matching inputs Run an lr sweep; check init stats; overfit a 32-example batch Loss decreases then suddenly spikes LR too high late in a long run, Adam / issues, a bad batch Clip gradients; lower peak lr; use for large models; use cosine decay Adam converges but generalizes worse than SGD Weak or coupled weight decay Use AdamW, tune , no decay on biases and norm parameters Weight decay \"does nothing\" in Adam Using Adam(weight decay=...) (L2 inside the gradient) Switch to AdamW Training is fine, eval accuracy is erratic BatchNorm in train mode at eval, or tiny batch statistics model.eval() ; use GroupNorm/LayerNorm for tiny batches Moved to bigger batch, accuracy dropped LR not rescaled, no warmup Scale lr (linear for SGD, sqrt for Adam), warm up, retune the schedule Scheduler seems to have no effect scheduler.step() not called, called per epoch instead of per step, or called before optimizer.step() Call optimizer.step() then scheduler.step() ; check optimizer.param groups[0][\"lr\"] Gradients of first layers are tiny, last layers huge Vanishing gradients: saturating activation, bad init, no normalization He/Glorot init, ReLU/GELU, add norm layers and residual connections Clipping fires every step lr far too high Lower lr; clipping is a safety net only Three habits catch most optimization bugs: (1) log training loss, gradient norm, the learning rate and update-to-weight ratio (healthy is around ); (2) overfit one small batch to prove the pipeline can learn; (3) change one thing at a "},{"p":"week-03.html","w":"Week 3","t":"Optimization for Deep Learning","h":"Exercises","a":"exercises","x":"Exercise 1: Stability of gradient descent on a quadratic For derive the condition on for gradient descent to converge, and the iterates after steps. What is the best learning rate for and how many steps to shrink the error by ? Solution , so , convergent iff , i.e. . For the two-dimensional case the best rate is with rate ; gives steps. With momentum at the optimal setting the rate becomes , so about 35 steps. Exercise 2: Variance of the mini-batch gradient Per-example gradients have mean and covariance . Show that for independent samples, . If and , what is the noise scale and what does it suggest for batch size? Solution with independent zero-mean , so the expected squared norm is . Noise scale . Below about the gradient is noise-dominated and larger batches are nearly free in terms of steps; far above 100 extra examples buy little per step. Exercise 3: Bias correction by hand Run two Adam steps ( , , , ) on a scalar with constant gradient . Compute and the updates, with and without bias correction. Solution Step 1: , ; , , update . Without correction the update would be (too big, since is biased more than ). Step 2: , ; , , update again. With constant gradients the corrected update is always . Exercise 4: Derive the He variance backward Repeat the He derivation for the backward pass of a ReLU layer and show the variance condition is . Why does it not matter much which one you pick? Solution and , so . Then , which is constant iff . Over a whole network the fan-in and fan-out conditions differ by the ratio at each layer, and these ratios telescope along a chain of layers (the product of is ), so using either rule changes the overall scale only by a bounded factor, not an exponential one. Exercise 5: Why weight decay and L2 differ under Adam For a scalar parameter with"},{"p":"week-03.html","w":"Week 3","t":"Optimization for Deep Learning","h":"Key takeaways","a":"key-takeaways","x":"Key takeaways - Neural loss surfaces are non-convex but mostly benign: high-dimensional critical points are overwhelmingly saddles, and the real difficulty is ill-conditioning (a large ratio of curvatures) rather than bad local minima. Flatness is suggestive but parameterization-dependent. - Mini-batch gradients are unbiased with covariance ; batch size trades steps for compute, and the linear scaling rule (with warmup) works until the batch exceeds the gradient noise scale. - Momentum accelerates along consistent directions ( on quadratics); adaptive methods rescale each coordinate; Adam adds bias-corrected first and second moments; AdamW decouples weight decay and is the default for transformers. - Use warmup, then cosine (or step) decay; one-cycle and cyclical schedules are useful when training budgets are short. - Initialize with for ReLU (He) and for tanh (Glorot) so that activation and gradient variances stay constant with depth; clip gradients by global norm as a safety net. - Normalization (BatchNorm for large-batch CNNs, LayerNorm/RMSNorm for transformers, GroupNorm for small batches) stabilizes scales, smooths the landscape and allows larger learning rates; BatchNorm behaves differently in train and eval. - Compare optimizers only at tuned learning rates, change one variable at a time, log gradient norms, and overfit a small batch first."},{"p":"week-03.html","w":"Week 3","t":"Optimization for Deep Learning","h":"Further reading","a":"further-reading","x":"- Essential. Kingma & Ba (2015). Adam: a method for stochastic optimization. ICLR. arXiv:1412.6980. - Essential. Ioffe & Szegedy (2015). Batch normalization: accelerating deep network training by reducing internal covariate shift. ICML. arXiv:1502.03167. - Essential. Loshchilov & Hutter (2019). Decoupled weight decay regularization. ICLR. arXiv:1711.05101. - Goodfellow, Bengio & Courville (2016). Deep Learning , Chapter 8: Optimization for training deep models. MIT Press. - Duchi, Hazan & Singer (2011). Adaptive subgradient methods for online learning and stochastic optimization. JMLR 12. - Polyak, B. (1964). Some methods of speeding up the convergence of iteration methods. USSR Computational Mathematics and Mathematical Physics 4(5). - Sutskever, Martens, Dahl & Hinton (2013). On the importance of initialization and momentum in deep learning. ICML. - Dauphin et al. (2014). Identifying and attacking the saddle point problem in high-dimensional non-convex optimization. NeurIPS. arXiv:1406.2572. - Hochreiter & Schmidhuber (1997). Flat minima. Neural Computation 9(1). - Keskar et al. (2017). On large-batch training for deep learning: generalization gap and sharp minima. ICLR. arXiv:1609.04836. - Dinh, Pascanu, Bengio & Bengio (2017). Sharp minima can generalize for deep nets. ICML. arXiv:1703.04933. - Goyal et al. (2017). Accurate, large minibatch SGD: training ImageNet in 1 hour. arXiv:1706.02677. - McCandlish et al. (2018). An empirical model of large-batch training. arXiv:1812.06162. - Shallue et al. (2019). Measuring the effects of data parallelism on neural network training. JMLR 20. arXiv:1811.03600. - Glorot & Bengio (2010). Understanding the difficulty of training deep feedforward neural networks. AISTATS. - He, Zhang, Ren & Sun (2015). Delving deep into rectifiers"},{"p":"week-04.html","w":"Week 4","t":"Regularization, Generalization, and Training Practice","h":"Regularization, Generalization, and Training Practice","a":"","x":"Learning objectives By the end of this week you should be able to: - Derive the bias-variance decomposition of squared error, and use it to diagnose underfitting and overfitting from learning curves. - Explain double descent and why classical \"more parameters means more overfitting\" intuition breaks for modern networks. - Derive L2 regularization and L1 regularization as MAP estimation under Gaussian and Laplace priors, explain their geometry, and state precisely when L2 regularization and weight decay differ. - Implement inverted dropout and DropConnect, explain the ensemble view, and know where dropout helps and hurts. - Apply label smoothing, early stopping, data augmentation, mixup and CutMix, and say what each one assumes about the data. - Run a sensible hyperparameter search (random search, successive halving, Bayesian optimization) and a learning-rate range test. - Debug a network systematically: overfit a single batch, check the initial loss, test gradients, read loss curves. - Build a reproducible training pipeline with logging, checkpointing, seeding and strict train/validation/test discipline, and run a regularization ablation. Week 3 taught you how to drive the training loss down. This week is about the quantity you actually care about: performance on data the model has never seen. A modern network has enough capacity to memorize its training set, including pure noise, so a low training loss by itself is nearly meaningless. What separates a model that works from one that merely fits is a combination of statistical ideas (the bias-variance trade-off, regularization, data augmentation) and unglamorous engineering practice (careful splits, logging, sanity checks, reproducible runs). The second half of the week is the part that most courses skip and most practit"},{"p":"week-04.html","w":"Week 4","t":"Regularization, Generalization, and Training Practice","h":"Generalization: what we are actually trying to do","a":"generalization","x":"Training a model means minimizing the empirical risk , the average loss over a finite training set drawn from some unknown distribution : What we care about is the population risk (also called expected risk or generalization error) . The difference is the generalization gap . We can never compute , but we can estimate it with data the optimizer did not touch, which is the entire purpose of the validation and test sets discussed later this week. Two things can go wrong, and they have opposite remedies: - Underfitting : the model cannot even fit the training data well. Training error is high. The fix is more capacity, better optimization, longer training, or less regularization. - Overfitting : the model fits the training data (including its noise) at the expense of new data. Training error is low, validation error is much higher. The fix is more data, a simpler model, more regularization, or better augmentation. The tension between these two failure modes is called the bias-variance trade-off, and it is worth deriving once, carefully."},{"p":"week-04.html","w":"Week 4","t":"Regularization, Generalization, and Training Practice","h":"The bias-variance trade-off","a":"bias-variance","x":"Suppose the data are generated by , where is the true function and is noise with and , independent of everything else. You draw a random training set of size and fit a model; call the resulting predictor . Because is random, is a random variable at every fixed test input . The bias-variance decomposition says how its expected squared error splits up. Derivation: bias-variance decomposition of squared error Fix a test point and write , (the average prediction over training sets), , and for a fresh noisy label. The expectation is over both the training set and the test noise . Add and subtract inside the square: The cross term vanishes because has zero mean and is independent of . So the error is . Now add and subtract : Since by definition of , the cross term disappears again and we obtain the three terms as follows. - Bias measures how far the average learned function is from the truth. It is a property of the hypothesis class and the learning procedure: a straight line fit to a sine wave has large bias no matter how much data you give it. - Variance measures how much the learned function changes from one training set to the next. A flexible model that chases noise in each sample has large variance. - Noise is the floor: no model can beat it under squared loss. Increasing capacity typically lowers bias and raises variance, producing the familiar U-shaped test-error curve. The classical picture: bias falls and variance rises with model capacity, so test error is U-shaped. The minimum sits at a model complexity that balances the two. You can measure all three terms empirically by refitting on many independent training sets, which is exactly what the demo below does and what the following chart reports. The setup: the truth is , the noise has , each training set has points"},{"p":"week-04.html","w":"Week 4","t":"Regularization, Generalization, and Training Practice","h":"Double descent","a":"double-descent","x":"The classical U-curve predicts that test error should explode once a model has enough parameters to interpolate the training data (zero training error). Practitioners had long noticed that neural networks ignore this advice: bigger networks often generalize better. Belkin, Hsu, Ma and Mandal (2019, PNAS, \"Reconciling modern machine-learning practice and the classical bias-variance trade-off\") put the two together in a single picture, the double descent curve : as capacity grows, test error first follows the classical U, peaks near the interpolation threshold where the model can just barely fit the training set exactly, and then falls again in the over-parameterized regime. Nakkiran et al. (2020, ICLR, \"Deep Double Descent\") showed the same shape for CNNs, ResNets and transformers, and also as a function of training time and of the number of training samples. Double descent (schematic). Test error peaks where the number of parameters is about the number of training points and then decreases again in the over-parameterized regime. Why does the peak appear? A concrete model { double-descent-why} The cleanest way to see it is linear regression with random features and training points. When you solve ordinary least squares. When there are infinitely many exact fits, and gradient descent started from zero converges to the one with the smallest norm , which is given by the pseudo-inverse: for a feature matrix . Near the feature matrix is square and nearly singular: its smallest singular values are tiny. The pseudo-inverse multiplies the label noise by , which is enormous, so the fitted weights are huge and the test predictions oscillate wildly. That is the peak. Far beyond the smallest nonzero singular value grows again, the minimum-norm solution becomes gentle, and the extra "},{"p":"week-04.html","w":"Week 4","t":"Regularization, Generalization, and Training Practice","h":"Weight decay: L2 and L1 regularization","a":"weight-decay","x":"The most widely used regularizer is also the simplest: add a penalty on the size of the weights to the loss. The intuition is that a function that changes slowly is simpler than one that changes quickly, and in a network with bounded inputs the slope of the function is controlled by the size of the weights. Small weights mean a smoother function, less able to chase noise. For a loss and a penalty strength : Why it is called weight decay { why-decay} With the L2 penalty the gradient is , so one SGD step with learning rate is Every step first multiplies the weights by a factor slightly below one (they \"decay\") and then takes the usual gradient step. For plain SGD, L2 regularization and weight decay are the same thing. For adaptive optimizers they are not, as we will see below. The Bayesian (MAP) view { map-view} Regularization has a clean probabilistic reading. The maximum a posteriori (MAP) estimate picks the parameters that maximize the posterior . Derivation: L2 is a Gaussian prior, L1 is a Laplace prior Take the logarithm and negate to get a minimization problem: The first term is the negative log-likelihood, which for i.i.d. data is a sum over examples and is what we call the loss (squared error for Gaussian noise, cross-entropy for categorical outputs). Gaussian prior. Let . Then . The MAP problem is . Dividing by gives L2 regularization with . A tighter prior (small ) means stronger regularization, and more data (large ) weakens the prior's relative influence. Laplace prior. Let . Then , giving the L1 penalty with . The Laplace density has a sharp peak at zero and heavier tails than the Gaussian, so it encodes the belief that most weights are exactly negligible while a few may be large. MAP view is a useful way to think , but be careful: deep-network weights are no"},{"p":"week-04.html","w":"Week 4","t":"Regularization, Generalization, and Training Practice","h":"Dropout","a":"dropout","x":"Dropout (Hinton et al., 2012 preprint, \"Improving neural networks by preventing co-adaptation of feature detectors\"; Srivastava, Hinton, Krizhevsky, Sutskever and Salakhutdinov, 2014, JMLR, \"Dropout: A Simple Way to Prevent Neural Networks from Overfitting\") is a regularizer with a strikingly different flavor. During training, each hidden unit is independently set to zero with probability . The network is forced to produce good predictions with a random subset of its units, so no unit can rely on the presence of specific other units. Hinton described the original intuition as preventing \"co-adaptation\": units that only work in combination. Inverted dropout { inverted-dropout} If a layer's output is masked with , i.i.d., the expected output is . The original paper kept all units at test time and scaled the weights by . Modern implementations use inverted dropout , which scales at training time so that inference needs no modification: At evaluation time dropout is the identity. The variance of each term is , which grows quickly as ; this is why above about is rarely used. Training uses a random thinned network (inverted scaling keeps expectations right); inference uses the full network. Dropout explorer: masks, scaling and output noise 540 The ensemble view { dropout-ensemble} A network with droppable units defines thinned sub-networks that share weights. Training with dropout is a stochastic approximation to training all of them at once; using the full network at test time approximates averaging their predictions. For a single linear layer followed by softmax the \"weight scaling\" rule is exact in the geometric mean sense, and for deep nonlinear networks it is an approximation that works well in practice. A more faithful but expensive estimate is Monte Carlo dropout : kee"},{"p":"week-04.html","w":"Week 4","t":"Regularization, Generalization, and Training Practice","h":"Label smoothing","a":"label-smoothing","x":"With hard one-hot targets, cross-entropy is minimized only when the correct logit is infinitely larger than the others, so a model that tries to fit the labels perfectly keeps growing its logits. The result is overconfident predictions. Label smoothing (Szegedy et al., 2016, CVPR, \"Rethinking the Inception Architecture for Computer Vision\") replaces the one-hot target with a mixture of the one-hot vector and the uniform distribution over classes: The loss is . With and the target for the correct class is and every other class receives . What the optimal logits look like For a target distribution , cross-entropy is minimized when , which means for every -- a finite gap. For , : . With a hard label the optimal gap is infinite, so the gradient never vanishes and the logits keep growing. : better calibrated probabilities, tighter clusters of penultimate-layer activations (Müller, Kornblith and Hinton, 2019, NeurIPS, \"When Does Label Smoothing Help?\"), and a small accuracy gain on many classification tasks. Downsides: it can hurt knowledge distillation , because it erases the information in the \"wrong\" classes' relative probabilities, and it changes what a predicted probability means."},{"p":"week-04.html","w":"Week 4","t":"Regularization, Generalization, and Training Practice","h":"Early stopping","a":"early-stopping","x":"Early stopping is the cheapest regularizer there is: monitor the validation loss during training and keep the weights from the epoch where it was lowest. The training procedure itself does the model selection, and the number of training steps becomes a hyperparameter chosen by validation data. For a linear model trained by gradient descent from zero initialization on a quadratic loss, early stopping is mathematically close to L2 regularization (Goodfellow, Bengio and Courville, Deep Learning , Ch. 7.8): directions with large curvature are learned first, directions with small curvature are learned slowly, so stopping at step leaves the small-curvature directions near zero, much like a ridge penalty with does. The equivalence is exact only for that simple setting, but it conveys why stopping helps: the model has not yet had time to fit the noise. A robust implementation has four ingredients: 1. Patience. Stop only after the validation metric has failed to improve for consecutive evaluations. Validation curves are noisy, so a patience of 1 stops far too early. 2. A minimum improvement min delta so that tiny fluctuations do not reset the counter. 3. Restore the best weights at the end (or simply load the best checkpoint), not the last ones. 4. Choose the monitored quantity deliberately. Validation loss and validation accuracy can disagree: loss can start increasing from overconfidence while accuracy still improves. Use the metric you will report, and keep the loss for diagnosis. Early stopping uses the validation set Because the stopping epoch is chosen with validation data, the validation score at that epoch is a slightly optimistic estimate of generalization. This is one more reason to keep a separate test set that you touch once."},{"p":"week-04.html","w":"Week 4","t":"Regularization, Generalization, and Training Practice","h":"Data augmentation","a":"data-augmentation","x":"If more data is the best regularizer, then manufacturing plausible new data is the next best. Data augmentation applies random label-preserving transformations to training examples so that the model sees a different variation every epoch. Each transformation encodes a piece of prior knowledge: \"a cat shifted by three pixels is still a cat\". The model is taught an invariance that it would otherwise have to learn from examples. Domain Typical augmentations Danger --- --- --- Images random crop with padding, horizontal flip, color jitter, rotation, scale, RandAugment, random erasing flipping digits or text; crops that cut out the object Audio time/pitch shift, additive noise, SpecAugment (masking time and frequency bands) pitch shift that changes the speaker or word Text synonym replacement, back-translation, token dropout/masking changes that flip sentiment or meaning Tabular noise injection, SMOTE for imbalance breaking correlations between features Rules of thumb: - Augment only the training set . Validation and test data get the deterministic preprocessing (resize, center crop, normalize) only. - Augmentations must be label-preserving for your task . Rotating a \"6\" by 180 degrees gives a \"9\". - Strong augmentation behaves like strong regularization: training loss stays higher and you must train longer. Do not read a high training loss under heavy augmentation as underfitting. - Apply augmentation on the fly in the data loader (each epoch sees fresh variants) rather than pre-generating a fixed larger dataset. - For a model that must be robust to a specific nuisance (lighting, noise, JPEG artifacts), include that nuisance in training. Notice the two dataset objects over the same files: the validation subset must be drawn from the copy with the evaluation transform. A fre"},{"p":"week-04.html","w":"Week 4","t":"Regularization, Generalization, and Training Practice","h":"Hyperparameter search","a":"hyperparameter-search","x":"Everything in this chapter has knobs: learning rate, weight decay, dropout rate, label-smoothing , batch size, width, depth, schedule length. Hyperparameters are the quantities you do not learn by gradient descent. The search problem: pick the configuration with the best validation score within a compute budget. Which hyperparameters matter most { which-hparams} Sensitivity is wildly uneven. For most vision and language models the order of importance is roughly: learning rate (by far the most important), then batch size and schedule length, then weight decay, then everything else (momentum , Adam , dropout, architecture width). Karpathy's advice is to fix a reasonable default for most knobs and spend the budget on the learning rate. Learning rate, weight decay and decay-like quantities should be searched on a logarithmic scale : the difference between and matters, the difference between and does not. Grid search versus random search { grid-random} Grid search tries every combination on a regular lattice. It scales exponentially with the number of hyperparameters and, more subtly, wastes budget when only a few hyperparameters matter: a grid tests only 3 distinct values of each hyperparameter. Random search samples every hyperparameter independently from its range; 9 random trials test 9 distinct values of each. Bergstra and Bengio (2012, JMLR, \"Random Search for Hyper-Parameter Optimization\") showed this is substantially more efficient when the response depends strongly on only a few of the dimensions, which is typical. Practical protocol: coarse to fine, with successive halving { hparam-protocol} 1. Start with a coarse random search over wide log-uniform ranges, using short runs (a few epochs or a fraction of the final budget). 2. Look at the results (a plot of score ve"},{"p":"week-04.html","w":"Week 4","t":"Regularization, Generalization, and Training Practice","h":"Debugging neural networks","a":"debugging","x":"Software bugs usually crash. Neural-network bugs usually train , just worse than they should. The model learns something, the loss goes down a bit, and you cannot tell whether the result reflects the method or a mistake. Karpathy's \"A Recipe for Training Neural Networks\" (2019) is the standard reference for the mindset: build up complexity slowly, verify every step, and never trust a result you cannot explain. The checks below are ordered from cheapest to most expensive; run them in this order every time you start a new model. Step 0: look at the data { look-at-data} Spend ten minutes looking at raw examples before writing a model. Print shapes, dtypes, value ranges, and class counts. Visualize images after the transformation pipeline (the tensor that actually enters the model), with labels. Look for duplicates, mislabeled examples, class imbalance, outliers and, for sequences, padding conventions. Many \"model\" problems are data problems. Step 1: check the initial loss { initial-loss} At initialization with small weights, the network's outputs are nearly uniform, so for -class cross-entropy the loss should be close to : for 10 classes, for 1000 classes, for a 50k-token vocabulary. For mean-squared-error regression on standardized targets expect about . If you see something far away (say 25 for CIFAR-10), the last layer's scale is wrong, the loss is applied to probabilities instead of logits, or labels and outputs are misaligned. Also check that the loss decreases when you add weight decay or regularization to zero and that the regularized loss at init equals the data loss plus the expected penalty. Step 2: overfit a single batch { overfit-single-batch} Take one small batch (say 8 to 32 examples), turn off augmentation, dropout and weight decay, and train on only that ba"},{"p":"week-04.html","w":"Week 4","t":"Regularization, Generalization, and Training Practice","h":"Experiment management","a":"experiment-management","x":"A result you cannot reproduce is an anecdote. Deep-learning experiments are expensive and noisy, so a little discipline pays for itself many times over. Train, validation and test discipline { splits} The three sets have three different jobs: - Training set: fit the weights. - Validation set (dev set): make decisions: hyperparameters, architecture, stopping epoch, which checkpoint to keep. - Test set: report a final number, once. The three splits and why the validation set slowly \"leaks\" into your choices while the test set must be touched only at the end. Why a separate test set? Every time you choose among options based on validation score, you fit the validation set a little. Try 200 hyperparameter configurations and the best validation score is biased upward (selection bias), by an amount that grows with the number of configurations and the noise in the metric. The test set has never influenced any decision, so it gives an unbiased estimate. Rules for keeping the discipline: 1. Split once, with a fixed seed, before any exploration , and store the indices. Never re-split in the middle of a project. 2. Prevent leakage. Fit normalization statistics, tokenizers, feature selectors and PCA on the training set only. Group related samples (patients, speakers, videos, near-duplicate images) into the same split. For time series split chronologically. 3. Test set once. If you look at it and then change the model, it is a validation set now. Report any number you get from repeated peeking as validation. 4. Match the deployment distribution in validation and test, not in training. You may train on synthetic or augmented data, but you evaluate on real, representative data. 5. Size matters. A test set of 1,000 examples gives a standard error of about percentage points at 90% accur"},{"p":"week-04.html","w":"Week 4","t":"Regularization, Generalization, and Training Practice","h":"Lab: a training pipeline with logging, checkpointing and a regularization ablation","a":"lab","x":"In this lab you build one reusable pipeline and use it for a controlled ablation. The task is deliberately small so the experiments run in minutes on a free Colab GPU or even a CPU: classify MNIST digits with an MLP, but train on only 5,000 examples so that overfitting is easy to provoke. (With the full 60,000 images an MLP barely overfits, which would make the ablation uninformative.) Goals. (1) A config-driven fit() with a deterministic split, per-epoch CSV logging, best/last checkpointing, resumption and early stopping. (2) An ablation over regularizers with 3 seeds each. (3) A short report with a table of mean and standard deviation and a paragraph of interpretation. Part 1: data and model { lab-data} Part 2: the pipeline { lab-pipeline} The fit function below does everything we discussed: seeding, parameter groups without decay on biases, optional label smoothing and mixup, cosine schedule with warmup, gradient-norm logging, a CSV log, best and last checkpoints, resumption and early stopping. Things to notice: the validation loss is always plain cross-entropy even when training uses label smoothing, so numbers are comparable across runs; the scheduler steps per iteration; we restore the best checkpoint before the final evaluation; the test set is not touched anywhere in fit . Part 3: the ablation { lab-ablation} Define the experiments. The baseline is an unregularized MLP; every other row adds exactly one regularizer, then a final row combines the best ones. Run three seeds for each. Part 4: analysis and the final test { lab-analysis} What to expect (qualitatively; do not copy numbers from here, run it yourself). The baseline's training loss approaches zero while the validation loss bottoms out early and rises, and its validation accuracy is the lowest. Each regula"},{"p":"week-04.html","w":"Week 4","t":"Regularization, Generalization, and Training Practice","h":"Assignment 1: what a strong submission contains","a":"assignment-1","x":"Assignment 1 (MLP from scratch, optimizers, and regularization experiments) is due at the end of this week. It combines Week 2 (backpropagation), Week 3 (optimizers, initialization) and this week. Consult the course page for the official handout; the points below describe what separates a strong submission from a merely complete one. Code - A correct NumPy MLP forward and backward pass, verified with a finite-difference gradient check (report the relative error, expect below in float64). - Optimizers (SGD, momentum, Adam) implemented from the update equations, tested on a small problem against a reference. - L2 regularization and dropout implemented correctly, with the train/eval distinction handled and tested. - Clean, vectorized, seeded, runnable from top to bottom, with a fixed split and no test-set peeking. Experiments and report - A single-batch overfit result and an initial-loss check, shown as evidence the implementation works. - Comparisons that change one factor at a time , with 3 or more seeds, mean and standard deviation, and curves rather than only final numbers. - A regularization study (weight decay strength, dropout rate) that finds the under- and over-regularized regimes, not just the best value. - Interpretation: why does a curve look the way it does? Link claims to the theory (bias-variance, geometry of L1/L2, noise in SGD). State what you tried that did not work. - Honest scoping: acknowledge limitations, the size of the validation set and what differences are within noise. Common reasons to lose points: reporting test accuracy for the best of many runs; a single seed; learning curves without axis labels; a gradient check done in float32; hyperparameters tuned on the test set; claims not supported by the plots."},{"p":"week-04.html","w":"Week 4","t":"Regularization, Generalization, and Training Practice","h":"Common mistakes and debugging tips","a":"common-mistakes","x":"Beyond the bug table, these are the mistakes that most often waste a week. - Tuning on the test set (even implicitly). Looking at test accuracy after every experiment turns it into a validation set. Symptom: your reported number is higher than any held-out data will ever give you. Fix: lock the test set away until the very end. - Judging regularization by training loss. Label smoothing and mixup raise training loss by design; dropout makes it noisy. Always compare validation metrics measured in eval mode. - Weight decay that does nothing, or too much. With a typical AdamW setup, versus is indistinguishable; collapses the weights. Search over several orders of magnitude, and remember that the effective strength depends on the learning rate. - Using Adam's weight decay argument and expecting AdamW behavior. Adam adds the penalty to the gradient (coupled); AdamW decouples. They behave differently. - Dropout in the wrong place. On the output layer, directly before the loss, or stacked with BatchNorm without thought. Symptom: noisy validation or train/eval mismatch. - Comparing runs with different seeds and concluding too much. A 0.3% accuracy difference on a 5,000-example validation set with one seed is noise. - Changing several things at once. Then you cannot tell which change mattered. One change per experiment, with the ablation logged. - Early stopping on a noisy metric with patience 1. You will stop in the first dip. Use patience of 5 to 20 evaluations. - A leaked split. Duplicates, near-duplicates, or the same subject in train and validation. Symptom: validation accuracy far above what the task should allow. Fix: deduplicate and split by group. - Resuming without optimizer state. The Adam moments restart at zero and the loss jumps. Save and load the optimizer and sche"},{"p":"week-04.html","w":"Week 4","t":"Regularization, Generalization, and Training Practice","h":"Exercises","a":"exercises","x":"Exercise 1: bias-variance by hand (derivation) A model predicts a constant fitted as the mean of noisy observations of a single target value (so the \"true function\" is constant), with . Compute bias , variance and expected squared test error. Now shrink the estimator: with . Show that for some the expected test error is lower than for , and find the best . Solution For : , so bias ; ; expected test error . For : mean , bias , variance . Expected error . Differentiate in : , so At the derivative is , so decreasing slightly lowers the error: introducing a little bias buys a larger reduction in variance. This is the James-Stein/ridge idea in one dimension. (The optimal depends on the unknown , which is why practical regularization strength is chosen by validation.) Exercise 2: ridge as MAP (derivation) Linear regression with , and prior . (a) Show that the MAP estimate minimizes and give . (b) Give the closed-form solution. Solution (a) . Multiply by : minimize , so (more noise or a tighter prior means more regularization). (b) Set the gradient to zero: , so . The matrix is always invertible for , which is also why ridge cures the ill-conditioning behind the interpolation peak. Exercise 3: soft-thresholding (derivation) Derive the minimizer of and show it is the soft-threshold . Solution The objective is convex. For the derivative is , zero at , valid if . For the derivative is , zero at , valid if . If , then the subdifferential at is , which contains exactly when , so . Combining the three cases gives the soft-threshold. Exercise 4: inverted dropout (derivation and coding) (a) Show that for inverted dropout on a unit with drop probability the mean is and the variance is . (b) Suppose you forget to call model.eval() at test time for a network with dropout. What happens to"},{"p":"week-04.html","w":"Week 4","t":"Regularization, Generalization, and Training Practice","h":"Key takeaways","a":"key-takeaways","x":"Key takeaways - Squared error splits into bias + variance + irreducible noise. Regularization trades a little bias for a lot less variance; the training loss alone cannot tell you the right trade. - Double descent shows that test error can fall again past the interpolation threshold. It does not remove the need for regularization: weight decay, augmentation and label smoothing smooth the peak. - L2 is a Gaussian prior and shrinks weights proportionally; L1 is a Laplace prior and produces exact zeros through its corners. With Adam use decoupled weight decay (AdamW) and skip biases and norm parameters. - Dropout multiplies activations by a random mask (inverted scaling keeps the expectation unchanged); it approximates an ensemble of thinned networks. Always switch it off with model.eval() . - Label smoothing caps the logit gap; mixup and CutMix train the model to behave linearly between examples; early stopping and augmentation are among the cheapest and most reliable regularizers. - Search hyperparameters on log scales with random search and successive halving; use Bayesian optimization when runs are expensive and few; use an LR range test to find the learning rate first. - Debug in order: look at the data, check the initial loss ( ), overfit a single batch, then scale up one change at a time; read loss curves as diagnoses. - Reproducible experiments mean fixed splits, seeded runs with several seeds, logged configs and metrics, resumable checkpoints and a test set touched exactly once."},{"p":"week-04.html","w":"Week 4","t":"Regularization, Generalization, and Training Practice","h":"Further reading","a":"further-reading","x":"Essential - Goodfellow, Bengio, Courville, Deep Learning (2016), Chapter 7, \"Regularization for Deep Learning\". Free online at deeplearningbook.org. - Srivastava, Hinton, Krizhevsky, Sutskever, Salakhutdinov (2014), \"Dropout: A Simple Way to Prevent Neural Networks from Overfitting\", JMLR 15. - Karpathy (2019), \"A Recipe for Training Neural Networks\" (blog post, karpathy.github.io). Generalization and double descent - Zhang, Bengio, Hardt, Recht, Vinyals (2017), \"Understanding deep learning requires rethinking generalization\", ICLR (arXiv:1611.03530). - Belkin, Hsu, Ma, Mandal (2019), \"Reconciling modern machine-learning practice and the classical bias-variance trade-off\", PNAS. - Nakkiran, Kaplun, Bansal, Yang, Barak, Sutskever (2020), \"Deep Double Descent: Where Bigger Models and More Data Hurt\", ICLR (arXiv:1912.02292). - Geman, Bienenstock, Doursat (1992), \"Neural Networks and the Bias/Variance Dilemma\", Neural Computation 4(1). Regularization techniques - Loshchilov, Hutter (2019), \"Decoupled Weight Decay Regularization\", ICLR (arXiv:1711.05101). - Wan, Zeiler, Zhang, LeCun, Fergus (2013), \"Regularization of Neural Networks using DropConnect\", ICML. - Szegedy, Vanhoucke, Ioffe, Shlens, Wojna (2016), \"Rethinking the Inception Architecture for Computer Vision\", CVPR (label smoothing; arXiv:1512.00567). - Müller, Kornblith, Hinton (2019), \"When Does Label Smoothing Help?\", NeurIPS (arXiv:1906.02629). - Zhang, Cisse, Dauphin, Lopez-Paz (2018), \"mixup: Beyond Empirical Risk Minimization\", ICLR (arXiv:1710.09412). - Yun, Han, Oh, Chun, Choe, Yoo (2019), \"CutMix: Regularization Strategy to Train Strong Classifiers with Localizable Features\", ICCV (arXiv:1905.04899). - Gal, Ghahramani (2016), \"Dropout as a Bayesian Approximation: Representing Model Uncertainty in Deep Lear"},{"p":"week-05.html","w":"Week 5","t":"Convolutional Neural Networks","h":"Convolutional Neural Networks","a":"","x":"Learning objectives By the end of this week you should be able to: - Explain why locality, weight sharing and translation equivariance make convolution a strong inductive bias for images, and prove that a linear shift-equivariant map is a convolution. - Distinguish convolution from cross-correlation, and compute output sizes for any combination of kernel, stride, padding and dilation. - Count parameters and multiply-accumulates (MACs) of standard, 1x1, grouped, and depthwise-separable convolutions. - Compute receptive fields layer by layer with the recurrence for size and jump. - Derive the gradients of a convolution layer with respect to its input, kernel and bias, and implement them in NumPy. - Describe LeNet-5, AlexNet and VGG in detail and say what each contributed. - Build a data pipeline with augmentation, train a CNN on CIFAR-10, and run controlled experiments on depth, kernel size and batch normalization. about 2012, the state of the art in computer vision was a pipeline: hand-designed features (SIFT, HOG), then a shallow classifier. Convolutional neural networks replaced the first half of that pipeline with features that are learned , and in doing so they set off the deep learning boom. But CNNs are not magic. They work because they build two facts about images directly into the architecture: nearby pixels are related, and the same pattern can appear anywhere. Understanding exactly how those facts become a layer, what the layer costs, what it can see, and how its gradients flow is the goal of this week. This week and the next form a pair. Here we build the toolbox (the convolution layer and its arithmetic, plus three classic networks and a training recipe). In Week 6 we use the toolbox to study modern architectures, transfer learning, detection and segmentation"},{"p":"week-05.html","w":"Week 5","t":"Convolutional Neural Networks","h":"Motivation: why not just use an MLP on pixels?","a":"motivation-why-not-just-use-an-mlp-on-pixels","x":"Take a modest color image of 224 x 224 pixels. Flattened, it is a vector with entries. Feed it to a fully connected hidden layer that has as many units as the image has pixels per channel, say hidden units (the size of a typical first convolutional feature map). The weight matrix has entries: about 1.9 terabytes in float32 for one layer . A convolution producing the same 64 feature maps has weights plus 64 biases. That is a factor of roughly fewer parameters. The saving is not an implementation trick; it is a statement about what we believe about the data. Fully connected versus locally connected versus convolutional connectivity. Locality shrinks each unit's fan-in; weight sharing (same colored arrows) reuses the same few weights at every position. Three assumptions A convolutional layer makes three assumptions, each of which reduces the hypothesis space in a different way. 1. Locality. The information needed to decide what is at a pixel is mostly contained in a small neighbourhood of that pixel. An edge, a corner or a texture patch is a local property. Instead of connecting every output unit to every input unit, we connect it only to a window of size . This cuts the fan-in from to . 2. Weight sharing (stationarity). The statistics of natural images are approximately the same everywhere: a vertical edge detector is useful in the top-left corner as well as in the bottom-right corner. So we use the same weights at every spatial position. This cuts the number of parameters by another factor of and also means that every training image supplies gradient contributions for each weight. 3. Translation equivariance. If the input pattern moves, the representation of that pattern should move by the same amount. This is not a third independent assumption: it is a consequence of us"},{"p":"week-05.html","w":"Week 5","t":"Convolutional Neural Networks","h":"Convolution and cross-correlation","a":"convolution-and-cross-correlation","x":"Definitions The discrete convolution of an image with a kernel is which flips the kernel (index ). The cross-correlation does not flip: Almost every deep learning library, including PyTorch's nn.Conv2d , implements cross-correlation but calls it convolution. The two differ only by a flip of the kernel, with . Because the kernel is learned , the distinction does not matter: if the optimal filter is , the network will learn in the cross-correlation parametrization. The flip only matters when you load a hand-designed kernel from signal-processing literature, when you want the associativity and commutativity of true convolution (relevant for theory, not for practice), or when you implement the backward pass (below), where a flipped kernel appears naturally. A single layer with channels For an input tensor and a weight tensor with bias , the output for channel is where is the stride, the dilation and the padding (out-of-range entries of are zero). Read this as: each output channel owns one 3D filter that spans all input channels and slides over space. The parameter count is independent of and . The cost, counted in multiply-accumulates (MACs), is that count of weights times the number of output positions: . A 3x3 kernel slides over a 5x5 input; each output entry is a dot product of the kernel with a window. The same nine weights are used at every position. From-scratch implementation The following NumPy implementation is deliberately naive (four nested loops) so that every index matches the formula above. It supports stride, zero padding and dilation, and we check it against the equivalent formulation with the flipped kernel. In real code you never write these loops: libraries lower convolution to a large matrix multiplication ( im2col , which unrolls every window into a col"},{"p":"week-05.html","w":"Week 5","t":"Convolutional Neural Networks","h":"Output-size arithmetic: stride, padding, dilation","a":"output-size-arithmetic-stride-padding-dilation","x":"Almost every shape bug in a CNN is an arithmetic mistake, so it pays to be fluent here. The formula For one spatial dimension with input length , kernel size , padding on each side, stride and dilation , the number of valid window positions is The derivation is one line of counting. The padded input has entries. A window with dilation spans entries. The first window starts at index and the last must start at or before . Starts are , so the number of starts is . Special cases worth memorizing: Setting Formula Example --- --- --- \"valid\" (p = 0, s = 1) 32, k=5 gives 28 (LeNet C1) \"same\" (s = 1, p = (k-1)/2, odd k) 3x3 conv with p = 1 keeps size halve resolution , , gives 56 gives 28 AlexNet conv1 gives 55 Stride, padding, dilation, intuitively - Stride evaluates the filter only at every -th position. It downsamples the feature map by about a factor of and reduces compute by , at the cost of skipping information. Strided convolution is a learned downsampling, an alternative to pooling. - Padding adds a border of (usually zero) values so that border pixels are covered by as many windows as interior pixels, and so that the output size can be controlled. Without padding, a stack of 3x3 convolutions loses 2 pixels per layer. Zero padding also lets the network detect the border (it sees the artificial zeros), which has been shown to be used to encode absolute position. Alternatives are reflection, replication and circular padding ( padding mode in PyTorch). - Dilation spaces the kernel taps pixels apart, so a 3x3 kernel with covers a 5x5 area using only 9 weights. It enlarges the receptive field without extra parameters and without downsampling, and is the backbone of DeepLab-style segmentation networks (Week 6) and of WaveNet-style temporal convolutions. The same 3x3 kernel wi"},{"p":"week-05.html","w":"Week 5","t":"Convolutional Neural Networks","h":"Channels, 1x1 convolutions, and depthwise-separable convolutions","a":"channels-1x1-convolutions-and-depthwise-separable-convolutions","x":"What \"channels\" mean A color image has three channels. After the first layer, an input with feature maps has channels, each map being the response of one filter. A single filter is three-dimensional (it spans all input channels), but it is slid only over the two spatial dimensions, hence \"2D convolution\". Each filter produces one output channel, and the number of filters is the main knob for model capacity: wider layers can detect more distinct patterns. The 1x1 convolution A convolution has . It looks at one pixel at a time and so it has no spatial extent: for each position it multiplies the channel vector by the same matrix . It is a per-pixel fully connected layer. What is it good for? - Channel mixing and dimensionality change. It lets you cheaply increase or decrease the number of channels (the \"bottleneck\" in ResNet, the \"reduction\" in Inception). - Adding nonlinearity and depth at almost no cost in receptive field (Network-in-Network, Lin et al. 2014). - Cost control. A 3x3 convolution from 256 to 256 channels costs weights. Squeezing to 64 channels with a 1x1, applying the 3x3 at 64 channels, and expanding back with another 1x1 costs weights, about fewer. Depthwise-separable convolution The standard convolution does two jobs in one step: it filters spatially and it mixes channels . The depthwise-separable convolution (Sifre's thesis 2014; popularized by Xception, Chollet 2017, and MobileNet, Howard et al. 2017) splits them: 1. Depthwise convolution: apply one filter per input channel (no mixing across channels). Weight tensor . In PyTorch: nn.Conv2d(C, C, k, groups=C) . 2. Pointwise convolution: a convolution that mixes the channels, . Standard convolution versus depthwise followed by pointwise, with parameter counts for C in = 64, C out = 128, k = 3. Counting p"},{"p":"week-05.html","w":"Week 5","t":"Convolutional Neural Networks","h":"Pooling and other ways to downsample","a":"pooling","x":"A convolution with stride 1 keeps the spatial resolution, but a classifier eventually needs to throw spatial detail away: the label \"cat\" does not depend on exactly which pixel the whiskers are at. Downsampling also makes deeper layers cheaper and enlarges their receptive field. The oldest tool for this is pooling. Max, average and global pooling { pooling-types} A pooling layer slides a window of size with stride over each channel independently and replaces every window with one number. It has no learned parameters and does not mix channels, so the number of channels is unchanged and the output size follows the same formula as a convolution. - Max pooling returns over the window. It keeps the strongest response of a feature detector in the window and discards its exact location within the window. The typical setting is (non-overlapping, halves the resolution) or (overlapping, used by AlexNet). - Average pooling returns the mean over the window. It is a fixed low-pass filter followed by subsampling. LeNet-5 used a trainable variant (a learned scale and bias per map); modern networks use the plain mean. - Global average pooling (GAP) uses a window equal to the whole feature map, turning a tensor into a -vector. It was introduced in Network-in-Network (Lin et al., 2014) and used by GoogLeNet and ResNet to replace the huge fully connected layers of AlexNet and VGG. A GAP followed by a linear layer is also what makes class-activation maps possible, since the logit of class is then a spatial average of a linear function of the last feature map. Why does max pooling help with invariance? Suppose a feature detector fires at position with value and the object shifts by one pixel so that it fires at instead. If both positions are in the same pooling window, the pooled output is "},{"p":"week-05.html","w":"Week 5","t":"Convolutional Neural Networks","h":"Receptive fields","a":"receptive-fields","x":"The receptive field of a unit is the set of input pixels that can influence its value. It answers a question that matters in practice: can this network even see enough context to solve the task? A detector for a 200-pixel-wide object cannot work if the final features only see 50 pixels. Tracing one unit of the third layer back through three 3-tap layers: its receptive field grows by (k-1) times the current jump at each layer. The recurrence { rf-recurrence} Track two quantities per layer : the receptive field size (in input pixels, along one dimension) and the jump , the distance in input pixels between the centers of two adjacent units in layer . At the input, and . A layer with kernel size , stride and dilation has effective kernel , and Why the recurrence holds A unit in layer looks at units of layer , whose centers are spaced input pixels apart. The outermost two of them are pixels apart, and each of them already has a receptive field of pixels. The union of the fields is therefore an interval of length (the fields overlap or touch whenever , which is always true). Strides multiply the jump because skipping units at layer skips input pixels. The center of the field follows , which tells you where in the image the field sits when padding is used. key observation is that the growth of is linear in depth for stride-1 layers (each 3x3 layer adds 2) but is multiplied by the accumulated stride after each downsampling: once the jump is , every further 3x3 layer adds . That is why downsampling is the cheapest way to see more context. Two checks with numbers: a stack of stride-1 3x3 layers has ; three of them have , exactly the same as a single 7x7 layer, which is the argument VGG made for small kernels (see below). Network (conv stack) Layers Final jump Receptive field --- "},{"p":"week-05.html","w":"Week 5","t":"Convolutional Neural Networks","h":"Backpropagation through a convolution layer","a":"backprop-conv","x":"A convolution layer is a linear map, so its backward pass is two more linear maps. The surprise is that they are themselves convolutions, which is why a framework can reuse the same optimized kernels for forward and backward. We derive the results in 1D with one channel first, then extend them. The backward pass of a 1D convolution (k = 3). The input gradient is a full convolution of the upstream gradient with the flipped kernel; the kernel gradient is a cross-correlation of the input with the upstream gradient. The 1D derivation { backprop-1d} Let the forward pass be for (stride 1, no padding), and let be the gradient arriving from above. The chain rule needs and when and 0 otherwise. Gradients of a 1D convolution Kernel. Each weight is used at every output position, so the contributions add up: This is a cross-correlation of the input with the upstream gradient (with playing the role of the kernel). Bias. . Input. The input entry feeds the outputs with (intersected with the valid range), each through the weight : Substituting shows this is a true convolution of the upstream gradient with . Equivalently it is a cross-correlation of , zero-padded by on each side, with the flipped kernel . This is the \"full\" convolution in signal-processing terms. consequences are worth stating explicitly. First, the shapes work out: has length , padding by on both sides gives , and a valid correlation with a length- kernel gives , the length of . Second, the weight gradient sums over all positions, so for a large feature map it is an average over many samples. This is part of the reason convolution kernels can be trained with few examples compared to fully connected layers, and also why the weight gradient is expensive: it costs as many MACs as the forward pass. Channels, stride and pad"},{"p":"week-05.html","w":"Week 5","t":"Convolutional Neural Networks","h":"Classic architectures: LeNet-5, AlexNet, VGG","a":"classic-architectures","x":"Three networks, spanning 1998 to 2014, contain most of the design vocabulary of CNNs. Reading their layer tables is worth the effort: you will practice the shape arithmetic, see where the parameters and the compute live, and understand which ideas each design introduced. LeNet-5, AlexNet and VGG-16 drawn on a common scheme: blue = convolution, amber = pooling, purple = fully connected. Resolution halves at each pool while channels grow. LeNet-5 (LeCun, Bottou, Bengio and Haffner, 1998) { lenet} LeNet-5 was designed for 32 x 32 grayscale images of handwritten digits (MNIST digits are 28 x 28 and padded to 32 so that characters are centered in the receptive fields of the top-level features). It is small enough to print in full. Layer Operation Output shape Parameters --- --- --- --- Input 32 x 32 image 1 x 32 x 32 0 C1 conv 5x5, 6 filters 6 x 28 x 28 S2 pool 2x2, stride 2 6 x 14 x 14 0 (12 in the original) C3 conv 5x5, 16 filters 16 x 10 x 10 (1,516 in the original) S4 pool 2x2, stride 2 16 x 5 x 5 0 (32 in the original) C5 conv 5x5, 120 filters 120 x 1 x 1 F6 fully connected 84 Output fully connected 10 Total 61,706 (about 60,850 in the original) Details that differ from the textbook \"modern\" version: the original used a scaled hyperbolic tangent, subsampling layers with a trainable coefficient and bias per map, a sparse connection table in C3 (each of the 16 maps looks at only a subset of the 6 input maps, which reduced parameters and broke symmetry), and radial-basis-function output units instead of softmax. The version found in most tutorials (and below) uses full connectivity, ReLU or tanh, average or max pooling and a softmax output. C5 is formally a convolution, but since its input is 5 x 5 and its kernel is 5 x 5, it is equivalent to a fully connected layer from 4"},{"p":"week-05.html","w":"Week 5","t":"Convolutional Neural Networks","h":"Data pipelines and augmentation","a":"data-pipelines","x":"A CNN is only as good as the data it is fed, and for image classification the way you feed it matters as much as the architecture. A good input pipeline does three jobs: it turns files into normalized tensors, it injects label-preserving randomness (augmentation) to regularize, and it does both fast enough that the GPU never waits. An image-classification input pipeline. Training uses random augmentation; evaluation uses a deterministic resize and center crop. Both use identical normalization. Preprocessing and normalization { normalization-input} Images arrive as uint8 arrays of shape with values in . PyTorch expects float32 tensors of shape (channels first). transforms.ToTensor() does the layout change and divides by 255. Then you standardize each channel with the dataset statistics, . The reasons are those of Week 3: inputs with zero mean and unit scale give well-conditioned first-layer gradients and make a single learning rate sensible for all layers. Typical constants are , for ImageNet and , for the CIFAR-10 training set. When you use a pretrained network, you must use the statistics it was trained with, whatever your own data looks like. Augmentation as a prior { augmentation} Augmentation encodes invariances you believe in: a cat remains a cat when flipped, shifted by a few pixels, slightly rotated, brighter or darker. Each augmented copy is a new, plausible training example, so the effective training set is much larger, which is a strong regularizer (see Week 4). Think of it as the cheapest way to inject the symmetry that the architecture itself lacks (CNNs are equivariant to translation only). Technique What it does Notes --- --- --- Random crop (with padding 4 on CIFAR; random-resized crop on ImageNet) translation and scale jitter The single most useful augme"},{"p":"week-05.html","w":"Week 5","t":"Convolutional Neural Networks","h":"Benchmarks: ImageNet and CIFAR","a":"benchmarks","x":"Two datasets frame most CNN research. Knowing their scale and the numbers on them lets you judge claims. ImageNet / ILSVRC. ImageNet (Deng et al., 2009) is a hierarchy of millions of labeled images organized by WordNet nouns. The ILSVRC classification task uses a subset: 1,000 classes, about 1.28 million training images (between 732 and 1,300 per class), 50,000 validation images and 100,000 test images, held out by the organizers (Russakovsky et al., 2015). The standard metrics are top-1 error (the highest-scoring class must be right) and top-5 error (the true class must be among the five highest). Images are variable-size photos; the usual training recipe takes 224 x 224 random-resized crops, and evaluation resizes to 256 and center-crops 224. The challenge ran from 2010 to 2017. CIFAR-10 and CIFAR-100. CIFAR-10 (Krizhevsky, 2009) has 60,000 color images of size 32 x 32 in 10 classes (airplane, automobile, bird, cat, deer, dog, frog, horse, ship, truck), 6,000 per class, split into 50,000 training and 10,000 test images. CIFAR-100 has the same images size but 100 classes with 600 images each. They are small enough to train a competitive model in minutes on a free GPU, which makes them the standard testbed for ideas, but they are also small enough to be easy to overfit by hyperparameter search against the test set: always hold out part of the training set for tuning. The ILSVRC story in numbers { ilsvrc-history} Three eras are visible. Until 2011 the winners used hand-crafted features (dense SIFT with Fisher vectors or similar) and improved error by a few points per year. In 2012 AlexNet cut the error by about ten points at once. After that, deeper and better-regularized CNNs improved it by a roughly constant factor each year until the challenge ended, passing the human"},{"p":"week-05.html","w":"Week 5","t":"Convolutional Neural Networks","h":"Lab: a CIFAR-10 classifier with controlled ablations","a":"lab","x":"This lab builds a small VGG-style network with batch normalization, trains it on CIFAR-10 with augmentation, evaluates it properly, and then runs three controlled ablations: depth, kernel size and batch normalization. It runs on a free Colab GPU; each training run of 30 epochs takes a few minutes. If you only have a CPU, set EPOCHS = 3 and train subset = 10 000 to check that the code works. The structure follows the pipeline from the data section: a deterministic train/validation split, augmentation on the training set only, the training loop from Week 4, and a final one-time look at the test set. Step 1: data with a proper validation split { lab-data} The 10,000 official test images are reserved for the very end. We carve 5,000 validation images out of the 50,000 training images for choosing hyperparameters and comparing ablations. Because the training subset needs augmentation and the validation subset does not, we create two dataset objects over the same files and select disjoint index sets from each. Check the printed per-channel mean and standard deviation: they should be near 0 and 1. If they are not, your normalization is wrong (a very common silent bug). Step 2: a configurable model { lab-model} The network is a stack of \"stages\". Each stage has n convs convolutions (kernel size k , optional batch norm, ReLU) followed by 2x2 max pooling. After the last stage, global average pooling and one linear layer produce the 10 logits. With channels 32, 64, 128 and three stages the default model has 288,746 parameters. The arguments let the ablations change depth, kernel size and normalization while keeping everything else identical. Notice that the parameter count is dominated by the last stage: its two 3x3 convolutions with 128 channels hold weights each in the second on"},{"p":"week-05.html","w":"Week 5","t":"Convolutional Neural Networks","h":"Common mistakes and debugging tips","a":"common-mistakes","x":"Symptom Likely cause Fix --- --- --- Validation accuracy far below training accuracy right away, but good in train() mode Forgot model.eval() so BN uses batch statistics, or evaluating with a tiny batch Call model.eval() ; evaluate with batch size at least 64; check model.training Val accuracy ~10% (chance) while train accuracy is fine Different normalization at train and test time, or labels shuffled in the val loader Reuse the same Normalize ; verify one val batch visually and by statistics Loss nan after a few steps Learning rate too high (especially without BN), a missing normalization of inputs, or fp16 overflow Lower LR, add warmup and gradient clipping, check xb.min()/max() Loss stuck at 2.30 Dead network: huge LR, wrong init, ReLU after the final layer, or labels not matching inputs Overfit one batch; remove activation on logits; lower LR Training accuracy is 100% but validation is much lower Overfitting (no augmentation, too large model) Add crop and flip, weight decay, label smoothing, or more data Augmentation applied to the validation set One transform shared by all splits Separate train tf and eval tf ; apply random ops to train only Shape error mat1 and mat2 shapes cannot be multiplied Hard-coded Linear(in features) after the conv stack when the input size changed Use global average pooling or print the shape after features RuntimeError: ... expected ... Float but found Byte or all-black images ToTensor() omitted or applied twice Check dtype and range of one batch Slow training, GPU idle Data loading bottleneck num workers 2 to 8, pin memory=True , persistent workers=True ; profile with a loop that only iterates the loader Results differ between runs of the same code Random seeds, non-deterministic CUDA kernels (cuDNN autotuning) Seed everything, and accep"},{"p":"week-05.html","w":"Week 5","t":"Convolutional Neural Networks","h":"Exercises","a":"exercises","x":"Exercise 1: output sizes (computation) A network begins with Conv2d(3, 64, kernel size=7, stride=2, padding=3) , then MaxPool2d(kernel size=3, stride=2, padding=1) , then Conv2d(64, 64, 3, padding=1, dilation=2) on a image. (a) Give the output spatial size after each layer. (b) What padding keeps the size unchanged for the dilated conv, and what is its effective kernel size? (c) Why does a stride-2, kernel-2 max pool on a map lose one row and column, and how does ceil mode=True change that? Solution Use . (a) Conv 7x7: . Pool 3x3 stride 2 padding 1: . Dilated 3x3 with , : , so . Final: . (b) For the size to be unchanged with stride 1 you need , so (in general ). Effective kernel size is 5 with only 9 non-zero taps. (c) : the last row and column are never covered, since windows start at 0, 2, 4 and the one starting at 6 would need index 7. ceil mode=True uses , allowing a partial window at the border. Exercise 2: parameters and MACs (computation) A feature map is and you apply a conv with 128 output channels. Count the parameters (ignore the bias) and MACs for (a) a standard conv, (b) a conv, (c) a depthwise-separable (depthwise then pointwise), (d) a grouped conv with 4 groups. What is the ratio (c)/(a) and how does it compare with the formula ? Solution (a) Params ; MACs . (b) Params ; MACs . (c) Depthwise plus pointwise gives parameters; MACs . (d) Each group maps input channels to output channels: parameters, a quarter of (a); MACs . Ratio (c)/(a) . The formula gives . Exactly equal here because both are the same algebra: . Exercise 3: receptive field (computation) Compute the receptive field size and jump after each layer of: conv 3x3 s1, conv 3x3 s2, conv 3x3 s1, conv 3x3 s1. Then compare against four 3x3 stride-1 convs. What is the receptive field of a stack of st"},{"p":"week-05.html","w":"Week 5","t":"Convolutional Neural Networks","h":"Key takeaways","a":"key-takeaways","x":"Key takeaways - A convolution layer is a linear map with three constraints: locality , weight sharing and the resulting translation equivariance . They cut parameters from to and encode the right prior for images. - Deep learning \"convolution\" is cross-correlation; the distinction matters only for the flip in the backward pass. - Output size: . Learn it, and print shapes every time. - Parameters and MACs scale as and respectively. 1x1 convolutions mix channels cheaply; depthwise-separable convolutions cut cost by roughly . - Receptive field grows by jump per layer: stride and dilation make it grow faster, and the effective receptive field is a smaller, Gaussian-like region. - The backward pass of a conv is again a conv: the input gradient is a transposed convolution, the kernel gradient is a correlation of input with output gradient. - LeNet introduced the recipe, AlexNet scaled it with ReLU, GPUs, dropout and augmentation, VGG showed that stacks of 3x3 convolutions beat large kernels; global average pooling removes the parameter-heavy head. - Training a CNN well is as much about the pipeline (normalization, augmentation, a clean validation split, a good schedule) as the architecture. Ablate one factor at a time, with several seeds."},{"p":"week-05.html","w":"Week 5","t":"Convolutional Neural Networks","h":"Further reading","a":"further-reading","x":"Essential - Yann LeCun, Leon Bottou, Yoshua Bengio, Patrick Haffner. Gradient-based learning applied to document recognition. Proceedings of the IEEE, 1998. The LeNet-5 paper, still the best introduction to the ideas. - Alex Krizhevsky, Ilya Sutskever, Geoffrey Hinton. ImageNet classification with deep convolutional neural networks. NeurIPS 2012. The AlexNet paper. - Karen Simonyan, Andrew Zisserman. Very deep convolutional networks for large-scale image recognition. ICLR 2015, arXiv:1409.1556. The VGG paper. Foundations and history - Kunihiko Fukushima. Neocognitron: a self-organizing neural network model for a mechanism of pattern recognition unaffected by shift in position. Biological Cybernetics, 1980. The architectural precursor with S-cells and C-cells. - David Hubel, Torsten Wiesel. Receptive fields, binocular interaction and functional architecture in the cat's visual cortex. Journal of Physiology, 1962. The neuroscience that inspired the Neocognitron. - Yann LeCun et al. Backpropagation applied to handwritten zip code recognition. Neural Computation, 1989. - Olga Russakovsky et al. ImageNet large scale visual recognition challenge. IJCV, 2015, arXiv:1409.0575. - Alex Krizhevsky. Learning multiple layers of features from tiny images. Technical report, University of Toronto, 2009 (CIFAR-10 and CIFAR-100). Convolution variants and analysis - Fisher Yu, Vladlen Koltun. Multi-scale context aggregation by dilated convolutions. ICLR 2016, arXiv:1511.07122. - Andrew Howard et al. MobileNets: efficient convolutional neural networks for mobile vision applications. 2017, arXiv:1704.04861. - Francois Chollet. Xception: deep learning with depthwise separable convolutions. CVPR 2017, arXiv:1610.02357. - Wenjie Luo, Yujia Li, Raquel Urtasun, Richard Zemel. Understanding the e"},{"p":"week-06.html","w":"Week 6","t":"Modern Vision Architectures, Transfer Learning, Detection and Segmentation","h":"Modern Vision Architectures, Transfer Learning, Detection and Segmentation","a":"","x":"Learning objectives By the end of this week you should be able to: - Explain the degradation problem and show, with a gradient-flow argument, why residual connections ease optimization of deep networks. - Draw and count parameters of basic and bottleneck residual blocks, ResNeXt blocks, dense blocks, Inception modules and MobileNetV2 inverted residuals. - Use EfficientNet-style compound scaling to trade depth, width and resolution against FLOPs, and describe how ConvNeXt modernizes a ResNet. - Choose between feature extraction, partial and full fine-tuning for a new dataset; handle batch norm, learning rates and domain shift correctly. - Compute IoU, run non-maximum suppression, and compute precision-recall curves, AP and mAP by hand and in code. - Describe how R-CNN, Fast/Faster R-CNN, SSD, YOLO, RetinaNet and anchor-free detectors differ, and what the loss and the output tensor of each look like. - Describe FCN, U-Net, DeepLab (atrous convolution, ASPP) and Mask R-CNN, and compute IoU and Dice for masks. - Fine-tune a pretrained ResNet, run and evaluate a pretrained detector, and train a small U-Net. Week 5 gave you the convolutional toolbox and three classic networks. Those networks stop at about 20 layers, and after VGG people discovered that simply stacking more layers did not work. This week starts with the idea that fixed that, the residual connection, and follows its consequences: networks that are hundreds of layers deep, networks that are cheap enough to run on a phone, and a recipe that makes almost every other vision problem easier, which is to start from a network pretrained on ImageNet. We then leave classification: detection asks what is where (a variable-size list of boxes) and segmentation asks which pixels belong to what . Both reuse the same backbones"},{"p":"week-06.html","w":"Week 6","t":"Modern Vision Architectures, Transfer Learning, Detection and Segmentation","h":"Residual networks","a":"resnet","x":"The degradation problem { degradation} By 2014 the trend was clear: deeper networks (AlexNet's 8 layers, VGG's 16 to 19, GoogLeNet's 22) were more accurate. Batch normalization (Week 3) had removed the worst vanishing and exploding signal problems at initialization, so one might expect that a 56-layer plain network would at least match a 20-layer one. It does not. He et al. (2016) trained plain CNNs of 20 and 56 layers on CIFAR-10 and found that the deeper network had higher training error as well as higher test error. This is the degradation problem . Notice what this does and does not say. It is not overfitting, because training error went up. It is not (mainly) vanishing gradients, because the networks use batch norm and their gradients were measured to be healthy. The deeper network is a superset in function space: you could construct a 56-layer network from a trained 20-layer one by adding 36 layers that compute the identity. A solver that is given more capacity should find a solution at least that good, so the failure is one of optimization : SGD cannot find the identity-like configuration for the extra layers, or finds it only very slowly. Learning \"do nothing\" with a stack of nonlinear layers (convolution, BN, ReLU) is surprisingly hard, because the identity is not a point that a randomly initialized stack is anywhere near. Residual learning { residual-learning} The fix is to change what the layers are asked to learn. Let be the input of a block and the mapping we want it to compute. Instead of making the stacked layers fit directly, make them fit the residual and add the input back: If the best thing for a block to do is nothing, the optimizer only has to push the weights of toward zero, which is easy. If the optimal mapping is close to the identity, only needs"},{"p":"week-06.html","w":"Week 6","t":"Modern Vision Architectures, Transfer Learning, Detection and Segmentation","h":"Efficient and scalable designs","a":"efficient-designs","x":"ResNet answered \"how do we train deep networks?\". The next question was economic: given a budget of parameters, FLOPs, memory or milliseconds on a phone, what is the best architecture? The designs in this section are better read as answers to different budgets than as a linear sequence of improvements. Inception: parallel branches and 1x1 bottlenecks { inception} GoogLeNet (Szegedy et al., 2014, published at CVPR 2015) won ILSVRC 2014 with 22 layers and only about 6.8M parameters, roughly 20 times fewer than VGG-16, by replacing the choice \"which kernel size should this layer use?\" with \"use all of them\". An Inception module applies, in parallel and to the same input, a convolution, a convolution, a convolution and a max-pool, then concatenates the resulting feature maps along the channel axis. The network can then choose, per module and per spatial position, how much to rely on small versus large receptive fields. The naive version is too expensive: every branch sees all input channels. The key trick is to put a convolution before the expensive and branches to shrink the channel count, exactly the same device as the ResNet bottleneck. Count it for an input with channels and a branch with 32 output channels: Branch Parameters (no bias) --- --- direct , reduce , then , A tenfold reduction, at the price of one extra nonlinearity (which is itself useful). The reduction branches turn the module from a \"wide\" layer into a family of narrow computations, and this idea, a convolution as a learned channel-mixing projection, is now ubiquitous. GoogLeNet also attached two auxiliary classifiers to intermediate layers during training, injecting gradient into the middle of the network. This was a pre-batch-norm workaround for the same optimization difficulty that ResNet later solved;"},{"p":"week-06.html","w":"Week 6","t":"Modern Vision Architectures, Transfer Learning, Detection and Segmentation","h":"Transfer learning and fine-tuning","a":"transfer-learning","x":"Almost nobody trains a vision network from scratch for a new problem. A network pretrained on a large dataset (ImageNet-1k with 1.28M images, or something larger) has learned a hierarchy of visual features, and those features are a far better starting point than random weights when your own dataset has hundreds or thousands of images instead of millions. Transfer learning is the practice of reusing such a network; fine-tuning is the case where some or all of the pretrained weights are updated on the new task. Why transfer works: features are general at the bottom { why-transfer-works} Yosinski, Clune, Bengio and Lipson (2014) measured how transferable the layers of an AlexNet-style network are by copying the first layers from a network trained on one half of ImageNet classes to another network, freezing or fine-tuning them, and training the rest on the other half. First-layer features (Gabor-like edge and colour detectors, see Week 5) transferred essentially perfectly, while features near the top were specific to the source task. A sensible mental model for a pretrained CNN is therefore: - early layers : edges, textures, colour blobs, nearly universal; - middle layers : parts and motifs, reusable across many natural-image tasks; - last layers : combinations specific to the source label set (the 1000 ImageNet classes), the part you most often replace. Kornblith, Shlens and Le (2019) later studied 12 image classification datasets and found that, with fine-tuning, better ImageNet accuracy generally predicts better transfer accuracy, so a stronger backbone is usually a better starting point; with a frozen backbone this correlation is weaker and depends on the regularization used during pretraining. Feature extraction versus fine-tuning { feature-extraction-vs-finetuning} Th"},{"p":"week-06.html","w":"Week 6","t":"Modern Vision Architectures, Transfer Learning, Detection and Segmentation","h":"Object detection","a":"object-detection","x":"Classification outputs one label per image. Object detection outputs a variable-size set of objects, each with a class label, a bounding box and a confidence score. That changes almost everything: the output size is not fixed, one image may contain 0 or 80 objects, boxes must be matched to ground truth to define a loss, and duplicates must be removed. Most of the history of detection is a sequence of answers to two questions: where do candidate boxes come from, and how do we turn dense candidate predictions into a clean set? Intersection over union and box encoding { iou} Everything in detection needs a notion of how well two boxes agree. The standard measure is intersection over union (IoU, also called the Jaccard index): It is 1 for identical boxes, 0 for disjoint ones, and is invariant to the scale of the boxes (which a plain distance of coordinates is not: a 5-pixel error is huge for a 20-pixel box and negligible for a 500-pixel one). Two boxes of shifted by 20 pixels horizontally overlap in pixels, so ; shifted by 50 pixels it falls to . Left: IoU as the area of the overlap divided by the area of the union. Middle: anchor boxes of several scales and aspect ratios tiled at every location of a feature map. Right: non-maximum suppression keeps the best-scoring box of each cluster of overlapping boxes. Detectors do not regress raw corner coordinates. Given a reference box (an anchor or proposal) with center and size and a ground-truth box , the standard parametrization of Girshick et al. predicts the offsets so that the targets are roughly scale-free and centered near zero, and a predicted box is recovered by inverting these formulas. The loss on these offsets is a smooth (Huber-like) loss: quadratic for small errors, linear for large ones, so a few badly localized box"},{"p":"week-06.html","w":"Week 6","t":"Modern Vision Architectures, Transfer Learning, Detection and Segmentation","h":"Semantic and instance segmentation","a":"segmentation","x":"Segmentation predicts a label for every pixel. Semantic segmentation assigns each pixel a class (\"road\", \"person\") without distinguishing individual objects; instance segmentation separates each object (\"person 1\", \"person 2\") and gives a mask per instance; panoptic segmentation combines both (countable \"things\" as instances, uncountable \"stuff\" such as sky as semantic regions). The output is a tensor of shape (class logits per pixel), so the architecture needs a way to go from a low-resolution, semantically rich feature map back to full resolution without losing detail. Fully convolutional networks { fcn} Long, Shelhamer and Darrell (2015) made the key observation: a classification CNN becomes a dense predictor if its fully connected layers are re-interpreted as convolutions. A fully connected layer on a map is the same as a convolution with 4096 filters; written that way, the network accepts images of any size and outputs a grid of class scores (a coarse heatmap, stride 32) rather than one vector. To get back to full resolution they upsample with transposed convolutions (learned, initialised as bilinear interpolation) and, crucially, fuse the coarse prediction with finer predictions from earlier layers: FCN-32s upsamples the stride-32 prediction directly (blurry), FCN-16s adds the stride-16 pooling layer, FCN-8s adds the stride-8 layer too. The principle, \"deep and coarse for what , shallow and fine for where \", is the template for everything after it. Transposed convolution A transposed convolution (sometimes loosely called deconvolution) is the adjoint of a strided convolution: with stride it maps an map to roughly by inserting zeros between the inputs (or, equivalently, scattering each input value times the kernel into the output). With a kernel size not divisible "},{"p":"week-06.html","w":"Week 6","t":"Modern Vision Architectures, Transfer Learning, Detection and Segmentation","h":"Lab: fine-tune a ResNet, evaluate a detector, train a U-Net","a":"lab","x":"This lab has three parts. Part A fine-tunes a pretrained ResNet-18 on a small dataset with the three recipes, Part B runs a pretrained Faster R-CNN and evaluates it with the AP code from above, and Part C trains a small U-Net on synthetic shapes. Everything runs on a free Colab GPU (Parts A and B also work on CPU, slowly). Set the runtime to GPU first. Throughout the lab, print shapes, and compare the recipes , not only the final number. Part A: fine-tuning ResNet-18 on Flowers102 { lab-finetune} Flowers102 has 102 flower categories; its official \"train\" split has only 10 images per class (1,020 images), which makes it a realistic small-data problem. Use the \"val\" split (also 1,020) for validation and a slice of \"test\" at the end. Next, the training function. It re-applies the freeze policy every epoch (see the batch norm warning above) and evaluates on the validation split. On this problem you should see the ordering \"full partial head\" in validation accuracy only if the learning rates are right; the linear probe is typically already good (flowers are close to ImageNet), and the interesting observations are how many epochs each recipe needs, how large the gap between train and validation is for the full recipe, and what happens when you remove the set mode call or set all learning rates to (the pretrained features are destroyed in the first epoch). Record your numbers; they vary with seed, so run at least two seeds before you conclude anything. Cache features for the linear probe For recipe A you can run the backbone once and train logistic regression on the 512-dimensional features. This is faster by a large factor and gives an accuracy baseline in under a minute: feats = nn.Sequential( list(model.children())[:-1]) then feats(x).flatten(1) over the dataset with torch."},{"p":"week-06.html","w":"Week 6","t":"Modern Vision Architectures, Transfer Learning, Detection and Segmentation","h":"Common mistakes and debugging tips","a":"common-mistakes","x":"Symptom Likely cause Fix --- --- --- Fine-tuned model is at chance level, or accuracy drops after the first epoch Learning rate too large for the backbone; or labels shuffled out of sync with images Backbone LR 10x to 100x smaller than the head; check a batch visually and overfit 20 images first Pretrained model gives much worse results than expected Wrong normalization (no ImageNet mean/std, or 0-255 range), BGR instead of RGB, wrong resolution Use weights.transforms() ; print x.mean() , x.std() per channel \"Frozen\" backbone still changes; validation fluctuates wildly BN layers left in train() mode (running stats update, batch stats used) model.eval() for frozen parts, re-apply after each model.train() Train accuracy 100%, validation poor, small dataset Overfitting by unfreezing too much; too little augmentation Freeze more, add augmentation, weight decay, early stopping, or use more data Training loss does not decrease at all with a frozen backbone Optimizer built before requires grad change, or no trainable parameters in the optimizer Build the optimizer from the final parameter list; print the trainable parameter count Detector outputs hundreds of overlapping boxes NMS missing or threshold too high; score threshold too low Check box nms thresh , class-wise NMS ( batched nms ), a higher score threshold Detector never detects small objects Input too small, anchors too large, stride of the finest map too coarse Increase input size, use FPN, adapt anchor sizes to the dataset statistics mAP is near zero although drawings look right Box format mix-up: (x, y, w, h) versus (x1, y1, x2, y2) ; image-size scaling mismatch; wrong label indexing (0 is background in torchvision) Assert x2 x1 , plot boxes on images, check label ids mAP much higher than published numbers Evaluating"},{"p":"week-06.html","w":"Week 6","t":"Modern Vision Architectures, Transfer Learning, Detection and Segmentation","h":"Exercises","a":"exercises","x":"Exercise 1: Gradient through a residual stack (derivation) Consider for . (a) Write as a sum. (b) Using the chain rule, show that contains the term with coefficient exactly 1. (c) Why does this not guarantee that the gradient is well scaled? Solution (a) by telescoping. (b) Differentiating, , so . The identity part passes the gradient unchanged to every layer. (c) The other terms can be large or have arbitrary sign, and their number grows with (the sum has up to terms and each depends on earlier branches). In the worst case, the gradient norm grows like a product of , which is why we initialise the last BN scale of each branch at zero or scale branches by a small constant. Exercise 2: Parameter counting (a) Count the weights (no biases or BN) of a ResNet basic block with 128 input and output channels, and of a bottleneck block with 512 input/output channels and bottleneck width 128. (b) Count the weights of a MobileNetV2 inverted residual with 32 input and output channels and expansion factor 6. (c) Compare each to a plain convolution from to channels with the same . Solution (a) Basic: . Bottleneck: . (b) Expand ; depthwise ; project ; total . (c) A single plain with has 147,456 weights, with 2.36M, and with 9,216. So the bottleneck block works on a 512-wide stream for about the cost of one -channel convolution plus the projections, and the inverted residual is only 1.5 times the cost of one dense convolution but with a 192-channel internal space. Exercise 3: Depthwise separable cost ratio Derive the ratio for the cost of a depthwise separable convolution relative to a standard one. For and , how many times cheaper is it? What happens when (as in ConvNeXt)? Solution Standard: multiply-adds. Depthwise: each of the channels is filtered by one kernel, . Pointwise: . The r"},{"p":"week-06.html","w":"Week 6","t":"Modern Vision Architectures, Transfer Learning, Detection and Segmentation","h":"Key takeaways","a":"key-takeaways","x":"Key takeaways - Depth hurts a plain network through optimization (the degradation problem), not capacity. A residual block gives an identity path for activations and gradients, and its unrolled form is an ensemble of paths of many lengths. - Bottlenecks (ResNet, Inception) and depthwise separable convolutions (MobileNet) are two ways to cut FLOPs; the inverted residual keeps skip connections between the narrow tensors and applies nonlinearities only in the wide space. - Compound scaling grows depth, width and resolution together ( , , , with so FLOPs double per ). ConvNeXt shows that much of the Transformer's advantage came from the recipe and macro-design, not attention. - Transfer learning is the default. Choose linear probe, partial or full fine-tuning according to data size and domain gap; use ImageNet normalization, small backbone learning rates, discriminative learning rates, and keep frozen BN layers in eval mode. - Detection = candidate boxes + classification + box regression + duplicate removal. R-CNN to Faster R-CNN shared and then learned the proposals; SSD, YOLO and RetinaNet predict densely (focal loss fixes class imbalance); FCOS and CenterNet drop anchors; DETR drops NMS. - IoU measures overlap; NMS keeps the best box per cluster; AP is the area under the precision-recall envelope per class and mAP averages over classes (COCO also over IoU thresholds 0.5 to 0.95). - Segmentation needs both context and resolution: FCN (convolutionalization and skip fusion), U-Net (encoder-decoder with concatenated skips), DeepLab (atrous convolutions and ASPP), Mask R-CNN (a per-RoI mask branch with RoIAlign). Evaluate with mIoU or Dice, never pixel accuracy alone. Assignment 2 Assignment 2 is due at the end of Week 8. The convolutional-network and transfer-learning materi"},{"p":"week-06.html","w":"Week 6","t":"Modern Vision Architectures, Transfer Learning, Detection and Segmentation","h":"Further reading","a":"further-reading","x":"Essential (marked with a star): - ( ) Kaiming He, Xiangyu Zhang, Shaoqing Ren, Jian Sun. \"Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition.\" CVPR 2016 (arXiv:1512.03385). The original ResNet paper, short and clear. - ( ) Mingxing Tan, Quoc V. Le. \"EfficientNet: Rethinking Model Scaling for Convolutional Neural Networks.\" ICML 2019 (arXiv:1905.11946). - ( ) Olaf Ronneberger, Philipp Fischer, Thomas Brox. \"U-Net: Convolutional Networks for Biomedical Image Segmentation.\" MICCAI 2015 (arXiv:1505.04597). - ( ) Joseph Redmon, Santosh Divvala, Ross Girshick, Ali Farhadi. \"You Only Look Once: Unified, Real-Time Object Detection.\" CVPR 2016 (arXiv:1506.02640). Architectures: - Kaiming He et al. \"Identity Mappings in Deep Residual Networks.\" ECCV 2016 (arXiv:1603.05027). - Saining Xie et al. \"Aggregated Residual Transformations for Deep Neural Networks\" (ResNeXt). CVPR 2017 (arXiv:1611.05431). - Gao Huang, Zhuang Liu, Laurens van der Maaten, Kilian Weinberger. \"Densely Connected Convolutional Networks.\" CVPR 2017 (arXiv:1608.06993). - Christian Szegedy et al. \"Going Deeper with Convolutions.\" CVPR 2015 (arXiv:1409.4842); and \"Rethinking the Inception Architecture for Computer Vision.\" CVPR 2016 (arXiv:1512.00567). - Andrew Howard et al. \"MobileNets: Efficient Convolutional Neural Networks for Mobile Vision Applications.\" 2017 (arXiv:1704.04861). - Mark Sandler et al. \"MobileNetV2: Inverted Residuals and Linear Bottlenecks.\" CVPR 2018 (arXiv:1801.04381). - Zhuang Liu et al. \"A ConvNet for the 2020s.\" CVPR 2022 (arXiv:2201.03545). - Andreas Veit, Michael Wilber, Serge Belongie. \"Residual Networks Behave Like Ensembles of Relatively Shallow Networks.\" NeurIPS 2016 (arXiv:1605.06431). Transfer learning: - Jason Yosinski, Jeff Clune, Yoshua Bengio, Hod Lipson. \"How transferable are featu"},{"p":"week-07.html","w":"Week 7","t":"Sequence Models: RNNs, LSTMs, and the Road to Attention","h":"Sequence Models: RNNs, LSTMs, and the Road to Attention","a":"","x":"Learning objectives By the end of this week you should be able to: - Classify sequence problems (one-to-many, many-to-one, many-to-many) and formulate language modeling as next-token prediction, including cross-entropy, bits per character and perplexity. - Write the equations of a vanilla RNN, unroll it, and implement the forward pass from scratch in NumPy and with torch.nn.RNN , with correct tensor shapes. - Derive backpropagation through time, show how the gradient is a product of Jacobians, and use the spectral radius to explain vanishing and exploding gradients. Apply truncated BPTT and gradient clipping. - Explain every gate of an LSTM and a GRU, and argue from the cell-state recursion why gating improves gradient flow. - Build bidirectional and stacked recurrent networks and know when a bidirectional model is not allowed (autoregressive generation). - Implement an encoder-decoder with teacher forcing, discuss exposure bias, and implement greedy and beam-search decoding. - Derive Bahdanau (additive) and Luong (multiplicative) attention, and read an attention heatmap as a soft alignment. - Compare recurrent models with 1D convolutional and temporal convolutional networks for translation, speech and time-series forecasting. far every input in this course had a fixed size: a vector of features, an image of pixels. Much of the world is not like that. A sentence has a variable number of words, an audio clip a variable number of samples, a patient record a variable number of visits, a stock price an ever-growing history. The order matters (\"dog bites man\" is not \"man bites dog\"), the length varies, and the relevant information may be separated by hundreds of steps. This week is the story of how neural networks learned to handle such data. It begins with the recurrent neu"},{"p":"week-07.html","w":"Week 7","t":"Sequence Models: RNNs, LSTMs, and the Road to Attention","h":"Sequential data and the task zoo","a":"sequence-tasks","x":"A sequence is an ordered list whose length may differ from example to example. Each element might be a one-hot vector for a character, an index into a vocabulary of words, a frame of audio features, or a vector of sensor measurements. Three properties make sequences different from the fixed-size inputs of previous weeks: 1. Variable length. A model must accept and with the same parameters. 2. Order dependence. Permuting the elements changes the meaning, so a model cannot treat the input as a bag. 3. Long-range dependencies. The right output at step may depend on something seen at step : the subject of a sentence determines verb agreement many words later, a closing bracket must match an opening one far earlier. Problems differ in how input and output sequences relate. It helps to name the common patterns, because each leads to a different wiring of the same recurrent building block. The four common wirings. Orange boxes are the same recurrent cell reused at every step; purple boxes in the last panel are a second network (the decoder) that starts from a summary of the first. The blue/green nodes show where inputs enter and outputs are read off. - One-to-many. One input, a sequence out. Image captioning feeds one image embedding into a recurrent decoder that emits words. Music or text generation from a seed also fits. - Many-to-one. A sequence in, a single label out. Sentiment classification, spam detection, classifying a time series (is this ECG abnormal?). You read the final hidden state, or pool over all of them. - Many-to-many, aligned. One output per input step, same length. Part-of-speech tagging, named-entity recognition, frame-by-frame speech labeling, per-step forecasting. - Many-to-many, unaligned (sequence-to-sequence). Input and output lengths differ and no st"},{"p":"week-07.html","w":"Week 7","t":"Sequence Models: RNNs, LSTMs, and the Road to Attention","h":"The recurrent neural network","a":"rnn","x":"Here is the central idea. Instead of a function of the whole input, learn a function that consumes one element at a time and updates a fixed-size summary of everything seen so far, the hidden state . The same function (the same weights) is applied at every step. The vanilla (Elman) recurrent network is with , , , , nonlinearity (usually ) and initial state (zeros, or learned). Read the first equation as: the new state is a squashed mixture of the current input and the previous state. The hidden state is the network's only memory. A vanilla RNN folded (left) and unrolled across four steps (right). Unrolling turns the recurrence into an ordinary deep feed-forward graph whose layers share weights. The depth equals the sequence length, which is the source of both the power and the trouble. Two observations organize everything that follows. Parameter sharing across time. The weights do not depend on . This is the temporal analogue of the convolution's weight sharing in Week 5: it encodes the assumption that the rule for updating memory is the same at every position, it lets the model handle any length, and it keeps the parameter count independent of . A model that saw \"the cat sat\" at positions 1 to 3 automatically knows what to do with \"the cat sat\" at positions 51 to 53. Unrolled, an RNN is a very deep network. A sequence of length 1000 produces a computation graph with 1000 layers, each using the same matrix . Week 3 taught you what products of many matrices do to gradients. Recurrence is that problem with no escape hatch: you cannot choose a shallower network without ignoring part of the sequence. Why not just feed a window to an MLP? { why-not-window} A fixed-window MLP (concatenate the last embeddings and classify) is the neural version of the -gram. It has the same de"},{"p":"week-07.html","w":"Week 7","t":"Sequence Models: RNNs, LSTMs, and the Road to Attention","h":"Backpropagation through time","a":"bptt","x":"Training an RNN means computing for the shared parameters. Because unrolling gives an ordinary acyclic graph, you do not need a new algorithm: apply reverse-mode autodiff (Week 2) to the unrolled graph and sum the contributions from every time step where a weight was used. This is backpropagation through time (BPTT) . The derivation is worth doing in full, because the structure of the result explains the main pathology of recurrent networks. Setup and notation { bptt-setup} Use the pre-activation form. Let and , with per-step loss and total loss (sum or mean, a constant factor). Write for the gradient at the pre-activation; it is the quantity that gets propagated backwards. Derivation: BPTT for a tanh RNN with softmax outputs Output layer. For softmax cross-entropy the gradient of with respect to the logits is -space error (predicted probabilities minus the one-hot target), exactly as in Week 2. Therefore Hidden state: two routes. The state influences the loss in two ways: directly through its own output , and indirectly through the next state , which feeds every later loss. By the multivariate chain rule, Since , the one-step Jacobian is where is diagonal with entries . Backward recursion. Multiplying through by the activation derivative gives a recursion for : Parameter gradients. Each weight is used at every step, so its gradient is a sum over time of the local outer products: unrolled graph. Forward arrows (solid) carry states; the backward recursion (dashed red) sends gradient from later steps to earlier ones through the Jacobian . The gradient reaching is the sum of contributions from every loss with . Unfold the recursion. The contribution of the loss at step to the gradient with respect to an earlier state ( ) is This product of Jacobians is the whole story. It "},{"p":"week-07.html","w":"Week 7","t":"Sequence Models: RNNs, LSTMs, and the Road to Attention","h":"Vanishing and exploding gradients in time","a":"vanishing-exploding","x":"Look again at the product in equation (1). To understand its behavior, start with the simplest case: a linear RNN with , where for every . Then . Suppose is diagonalizable, . Then and each eigen-direction is scaled by . If the largest eigenvalue magnitude, the spectral radius , is below 1 the product shrinks geometrically, like ; if it is above 1 it grows geometrically. A gradient that must travel steps back is multiplied by roughly : with that is , with it is , with it is . Bounding the gradient with the spectral norm The spectral radius gives the asymptotic rate; a clean bound uses the induced 2-norm. Since and the diagonal matrix has ( for tanh, for the sigmoid), Sufficient condition for vanishing: if , the influence of on decays at least exponentially in . This is the Pascanu, Mikolov and Bengio (2013) argument. For tanh ( ) it means that a recurrent matrix with largest singular value below 1 guarantees vanishing gradients. The converse does not hold: does not guarantee explosion (the bound is an upper bound; the product can still shrink if the large singular directions do not align between steps), but is a necessary condition for exploding gradients in the linear case, and in the nonlinear case it gives growth along the directions where the activation is not saturated. caveats make the real picture richer than \" vanishes, explodes\". 1. Saturation changes the picture. With tanh, has entries and often much smaller: when a unit saturates ( ), and that unit's gradient is killed. So a tanh RNN with moderately above 1 often does not explode, because saturation pulls the effective gain back down, as the measurements below show. 2. Vanishing and exploding are two sides of one coin. The product of random-ish matrices has singular values that spread out exponentially (a fact"},{"p":"week-07.html","w":"Week 7","t":"Sequence Models: RNNs, LSTMs, and the Road to Attention","h":"LSTM: long short-term memory","a":"lstm","x":"The diagnosis in the previous section suggests the cure. The trouble is that the state is rewritten multiplicatively by a matrix and squashed at every step, so information and gradient both pass through a long chain of transformations. What if the memory had a route that was updated additively , with a gain near 1 by default? Hochreiter and Schmidhuber (1997) proposed exactly this, calling it the constant error carousel , and wrapped it in learned gates that decide what to write, keep and read. The LSTM keeps two vectors per layer: the cell state (long-term memory, the conveyor belt in the figure) and the hidden state (the short-term output that other layers see). Let be the logistic sigmoid and the concatenation. The LSTM cell. The thick horizontal line is the cell state. The forget gate multiplies it (erase), the input gate times the candidate is added to it (write), and the output gate controls how much of the squashed cell is exposed as (read). All gates see the previous hidden state and the current input. Every gate, explained { lstm-gates} Gates are soft switches. A sigmoid output lies in and is applied elementwise, so each coordinate of the memory gets its own dial between \"completely closed\" (0) and \"completely open\" (1). Gates are learned functions of the context , not fixed numbers: the network decides at every step, per unit, how much to erase and write. - Forget gate (erase). Multiplies the old cell state. keeps a memory cell unchanged, wipes it. It lets the network reset memory at natural boundaries (a new sentence, a closed bracket). It was not in the original 1997 LSTM, which had a constant carry of 1 and so could never erase; Gers, Schmidhuber and Cummins (2000) added it, and it turned out to be the most important gate (Greff et al. 2017, \"LSTM: a search"},{"p":"week-07.html","w":"Week 7","t":"Sequence Models: RNNs, LSTMs, and the Road to Attention","h":"GRU: gated recurrent unit","a":"gru","x":"Cho et al. (2014) introduced a lighter gated cell while building an encoder-decoder for translation. The GRU merges the cell and hidden state into one vector and uses two gates instead of three: The GRU cell. The update gate interpolates between keeping the old state and adopting the candidate; the reset gate controls how much of the old state is visible when proposing the candidate. - Update gate plays the roles of both the LSTM forget and input gates, tied together: the amount of old state kept is and the amount of new content written is (a convex combination, so the state stays bounded). When the unit copies forward unchanged, the same gradient highway as the LSTM, with . - Reset gate controls how much of the previous state is used to compute the candidate . With the candidate depends only on the current input, as if the unit were starting a new segment, which is useful for short-term dependencies. Without it, the candidate would always be contaminated by old state. Be aware of a convention clash: some references (and the original paper) write , swapping the roles of and . PyTorch follows the latter form and additionally applies the reset gate after the recurrent matrix product, . These variants are functionally equivalent in expressive power; just don't mix equations from different sources when debugging. LSTM versus GRU { lstm-vs-gru} LSTM GRU --- --- --- State only Gates forget, input, output (+ candidate) update, reset (+ candidate) Parameters per layer in PyTorch in PyTorch Output exposed , can hide memory full state Typical result slightly better on tasks needing precise counting or long memory similar accuracy, about 25% fewer parameters, a little faster Chung, Gulcehre, Cho and Bengio (2014) compared both and found them similar overall, with both clearly bett"},{"p":"week-07.html","w":"Week 7","t":"Sequence Models: RNNs, LSTMs, and the Road to Attention","h":"Bidirectional and stacked recurrent networks","a":"bi-stacked","x":"Left: a bidirectional RNN runs one cell left to right and another right to left, and concatenates the two states at every position. Right: a stacked RNN feeds the hidden sequence of one layer as the input sequence to the next. Bidirectional RNNs (Schuster and Paliwal, 1997). A unidirectional RNN at position has only seen . Many tasks, such as tagging a word as a person's name, depend on the words after it too (\"Washington was founded in...\" versus \"Washington said...\"). A bidirectional RNN runs two independent recurrent networks, a forward one producing from and a backward one producing from , and concatenates . Each position now has a summary of the full context. The cost is twice the parameters and compute. The restriction to remember: a bidirectional model needs the whole input in advance. It is perfect for encoders (tagging, classification, the encoder of a translation model, the acoustic model in offline speech recognition) and impossible for autoregressive generation, where the future does not exist yet. Using a bidirectional layer in a next-token language model leaks the answer: the backward state at position has already seen , the very token you are asked to predict, and your validation loss would look miraculous while the model is useless. This is the same issue that motivates masked language modeling in BERT (Week 10). Stacked (deep) RNNs. The recurrence is already deep in time, but each step is a single shallow transformation. Stacking layers ( ) adds depth in the feature direction , giving the model room for hierarchical processing: lower layers tend to capture local, syntactic structure and upper layers more abstract features. Two to four layers is typical for LSTM models (Google's 2016 neural translation system used eight, which required residual connectio"},{"p":"week-07.html","w":"Week 7","t":"Sequence Models: RNNs, LSTMs, and the Road to Attention","h":"Sequence-to-sequence learning","a":"seq2seq","x":"Machine translation breaks the aligned many-to-many assumption: \"je suis étudiant\" has three tokens, \"I am a student\" has four, and the word order may differ. The encoder-decoder (or seq2seq ) architecture, introduced independently by Cho et al. (2014) and Sutskever, Vinyals and Le (2014), solves this by factoring the problem into two networks: 1. The encoder RNN reads the source and compresses it into a representation (in the simplest version, its final hidden state , the \"context vector\"). 2. The decoder RNN is an autoregressive language model over the target, conditioned on that context: . Its initial state is set from the encoder's summary. A sequence-to-sequence model. The encoder reads the source and passes its final state to the decoder, which generates the target token by token. Training feeds the gold previous token (teacher forcing); at test time the decoder feeds back its own predictions. Special tokens make this work. A (start) token seeds the decoder, and (end) is a vocabulary item the decoder learns to emit when it is done; that is how a variable-length output is produced. Training minimizes the cross-entropy summed over target positions, backpropagating through the decoder, into the encoder, and into the embeddings. Sutskever et al. (2014) used a 4-layer deep LSTM, and reported a striking trick: reversing the source sentence improved results, because it shortens the distance between the first source words and the first target words and hands the optimization many short-term dependencies. The information bottleneck { bottleneck} The weakness is visible in the figure: everything the decoder knows about the source must pass through one fixed-size vector. Whether the source has 5 words or 50, the context is with the same . Cho et al. (2014, \"On the properties"},{"p":"week-07.html","w":"Week 7","t":"Sequence Models: RNNs, LSTMs, and the Road to Attention","h":"Attention: removing the bottleneck","a":"attention","x":"Squeezing a 50-word sentence into one vector and asking a decoder to unfold it is cruel. When a human translates, they glance back at the relevant source words for each target word. Attention lets the decoder do the same: at every output step it computes a weighted average of all encoder states, with weights that depend on what it is about to produce. Bahdanau, Cho and Bengio (2015, \"Neural machine translation by jointly learning to align and translate\") introduced this for translation; the idea of a learned soft alignment is the direct ancestor of the attention in Week 9. Bahdanau (additive) attention { bahdanau} Let the encoder produce one annotation per source position, (typically from a bidirectional RNN, so each summarizes the source around position ). Let be the decoder state before producing output . For each decoder step: 1. Score how well each source position matches the current decoding need with a small feed-forward network: 2. Normalize the scores over source positions into weights: , so and . 3. Pool : the context vector is the expected annotation under that distribution, . 4. Decode : use in the recurrent update and the output, e.g. and . Additive attention at one decoder step. Scores are computed between the previous decoder state and every encoder annotation, softmaxed into weights, and used to form a weighted sum, the context, which feeds the next decoder state. The key points are conceptual. The \"alignment\" is soft (a distribution, not a hard choice), which makes the whole mechanism differentiable: gradients flow from the loss through into both the encoder states and the scoring network, and the alignment is learned without any alignment labels , purely from the translation loss. The context vector is different at every step : the fixed-size bottleneck"},{"p":"week-07.html","w":"Week 7","t":"Sequence Models: RNNs, LSTMs, and the Road to Attention","h":"Applications","a":"applications","x":"Machine translation { mt} Neural machine translation was the application that drove this whole line of work. The pipeline: tokenize the source and target (subword units, see Week 8), embed, encode with a (bi)LSTM, decode with an attentional LSTM, and search with beam search. The 2014 to 2016 sequence of results is a good case study in how quickly the field moved. Sutskever et al. (2014) showed a plain LSTM seq2seq could approach phrase-based statistical MT on English to French; attention (Bahdanau 2015, Luong 2015) removed the length degradation; and Wu et al. (2016) deployed an eight-layer attentional LSTM with residual connections as Google Translate's production system, reporting large human-evaluated error reductions on several language pairs. The metric to know is BLEU : a geometric mean of -gram precisions ( ) against reference translations, multiplied by a brevity penalty that punishes outputs shorter than the reference. It is cheap, correlates only roughly with human judgment, and is only comparable when computed with the same tokenization and tooling (use sacreBLEU ). Speech recognition and CTC { speech-ctc} In speech recognition, the input is a sequence of acoustic frames (typically 100 per second of audio, as log-mel filterbank features) and the output is a shorter character or word-piece sequence. Alignment between frames and letters is unknown. Attention-based seq2seq models can learn it (Chan et al. 2016, \"Listen, attend and spell\"), but a different loss, connectionist temporal classification (Graves, Fernandez, Gomez and Schmidhuber, 2006), is widely used because it needs no decoder at all and supports streaming. The idea: let a (bi)RNN emit, at every frame, a distribution over the output alphabet plus a special blank symbol . A frame-level labeling is ma"},{"p":"week-07.html","w":"Week 7","t":"Sequence Models: RNNs, LSTMs, and the Road to Attention","h":"Lab 1: a character-level language model with LSTM and GRU","a":"lab-char-lm","x":"Goal: train a character-level language model on Tiny Shakespeare (about 1.1 million characters), using truncated BPTT with a carried hidden state, gradient clipping, and a switchable LSTM/GRU core; evaluate in bits per character; and sample text at several temperatures. Run it on a Colab GPU; an epoch takes well under a minute. Step 1: data. Encode characters as integers and arrange the stream into parallel columns, so that consecutive minibatches continue each column's text (required for carrying the hidden state). Step 2: model and utilities. Step 3: training and evaluation. The BPC is the cross-entropy in nats divided by . Expect the validation BPC to drop from about 6 (the uniform baseline) to roughly 2.3 bits after the first epoch and to 1.7 to 1.9 after a handful of epochs; the exact numbers depend on hardware and seed, and the two cells usually finish within a few hundredths of each other. If your first-batch loss is far from , check the initialization and the target shift before anything else. Step 4: sampling. Generation feeds the model's own output back, one character at a time. The temperature rescales the logits before the softmax: . Low approaches greedy decoding (repetitive, safe), samples from the model, high flattens toward uniform (random, noisy). Experiments to run (each answers a question from this week): 1. Set clip=1e9 (no clipping) and lr=2e-2 : do you see a spike in the printed gradient norm and then divergence or NaN? Compare with the clipped run. 2. Change the window K from 128 to 16 and to 512. How does validation BPC change, and what does that tell you about the useful credit-assignment horizon in this data? 3. Remove the forget-gate bias initialization. Does the early training curve differ? 4. Replace nn.LSTM by a vanilla nn.RNN(nonlinearity="},{"p":"week-07.html","w":"Week 7","t":"Sequence Models: RNNs, LSTMs, and the Road to Attention","h":"Lab 2: sequence-to-sequence with attention on a toy translation task","a":"lab-seq2seq","x":"Real translation needs data and hours of GPU time. To see every mechanism in minutes, we invent a toy language pair whose structure is known: the source is a random string of 6 to 8 letters from an alphabet of 12 symbols; the target is the reversed string with each letter mapped through a fixed substitution cipher. The correct alignment is therefore the anti-diagonal. A model without attention must squeeze the whole string into one vector; a model with attention should recover the anti-diagonal in its attention map, which gives us a ground truth to compare against, something real translation never offers. Step 1: the data. Step 2: the model. A bidirectional GRU encoder, additive attention, and a GRU-cell decoder that, at each step, attends with its previous state, concatenates the context to the input embedding and predicts from state plus context. Step 3: train with and without attention, and measure exact-match accuracy. Step 4: inspect the alignment. With attention, the matrix of for a test sentence should light up the anti-diagonal. What to expect (do run it, and treat the following as a hypothesis to test rather than a promise): the attentional model should reach near-perfect exact-match accuracy within the 1500 steps, with the heatmap showing a clean anti-diagonal; the model without attention should do noticeably worse, and the gap should grow if you increase the string length to 15 to 20, because a fixed 128-dimensional vector must now carry more symbols. Try it: set min len=14, max len=16 (and update the padding length) and compare. Then use the attention demo above to build intuition for what the weights look like when training is imperfect."},{"p":"week-07.html","w":"Week 7","t":"Sequence Models: RNNs, LSTMs, and the Road to Attention","h":"Common mistakes and debugging tips","a":"pitfalls","x":"Recurrent models fail in characteristic ways. This table maps symptoms to the usual cause and the fix; most of them cost somebody a weekend. Symptom Likely cause Fix --- --- --- Loss is nan after a few hundred steps, or spikes by orders of magnitude Exploding gradients through time (spectral radius of the recurrent Jacobian above 1) clip grad norm with a global threshold (0.5 to 5), lower the learning rate, check the orthogonal or identity-like initialization of Loss plateaus at roughly (the unigram or uniform level) and never moves Vanishing gradients, or a data bug (inputs and targets not shifted by one) Print one decoded (input, target) pair; switch to LSTM/GRU, set the forget-gate bias to 1, shorten the BPTT window while debugging Train loss very low, val loss poor in a language model Hidden state carried across the train/val boundary, or overlap between train and val text Re-initialize state at the start of evaluation; split the corpus by contiguous chunk, not by random window Training loss is excellent under teacher forcing but free-running samples are repetitive or drift Exposure bias Scheduled sampling, lower decoding temperature, beam search with a length penalty, or more data; always report metrics from free-running decoding Padding tokens change the result, or batch size changes the output Padding is processed as real input, or loss is averaged over pad positions Use pack padded sequence (or a mask), ignore index=PAD in the loss, and take the hidden state at the true last position RuntimeError: Trying to backward through the graph a second time Hidden state carried across truncated-BPTT chunks without detach() h = h.detach() (for LSTM, detach both and ) at the start of each chunk Memory grows every step in a training loop The same cause as above, in the other"},{"p":"week-07.html","w":"Week 7","t":"Sequence Models: RNNs, LSTMs, and the Road to Attention","h":"Exercises","a":"exercises","x":"Exercise 1: Perplexity and bits per character A character-level model reaches a validation cross-entropy of 1.10 nats per character. (a) Convert it to bits per character and to perplexity. (b) The same text tokenized into words averages 5.6 characters per word including the space. If a word-level model has perplexity 140 per word, which model is better per character? Solution (a) BPC bits. Perplexity per character is (equivalently ). (b) Convert the word model to a per-character cross-entropy: nats per character, which is bits. That is lower than nats bits, so the word model is better per character here. The comparison is only valid when both models are measured over exactly the same text and the word model handles out-of-vocabulary words in the same way (or has no unknown tokens). Exercise 2: Gradient of a linear RNN Consider the scalar linear RNN with and loss . (a) Compute for . (b) For , what is the gradient with respect to when , , ? (c) What does this say about learning dependencies of length 100? Solution (a) depends on through applications of , so . (b) With : , , . (c) Only keeps the signal intact, and it is a knife edge: any mismatch decays or explodes exponentially in the distance. A vanilla RNN with a nonlinearity has the same structure with replaced by the Jacobian , so its long-range gradients behave like for the relevant spectral radius . This is exactly what the LSTM's additive cell state with a forget gate near 1 tries to realize. Exercise 3: Why clipping by norm and not by value? Explain the difference between clip grad value and clip grad norm for a gradient and a threshold of 1. Which preserves the update direction? Solution Value clipping clamps each coordinate: . The direction changed from almost exactly the first axis (angle ) to about away from i"},{"p":"week-07.html","w":"Week 7","t":"Sequence Models: RNNs, LSTMs, and the Road to Attention","h":"Key takeaways","a":"takeaways","x":"Key takeaways - A recurrent network applies the same cell at every step, carrying a state; unrolled, it is a deep network whose depth is the sequence length and whose layers share weights. BPTT is ordinary backprop on that graph, with gradients summed over time into the shared parameters. - The gradient to a state steps back is a product of Jacobians; its size is governed by the spectral radius of . Below 1 it vanishes (no long-range learning), above 1 it explodes. Clipping handles explosion; architecture handles vanishing. - LSTM and GRU use additive, gated updates of the state so that the Jacobian along the memory path is a learned diagonal near 1 rather than a repeated dense matrix. Initialize the LSTM forget-gate bias to about 1. - Truncated BPTT bounds memory and compute by detaching the state between chunks; the state carries forward but the gradient horizon is the chunk. - Bidirectional and stacked RNNs enrich representations but a bidirectional model cannot be used to generate or to train a next-token objective. - Seq2seq trains with teacher forcing and tests with self-feeding, which causes exposure bias; decode with beam search plus length normalization, and evaluate with free-running outputs. - A single fixed vector is a bottleneck. Attention computes a weighted average of all encoder states with data-dependent weights, giving a short gradient path and an interpretable soft alignment. Bahdanau uses an additive MLP score, Luong a multiplicative one. - 1D and temporal convolutions are parallel, stable alternatives with a receptive field that grows linearly (or exponentially with dilation); the next step, attention without recurrence, is Week 9."},{"p":"week-07.html","w":"Week 7","t":"Sequence Models: RNNs, LSTMs, and the Road to Attention","h":"Further reading","a":"further-reading","x":"Essential - Hochreiter, S. and Schmidhuber, J. (1997). Long Short-Term Memory. Neural Computation 9(8). - Sutskever, I., Vinyals, O. and Le, Q. V. (2014). Sequence to Sequence Learning with Neural Networks. NeurIPS. arXiv:1409.3215. - Bahdanau, D., Cho, K. and Bengio, Y. (2015). Neural Machine Translation by Jointly Learning to Align and Translate. ICLR. arXiv:1409.0473. Recommended - Goodfellow, I., Bengio, Y. and Courville, A. (2016). Deep Learning , Chapter 10: Sequence Modeling: Recurrent and Recursive Nets. MIT Press. - Olah, C. (2015). Understanding LSTM Networks. colah.github.io. - Bengio, Y., Simard, P. and Frasconi, P. (1994). Learning long-term dependencies with gradient descent is difficult. IEEE Transactions on Neural Networks 5(2). - Pascanu, R., Mikolov, T. and Bengio, Y. (2013). On the difficulty of training recurrent neural networks. ICML. arXiv:1211.5063. - Cho, K. et al. (2014). Learning Phrase Representations using RNN Encoder-Decoder for Statistical Machine Translation. EMNLP. arXiv:1406.1078. - Luong, M.-T., Pham, H. and Manning, C. D. (2015). Effective Approaches to Attention-based Neural Machine Translation. EMNLP. arXiv:1508.04025. - Gers, F. A., Schmidhuber, J. and Cummins, F. (2000). Learning to Forget: Continual Prediction with LSTM. Neural Computation 12(10). - Graves, A., Fernandez, S., Gomez, F. and Schmidhuber, J. (2006). Connectionist Temporal Classification. ICML. - Bai, S., Kolter, J. Z. and Koltun, V. (2018). An Empirical Evaluation of Generic Convolutional and Recurrent Networks for Sequence Modeling. arXiv:1803.01271. - Wu, Y. et al. (2016). Google's Neural Machine Translation System. arXiv:1609.08144. - Bengio, S., Vinyals, O., Jaitly, N. and Shazeer, N. (2015). Scheduled Sampling for Sequence Prediction with Recurrent Neural Networ"},{"p":"week-08.html","w":"Week 8","t":"Representation Learning, Embeddings, and Midterm","h":"Representation Learning, Embeddings, and Midterm","a":"","x":"Learning objectives By the end of this week you should be able to: - Explain why dense, learned, distributed representations beat one-hot and hand-built features, and state the distributional hypothesis. - Derive the skip-gram objective with negative sampling from a binary-classification view, write its gradients, and implement it in NumPy and PyTorch. - Contrast word2vec, GloVe and fastText, and state the sense in which skip-gram with negative sampling factorizes a shifted PMI matrix. - Evaluate embeddings intrinsically (similarity, analogy) and extrinsically (a downstream task), and name the pitfalls of each. - Measure and discuss social bias in embeddings with a projection test and WEAT, and say what debiasing can and cannot do. - Implement byte-pair encoding from scratch and explain how WordPiece and SentencePiece/Unigram differ; reason about character- and byte-level models. - View users, items, nodes and entities as embeddings, and see matrix factorization as an embedding model. - Write and compare contrastive, triplet and InfoNCE losses, and explain the role of temperature and negatives. - Revisit the course so far (Weeks 1 to 7), reproduce the key derivations, and complete a practice midterm. to now the inputs were already numbers: pixels, features, audio samples. Language, products, users and graph nodes are not. They are discrete symbols , and a neural network can only consume vectors. The bridge between the two is a representation : a map from each symbol to a point in that the rest of the network can use. In Week 7 you used an nn.Embedding layer without asking where its numbers come from. This week answers that question, and it turns out to be one of the central ideas of modern deep learning. Learn good vectors, and a simple classifier on top is often enough"},{"p":"week-08.html","w":"Week 8","t":"Representation Learning, Embeddings, and Midterm","h":"Distributed representations","a":"distributed-representations","x":"Suppose your vocabulary has words. The most obvious representation is one-hot : word becomes the vector . It is exact and it is useless for generalization. Every pair of distinct words is orthogonal and at the same distance, , so \"cat\" is exactly as far from \"kitten\" as it is from \"carburetor\". A model that learns something about \"cat\" learns nothing about \"kitten\". The input layer also has dimensions and grows with the vocabulary. A distributed representation replaces this with a dense vector with typically between 50 and 1000. \"Distributed\" means that a concept is spread across many coordinates and each coordinate takes part in representing many concepts, in contrast to a local (one-hot) code where one unit means one thing. Two properties follow. 1. Similarity becomes geometry. Words used in similar ways get nearby vectors, so what the model learns for one transfers to its neighbours. 2. Compositional capacity. real numbers can encode exponentially many distinguishable combinations of features, whereas one-hot units encode only items. An embedding layer is mathematically trivial: with the embedding matrix , the lookup for token is , a row selection. It is the same as a linear layer applied to a one-hot input, implemented without ever materializing the one-hot vector. Everything interesting is in how is trained . The distributional hypothesis { distributional-hypothesis} How can a machine learn what words mean from raw text, without labels? The linguist J. R. Firth's slogan (1957) is the standard answer: you shall know a word by the company it keeps. Words that occur in similar contexts tend to have similar meanings. This is the distributional hypothesis , and every method this week is an implementation of it. The only difference between methods is how \"context\" is def"},{"p":"week-08.html","w":"Week 8","t":"Representation Learning, Embeddings, and Midterm","h":"word2vec","a":"word2vec","x":"Word2vec is not one model but a family of two architectures and two output approximations. All of them learn two embedding tables of size : input vectors (used when is the centre or the \"query\") and output vectors (used when is a target or a \"context\"). After training, people usually keep , or the average . The two word2vec architectures on the same window. Skip-gram (left) predicts every context word from the centre word, producing up to training pairs per position. CBOW (right) averages the context embeddings and predicts the centre word once. Skip-gram { skip-gram} Slide a window of radius over a corpus . At position , the word is the centre and each of ( ) is a context word . Skip-gram models the probability of a context word given the centre as a softmax over the whole vocabulary, and maximizes the average log-likelihood of all (centre, context) pairs, Reading the dot product as a compatibility score, training pushes the vectors of words that co-occur to have a large inner product. Two words that appear in the same contexts (cat and dog both co-occur with \"pet\", \"fed\", \"barked at\" or \"purred at\") are pulled toward the same context vectors and therefore end up close to each other. The model is a log-bilinear classifier: there is no hidden layer and no nonlinearity, which is why it is fast and why its geometry is so regular. The catch is the denominator. Each pair costs to evaluate and to differentiate. With and that is multiply-adds per training pair, and a corpus has billions of pairs. Two remedies were proposed: hierarchical softmax (a binary tree over the vocabulary, per pair) and negative sampling , which is the one that took over. CBOW { cbow} The continuous bag of words model flips the direction: it averages the input vectors of the context words and predicts "},{"p":"week-08.html","w":"Week 8","t":"Representation Learning, Embeddings, and Midterm","h":"Embedding geometry, analogies and evaluation","a":"geometry-evaluation","x":"Cosine similarity and nearest neighbours { cosine} The standard way to compare two vectors is cosine similarity , . Raw dot products are dominated by vector length, and in word2vec-style models the length correlates with word frequency, so frequent words win just by being long. Cosine compares directions only; after normalizing every row of the embedding matrix to unit length, a matrix product gives all cosines at once. Always normalize before looking at neighbours. Linear structure and the analogy test { analogies} The result that made word2vec famous is that relations between words appear as roughly constant offsets : , and . The analogy task \" is to as is to \" is answered by The parallelogram picture of analogies, with hand-drawn illustrative positions. If \"woman minus man\" and \"queen minus king\" are nearly the same offset, then adding that offset to king lands near queen. Capital-of is another approximately constant offset. Why should this happen at all? The PMI derivation above gives the beginning of an answer. If and the PMI difference between \"king\" and \"queen\" is similar across context words for every context that distinguishes gender (she/he, her/his, mother/father...) and negligible for the others (ruled, crown, palace), then must be nearly the same vector as when tested against all contexts. Explicit regularities in co-occurrence statistics become linear regularities in the embedding. Try the explorer below. It uses a hand-built 2D \"embedding\" so that you can see the geometry, including how the answer depends on excluding the input words and how quickly noise destroys a parallelogram. Analogy explorer on a hand-built 2D embedding 560 Analogies are a demo, not a benchmark of understanding Three caveats that every practitioner should know. (1) The three input w"},{"p":"week-08.html","w":"Week 8","t":"Representation Learning, Embeddings, and Midterm","h":"Bias in embeddings","a":"embedding-bias","x":"Embeddings are fitted to text written by people, and text reflects the world, including its stereotypes and inequities. Bolukbasi et al. (2016) showed that word2vec vectors trained on news text encode gendered associations: the analogy \"man is to computer programmer as woman is to ...\" completes with homemaker, and occupations such as nurse or engineer sit measurably on one side of a \"he minus she\" direction. Caliskan, Bryson and Narayanan (2017) showed that associations between target words (flowers vs insects, European-American vs African-American names) and attribute words (pleasant vs unpleasant) in GloVe vectors closely mirror the classic human Implicit Association Test results. The embeddings did not invent these associations; they are compressed statistics of how words are used. But a model built on them can replicate and amplify them in applications such as résumé screening, search ranking and autocomplete. Measuring bias: projections and WEAT { measuring-bias} Two measurements are standard, and both are short to implement. Direction projection. Estimate a \"gender direction\" from definitional pairs (he-she, man-woman, his-her, ...), e.g. as the top principal component of the pair differences or simply the mean difference. Then the projection of a word that should be neutral with respect to gender (a profession) measures its lean along that axis. WEAT (the Word Embedding Association Test of Caliskan et al., modelled on the IAT) takes two target sets (for example career-related and family-related words) and two attribute sets (male and female terms). Define the association of a word with the attributes as . The effect size is Discussion: what to conclude, and what not to { bias-discussion} A few points, stated carefully because this topic is easy to oversimplify i"},{"p":"week-08.html","w":"Week 8","t":"Representation Learning, Embeddings, and Midterm","h":"Subword tokenization","a":"subword-tokenization","x":"Every model so far needed a decision about the unit of text. Words are the natural choice but have two problems. The vocabulary is open: new words, names, typos, numbers and inflections keep arriving, so any fixed word list leaves some inputs as , and an token throws away all information. And the softmax over a 500,000-word vocabulary is expensive and statistically thin, since most words are rare. Characters have the opposite profile. The vocabulary is tiny and nothing is ever unknown, but sequences become 4 to 6 times longer, the model must spend capacity re-learning spelling, and long-range dependencies stretch over more steps (see Week 7 on why that hurts recurrent models; attention costs grow quadratically with length too). Subword tokenization sits between the two. Frequent words stay whole (\"the\", \"language\"); rarer words are split into reusable pieces (\"un\", \"friend\", \"li\", \"est\"); and in the worst case everything falls back to single characters or bytes, so nothing is unknown. The vocabulary size (typically 8k to 100k pieces) is a hyperparameter you pick. Byte-pair encoding (BPE) { bpe} BPE was invented as a data-compression scheme by Philip Gage (1994), which repeatedly replaces the most frequent pair of adjacent bytes by a new symbol. Sennrich, Haddow and Birch (2016) adapted it to word segmentation for neural machine translation and showed that it handles rare and unseen words well. The learning algorithm is almost embarrassingly simple. 1. Pre-tokenize the training text into words (split on whitespace) and count them. Represent each word as a sequence of characters, with a special end-of-word marker so that word-final pieces are distinguishable from word-internal ones. 2. Count all adjacent symbol pairs across the corpus, weighted by word frequency. 3. Merge"},{"p":"week-08.html","w":"Week 8","t":"Representation Learning, Embeddings, and Midterm","h":"Character-level and byte-level models","a":"character-models","x":"The extreme end of the spectrum drops the vocabulary entirely. You already built a character-level language model in the Week 7 lab: the input is a sequence of characters (an embedding table of a few dozen rows), and the model predicts the next character. The strengths are the ones listed at the start of this section: no out-of-vocabulary tokens, robustness to typos and morphology, a tiny softmax, and the ability to model any string. The weaknesses are as concrete: sequences are about 5 times longer than word sequences, so the compute per sentence rises and dependencies span more steps, and the model has to rediscover spelling and word boundaries before it can use semantics. Several designs recover the benefits without the cost. - Character-aware word models. Kim, Jernite, Sontag and Rush (2016) build a word representation from its characters with a small 1D CNN and a max-over-time pooling layer (the 1D convolutions of Week 7), feed the result to a word-level LSTM, and obtain good language-model perplexity with far fewer parameters than a word-embedding table. This is a learned counterpart to fastText's -gram sum. - Hierarchical and downsampling models. The character sequence is compressed by strided convolutions or pooling (as in CANINE, Clark et al., 2022) so that the expensive layers run on a shorter sequence. - Byte-level pretrained models. ByT5 (Xue et al., 2022) trains an encoder-decoder directly on bytes and is more robust to noise and spelling variation, at the price of longer sequences and more compute per example. For most text applications at scale, subword tokenization remains the pragmatic default because the cost of sequence length dominates. Character- or byte-level modelling is the right choice for small-vocabulary or noisy domains (DNA, chemical strings"},{"p":"week-08.html","w":"Week 8","t":"Representation Learning, Embeddings, and Midterm","h":"Embeddings beyond text","a":"beyond-text","x":"Nothing in word2vec is specific to words. The recipe is: treat the objects as symbols, define what it means for two to be in the same context, and learn vectors that predict context . Whatever the domain, the resulting table of vectors can feed a downstream model, be used for nearest-neighbour retrieval, or be visualized. - Items in sessions (item2vec, Barkan and Koenigstein, 2016): a shopping basket or listening session plays the role of a sentence, and the items in it are the words. Products bought together get nearby vectors. - Nodes in graphs (DeepWalk, Perozzi et al., 2014; node2vec, Grover and Leskovec, 2016): random walks on the graph produce \"sentences\" of node ids, and skip-gram is run on them. The two methods differ in how the walks are biased (breadth-first versus depth-first exploration). - Entities and relations in knowledge graphs (TransE, Bordes et al., 2013): a fact (head , relation , tail ) is modelled as a translation in embedding space, , trained with a margin-based ranking loss against corrupted triples. The word2vec offset structure becomes a modelling assumption rather than a discovery. - Categorical features in tabular data: user ids, zip codes, product categories. An nn.Embedding per categorical feature, trained jointly with the rest of the model, replaces one-hot encoding (a standard trick, popularized for tabular deep learning and for ranking systems such as the YouTube recommender of Covington et al., 2016). Matrix factorization as an embedding model { matrix-factorization} The cleanest bridge to non-text data is collaborative filtering. You have a sparse matrix of ratings by users on items, with only a small fraction of entries observed. Matrix factorization , the core of the winning approaches in the Netflix Prize (Koren, Bell and Volinsky, "},{"p":"week-08.html","w":"Week 8","t":"Representation Learning, Embeddings, and Midterm","h":"Metric learning: contrastive, triplet and InfoNCE","a":"metric-learning","x":"So far the objective was predictive (guess the context). Metric learning states the goal geometrically instead: learn an embedding such that similar inputs are close and dissimilar inputs are far under a chosen distance (usually Euclidean on unit vectors, or cosine). It applies to any pair of inputs for which you can define \"similar\": two photos of the same face, a query and a clicked document, an image and its caption, two augmentations of the same image. The classifier-with- -outputs trick fails when classes are open-ended (faces of people never seen in training), whereas an embedding of a new example can be compared with stored ones by distance. Left: the triplet loss pulls a positive within a margin of the anchor relative to a negative. Right: InfoNCE treats the other items of a batch as negatives for each anchor; the diagonal of the similarity matrix holds the positive pairs. Contrastive loss (pairs) { contrastive-loss} Hadsell, Chopra and LeCun (2006) introduced the pairwise contrastive loss. For a pair with label if similar and if not, and : Similar pairs are pulled together; dissimilar pairs are pushed apart only until they are apart (the margin), after which they contribute nothing. Without the margin the model would push negatives to infinity. Triplet loss { triplet-loss} The triplet loss of FaceNet (Schroff, Kalenichenko and Philbin, 2015) takes an anchor , a positive of the same identity, and a negative of a different identity: with embeddings L2-normalized onto the unit sphere. It only asks for the relative ordering: the positive must be closer than the negative by at least . Triplets already satisfying the constraint give zero loss and zero gradient, so as training progresses most random triplets become useless. The practical difficulty of the method is th"},{"p":"week-08.html","w":"Week 8","t":"Representation Learning, Embeddings, and Midterm","h":"Lab: train skip-gram embeddings, probe them, and use them downstream","a":"lab","x":"In this lab you will (1) train skip-gram with negative sampling in PyTorch on the first couple of million words of text8 (a cleaned, lower-cased 100 MB slice of English Wikipedia from 2006, a standard small corpus for word-vector experiments), (2) inspect nearest neighbours and analogies, (3) run bias probes, and (4) use the vectors as features in a news-topic classifier and compare with a bag-of-words baseline. It runs in Colab on a GPU in a few minutes, and on a CPU in somewhat longer. Step 1: data, vocabulary, subsampling. Step 2: the model and a batch sampler. We sample positions at random and, for each, a random signed offset in , which approximates word2vec's dynamic window without storing every pair. For orientation: at initialization the output vectors are zero, so every score is 0 and the loss is for . If your first printed loss is far from that, check your shapes before anything else. The loss will fall to something in the low 2s; do not expect it to approach zero (see the discussion of the noise floor above). Step 3: nearest neighbours and analogies. With this little data and training, expect sensible neighbours for common words and unreliable analogies, especially for syntactic relations; the analogy outputs are the place where you should be most skeptical. Try more steps, a larger corpus slice, or and watch the answers improve. Plot a 2D projection (PCA or t-SNE from scikit-learn) of a few hundred words to see topical clusters. Step 4: bias probes. Re-use gender direction , projection table and weat effect size from the earlier bias section, with the word lists below. Check each list for words missing from the vocabulary, and keep lists short and explicit. Interpret the output with the caveats of the bias section: a small corpus gives noisy numbers, so repe"},{"p":"week-08.html","w":"Week 8","t":"Representation Learning, Embeddings, and Midterm","h":"Common mistakes and debugging tips","a":"pitfalls","x":"Symptom Likely cause Fix --- --- --- Nearest neighbours are all very frequent words, or look random Vectors not normalized before cosine; undertrained; no subsampling of frequent words Row-normalize; train more; subsample with to Initial loss is not about Output table not initialized to zero, wrong score sign for negatives, loss not summed over Check -logsigmoid(-neg score) for negatives; print the first-step loss Loss goes to nan log(sigmoid(x)) computed naively with large , or learning rate too high for plain SGD Use F.logsigmoid / binary cross entropy with logits ; use a smaller rate Analogies return one of the input words Inputs not excluded from the candidate list Mask , , before the argmax Training is slow with a huge vocabulary Dense optimizer updating all rows each step sparse=True embeddings with SparseAdam / SGD, or just keep the batch large enough Downstream model does worse than random embeddings Token to row mapping mismatch (different vocab or tokenizer at train and test), padding index being averaged in Build features from the same idx ; mask padding when averaging ( padding idx or lengths) Pretrained vectors do not help Domain mismatch, case mismatch (GloVe is lowercased), tokenization mismatch Check coverage (fraction of tokens in vocabulary); lowercase; fine-tune Out-of-vocabulary words dominate the data Word-level vocabulary on noisy text Switch to subword tokens or fastText-style -gram vectors BPE output differs from the reference tokenizer Different pre-tokenization (whitespace, punctuation, byte mapping) or merge order Use the library tokenizer that the model shipped with; unit-test a few sentences Triplet or contrastive loss collapses (all embeddings equal) Hardest negatives too early, no normalization, margin or temperature mis-set Semi-hard or b"},{"p":"week-08.html","w":"Week 8","t":"Representation Learning, Embeddings, and Midterm","h":"Exercises","a":"exercises","x":"Exercise 1: The skip-gram gradient (derivation) For the loss derive , and check it numerically with finite differences for , . Solution Let , . Since and , we get and . Because , Check: Exercise 2: Cost of the full softmax A vocabulary has words and . Compare the multiply-adds needed to compute the loss and the gradients for one (centre, context) pair with the full softmax versus negative sampling with . How many rows of the output table are updated in each case? Solution Full softmax: computing all scores costs multiply-adds, and the gradient touches the entire output table (every row receives a nonzero gradient proportional to its softmax probability), another . Roughly operations and rows. Negative sampling: scores, i.e. multiply-adds, plus about as many for the gradients, and only output rows plus 1 input row are updated. That is about five orders of magnitude fewer operations, which is the whole reason it made billion-word training feasible. Exercise 3: What the shifted PMI result predicts Using the SGNS optimum , what is the optimal dot product for two words that co-occur exactly as often as independence predicts, for ? What happens to the optimum for a pair that never co-occurs in the data? Solution Independence means , so and the optimal score is . Negative scores are normal: most pairs should look \"unlikely\". For a pair that never co-occurs, , the positive term vanishes and the derivation gives , so the optimum is ; in practice the score is pushed toward large negative values but only as far as the finite number of negative samples and the regularization allow. This is why the factorized matrix is shifted PMI with unobserved pairs handled implicitly, not an explicit entry. Exercise 4: Noise distribution (coding) Write code that samples negatives from for a Zipf"},{"p":"week-08.html","w":"Week 8","t":"Representation Learning, Embeddings, and Midterm","h":"Midterm review: Weeks 1 to 7","a":"midterm-review","x":"The midterm takes place in the second session of this week. It covers Weeks 1 to 7 (Week 8 material is not examined). Expect a mix of short conceptual questions, derivations, shape and parameter-count calculations, and \"what would happen if\" diagnosis questions. The best preparation is to derive things yourself on paper , so the review below is built as a map, a list of derivations, and a practice exam. Concept map { concept-map} How the first seven weeks fit together. Foundations (blue): tensors and autograd, MLPs and backprop, optimization. Regularization (green) cuts across all of them. Vision architectures (purple) specialise the MLP with convolution and residual connections; sequence models (amber) specialise it with recurrence and lead to attention. Representations (Week 8) feed every model. Three threads connect the weeks, and exam questions often live in the connections. - Gradient flow. Backpropagation (Week 2) is the chain rule. Initialization and normalization (Week 3) keep the per-layer Jacobians near norm 1. Residual connections (Week 6) add an identity path so the Jacobian is . LSTM cell states (Week 7) do the same across time. Every \"vanishing gradient\" question is a question about a product of Jacobians. - Inductive bias and parameter sharing. An MLP shares nothing; a CNN shares weights across space (translation equivariance, local connectivity); an RNN shares weights across time; attention shares weights across positions and makes the connectivity data-dependent. Each choice trades flexibility for sample efficiency. - Generalization. Optimization finds a minimum (Week 3); regularization, data augmentation, early stopping and transfer learning (Weeks 4 and 6) decide whether it generalizes. Bias-variance and the train/validation/test discipline apply to e"},{"p":"week-08.html","w":"Week 8","t":"Representation Learning, Embeddings, and Midterm","h":"Assignment 2 and the final project proposal","a":"assignment-proposal","x":"Two deliverables are due at the end of this week. Assignment 2: CNN with transfer learning, and an RNN sequence task { assignment-2} The assignment has a code part (auto-checked where possible) and a 2 to 4 page report. It covers Weeks 5 to 7: a convolutional classifier with transfer learning, and a recurrent model on a sequence task. A strong submission contains: - Part A (CNN and transfer learning). A from-scratch small CNN baseline, then a pretrained backbone fine-tuned on the target dataset with a justified recipe (frozen head first, then unfreezing, a learning-rate choice, augmentation). Report test accuracy for both, with at least three seeds and the standard deviation, a training-curve plot, and a short analysis of errors (a confusion matrix or example failures). Include an ablation of at least one choice (for example number of unfrozen blocks or augmentation). - Part B (sequence task). An RNN, LSTM or GRU on the assigned task (for example a character-level model or sequence classification), with a correct masking and padding scheme, gradient clipping, and a comparison between at least two cell types or between unidirectional and bidirectional. Include a gradient-norm plot and a discussion of what failed before it worked. - A clear report. Problem, method, experiment table with baselines, ablations, and honest discussion of limits. Figures with labelled axes and units; every claim backed by a number. - Reproducibility. Seeds, versions, a README that runs end to end, and no test-set tuning (hyperparameters chosen on validation data only). Final project proposal (1 page) { project-proposal} The proposal is the first milestone of the final project (teams of 1 to 3). A good one-page proposal contains four things, each short and concrete: 1. Problem. What are you tryi"},{"p":"week-08.html","w":"Week 8","t":"Representation Learning, Embeddings, and Midterm","h":"Key takeaways","a":"takeaways","x":"Key takeaways - A representation maps discrete objects to vectors so that a network can use them. Distributed representations make similarity geometric and let statistical strength be shared between related items. - The distributional hypothesis (words in similar contexts have similar meanings) drives word2vec, GloVe and fastText. Skip-gram with negative sampling trains two embedding tables with binary logistic losses per pair at cost , instead of a -way softmax; its optimum is a PMI matrix shifted by . - GloVe regresses on log co-occurrence counts with a weighting function; fastText sums character -gram vectors so that rare and unseen words get vectors. - Use cosine similarity with normalized vectors. Analogy tests are a sanity check with known caveats; evaluate embeddings both intrinsically and on a downstream task. - Embeddings absorb associations in the training text, including social stereotypes. Measure with projections and WEAT, report the word lists and limits, and remember that debiasing by projection is partial. - Subword tokenization (BPE, WordPiece, Unigram/SentencePiece, byte-level BPE) balances vocabulary size against sequence length and removes unknown words. BPE greedily merges the most frequent adjacent pair; the tokenizer is part of the model and must match. - Character and byte-level models trade a tiny vocabulary and robustness for longer sequences. - Users, items, nodes and entities can all be embedded; matrix factorization is an embedding model with a dot-product score trained on observed entries only. - Metric learning shapes distances directly: contrastive and triplet losses use margins and need mining, while InfoNCE is a softmax over negatives whose temperature controls how hard negatives are emphasized and whose value is bounded by . - The midt"},{"p":"week-08.html","w":"Week 8","t":"Representation Learning, Embeddings, and Midterm","h":"Further reading","a":"further-reading","x":"Essential - Mikolov, T., Sutskever, I., Chen, K., Corrado, G. and Dean, J. (2013). Distributed Representations of Words and Phrases and their Compositionality. NeurIPS. arXiv:1310.4546. (Negative sampling, subsampling and phrases.) - Pennington, J., Socher, R. and Manning, C. D. (2014). GloVe: Global Vectors for Word Representation. EMNLP. - Sennrich, R., Haddow, B. and Birch, A. (2016). Neural Machine Translation of Rare Words with Subword Units. ACL. arXiv:1508.07909. Recommended - Mikolov, T., Chen, K., Corrado, G. and Dean, J. (2013). Efficient Estimation of Word Representations in Vector Space. arXiv:1301.3781. - Levy, O. and Goldberg, Y. (2014). Neural Word Embedding as Implicit Matrix Factorization. NeurIPS. - Levy, O., Goldberg, Y. and Dagan, I. (2015). Improving Distributional Similarity with Lessons Learned from Word Embeddings. TACL 3. - Bojanowski, P., Grave, E., Joulin, A. and Mikolov, T. (2017). Enriching Word Vectors with Subword Information. TACL 5. arXiv:1607.04606. - Bolukbasi, T., Chang, K.-W., Zou, J., Saligrama, V. and Kalai, A. (2016). Man is to Computer Programmer as Woman is to Homemaker? Debiasing Word Embeddings. NeurIPS. arXiv:1607.06520. - Caliskan, A., Bryson, J. J. and Narayanan, A. (2017). Semantics derived automatically from language corpora contain human-like biases. Science 356. - Gonen, H. and Goldberg, Y. (2019). Lipstick on a Pig: Debiasing Methods Cover up Systematic Gender Biases in Word Embeddings But do not Remove Them. NAACL. arXiv:1903.03862. - Kudo, T. and Richardson, J. (2018). SentencePiece: A simple and language independent subword tokenizer and detokenizer for Neural Text Processing. EMNLP (demo). arXiv:1808.06226. - Kudo, T. (2018). Subword Regularization: Improving Neural Network Translation Models with Multiple Subword "},{"p":"week-09.html","w":"Week 9","t":"The Transformer Architecture","h":"The Transformer Architecture","a":"","x":"Learning objectives By the end of this week you should be able to: - Derive scaled dot-product attention from the idea of a soft dictionary lookup, and prove why the factor keeps the softmax out of its saturated regime. - Write multi-head attention with exact tensor shapes at every step, and implement it in PyTorch without nn.MultiheadAttention . - Explain the roles of the position-wise feed-forward network, residual connections, layer normalization and RMSNorm, and argue when pre-norm is preferable to post-norm. - Compare sinusoidal, learned, relative, rotary (RoPE) and ALiBi position schemes, and derive why RoPE makes attention scores depend only on relative offsets. - Distinguish encoder-only, decoder-only and encoder-decoder models, and implement causal and padding masks correctly. - Count the parameters and FLOPs of a Transformer from its hyperparameters, explain the quadratic cost of attention, and compare sparse, sliding-window and linear attention. - Apply the standard training recipe (AdamW, warmup, label smoothing, mixed precision) and train a GPT-style decoder from scratch on a tiny corpus, then sample from it. Week 7 you saw attention as an add-on to a recurrent encoder-decoder: a decoder looked back at the encoder's hidden states with a learned weighted average. It worked so well that a natural question arose. If attention lets every output position reach every input position directly, what is the recurrence still for? Vaswani, Shazeer, Parmar, Uszkoreit, Jones, Gomez, Kaiser and Polosukhin answered in 2017 with \"Attention Is All You Need\": nothing. Delete the recurrence, keep the attention, add some feed-forward layers and normalization, and you get a model that is easier to parallelize on GPUs, easier to optimize at depth, and that scales to sizes no recu"},{"p":"week-09.html","w":"Week 9","t":"The Transformer Architecture","h":"From recurrence to attention-only models","a":"history","x":"Recurrent networks have two structural problems, both visible in Week 7. First, they are sequential : the hidden state needs , so a sequence of length requires dependent steps and a GPU sits mostly idle. Second, information from step to step must pass through nonlinear transformations , so long-range dependencies are hard to learn even with gating. Convolutional sequence models (Week 7) shorten that path to with dilation, but still not to one. Self-attention has a path length of exactly one between any pair of positions, and all positions are computed in parallel. The price is that it costs time and memory in the sequence length, and that it is blind to order unless we inject position information separately. Both costs are real and shape most of this week. Layer type Sequential ops Max path length Cost per layer --- --- --- --- Recurrent Convolutional (kernel ) with dilation Self-attention Where the pieces came from Attention in neural translation is due to Bahdanau, Cho and Bengio (2014/2015) and Luong et al. (2015). Self-attention (a sequence attending to itself) appeared in Cheng et al. (2016), Parikh et al. (2016) and Lin et al. (2017), usually inside recurrent models. The 2017 paper combined self-attention with residual connections (He et al. 2016), layer normalization (Ba, Kiros and Hinton, 2016), multi-head projections and sinusoidal position signals, and trained it for translation in a few days on eight GPUs, beating the best recurrent and convolutional systems. The architecture was subsequently reused almost unchanged for GPT (2018), BERT (2018) and everything since."},{"p":"week-09.html","w":"Week 9","t":"The Transformer Architecture","h":"Self-attention from first principles","a":"self-attention","x":"Attention as a soft dictionary lookup { soft-dictionary} Start with something familiar: a Python dictionary. A lookup d[query] compares the query against each stored key ; if one key matches exactly, it returns that key's value . This is hard, all-or-nothing retrieval. Now make it soft. Suppose keys and queries are vectors, and \"match\" means a high dot product. Instead of returning one value, return a weighted average of all values, where the weights are larger for keys that match the query better. Weights must be non-negative and sum to one, which is exactly what the softmax produces: That is the whole mechanism. When the query matches one key much better than the others, is nearly one-hot and the result is nearly a hard lookup. When the query matches many keys similarly, the result is an average. The three roles are worth naming precisely: - the query says \"what am I looking for?\" - the key says \"what do I contain, as far as matching is concerned?\" - the value says \"what will I contribute if I am selected?\" Where Q, K and V come from { qkv} In self -attention, queries, keys and values are all computed from the same sequence of token representations. Let be the matrix whose row is the vector of token ( tokens, model width ). Three learned linear maps produce the three roles: with and . Why three different projections of the same input rather than using directly? Because the three jobs differ. In the sentence \"the animal didn't cross the street because it was too tired\", the word \"it\" should look for a noun-like thing (a query feature), the word \"animal\" should advertise that it is a noun and an animate subject (a key feature), and what \"animal\" passes along once selected (the value) may be a different set of features again. Separate projections let the model decouple m"},{"p":"week-09.html","w":"Week 9","t":"The Transformer Architecture","h":"Multi-head attention","a":"mha","x":"One attention head produces one weighted average per token. But a token typically needs several different kinds of information at once: the previous token, the syntactic head, a coreferent noun, a matching bracket. A single softmax has to compromise. Multi-head attention runs attention operations in parallel in lower-dimensional subspaces and concatenates the results. Definition and the shape bookkeeping { mha-shapes} Let be the model width and the number of heads, with . For head : where and . The cost is the same as one big head: matrices of shape hold the same number of weights as one matrix. In practice one fuses them: compute with a single matrix and then reshape the result into heads. Here are the exact tensor shapes with a batch dimension , sequence length : Shape trace through multi-head attention for , , . Heads are created by a view and transpose, not by separate matrices; the attention matrices are per head and per batch element; the heads are merged by the inverse transpose and reshape and mixed by . Step Operation Shape --- --- --- input (B, T, d) project X @ W Q (also K, V) (B, T, d) split heads view(B, T, h, d k) then transpose(1, 2) (B, h, T, d k) scores Q @ K.transpose(-2, -1) / sqrt(d k) (B, h, T, T) weights softmax(dim=-1) (B, h, T, T) weighted values A @ V (B, h, T, d k) merge heads transpose(1, 2) then reshape(B, T, d) (B, T, d) output projection @ W O (B, T, d) The output has the same shape as the input, which is what lets us stack layers and add residual connections. Why subspaces rather than full-width heads? If every head used the full width , cost would grow -fold. Splitting into slices of width keeps the budget constant while giving each head its own softmax, hence its own attention pattern. The trade-off is that each head's queries and keys l"},{"p":"week-09.html","w":"Week 9","t":"The Transformer Architecture","h":"The position-wise feed-forward network","a":"ffn","x":"After attention mixes information across positions, each token passes independently through the same two-layer MLP: with by convention (2048 for in the original paper) and originally ReLU, later GELU (Hendrycks and Gimpel, 2016) in BERT and GPT. \"Position-wise\" means the same weights are applied to every position; it is equivalent to two convolutions. Why do we need it? { why-ffn} Without the FFN a stack of attention layers is surprisingly weak. Each attention output is a convex combination of linearly projected inputs; stacking just re-mixes linear functions of the token embeddings through input-dependent weights. The FFN supplies the element-wise non-linearity and the bulk of the capacity: it holds about two thirds of the parameters of a block ( versus for attention, derived below). A useful reading, from Geva, Schuster, Berant and Levy (2021), treats the FFN as a key-value memory : the rows of act as keys that detect patterns in the residual stream, and the columns of are the values written back when the corresponding hidden unit fires. Attention then moves information between tokens, and the FFN looks facts up within a token. This division (communication versus computation) is a good heuristic for where to look when you study what a model \"knows\". Gated variants: GLU, SwiGLU { swiglu} Shazeer (2020, \"GLU Variants Improve Transformer\") showed that replacing the first linear layer and activation by a gated product gives consistently lower loss at the same compute: where and is element-wise multiplication. There are now three matrices instead of two, so to keep the parameter count equal models shrink the hidden width to roughly (rounded to a hardware-friendly multiple). LLaMA-family and many other open models use this form. The gate lets each hidden unit decide how muc"},{"p":"week-09.html","w":"Week 9","t":"The Transformer Architecture","h":"Residual connections and normalization","a":"norms","x":"The residual stream { residual-stream} Each sublayer (attention or FFN) is wrapped in a residual connection: the output is , as in ResNets (Week 6). In a Transformer this has a particularly clean interpretation due to Elhage et al. (2021): the vector at each position is a residual stream , a running sum to which every attention head and every FFN adds its contribution. Layer reads from the stream (through a projection), computes something, and writes the result back by addition. Nothing is ever overwritten; information from the embedding can flow unchanged to the last layer. That has an optimization benefit identical to ResNets (the gradient of the loss with respect to an early layer contains a direct identity path), and a conceptual one: a 96-layer model is not a 96-step bottleneck but a shared workspace that layers incrementally edit. Layer normalization { layernorm} Batch normalization (Week 3) is awkward for sequences: batch statistics are noisy with variable lengths and padding, and in autoregressive decoding the batch may be a single sequence. Layer normalization (Ba, Kiros and Hinton, 2016) normalizes each token vector over its feature dimension, independently of the batch and of the other positions: with learned gain and bias in and for numerical safety. In PyTorch, nn.LayerNorm(d) normalizes over the last dimension: the statistics are computed per token , so (B, T, d) produces separate means and variances. RMSNorm { rmsnorm} Zhang and Sennrich (2019) observed that re-centering (subtracting ) contributes little and that the benefit of LayerNorm comes mostly from re-scaling. RMSNorm drops the mean and the bias: It is cheaper (one reduction instead of two, no bias) and works as well in practice; LLaMA, Mistral, Gemma and many others use it. Both normalizations are"},{"p":"week-09.html","w":"Week 9","t":"The Transformer Architecture","h":"Positional information","a":"positions","x":"Since self-attention is permutation-equivariant, a model fed only token embeddings sees a bag of words. We must tell it where each token is. There are two broad strategies: - Absolute schemes add (or concatenate) a vector that depends on the position to the token embedding, once, at the input. - Relative schemes change the attention score so it depends on the offset between query position and key position , applied in every layer. Which is better is an empirical question that the field answered gradually in favor of relative schemes, especially RoPE. We cover each, with the derivations that explain their properties. Sinusoidal encodings { sinusoidal} The original paper used fixed (not learned) vectors , with pairs of coordinates oscillating at geometrically spaced frequencies: Think of it as a bank of clocks : pair 0 ticks fast (wavelength positions), the last pair ticks very slowly (wavelength about ). Reading all the clocks together identifies a position uniquely, like reading seconds, minutes and hours; and like a binary counter the low-order bits flip fast and the high-order bits slowly. The encoding is added to the token embedding: . Try the visualizer; the left columns of the matrix oscillate quickly, the right ones slowly. Position encodings: matrix, offset similarity, RoPE and ALiBi 560 Why sinusoids: shifts are linear maps, and dot products depend on the offset Fix a frequency . Write the pair at position as . The angle-addition formulas give So , where the matrix (a rotation by in this coordinate order) depends on the offset only, not on . Hence for any fixed , is a linear function of , which a linear layer could in principle exploit to attend by offset. Moreover the dot product of two encodings is using . It depends only on the offset , is symmetric in , and "},{"p":"week-09.html","w":"Week 9","t":"The Transformer Architecture","h":"Encoder-only, decoder-only and encoder-decoder models","a":"architectures","x":"The same block (attention, FFN, residuals, norms) can be assembled in three ways, distinguished by which attention masks they use and what they are trained to do. Attention masks of the three families. Rows are queries, columns are keys. Colored cells are allowed; gray cells are masked. The encoder-decoder uses all three kinds: bidirectional self-attention in the encoder, causal self-attention in the decoder, and unmasked cross-attention. Encoder-only (BERT, RoBERTa, DeBERTa). Bidirectional self-attention: every token sees the whole input. Output: a contextual vector for each token. Ideal for understanding tasks where the full input is available (classification, tagging, retrieval embeddings). It cannot generate text left to right because it has no autoregressive factorization. Trained with masked language modeling (Week 10). Decoder-only (GPT family, LLaMA, most chat models). Causal self-attention: token sees tokens . Trained to predict the next token, which gives a proper joint distribution . Generates by sampling one token at a time, feeding it back in. It is the simplest design, uses every position as a training signal, and scales well, which is why it dominates large language models. Encoder-decoder (original Transformer, T5, BART, many speech and translation systems). The encoder reads the input bidirectionally; the decoder generates the output causally while cross-attending to the encoder output. Natural for sequence-to-sequence problems with a clear input/output split (translation, summarization). It spends parameters on two stacks and cross-attention. Between them lies the prefix-LM : a single stack where the first part (the prompt or prefix) is attended bidirectionally and the rest causally (rightmost panel of the figure above). It gives an encoder's richer re"},{"p":"week-09.html","w":"Week 9","t":"The Transformer Architecture","h":"Computational cost and efficient attention","a":"complexity","x":"Counting the cost of a layer { cost-derivation} Count multiply-adds as 2 FLOPs. For sequence length and width (one sequence, ignoring biases and normalization): FLOPs of one Transformer layer - Q, K, V projections: three matrix products : . - Scores across all heads: products : . - Weighted values : another . - Output projection: . - FFN with : two products, . Total: The quadratic term equals the linear one when , i.e. at . For that is ; for (GPT-3 scale) it is . at the context lengths of the original paper ( to , ) attention is a minor part of the FLOPs, and the FFN and projections dominate. The quadratic term only becomes the bottleneck at long contexts: But FLOPs understate the problem. Two further costs grow as : - Memory for the score matrix. Materializing for , in 16-bit precision takes GB per layer per batch element (plus the same again for the softmax output, plus saved activations for backward). - Memory traffic. On a GPU, attention at moderate is limited by reading and writing these tensors to slow high-bandwidth memory, not by arithmetic. FlashAttention (Dao, Fu, Ermon, Rudra and Ré, 2022) attacks the second cost without approximation. It computes attention in tiles that fit in fast on-chip SRAM, maintaining a running maximum and running denominator for each row (the \"online softmax\" trick), so the matrix is never written to main memory. The result is the exact same output , with memory linear in and a substantial wall-clock speedup. FLOPs are still . In PyTorch you obtain it through F.scaled dot product attention . This is the pragmatic first answer to \"attention is quadratic\": use an exact IO-aware kernel before reaching for approximations. The KV cache and the cost of generation { kv-cache} At inference a decoder generates token given previous tokens. The "},{"p":"week-09.html","w":"Week 9","t":"The Transformer Architecture","h":"Counting parameters and FLOPs","a":"counting","x":"Parameters per layer { param-count} Take a standard pre-norm decoder block with width , FFN width , learned biases, and two LayerNorms: - attention: : weights plus biases; - FFN: weights plus biases; - two LayerNorms: . So a block has parameters, and blocks give . Add the token embedding (shared with the output head when weights are tied), a learned position table if used, and the final LayerNorm : For GPT-2 small ( , , , ): M, plus M, plus M, giving 124.4M, the published 124M. The \" \" term is the one to remember: non-embedding parameters . Use the calculator to check your own arithmetic against the presets, and to see how vocabulary size and learned positions matter for small models. Parameter and FLOP calculator for decoder-only Transformers 520 Training FLOPs: the 6N rule { flops-rule} A matrix multiply of an matrix by a matrix costs FLOPs. A token passing through all weight matrices of the model does one multiply-add per weight, so the forward pass costs about FLOPs per token ( counting matrix-multiplied weights; embedding lookups are free but the output head is a real matmul). The backward pass computes two products per weight matrix (the gradient with respect to the input, and with respect to the weights), each the size of the forward product, so it costs about . Forward plus backward gives the widely used estimate which ignores the attention-score term per token (forward and backward) that matters only when . GPT-2 small on 10 billion tokens costs about FLOPs: at a realistic 40% utilization of a 300 TFLOP/s accelerator (120 TFLOP/s effective) that is about 17 hours on one device. This rule is the basis of the scaling-law analysis in Week 10. Memory budget of training For mixed-precision training with Adam, each parameter costs roughly 16 bytes: 2 for the bf16/fp1"},{"p":"week-09.html","w":"Week 9","t":"The Transformer Architecture","h":"Training Transformers","a":"training","x":"Transformers are notoriously sensitive to optimization details. The recipe below is a compact summary of what practitioners converge on; each item has a reason, not only a tradition. Optimizer: AdamW and its betas { adamw} Use Adam with decoupled weight decay (AdamW, Loshchilov and Hutter, 2019). The original paper used , ; most LLM recipes use (a shorter memory of squared gradients reacts faster to the loss spikes that large models exhibit), , weight decay applied to weight matrices but not to biases, LayerNorm gains or embeddings, and gradient-norm clipping at 1.0. Learning-rate warmup { warmup} Almost every Transformer recipe increases the learning rate linearly from near zero over the first few hundred to few thousand steps. Why? 1. Adam's second-moment estimate is unreliable at the start. After a few steps is computed from very few gradients, so the effective per-parameter step can be wildly too large for some parameters. Liu et al. (2020, \"On the Variance of the Adaptive Learning Rate and Beyond\", RAdam) give this analysis and show that warmup is a heuristic cure for the high variance of the adaptive learning rate early on. 2. Post-norm layers have large initial gradients near the output (Xiong et al., 2020). A big step in a badly conditioned region can push the model into a bad basin it never leaves. Pre-norm substantially reduces this need for warmup, though large models still use it. 3. Attention is sharp at initialization for some heads, and large early updates can saturate softmaxes. The original paper's schedule, with , rises linearly and then decays as . Modern LLM recipes prefer linear warmup followed by cosine decay to about 10% of the peak (Week 3). Label smoothing { label-smoothing} For a vocabulary of classes the one-hot target pushes the model's logit"},{"p":"week-09.html","w":"Week 9","t":"The Transformer Architecture","h":"Lab: a GPT from scratch in PyTorch","a":"lab","x":"We now assemble every piece into a small GPT-style decoder (about 800k parameters), train it as a character-level language model on a tiny corpus, and sample text. The structure mirrors Karpathy's nanoGPT, which you should read afterwards. A GPU makes it fast (about two minutes on a Colab T4), but it also runs on CPU with smaller settings. Step 1: data { lab-data} Step 2: the model { lab-model} Two sanity checks before training. First, the initial loss should be close to (about 4.17 for ), because a model with tiny initial weights predicts a nearly uniform distribution. Second, a causality test : change a future token and verify that earlier logits are unchanged. Step 3: training { lab-train} On tinyshakespeare you should see the loss fall from about 4.2 at step 0 to roughly 1.6 to 1.8 on validation after 2000 steps (numbers vary with seed and hardware; a larger model with more steps reaches about 1.5). If your loss stays above 3, see the debugging checklist below. (Illustrative output; your numbers will differ in the second decimal.) Step 4: generation and the effect of temperature { lab-generate} Low temperature yields repetitive but locally well-formed text, temperature 1 samples from the model's actual distribution and produces more variety (and more typos), and top- trims the improbable tail. Week 10 analyzes these decoding strategies carefully and gives an interactive playground. Step 5: speed up generation with a KV cache (extension) { lab-kvcache} The generate method above recomputes everything for the whole context at each step. As an extension, modify CausalSelfAttention.forward to accept and return a (k, v) cache: when generating, feed only the newest token, concatenate its key and value to the cached ones along the time axis, and compute attention with is ca"},{"p":"week-09.html","w":"Week 9","t":"The Transformer Architecture","h":"Common mistakes and debugging tips","a":"pitfalls","x":"Symptom Likely cause Fix --- --- --- Loss is NaN from the first steps A row of the mask is all (softmax of all ); or fp16 overflow Ensure every query can attend to at least one key; use bf16 or a loss scaler; compute softmax in fp32 Loss decreases suspiciously fast to near zero Information leak : missing or wrong causal mask, or targets not shifted Run the causality test above; check y = x shifted by one Loss stuck near Learning rate too high or too low, no warmup, wrong init, forgot to call opt.step() or zero grads Overfit a single batch first; add warmup; lower LR Loss good but generations are garbage model.eval() not set during generation (dropout active); wrong context cropping; temperature bug Call eval() ; check that the last position's logits are sampled, not the first Boolean/float mask confusion True means allowed in scaled dot product attention but masked in nn.MultiheadAttention 's attn mask and key padding mask Read the docs for each API; write a unit test with a tiny known case Padding tokens influence outputs Pad keys not masked Build a key padding mask; test that appending pads does not change non-pad outputs Shapes are right but results wrong after reshaping heads Used view(B, h, T, dk) directly instead of view(B, T, h, dk).transpose(1, 2) The head split must keep each token's features contiguous; transpose after view Out-of-memory at longer sequence score matrices materialized Use fused SDPA, gradient checkpointing, smaller batch, shorter context Model cannot use word order Position information missing, or added to the wrong tensor Verify that permuting input tokens changes the output Training unstable at larger scale Post-norm without warmup, , attention logit growth Pre-norm, , grad clipping, QK-norm, lower LR The three debugging tests every Transform"},{"p":"week-09.html","w":"Week 9","t":"The Transformer Architecture","h":"Exercises","a":"exercises","x":"Exercise 1: variance and the scale factor (derivation) Suppose queries and keys have i.i.d. components with mean 0 and variance , independent of each other. (a) What is ? (b) What scaling factor restores unit variance? (c) After training, LayerNorm'd inputs and learned projections often give . Why does the factor still matter? Solution (a) Each product has variance , and the terms are independent, so . (b) Divide by ; with this is . (c) The factor fixes the initial statistics so that training starts in a well-conditioned regime; the network can then learn larger or smaller effective temperatures through the norms of . Without it, the scale at initialization grows with and the softmax saturates (gradients vanish) before the network has had a chance to adapt. Exercise 2: shapes of multi-head attention (conceptual) A model has , . For batch size 4 and sequence length 100, give the shape of every tensor from input to output of one self-attention layer, the number of parameters in the layer (with biases), and the number of floats stored for the attention probabilities. Solution . Input (4, 100, 768) ; after the fused QKV projection (4, 100, 2304) , split into three (4, 100, 768) ; heads (4, 12, 100, 64) ; scores and probabilities (4, 12, 100, 100) ; weighted values (4, 12, 100, 64) ; merged (4, 100, 768) ; output (4, 100, 768) . Parameters: . Attention probabilities: floats per layer (about 0.96 MB in fp16), which for would be floats: the quadratic cost in action. Exercise 3: implement masks (coding) Write a function make mask(lengths, T) returning a boolean (B, 1, T, T) tensor that combines a causal mask with a key padding mask for sequences of the given true lengths. Test that (i) future entries are False, (ii) padded keys are False for every query, and (iii) the output of"},{"p":"week-09.html","w":"Week 9","t":"The Transformer Architecture","h":"Key takeaways","a":"takeaways","x":"Key takeaways - Attention is a soft, differentiable dictionary lookup : output , each token's output a convex combination of value vectors. It is permutation-equivariant, so positions must be injected. - The factor keeps the logit variance at 1 (the raw variance is ), preventing softmax saturation and vanishing gradients at initialization. - Multi-head attention splits into subspaces by view + transpose : tensors are (B, h, T, d k) and the score tensor is (B, h, T, T) . Heads are merged and mixed by . - Block = communication + computation : attention moves information between tokens, the FFN (about two thirds of the parameters, ) transforms each token, and residual connections form the stream both write to. - Pre-norm (with RMSNorm) keeps an identity path and trains stably; post-norm needs warmup and care. - Position : sinusoidal and learned absolute vectors are added at the input; relative biases, RoPE (rotate so scores depend on ) and ALiBi act on the scores. RoPE is the modern default, with scaling for longer contexts. - Architectures differ by mask : encoder-only (full), decoder-only (causal), encoder-decoder (full + causal + cross). The causal mask enables parallel training and requires a KV cache for fast generation. - Cost : a layer is FLOPs; parameters ; training . The quadratic term bites beyond and in memory first. FlashAttention (exact), GQA and KV caching are the baseline fixes; sparse and linear variants are approximations with trade-offs. - Training recipe : AdamW with , linear warmup then cosine decay, gradient clipping, bf16 mixed precision (fp16 needs loss scaling), weight decay on matrices only, scaled residual-output init."},{"p":"week-09.html","w":"Week 9","t":"The Transformer Architecture","h":"Further reading","a":"further-reading","x":"- (Essential) Vaswani, Shazeer, Parmar, Uszkoreit, Jones, Gomez, Kaiser, Polosukhin. \"Attention Is All You Need.\" NeurIPS 2017 (arXiv:1706.03762). - (Essential) Jay Alammar, \"The Illustrated Transformer\" (blog, 2018): the best visual walkthrough. - (Essential) Andrej Karpathy, \"Let's build GPT: from scratch, in code, spelled out\" (video, 2023) and the nanoGPT repository. - Elhage et al. \"A Mathematical Framework for Transformer Circuits.\" Anthropic, 2021: the residual-stream view. - Xiong et al. \"On Layer Normalization in the Transformer Architecture.\" ICML 2020 (arXiv:2002.04745): pre-norm versus post-norm. - Su, Lu, Pan, Murtadha, Wen, Liu. \"RoFormer: Enhanced Transformer with Rotary Position Embedding.\" 2021 (arXiv:2104.09864). - Press, Smith, Lewis. \"Train Short, Test Long: Attention with Linear Biases Enables Input Length Extrapolation.\" ICLR 2022 (arXiv:2108.12409). - Shaw, Uszkoreit, Vaswani. \"Self-Attention with Relative Position Representations.\" NAACL 2018. - Dao, Fu, Ermon, Rudra, Ré. \"FlashAttention: Fast and Memory-Efficient Exact Attention with IO-Awareness.\" NeurIPS 2022 (arXiv:2205.14135). - Katharopoulos, Vyas, Pappas, Fleuret. \"Transformers are RNNs: Fast Autoregressive Transformers with Linear Attention.\" ICML 2020 (arXiv:2006.16236). - Child, Gray, Radford, Sutskever. \"Generating Long Sequences with Sparse Transformers.\" 2019 (arXiv:1904.10509); Beltagy, Peters, Cohan. \"Longformer.\" 2020 (arXiv:2004.05150). - Zhang, Sennrich. \"Root Mean Square Layer Normalization.\" NeurIPS 2019; Shazeer. \"GLU Variants Improve Transformer.\" 2020 (arXiv:2002.05202). - Geva, Schuster, Berant, Levy. \"Transformer Feed-Forward Layers Are Key-Value Memories.\" EMNLP 2021. - Radford et al. \"Language Models are Unsupervised Multitask Learners\" (GPT-2), 2019; Micikevicius et al"},{"p":"week-10.html","w":"Week 10","t":"Large Language Models and Pretrained NLP","h":"Large Language Models and Pretrained NLP","a":"","x":"Learning objectives By the end of this week you should be able to: - Compare masked language modeling, autoregressive modeling and span corruption: what each loss predicts, which architecture it pairs with, and what each is good and bad at. - Fit and use scaling laws: read a power law, derive the compute-optimal split of a budget between parameters and tokens from the Chinchilla loss model, and say what the laws do not tell you. - Evaluate claims about \"emergent abilities\" critically, including the role of the metric. - Derive the parameter savings of LoRA, size an adapter budget for a given model, and explain QLoRA's three ingredients, adapters, and prefix/prompt tuning. - Describe the alignment pipeline (SFT, reward modeling, RLHF with a KL penalty) and derive the DPO loss from the KL-regularized reward-maximization objective. - Explain in-context learning, prompting and chain-of-thought, and design a retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) system with its failure modes. - Evaluate language models with perplexity and benchmarks, and recognize contamination, hallucination and other failure modes. - Implement and compare decoding strategies (greedy, beam, temperature, top-k, top-p) and fine-tune BERT and a small causal LM with LoRA using Hugging Face. - Read, summarize and present a research paper in a way that exposes its claims, evidence and limits. Week 9 ended with a model you could train from scratch on a tiny corpus. This week asks what changes when the corpus has a trillion tokens and the model has billions of parameters. The surprising answer is that the architecture hardly changes at all. What changes is the recipe around it: how you turn raw text into a supervised problem without labels, how you decide how big to make the model and how long to train it, how you a"},{"p":"week-10.html","w":"Week 10","t":"Large Language Models and Pretrained NLP","h":"Pretraining objectives","a":"pretraining","x":"Supervised learning needs labels, and labels are expensive. Text, however, labels itself: any part of a document can be used to predict any other part. A pretraining objective is a rule for hiding part of the text and asking the model to recover it. The choice of rule determines which architecture is natural (recall the encoder/decoder/encoder-decoder families in Week 9), what the model is good at, and how efficiently it uses each training token. The three canonical pretraining objectives on the same sentence. BERT hides tokens and reads in both directions; GPT predicts every next token from the left context; T5 replaces spans by sentinel tokens and generates the dropped spans. Masked language modeling (BERT) { mlm} Devlin, Chang, Lee and Toutanova (2019) trained a bidirectional Transformer encoder to fill in blanks. Pick a random set of about 15% of the positions in a sequence , corrupt them, and train the model to reconstruct the originals from the corrupted sequence : The corruption is slightly subtle. Of the selected positions, 80% are replaced by a special [MASK] token, 10% by a random token, and 10% are left unchanged. The reason is a train/test mismatch: [MASK] never appears when you later fine-tune on a classification task, so if every selected token were masked the network could learn representations that only \"switch on\" at mask positions. Leaving some tokens unchanged or random forces the model to build a good representation of every input token, because any of them might be the one it will be graded on. The model is the encoder stack from Week 9 (12 layers, hidden size 768, 12 heads, about 110M parameters for BERT-base; 24 layers, 1024, 16 heads, about 340M for BERT-large) with a linear-plus-softmax head over the vocabulary applied to the final hidden state "},{"p":"week-10.html","w":"Week 10","t":"Large Language Models and Pretrained NLP","h":"Tokenization, briefly revisited","a":"tokenization","x":"Week 8 derived byte-pair encoding (BPE). For LLMs the tokenizer is part of the model, fixed before pretraining, and its quirks leak into behavior. Three families dominate: - WordPiece (BERT): greedy longest-match subword vocabulary with continuation markers, vocabulary about 30k. - Byte-level BPE (GPT-2 and successors): merges operate on UTF-8 bytes, so any string is representable and there is no unknown token. Vocabularies range from about 50k to over 100k. - SentencePiece (T5, LLaMA): operates directly on raw text (treating whitespace as a symbol), with BPE or a unigram language model objective, which makes the tokenizer language-agnostic. Why should you care? Because the tokenizer determines the unit of prediction. Numbers may be split into arbitrary chunks, which makes arithmetic harder; the same word with and without a leading space can be different tokens; non-English languages may need two to five times more tokens per word (\"fertility\"), making them more expensive and giving them a shorter effective context; and rare strings can become \"glitch tokens\" that appear in the vocabulary but almost never in training data, producing erratic output. Special tokens ( [CLS] , [SEP] , , chat-role markers) are part of the interface, and forgetting them or adding them twice is one of the most common silent bugs. You will see that \" hello\" and \"hello\" differ for the byte-level BPE tokenizer but not for BERT's, and that the number string is split into several pieces by every tokenizer. The BERT pair encoding begins with [CLS] , separates the segments with [SEP] , and the token type ids tell the model which segment each token belongs to."},{"p":"week-10.html","w":"Week 10","t":"Large Language Models and Pretrained NLP","h":"Scaling laws","a":"scaling-laws","x":"Training a frontier model costs millions of dollars, and you only get a handful of attempts. How do you choose the model size, the dataset size and the training length without running every combination? The empirical answer of the last few years is that language-model loss is a remarkably smooth, predictable function of scale , so you can run small experiments and extrapolate. Power laws: Kaplan et al. { kaplan} Kaplan, McCandlish, Henighan, Brown, Chess, Child, Gray, Radford, Wu and Amodei (OpenAI, 2020) trained Transformer language models over many orders of magnitude and measured the held-out cross-entropy loss (in nats per token). When one factor is the bottleneck and the others are ample, the loss follows a power law : where is the number of non-embedding parameters, the number of training tokens, the compute, and the fitted exponents were roughly , and (with and ). The exponents are small, so each factor-of-ten in scale lowers the loss by a modest, constant multiplicative factor: for parameters. On log-log axes the law is a straight line, and that straightness over seven or more orders of magnitude is what makes extrapolation credible. What a power law with a small exponent means A power law has no characteristic scale and no plateau until you hit the irreducible entropy of the data. Going from 1B to 10B parameters buys the same relative improvement as 10B to 100B, but the absolute cost grows tenfold each time. Progress is therefore steady but expensive: you pay exponentially more for linear gains on a log scale. It also means that improvements to the \"constant\" (better data, better architectures) are worth as much as many doublings of compute. et al. also reported that, for a fixed compute budget, loss depends only weakly on model shape (depth versus width), that"},{"p":"week-10.html","w":"Week 10","t":"Large Language Models and Pretrained NLP","h":"Emergent abilities and their critiques","a":"emergent","x":"Looking at downstream tasks rather than loss, Wei et al. (2022) catalogued \"emergent abilities\": capabilities that were near chance for small models and then rose sharply above some scale, such as multi-digit arithmetic, some word-unscrambling tasks and, later, chain-of-thought reasoning. They defined an ability as emergent if it is not present in smaller models but present in larger ones, so that it cannot be predicted by extrapolating the small-model curve. This framing gave rise to the widespread idea that new skills appear \"suddenly\" at certain sizes. Schaeffer, Miranda and Koyejo (NeurIPS 2023) pointed out that much of the sharpness may come from the metric rather than the model . Suppose the per-token accuracy improves smoothly with scale. A task scored by exact match on a -token answer succeeds only if all tokens are right, so the score is . Even for a perfectly smooth , for large looks like a hockey stick: flat near zero for a long stretch and then a steep rise. Switch to a continuous metric (token-level accuracy, log-likelihood of the right answer) and the curve becomes smooth and predictable. They also showed that sharp transitions can be made to appear or vanish by changing the metric on the same model outputs. The critique is not a complete refutation. Some points that a careful reader should hold together: - Exact-match behavior is what a user experiences on a task where partial credit does not exist, so \"the capability becomes usable only at large scale\" is a legitimate observation even if the underlying competence grew smoothly. - Some behaviors, in particular multi-step reasoning, may genuinely require components to be simultaneously correct, so there may be real thresholds (a composition of steps each with probability is again ). The metric argument and"},{"p":"week-10.html","w":"Week 10","t":"Large Language Models and Pretrained NLP","h":"Fine-tuning strategies","a":"finetuning","x":"A pretrained model is a general-purpose starting point. Fine-tuning continues training on a smaller, task-specific dataset so that the model specializes. The design question is which parameters you update , and the answers range from all of them to a few thousand. Full fine-tuning { full-finetuning} The classical recipe, used for BERT: add a small head (for classification, a linear layer on the [CLS] hidden state), initialize everything else from the pretrained checkpoint, and train all parameters on the labeled data with a small learning rate (typically to for BERT-size encoders), a few epochs, linear warmup and decay. It is the strongest baseline and the simplest to reason about. It also has real costs: - Memory. With mixed-precision AdamW you store, per parameter, a 16-bit weight copy (2 bytes), a 16-bit gradient (2), an FP32 master weight (4) and two FP32 Adam moments (8): about 16 bytes per parameter , before activations. A 7B-parameter model needs roughly 112 GB just for this state, far beyond one consumer GPU. - Storage. One full copy of the model per task. - Forgetting and instability. A large learning rate or too many epochs on a small dataset can overwrite useful pretrained features (catastrophic forgetting) and results can vary noticeably across random seeds on small datasets. Remedies include small learning rates, layer-wise learning-rate decay, early stopping and mixing in some pretraining-style data. Fine-tuning a classifier on top of frozen features The cheapest baseline is to freeze the encoder, extract features once, and train a linear classifier (a \"linear probe\"). It is fast and a useful diagnostic of representation quality, but usually falls short of full fine-tuning on text tasks. Parameter-efficient methods aim to close that gap while training a ti"},{"p":"week-10.html","w":"Week 10","t":"Large Language Models and Pretrained NLP","h":"Instruction tuning, in-context learning and prompting","a":"instruction-tuning","x":"From pretrained model to instruction follower { instruction-tuning-what} A pretrained autoregressive model is a text continuer , not an assistant. Given \"What is the capital of France?\" it may continue with more quiz questions, because that is what such text looks like on the web. Instruction tuning fine-tunes the model on many (instruction, response) pairs so that following the instruction becomes the most likely continuation. The first demonstrations of the idea were multitask: FLAN (Wei et al., 2022) and T0 (Sanh et al., 2022) rewrote dozens of existing NLP datasets as natural-language instructions and fine-tuned on the mixture. The surprising result was zero-shot generalization to held-out task types : a model tuned on many instruction-formatted tasks did better on unseen tasks than the same model without that tuning, and scaling the number of tasks and the model size both helped (Chung et al., 2022). A second line collected open-ended prompts and high-quality human-written answers. InstructGPT (Ouyang et al., 2022) used about 13,000 labeler-written demonstrations; Self-Instruct (Wang et al., 2023) had a model generate its own instructions and answers from a small seed set, which inspired cheap imitation datasets such as Alpaca. LIMA (Zhou et al., 2023) showed that about a thousand carefully curated examples were enough to produce a strong chat model from a strong base, supporting the superficial alignment hypothesis : most knowledge and ability come from pretraining, and instruction tuning mainly teaches the format and style of the interaction. Quality, diversity and correctness of the demonstrations matter far more than their count. Mechanically, supervised fine-tuning (SFT) is the same next-token cross-entropy as pretraining with two changes. The input is rendere"},{"p":"week-10.html","w":"Week 10","t":"Large Language Models and Pretrained NLP","h":"Alignment: SFT, reward modeling, RLHF and DPO","a":"alignment","x":"Instruction tuning on demonstrations has a ceiling: writing a perfect answer is hard, while comparing two answers is much easier. Alignment methods exploit that asymmetry. The recipe of InstructGPT (Ouyang et al., 2022), built on earlier work on learning from human preferences (Christiano et al., 2017; Stiennon et al., 2020), has three stages after pretraining. The InstructGPT-style pipeline. Stage 1 supervised fine-tuning on demonstrations; stage 2 a reward model trained on human rankings of sampled responses; stage 3 policy optimization against the reward with a KL penalty to the SFT model. DPO collapses stages 2 and 3 into one supervised-style loss. Reward modeling { reward-model} Collect prompts , sample several responses from the SFT model, and ask labelers to rank them. Each ranking yields preference pairs with a winner and loser . The Bradley-Terry model says the probability that is preferred is a logistic function of a scalar reward difference: The reward model is usually the SFT Transformer with the vocabulary head replaced by a linear layer that outputs one scalar from the final token's hidden state. Only reward differences matter, so the absolute scale is arbitrary. RLHF with a KL penalty { rlhf} Treat the language model as a policy and maximize the learned reward, but keep the policy close to a reference (the SFT model ) so that it does not drift into text that fools the reward model: This is optimized with a policy-gradient method, typically PPO (Schulman et al., 2017), with the KL term applied per token and a learned value function to reduce variance. The setup is heavy: you keep the policy, a reference model, a reward model and a value model in memory, and training can be unstable. The KL coefficient is the central knob. Too small and the policy exploits "},{"p":"week-10.html","w":"Week 10","t":"Large Language Models and Pretrained NLP","h":"Decoding strategies","a":"decoding","x":"A trained language model gives you a distribution at each step. Decoding is the algorithm that turns those distributions into a text. The choice is not a detail: the same model can produce dull, repetitive text or creative, error-prone text depending only on how you sample. Greedy and beam search { greedy-beam} Greedy decoding picks at each step. It is deterministic and fast, but myopic: the best token now may lead to a worse sequence overall. Beam search , which you met for translation in Week 7, keeps the highest-scoring partial sequences (by total log-probability, usually length-normalized) and extends all of them. Beam search finds sequences of higher probability than greedy search, which is what you want when there is a right answer close to the mode, as in translation, summarization or speech recognition. For open-ended generation, high probability is the wrong target. Holtzman, Buys, Du, Forbes and Choi (2020) showed that the most likely continuations of a large language model are bland and repetitive (\"text degeneration\"), while human text is not the mode of the model distribution: it contains many individually low-probability words. Greedy and beam outputs often fall into loops, which is a sign that the model's probability mass concentrates on repetition once it has begun. Temperature { temperature} Sampling draws . To control randomness, rescale the logits by a temperature before the softmax: As the distribution collapses onto the argmax (greedy); is the model's own distribution; as it flattens to uniform. Temperature does not change the ranking of tokens, only how much mass the top tokens get. It is the same temperature that appeared in attention and in the InfoNCE loss (Week 8), and the same trade-off: low temperature means confident and repetitive, high tem"},{"p":"week-10.html","w":"Week 10","t":"Large Language Models and Pretrained NLP","h":"Retrieval-augmented generation","a":"rag","x":"A language model's knowledge is frozen into its weights at training time. That causes three problems: the knowledge goes stale, the model cannot cite where a claim came from, and rarely seen facts are stored unreliably. Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG; Lewis et al., 2020) addresses all three by fetching relevant documents at query time and placing them in the prompt, so that the model can read the answer instead of remembering it. A typical RAG pipeline. Offline, the corpus is chunked and embedded into a vector index. At query time the question is embedded, the nearest chunks are retrieved, and the generator receives instructions, the retrieved passages and the question. The components { rag-components} 1. Chunking. Split documents into passages (a few hundred tokens, often with overlap and with titles or section headers attached). Chunks that are too large dilute the relevant sentence; too small and they lose context. 2. Embedding and indexing. Encode each chunk with an embedding model, usually a BERT-style encoder trained contrastively (the same InfoNCE idea as in Week 8, with queries and relevant passages as positive pairs; DPR, Karpukhin et al., 2020). Store vectors in an approximate nearest-neighbor index (HNSW or IVF structures, as in FAISS) so search scales to millions of chunks. 3. Retrieval. Embed the query, fetch the top- chunks by cosine or inner-product similarity. Many systems combine dense retrieval with lexical retrieval (BM25) in a hybrid search, because dense models are weak on rare names and exact identifiers while BM25 is weak on paraphrase. 4. Reranking (optional). A cross-encoder that reads query and passage together rescores the top 50 to 100 candidates much more accurately than the bi-encoder, at higher cost per pair. 5. Generation. Insert the"},{"p":"week-10.html","w":"Week 10","t":"Large Language Models and Pretrained NLP","h":"Evaluating language models","a":"evaluation","x":"Perplexity { perplexity} The intrinsic metric of a language model is its held-out cross-entropy. Perplexity is its exponential: Intuitively, a perplexity of 20 means the model is on average as uncertain as if it were choosing uniformly among 20 equally likely tokens. A uniform model over a vocabulary of size has perplexity . Three cautions. (i) Perplexity is measured per token , so models with different tokenizers are not comparable ; use bits per byte or bits per character, which normalize by the raw text length. (ii) Perplexity requires a proper left-to-right probability model, so it is not defined for BERT-style masked models without pseudo-likelihood tricks. (iii) Lower perplexity does not guarantee better behavior on tasks, especially after instruction tuning and RLHF, which deliberately move the model away from the data distribution; it is nonetheless the metric that scaling laws are stated in, and it is a good sanity check for pretraining and for continued-pretraining or quantization (a few percent change is a warning sign). Benchmarks { benchmarks} Task benchmarks test capabilities that perplexity cannot. Representative ones: GLUE and SuperGLUE (language understanding, classic fine-tuning era); MMLU (Hendrycks et al., 2021; 57 subjects of multiple-choice questions from elementary to professional level); BIG-bench (Srivastava et al., 2022; hundreds of diverse tasks); HELM (Liang et al., 2022; a holistic framework that reports accuracy together with calibration, robustness, fairness, toxicity and efficiency); GSM8K (grade-school math word problems); HumanEval (Chen et al., 2021; Python function synthesis checked by unit tests); TruthfulQA (Lin et al., 2022; questions where imitating common human misconceptions yields false answers). Pairwise human preference platf"},{"p":"week-10.html","w":"Week 10","t":"Large Language Models and Pretrained NLP","h":"Hallucination and other failure modes","a":"failure-modes","x":"Hallucination { hallucination} A hallucination is fluent output that is not supported by the input ( intrinsic , contradicting the provided source) or not supported by the world ( extrinsic , an invented fact, citation or API). The term is imperfect, and some authors prefer \"confabulation\", but it names a real and central failure. Why does it happen? The training objective rewards plausible continuation , not truth: there is no term that penalizes a fluent falsehood more than a fluent truth unless the data happen to. When the model's knowledge is thin (rare entities in the \"long tail\"), the most plausible continuation of \"The author of this obscure paper is\" is still a well-formed name. Once a wrong token is generated it becomes part of the context, and later tokens condition on it ( error snowballing ). Preference training can make this worse by rewarding confident, helpful-sounding answers over \"I don't know\". On the positive side, models carry some usable signal about their own uncertainty: Kadavath et al. (2022) found that large models' stated probabilities of correctness are reasonably calibrated on multiple-choice questions, and that they can be trained to predict whether they know an answer. Mitigations form a stack: retrieval and grounding with citations (RAG); instructing and training the model to abstain; sampling several answers and checking agreement (self-consistency); verifying claims with tools such as code execution or a calculator or search; constraining output formats; and human review for high-stakes content. None gives a guarantee. Other failure modes you should know { other-failures} - Prompt injection and jailbreaks. Instructions hidden in user input or retrieved documents can override the developer's instructions, because the model has no hard bou"},{"p":"week-10.html","w":"Week 10","t":"Large Language Models and Pretrained NLP","h":"Lab: fine-tuning BERT, then LoRA on a small causal LM","a":"lab","x":"This lab uses Hugging Face transformers , datasets and peft . A free Colab GPU is enough. Install once: Part A: fine-tune BERT for sentiment classification { lab-bert} We fine-tune bert-base-uncased on SST-2 (binary movie-review sentiment, part of GLUE). Expect roughly 92% validation accuracy after two epochs. The pretrained encoder is reused; the classification head (a linear layer on the pooled [CLS] representation) is new and randomly initialized, which is why Transformers prints a warning that some weights are newly initialized. Things to try: freeze the encoder ( for p in model.bert.parameters(): p.requires grad = False ) and compare accuracy and training time with full fine-tuning; train on only 500 examples and compare with a TF-IDF plus logistic regression baseline; change the learning rate to and watch the model collapse to predicting one class. Part B: LoRA on a small causal language model { lab-lora} We adapt facebook/opt-125m (12 layers, hidden size 768) to the style of children's stories using the TinyStories dataset, training only low-rank adapters on the attention query and value projections. The same code works for larger models by changing the checkpoint and target modules (for LLaMA-style models use q proj , k proj , v proj , o proj , gate proj , up proj , down proj ). You should see the held-out loss drop clearly (perplexity falling by a large factor), the generations adopt the simple vocabulary and sentence structure of the dataset, and the saved adapter file be about a megabyte whereas the full model is about 250 MB. Experiments that make the theory concrete 1. Sweep and plot held-out loss against trainable parameters. Where does it saturate? 2. Compare q proj, v proj against all linear layers ( q proj, k proj, v proj, out proj, fc1, fc2 in OPT) at "},{"p":"week-10.html","w":"Week 10","t":"Large Language Models and Pretrained NLP","h":"How to read and present a paper","a":"reading-papers","x":"Starting this week, one or two students present a paper each session from the curated list. Reading research papers is a skill, and it is the most transferable one in this course: architectures change, but the habit of extracting claims and checking evidence does not. This section is a practical guide. The three-pass approach to reading a paper (after S. Keshav, \"How to Read a Paper\", 2007). Spend the time proportional to how much you need from the paper; most papers deserve only the first pass. Reading: three passes { three-pass} Pass 1 (5 to 10 minutes): survey. Read the title, abstract and introduction, glance at the section headings, read the conclusion, and look at every figure and table without reading the text. Answer: What category of paper is this (new method, analysis, benchmark, theory, system)? What is the claimed contribution? Is it relevant to what I need? Stop here for most papers. Pass 2 (about an hour): grasp the content. Read carefully but skip derivation details. Study the main figure of the method until you can redraw it. For each experiment table, ask: what is the comparison, what is held fixed, and what would the numbers look like if the claim were false? Mark terms and references you do not know. Pass 3 (several hours): reproduce. Re-derive key equations, try to predict the experiments before reading them, and read the appendix, where the hyperparameters and honest details often hide. For a paper you will implement, check the code release against the text. A checklist of questions { paper-questions} Question What to look for --- --- What problem, and why does it matter? A concrete task or failure of prior work, stated without buzzwords What is the key idea, in one sentence? If you cannot say it plainly, you have not understood it yet What exactly "},{"p":"week-10.html","w":"Week 10","t":"Large Language Models and Pretrained NLP","h":"Common mistakes and debugging tips","a":"mistakes","x":"Symptom Likely cause Fix --- --- --- Fine-tuned BERT predicts one class for everything, loss stuck near 0.69 Learning rate too high (e.g. ), or no warmup Use with warmup; check labels are 0/1 and not shuffled against inputs Training loss near zero but validation accuracy poor Label leakage, duplicate train/val examples, or overfitting small data Deduplicate; use fewer epochs and early stopping; evaluate on a clean split Newly added classification head has terrible accuracy before training Expected: the head is random. Warning message is normal Train it; do not evaluate before fine-tuning LoRA run loss does not decrease Wrong target modules names (nothing adapted), LR too low, or adapters not in train mode Inspect model.print trainable parameters() and model.named modules() for the right names; use LR about to LoRA model is worse than the base on other tasks Adapter learned a narrow distribution; rank or LR too high Lower or LR, add regularizing data, or scale the adapter down at inference Model never stops generating, or repeats the prompt End-of-sequence token missing from the training targets, or wrong chat template Append the EOS or end-of-turn token to every training example and keep it in the loss Loss computed on prompt tokens in SFT Labels equal inputs for the whole sequence Set the label to -100 for prompt tokens so only responses are trained Padding tokens influence training Labels at padding positions not masked Use -100 on pads; ensure the attention mask is passed temperature / top p have no effect in generate do sample=False (the default) Set do sample=True Out-of-memory when fine-tuning Full-precision full fine-tune; long sequences; large batch Use LoRA/QLoRA, gradient checkpointing, gradient accumulation, shorter maximum length, BF16 Right-padded batch giv"},{"p":"week-10.html","w":"Week 10","t":"Large Language Models and Pretrained NLP","h":"Exercises","a":"exercises","x":"Exercise 1: the BERT masking recipe (conceptual) BERT selects 15% of tokens; of those, 80% become [MASK] , 10% a random token and 10% stay unchanged. (a) What fraction of all tokens in a sequence is replaced by [MASK] , replaced by a random token, and left unchanged but graded? (b) Why would \"always use [MASK] \" create a train/test mismatch? (c) Is the model told which of the 15% positions are \"random\" or \"unchanged\"? Solution (a) are [MASK] , are random tokens, and are unchanged but still in the loss; 85% of tokens contribute no loss. (b) At fine-tuning and inference time the input never contains [MASK] ; a model trained only on masked positions could learn to treat the mask symbol as the only place where careful prediction is required, and to produce weak representations for ordinary tokens. (c) No. For a position holding an ordinary-looking token the model cannot know whether it is genuine, corrupted or is being graded, so it must keep a careful representation of every position. Exercise 2: compute-optimal sizing (calculation) You have a budget of FLOPs. (a) Using and the rule of thumb , find and . (b) With the parametric fit of the text, ( , , , , ) compute the predicted loss for your answer to (a). (c) A deployment team says the model will serve a huge number of requests; which direction should you move and and why? Solution (a) , so (91B parameters) and tokens. (b) : , giving . , giving . Sum: . (c) Move toward a smaller model trained on more tokens: inference cost per query scales with , so paying extra training compute to reach the same loss with a smaller pays off over many queries (this is what over-trained models like LLaMA do). Exercise 3: sizing LoRA (calculation) A model has 32 layers, hidden size and FFN width 11008 (gated FFN with gate, up and down proje"},{"p":"week-10.html","w":"Week 10","t":"Large Language Models and Pretrained NLP","h":"Key takeaways","a":"takeaways","x":"Key takeaways - Pretraining objectives differ in what they hide and what the model sees: MLM (encoder, bidirectional, about 15% of positions graded), autoregressive (decoder, every position graded, generative), span corruption (encoder-decoder, text-to-text). Decoder-only autoregressive models dominate general-purpose LLMs. - Loss falls as a smooth power law in parameters, data and compute. Chinchilla showed that for a fixed compute budget you should scale and about equally (roughly 20 tokens per parameter); over-training smaller models is justified by inference cost. The exact constants are empirical. - \"Emergent abilities\" are partly an artifact of discontinuous metrics (exact match is ); loss extrapolates well, specific skills do not. - Fine-tuning ranges from full (16 bytes per parameter of training state) to LoRA (train parameters per matrix, mergeable, no inference cost), QLoRA (4-bit NF4 base plus LoRA), adapters, and prefix/prompt tuning. - Instruction tuning teaches format and behavior; in-context learning and chain-of-thought elicit behavior at inference time without weight updates. - Alignment: SFT, reward model with the Bradley-Terry loss, KL-regularized RL, or DPO, which derives a supervised loss from the same objective by eliminating the reward model. - RAG supplies fresh, attributable knowledge but adds retrieval failure modes; evaluate retriever and generator separately. - Evaluate honestly: perplexity (tokenizer-dependent), benchmarks (prompt-sensitive, contamination-prone), pass@k, human and LLM judges (biased). Hallucination is a consequence of the objective, mitigated but not eliminated by grounding and verification. - Decoding is a modeling choice: greedy and beam for tasks with right answers; temperature, top- and top- for open-ended text. - Readin"},{"p":"week-10.html","w":"Week 10","t":"Large Language Models and Pretrained NLP","h":"Further reading","a":"further-reading","x":"Essential - Devlin, Chang, Lee, Toutanova. BERT: Pre-training of Deep Bidirectional Transformers for Language Understanding. NAACL 2019. arXiv:1810.04805. - Hoffmann et al. Training Compute-Optimal Large Language Models (Chinchilla). 2022. arXiv:2203.15556. - Hu et al. LoRA: Low-Rank Adaptation of Large Language Models. ICLR 2022. arXiv:2106.09685. Pretraining and scaling - Radford et al. Improving Language Understanding by Generative Pre-Training (GPT-1), 2018; Language Models are Unsupervised Multitask Learners (GPT-2), 2019. OpenAI technical reports. - Brown et al. Language Models are Few-Shot Learners (GPT-3). NeurIPS 2020. arXiv:2005.14165. - Raffel et al. Exploring the Limits of Transfer Learning with a Unified Text-to-Text Transformer (T5). JMLR 2020. arXiv:1910.10683. - Liu et al. RoBERTa: A Robustly Optimized BERT Pretraining Approach. 2019. arXiv:1907.11692. - Kaplan et al. Scaling Laws for Neural Language Models. 2020. arXiv:2001.08361. - Touvron et al. LLaMA: Open and Efficient Foundation Language Models. 2023. arXiv:2302.13971. - Wei et al. Emergent Abilities of Large Language Models. TMLR 2022. arXiv:2206.07682. Schaeffer, Miranda, Koyejo. Are Emergent Abilities of Large Language Models a Mirage? NeurIPS 2023. arXiv:2304.15004. Fine-tuning - Houlsby et al. Parameter-Efficient Transfer Learning for NLP (adapters). ICML 2019. arXiv:1902.00751. - Li, Liang. Prefix-Tuning: Optimizing Continuous Prompts for Generation. ACL 2021. arXiv:2101.00190. Lester, Al-Rfou, Constant. The Power of Scale for Parameter-Efficient Prompt Tuning. EMNLP 2021. arXiv:2104.08691. - Dettmers, Pagnoni, Holtzman, Zettlemoyer. QLoRA: Efficient Finetuning of Quantized LLMs. NeurIPS 2023. arXiv:2305.14314. Instruction tuning, prompting, alignment - Wei et al. Finetuned Language Models Ar"},{"p":"week-11.html","w":"Week 11","t":"Vision Transformers, Multimodal Models, and Self-Supervised Learning","h":"Vision Transformers, Multimodal Models, and Self-Supervised Learning","a":"","x":"Learning objectives By the end of this week you should be able to: - Trace a image through a ViT-B/16 and state the exact tensor shape after patch embedding, after adding the class token and position embeddings, inside each attention head, and at the classifier. - Explain what a CNN builds in (locality, weight sharing, translation equivariance, hierarchy), what a plain ViT must learn from data, and summarize the evidence about data efficiency, including how DeiT and Swin recover some of the missing structure. - Describe DETR as set prediction with Hungarian matching and give the high-level design of the Segment Anything Model. - Derive the InfoNCE loss as a bound on mutual information, explain the role of temperature, and compare contrastive (SimCLR, MoCo), non-contrastive (BYOL, DINO) and masked-modeling (MAE) objectives, including how each avoids representational collapse. - Train and use CLIP-style models: write the symmetric contrastive loss, perform zero-shot classification with prompt templates, and perform image-text retrieval. - Sketch how a vision encoder is connected to a large language model (projector, Q-Former, cross-attention) and how captioning and visual question answering fit that picture. - Fine-tune a ViT, evaluate CLIP zero-shot, and linear-probe self-supervised features with Hugging Face, torchvision and open clip. Week 9 built the Transformer for text, and Week 10 showed what happens when you pretrain it on enormous amounts of unlabeled text with nothing but a prediction objective. Two questions follow directly. First: does the architecture need to be changed for images, or can we hand it image patches and let it learn? Second: the success of language models came from self-supervision , learning from the structure of the data itself, so can vision "},{"p":"week-11.html","w":"Week 11","t":"Vision Transformers, Multimodal Models, and Self-Supervised Learning","h":"Vision Transformers","a":"vit","x":"From convolutions to patches { from-conv} For a decade, computer vision meant convolutional networks (Week 5, Week 6). Convolutions are a very good fit for images: a small filter slides over the picture (locality), the same filter is used everywhere (weight sharing), and so a shifted input produces a shifted output (translation equivariance). Stacking them with pooling builds a hierarchy of increasingly large, increasingly abstract features. Several groups tried to put attention into vision before 2020, but as an addition to convolutions (non-local blocks, attention-augmented convolutions) or with restricted attention windows that were awkward to run at scale. The obstacle to using full self-attention on pixels is arithmetic. A image has pixels, and self-attention over that many positions needs a score matrix with entries per head per layer. Nobody can afford that. Dosovitskiy and colleagues at Google (the ViT paper, \"An Image is Worth 16x16 Words\", ICLR 2021) took the most direct possible route around the cost: do not treat pixels as tokens; treat non-overlapping patches as tokens . A patch is numbers (three colour channels). A image becomes patches, and the attention matrix is a modest . After that, the model is the Transformer encoder of Week 9 with essentially no vision-specific machinery. Why 2020, not 2017? Nothing about the idea required new mathematics, and the Transformer had existed since 2017. What changed was data and compute. The ViT paper's central finding is that this simple model, trained on ImageNet-1k alone (1.3 million images), is worse than a comparable ResNet, but trained on JFT-300M (a large private Google dataset of about 300 million labeled images) it matches or beats the best ResNets at a fraction of the pretraining compute. The architecture had"},{"p":"week-11.html","w":"Week 11","t":"Vision Transformers, Multimodal Models, and Self-Supervised Learning","h":"Data efficiency: DeiT and distillation","a":"deit","x":"Recall the ViT headline: trained on ImageNet-1k alone, a ViT underperforms a good CNN, and it needs hundreds of millions of images to win. For most people that makes ViT unusable. Touvron et al. (Facebook AI, ICML 2021, \"Training data-efficient image transformers & distillation through attention\") showed that most of the gap was a training recipe problem. With heavy data augmentation (RandAugment, Mixup, CutMix, random erasing), repeated augmentation, stochastic depth, label smoothing and a long AdamW schedule, a ViT-B trained on ImageNet-1k alone reaches about 82% top-1 on a single machine in a few days, which was competitive with strong CNNs of the same size. The lesson, that Transformers lean on regularization and augmentation to substitute for the missing inductive biases, is important and recurs throughout this week. The distillation token { distill-token} DeiT's second contribution was a way to import the inductive bias of a CNN without changing the architecture. A distillation token is added next to the class token. Both tokens are learned vectors that participate in attention like any other token, and each has its own linear head: - the class head is trained against the true label with cross-entropy; - the distillation head is trained to match a teacher (in the paper, a convolutional RegNet) by hard-label distillation , i.e. cross-entropy against the teacher's arg-max prediction . with the softmax. Interestingly, the hard label worked better than the usual soft-label KL distillation of Hinton, Vinyals and Dean (2015) in their experiments. At test time you average the two heads' softmax outputs. The two tokens end up with different representations (cosine similarity between them is only moderate in the early layers and increases toward the last), and the authors "},{"p":"week-11.html","w":"Week 11","t":"Vision Transformers, Multimodal Models, and Self-Supervised Learning","h":"Hierarchical transformers: Swin","a":"swin","x":"A plain ViT keeps a single resolution ( ) at every layer and uses global attention. Convolutional backbones are hierarchical: resolution falls (stride 4, 8, 16, 32) while channel width rises, giving the multi-scale feature maps that detection and segmentation heads (FPN, U-Net) expect. The Swin Transformer (Liu et al., ICCV 2021, \"Swin Transformer: Hierarchical Vision Transformer using Shifted Windows\") brings both properties to a Transformer. Patch merging and the hierarchy { patch-merging} Swin starts with small patches, so a image gives a token grid of width (96 in Swin-T). Each stage consists of Transformer blocks at a fixed resolution. Between stages, patch merging concatenates each neighbourhood of tokens (giving features) and applies a linear layer to : the grid goes and the width . Swin-T has stage depths and heads , about 29M parameters and 4.5 GFLOPs. Window attention and its cost { window-attention} Global attention over tokens would be far too expensive. Swin partitions the grid into non-overlapping windows of tokens ( ) and computes attention within each window only . With tokens and width : The second term is linear in the number of tokens for a fixed window size, which is the key to scaling to large images (see the cost chart above). Attention is also computed with a learned relative position bias , looked up from a table of parameters indexed by the relative offset between two tokens: Shifted windows { shifted-windows} Windowing alone has a flaw: tokens in different windows never communicate. Swin alternates two block types. In the first, windows are the regular grid. In the second, the grid is shifted by , so each new window straddles the borders of four old ones and information crosses window boundaries. Stacking the two gives every token a growing rec"},{"p":"week-11.html","w":"Week 11","t":"Vision Transformers, Multimodal Models, and Self-Supervised Learning","h":"CNN versus ViT: inductive biases and the data-efficiency evidence","a":"inductive-bias","x":"What a CNN assumes { cnn-assumptions} An inductive bias is an assumption built into a model that makes some functions easier to learn than others. A convolution layer assumes: 1. Locality : output at a position depends only on a small neighbourhood. 2. Weight sharing / translation equivariance : ; the same detector is used everywhere. 3. Hierarchy (with pooling): features compose from small to large. These assumptions are almost true for natural images, so they shrink the hypothesis space in the right direction and make small datasets workable. A ViT assumes much less. The patch embedding is local and shared, but after that every token interacts with every token from layer 1, and (with learned absolute positions) translation equivariance has to be learned. What the evidence says { evidence} - Dataset scale. The ViT paper's Figure on pretraining set size shows the crossover: with ImageNet-1k, BiT ResNets beat ViTs; with ImageNet-21k they are comparable; with JFT-300M ViTs win, and the larger the model the larger the gain. This is the cleanest demonstration that the missing biases can be bought back with data. - Recipes close much of the gap. DeiT (above) and Steiner et al. (\"How to train your ViT?\", 2021) showed that augmentation and regularization recipes are worth as much as more data in the ImageNet-1k regime. Beyer et al. (\"Better plain ViT baselines for ImageNet-1k\", 2022) reached about 76.5% top-1 for ViT-S/16 in 90 epochs and about 80% in 300 epochs with a simple recipe, undercutting the narrative that ViTs are inherently data-hungry. - Early convolutions help. Xiao et al. (\"Early Convolutions Help Transformers See Better\", NeurIPS 2021) replaced the large-stride patchify layer with a small stack of stride-2 convolutions and found ViTs optimize more stably and rea"},{"p":"week-11.html","w":"Week 11","t":"Vision Transformers, Multimodal Models, and Self-Supervised Learning","h":"Transformers for detection and segmentation","a":"detection","x":"DETR: detection as set prediction { detr} Object detectors before 2020 (Faster R-CNN, YOLO, RetinaNet) are full of hand-designed components: anchor boxes, region proposals, and non-maximum suppression (NMS) to remove duplicate predictions. Carion et al. (Facebook AI, ECCV 2020, \"End-to-End Object Detection with Transformers\") removed all of it by formulating detection as set prediction . DETR. A CNN backbone and a Transformer encoder produce a feature sequence; the decoder takes learned object queries and outputs (class, box) predictions in parallel. Hungarian matching assigns each ground-truth object to exactly one prediction, and the remaining predictions are trained to say \"no object\". The architecture: a CNN (ResNet-50) produces a feature map that is flattened to tokens with added 2-D position encodings; a Transformer encoder refines them; a Transformer decoder takes object queries (learned embeddings, in the paper, larger than the number of objects in any image) and cross-attends to the encoder output. Each decoder output is passed through small feed-forward heads that predict a class (including a special \"no object\" class) and a normalized box . All predictions are produced in parallel , not autoregressively. Hungarian matching and the set loss { hungarian} The network outputs an unordered set and the ground truth is a set padded with up to size . There is no natural alignment between them, and the loss must not depend on how predictions are ordered. DETR finds the best one-to-one assignment among permutations of elements: This is a linear assignment problem solved exactly in by the Hungarian algorithm (Kuhn, 1955), available as scipy.optimize.linear sum assignment . Given the matching, the training loss is the sum over matched pairs of a classification cross-entr"},{"p":"week-11.html","w":"Week 11","t":"Vision Transformers, Multimodal Models, and Self-Supervised Learning","h":"Self-supervised learning","a":"ssl","x":"Everything so far assumed labels. Labels are expensive, they encode only the categories someone thought of, and a single class index is a thin training signal for a network with hundreds of millions of parameters. Self-supervised learning (SSL) manufactures a supervisory signal from the data itself: hide or transform part of the input and ask the network to relate it to the rest. The learned representation is then evaluated by how well it serves downstream tasks, usually via a linear probe (train only a linear classifier on frozen features), -NN, or fine-tuning. A compressed timeline of self-supervised visual learning. The field moved from hand-designed pretext tasks to instance discrimination and contrastive learning, then to methods without negatives, and finally to masked modeling and web-scale language-supervised pretraining. Pretext tasks: where it started { pretext} Before 2018 the standard recipe was a pretext task : a puzzle whose answer is known for free, designed so that solving it requires understanding something about images. - Exemplar CNN (Dosovitskiy et al., 2014): treat each image, and its random augmentations, as its own class. This is the ancestor of instance discrimination. - Context prediction (Doersch, Gupta and Efros, ICCV 2015): given two patches from an image, predict their relative position (one of eight). - Jigsaw (Noroozi and Favaro, ECCV 2016): shuffle nine tiles and predict the permutation. - Colorization (Zhang, Isola and Efros, ECCV 2016): predict colour from grayscale. - Context Encoders (Pathak et al., CVPR 2016): inpaint a missing region. This is the direct ancestor of MAE. - Rotation prediction (Gidaris, Singh and Komodakis, ICLR 2018): rotate by 0, 90, 180 or 270 degrees and classify the rotation. Surprisingly strong for its simplicit"},{"p":"week-11.html","w":"Week 11","t":"Vision Transformers, Multimodal Models, and Self-Supervised Learning","h":"Language supervision: CLIP","a":"clip","x":"Everything so far removed labels but kept images alone. There is a second, enormous source of free supervision: the text that already sits next to images on the web (alt text, captions, surrounding sentences). The text is noisy, but it is open vocabulary : it can name anything, not just the 1,000 ImageNet classes. CLIP (Radford, Kim, Hallacy, Ramesh, Goh, Agarwal, Sastry, Askell, Mishkin, Clark, Krueger and Sutskever, ICML 2021, \"Learning Transferable Visual Models From Natural Language Supervision\") trained an image encoder and a text encoder from scratch on 400 million image-text pairs collected from the internet, with a contrastive objective that is InfoNCE, applied across modalities. CLIP. A batch of N image-text pairs yields N image embeddings and N text embeddings. All N x N cosine similarities, scaled by a learnable temperature, form a logit matrix whose diagonal holds the true pairs. A cross-entropy loss is applied along every row (which caption matches this image?) and every column (which image matches this caption?). The symmetric contrastive loss { clip-loss} Let and be unit-norm embeddings in a shared space of dimension (512 for the common ViT-B models), and let be a learnable scale equal to the inverse temperature, . The logits are , and the loss is the average of two cross-entropies, This is exactly the InfoNCE of the previous section, with the \"two views\" being a picture and its caption, and with the other captions (images) in the batch as negatives. Three practical facts matter. First, the temperature is learned (as the log of the scale, initialized at and clipped so the scale never exceeds 100), rather than tuned. Second, the batch is huge (32,768 in the paper), because more negatives make the task harder and the representation better; the batch is spli"},{"p":"week-11.html","w":"Week 11","t":"Vision Transformers, Multimodal Models, and Self-Supervised Learning","h":"Captioning, VQA and the road to multimodal LLMs","a":"multimodal","x":"CLIP matches images and text, but it does not generate text. The classical generative tasks are: - Image captioning. Produce a sentence describing an image. The first neural approaches (Vinyals et al., \"Show and Tell\", CVPR 2015; Xu et al., \"Show, Attend and Tell\", ICML 2015) encoded the image with a CNN and decoded text with an LSTM, the latter attending to a grid of CNN features at each word. Today a Transformer decoder with cross-attention (or prefix tokens) over ViT features does the same job, trained with ordinary next-token cross-entropy on caption text. - Visual question answering (VQA). Given an image and a question, produce an answer (Antol et al., \"VQA\", ICCV 2015). Early models treated it as classification over the most common answers after fusing image and question features. Modern systems generate the answer as text. BLIP (Li, Li, Xiong and Hoi, ICML 2022) trains one model with three objectives at once, image-text contrastive (CLIP-like), image-text matching (a binary classifier on fused features that sees hard negatives) and caption generation, and bootstraps cleaner captions by filtering and re-captioning noisy web data. Connecting a vision encoder to a language model { mllm} The recipe that emerged in 2022 to 2023 is conceptually simple: take a strong pretrained image encoder, a strong pretrained language model, and train a small connector between them . Every part of the pipeline is something you have already seen: the image becomes a sequence of patch embeddings, the connector maps them into the LLM's embedding space, and the LLM treats them as extra \"tokens\" in its context and is trained with next-token prediction on the text that follows. A multimodal LLM. A frozen or lightly tuned CLIP ViT turns a 336x336 image into 576 patch embeddings of width 102"},{"p":"week-11.html","w":"Week 11","t":"Vision Transformers, Multimodal Models, and Self-Supervised Learning","h":"Lab: fine-tune a ViT, CLIP zero-shot and retrieval, and a linear probe","a":"lab","x":"The lab has three parts, each runnable in a Colab notebook with a free GPU. You will need pip install transformers datasets open clip torch scikit-learn . Part A: fine-tune a pretrained ViT on CIFAR-10 { lab-finetune} CIFAR-10 images are , but ImageNet-pretrained ViTs expect (and the position embeddings were learned for 196 patches). The simplest correct approach is to resize the images. We fine-tune google/vit-base-patch16-224-in21k , using a small learning rate and the standard warmup and weight decay. Questions to explore afterwards: what happens with learning rate (see the pitfalls section)? What if you freeze everything but the head (a linear probe on supervised features) versus unfreezing the last 4 blocks versus all? How does accuracy change if you do not resize and instead interpolate the position embeddings for inputs with ? Part B: CLIP zero-shot classification and retrieval { lab-clip} We evaluate zero-shot accuracy on the CIFAR-10 test set with the class-embedding code from above, then use the same embeddings for text-to-image retrieval over 1,000 test images. Compare the accuracy with and without prompt ensembling, with the bare class name as the prompt, and with a deliberately bad prompt (\"a photo of a {} on the moon\"). Then query for something no label covers (\"a red vehicle\", \"an animal with four legs on grass\") and inspect the results. Part C: linear probe of self-supervised features { lab-probe} Extract frozen features from a self-supervised model and train a logistic regression on them. We use DINO ViT-S/16 ( facebook/dino-vits16 ), whose class-token features are known to be linearly separable. The probe protocol: freeze the encoder, extract features once, standardize them, and fit a regularized linear classifier. Extensions: swap the encoder for an M"},{"p":"week-11.html","w":"Week 11","t":"Vision Transformers, Multimodal Models, and Self-Supervised Learning","h":"Common mistakes and debugging tips","a":"mistakes","x":"Symptom Likely cause Fix --- --- --- Fine-tuned ViT diverges or accuracy collapses to chance in the first epoch Learning rate too high for fine-tuning (e.g. with Adam) or no warmup Use to with AdamW, a warmup of a few hundred steps, gradient clipping at 1.0 Pretrained ViT gives poor accuracy with the right code Wrong normalization constants or image size; mean/std of the checkpoint differ (0.5 vs ImageNet statistics) Use the checkpoint's own image processor / preprocess size mismatch for position embeddings when changing resolution Position embeddings are tied to the number of patches Resize via 2-D bicubic interpolation of the grid (not of the flattened sequence), keep the class token separate SimCLR loss stuck at Learning rate too low or augmentations too weak, or no projection head, or the diagonal was not masked Check the loss at initialization, mask the diagonal, use a LARS/AdamW setup with warmup, use strong crops and color jitter Contrastive loss drops to near 0 immediately Information leak: both views share low-level cues (same crop, same color histogram) or negatives contain the positive Use random resized crops with a small minimum scale and color jitter; check indexing of the positive BYOL/DINO outputs become constant, k-NN accuracy at chance Collapse: missing stop-gradient, EMA momentum too low, or no centering/sharpening in DINO Detach the target, use momentum 0.99 to 0.996 (increase to 1 over training), center and sharpen teacher outputs CLIP zero-shot much worse than reported Prompts that are bare class names, image preprocessing different from training, or class names that are ambiguous Use templates and ensembling, the model's own preprocess , disambiguating class names CLIP training: loss does not go below Batch contains duplicates (same caption twice)"},{"p":"week-11.html","w":"Week 11","t":"Vision Transformers, Multimodal Models, and Self-Supervised Learning","h":"Exercises","a":"exercises","x":"Exercise 1: shapes through ViT-L/14 A ViT-L/14 takes RGB inputs. Give the number of patches, the shape of the patch matrix before the linear embedding, the shape of the token sequence entering the first block, the per-head dimension, and the number of parameters in the patch embedding (weights and bias). Solution Patches: . Each patch has values, so the unrolled patch matrix is . With width the embedding matrix is and the sequence after adding the class token is (batch dimension excluded). With 16 heads, the per-head dimension is . Patch embedding parameters: . The position embedding has entries. Exercise 2: attention cost For a ViT-B/16 block ( ) compute the FLOPs of the attention score and weighted-sum matrix multiplications ( multiply-accumulates) versus the linear layers ( ) at , and pixels, and find the resolution at which the two are equal. Then state what the equivalent crossover would be for Swin with windows. Solution Equality: tokens, i.e. patches per side, or about pixels at . At ( ): attention G MACs against G for the linear part. At ( ): G against G. At ( ): G against G. In windowed attention each token attends to keys, so the attention term is , so the ratio to the linear layers is at every resolution: there is no crossover, the cost is linear in . Exercise 3: InfoNCE limits Prove that for a batch of candidates, the InfoNCE loss of any encoder is at least 0 and that the loss of an encoder whose scores are all equal is exactly . Then use the bound to explain why using more negatives can only tighten what you can certify , and why a small batch can never certify more than nats. Solution The loss is with , so it is . If all scores are equal, the softmax is uniform and , giving . Because , the bound : whatever the encoder does, the bound cannot certify more th"},{"p":"week-11.html","w":"Week 11","t":"Vision Transformers, Multimodal Models, and Self-Supervised Learning","h":"Key takeaways","a":"key-takeaways","x":"Key takeaways - A ViT is a Transformer applied to a sequence of linearly embedded image patches: with gives 196 tokens plus a class token, width for ViT-B, 12 heads of dimension 64, and a classifier on the class token (or mean-pooled tokens). - ViT lacks the CNN's locality and translation-equivariance priors, so it needs more data or stronger recipes: DeiT showed that augmentation, regularization and a distillation token let ViTs train on ImageNet-1k, and Swin reintroduces locality and hierarchy with shifted windows and linear cost. - Quadratic attention cost dominates at high resolution; windowed and hierarchical designs fix it. - DETR casts detection as set prediction with a Hungarian matching loss and no NMS; SAM is a promptable segmentation model built from an MAE-pretrained image encoder, a prompt encoder and a light mask decoder. - InfoNCE is a categorical cross-entropy that lower-bounds mutual information by ; temperature controls how much hard negatives dominate. - Collapse is the central problem of SSL. Negatives (SimCLR, MoCo), asymmetry plus stop-gradient and EMA (BYOL, DINO), variance and covariance terms (VICReg, Barlow Twins) and masking with reconstruction (MAE) each rule it out differently. - Contrastive and DINO features are linearly separable; MAE features are better for fine-tuning than for linear probing. - CLIP applies a symmetric InfoNCE to image-text pairs; zero-shot classification builds a linear classifier from text prompts, and prompt wording and ensembling matter. - Multimodal LLMs connect a pretrained vision encoder to an LLM through a projector or Q-Former and are tuned in two stages (alignment, then visual instruction tuning). Hallucination and fine-grained perception are their main weaknesses. - Always report the evaluation protocol for SS"},{"p":"week-11.html","w":"Week 11","t":"Vision Transformers, Multimodal Models, and Self-Supervised Learning","h":"Further reading","a":"further-reading","x":"Essential - Dosovitskiy et al., \"An Image is Worth 16x16 Words: Transformers for Image Recognition at Scale\", ICLR 2021 (arXiv:2010.11929). Essential. - Radford et al., \"Learning Transferable Visual Models From Natural Language Supervision\" (CLIP), ICML 2021 (arXiv:2103.00020). Essential. - He et al., \"Masked Autoencoders Are Scalable Vision Learners\", CVPR 2022 (arXiv:2111.06377). Essential. Vision Transformers and detection - Touvron et al., \"Training data-efficient image transformers and distillation through attention\" (DeiT), ICML 2021 (arXiv:2012.12877). - Liu et al., \"Swin Transformer: Hierarchical Vision Transformer using Shifted Windows\", ICCV 2021 (arXiv:2103.14030). - Carion et al., \"End-to-End Object Detection with Transformers\" (DETR), ECCV 2020 (arXiv:2005.12872). - Kirillov et al., \"Segment Anything\", ICCV 2023 (arXiv:2304.02643). - Liu et al., \"A ConvNet for the 2020s\", CVPR 2022 (arXiv:2201.03545). Self-supervised learning - Oord, Li and Vinyals, \"Representation Learning with Contrastive Predictive Coding\" (InfoNCE), 2018 (arXiv:1807.03748). - Chen, Kornblith, Norouzi and Hinton, \"A Simple Framework for Contrastive Learning of Visual Representations\" (SimCLR), ICML 2020 (arXiv:2002.05709). - He et al., \"Momentum Contrast for Unsupervised Visual Representation Learning\" (MoCo), CVPR 2020 (arXiv:1911.05722). - Grill et al., \"Bootstrap Your Own Latent\" (BYOL), NeurIPS 2020 (arXiv:2006.07733). - Caron et al., \"Emerging Properties in Self-Supervised Vision Transformers\" (DINO), ICCV 2021 (arXiv:2104.14294). - Chen and He, \"Exploring Simple Siamese Representation Learning\" (SimSiam), CVPR 2021 (arXiv:2011.10566). - Wang and Isola, \"Understanding Contrastive Representation Learning through Alignment and Uniformity on the Hypersphere\", ICML 2020 (arXiv:2005.1024"},{"p":"week-12.html","w":"Week 12","t":"Generative Models I: Autoencoders, VAEs, and GANs","h":"Generative Models I: Autoencoders, VAEs, and GANs","a":"","x":"Learning objectives By the end of this week you should be able to: - Place a generative model in the taxonomy of explicit versus implicit density models and say what each family can and cannot compute. - Explain what an autoencoder learns, how undercomplete, denoising and sparse variants regularize the code, and why a plain autoencoder is not a generative model. - Derive the evidence lower bound (ELBO) two ways, derive the closed-form KL divergence between a diagonal Gaussian and the standard normal, and explain why the reparameterization trick is needed. - Describe beta-VAE, posterior collapse and VQ-VAE, and diagnose collapse from training curves. - Derive the optimal discriminator and show that the GAN objective equals a Jensen-Shannon divergence at the optimum; explain the non-saturating loss, mode collapse, and why training is unstable. - State the Wasserstein distance, the WGAN objective, and the gradient penalty; sketch DCGAN, conditional GANs, CycleGAN and StyleGAN. - Compute and interpret Inception Score, Frechet Inception Distance and precision/recall, including their weaknesses. - Implement a VAE and a DCGAN on MNIST, and produce latent-space interpolations. Logistics this week Assignment 3 is due at the end of Week 12. Project feedback meetings also take place this week: bring your problem statement, your baseline results and the one question you most want answered. Generative models are a popular project topic, so expect questions about evaluation (how will you know your samples are good?), which is the last section of this page. model you have seen so far answers a discriminative question: given , what is ? A generative model answers a different one: what does the data itself look like? It learns a distribution close to the true data distribution so that w"},{"p":"week-12.html","w":"Week 12","t":"Generative Models I: Autoencoders, VAEs, and GANs","h":"A map of generative models","a":"taxonomy","x":"A taxonomy of deep generative models by how they represent the density. Explicit models can evaluate exactly or bound it; implicit models only provide a sampler. The first question to ask of any generative model is what can you compute with it? - Explicit, tractable density. You can evaluate exactly. Autoregressive models factor (PixelCNN, the GPT family of Week 10). Normalizing flows push a simple density through an invertible map and track the change of variables. Exact likelihood is wonderful for model comparison, but the architectural constraints (ordering, invertibility) are costly, and autoregressive sampling is sequential. - Explicit, approximate density. The model defines a density but you only evaluate a bound or an unnormalized value. VAEs maximize the ELBO. Energy-based models define with an intractable normalizer. - Implicit density. There is a sampler but no way to evaluate . GANs are the main example. Sampling is a single forward pass, which is fast, but there is no likelihood to monitor and no guarantee that every part of the data is covered. - Iterative and score-based models. Diffusion models (Week 13) learn to reverse a gradual noising process. They optimize a variational bound (so they are close cousins of VAEs) but generate by many small steps (so they behave like samplers). Three desiderata pull against each other: sample quality , mode coverage and likelihood evaluation , and sampling speed . For several years the \"generative trilemma\" was a fair summary: GANs gave fast, sharp samples but poor coverage and unstable training; VAEs gave good coverage and stable training but blurry samples; autoregressive models gave exact likelihoods but slow sampling. Diffusion improved all three at the cost of speed. Knowing which axis a method sacrifices lets you "},{"p":"week-12.html","w":"Week 12","t":"Generative Models I: Autoencoders, VAEs, and GANs","h":"Autoencoders","a":"autoencoders","x":"An autoencoder (AE) is a network trained to copy its input to its output through a narrow channel. It has an encoder that maps to a code and a decoder that returns a reconstruction . Training minimizes a reconstruction loss, usually squared error for real-valued data or binary cross-entropy for pixels in : An autoencoder squeezes the input through a bottleneck code with units and reconstructs it. The loss compares with ; no labels are needed. Copying is trivial unless the network is prevented from learning the identity function. Every variant of the autoencoder is a different way of preventing this, and the regularizer shapes what the code means. Undercomplete autoencoders and PCA { undercomplete} The simplest constraint is a bottleneck : choose so the code cannot hold everything. With a linear encoder and decoder and squared error, the optimum has a closed form. The decoder spans the top- principal subspace, so a linear autoencoder learns exactly the same subspace as principal component analysis (Baldi and Hornik, 1989), although not necessarily the same basis within it. The reconstruction error is the sum of the discarded eigenvalues of the data covariance. The chart shows this on the digits dataset from scikit-learn (1,797 images, ): the error falls quickly because digits are highly redundant. With nonlinear layers (convolutional or MLP encoders and decoders), the autoencoder can follow a curved manifold rather than a flat subspace, which is why a deep autoencoder with separates MNIST digits far better than PCA with . Hinton and Salakhutdinov made that point in 2006. Denoising autoencoders { denoising} A denoising autoencoder (Vincent, Larochelle, Bengio and Manzagol, ICML 2008) corrupts the input, (Gaussian noise, random pixel dropout, or masking), and trains the ne"},{"p":"week-12.html","w":"Week 12","t":"Generative Models I: Autoencoders, VAEs, and GANs","h":"Variational autoencoders","a":"vae","x":"The latent-variable model { latent-variable} A VAE (Kingma and Welling, ICLR 2014, \"Auto-Encoding Variational Bayes\") posits a simple generative story: 1. Draw a latent code with . 2. Draw the data , where the parameters of this conditional are produced by a neural network (the decoder ): a Bernoulli over pixels with logits , or a Gaussian with mean and fixed variance. The marginal likelihood is , an integral over all latent codes. For a neural-network decoder it has no closed form, and a naive Monte Carlo estimate with is hopeless in high dimension: almost every random explains terribly, so the estimator is dominated by rare lucky samples. The posterior , which would tell us which codes are plausible for a given , is intractable for the same reason. The variational trick is to introduce an approximate posterior (the encoder ), usually a diagonal Gaussian , and to optimize a lower bound on that involves . Deriving the ELBO { elbo} Derivation 1: from the log-evidence and an importance weight (Jensen) Start from the quantity we want, . Multiply and divide by inside the integral, so that it becomes an expectation under : The logarithm is concave, so Jensen's inequality ( ) gives Now split the joint, : Derivation 2: the gap is a KL divergence Jensen's route shows the inequality but not how loose it is. Start instead from the true posterior. For any , . Take the expectation under (the left side does not depend on ) and add and subtract : Since KL is non-negative, , with equality exactly when . For a fixed , maximizing the ELBO over is the same as minimizing the KL from the approximate to the true posterior. Over , it pushes up a lower bound on the log-likelihood. The gap is the quality of the encoder, and we can never evaluate it, because it involves the posterior we cannot "},{"p":"week-12.html","w":"Week 12","t":"Generative Models I: Autoencoders, VAEs, and GANs","h":"Generative adversarial networks","a":"gan","x":"The VAE needs a likelihood, and with a Gaussian or Bernoulli decoder that likelihood treats pixels as independent given , which is a major reason VAE samples are blurry: under squared error the best guess for an uncertain pixel is the average of the possibilities. Goodfellow, Pouget-Abadie, Mirza, Xu, Warde-Farley, Ozair, Courville and Bengio (NeurIPS 2014, \"Generative Adversarial Nets\") proposed to drop the likelihood and replace it by a learned measure of sample quality: a second network that tries to tell real from fake. The minimax game { minimax} Two networks play against each other. The generator maps noise to a sample , implicitly defining a distribution over . The discriminator outputs the probability that its input came from the data rather than from . The value function is The discriminator maximizes : it is a binary classifier with cross-entropy loss, labelling real data 1 and fakes 0. The generator minimizes : it wants to be large. In practice we alternate a few gradient steps on and one on , each with a minibatch. The GAN game. The generator turns noise into samples; the discriminator scores real and generated samples. The generator is trained through the discriminator: the gradient of 's output with respect to its input flows back through to the generator's parameters. The optimal discriminator { optimal-discriminator} Why should this game produce a good generator? Analyse it in the idealized setting where can represent any function and is trained to optimality for a fixed . Derivation: the optimal discriminator and the Jensen-Shannon connection For fixed , write the second expectation as an integral over using : The integrand can be maximized pointwise in . For , , the function on has derivative , which vanishes at and is concave, so Substitute back. Let "},{"p":"week-12.html","w":"Week 12","t":"Generative Models I: Autoencoders, VAEs, and GANs","h":"Wasserstein GAN","a":"wgan","x":"Consider two unit-variance Gaussians whose means are a distance apart (a stand-in for a generator that has not yet reached the data). The Jensen-Shannon divergence saturates as soon as the distributions stop overlapping, while the Wasserstein-1 distance keeps growing in proportion to : For distributions on low-dimensional manifolds that never overlap, the difference is dramatic: JS is exactly for any separation, while a Wasserstein distance (the cost of moving one pile of probability mass onto another, hence \"earth mover's distance\") decreases smoothly as the generated samples approach the data. The Wasserstein distance and the Kantorovich-Rubinstein dual { kantorovich} where is the set of joint distributions with marginals and . Computing this directly is intractable, but the Kantorovich-Rubinstein duality turns it into a maximization over 1-Lipschitz functions: Arjovsky, Chintala and Bottou (ICML 2017, \"Wasserstein GAN\") parameterize with a network called the critic (it outputs an unbounded real number and not a probability, so it is not a \"discriminator\"), constrain it to be (approximately) 1-Lipschitz, and train to minimize the critic's estimate of . The losses are very simple: With disjoint supports, a JS-based discriminator saturates (flat, zero gradient for the generator) while a 1-Lipschitz critic provides a slope that tells the generator which way to move. The critic's value is a meaningful quantity: because it estimates a distance, the critic loss correlates with sample quality and can be plotted as a training curve, a rare convenience in the GAN world. Practical consequences of the WGAN framework: train the critic more than the generator (5 critic steps per generator step in the paper), use no sigmoid, and avoid momentum-based optimizers for the critic in the"},{"p":"week-12.html","w":"Week 12","t":"Generative Models I: Autoencoders, VAEs, and GANs","h":"DCGAN: architecture rules that made GANs trainable","a":"dcgan","x":"The original GAN used fully connected networks on MNIST and small images. DCGAN (Radford, Metz and Chintala, ICLR 2016, \"Unsupervised Representation Learning with Deep Convolutional Generative Adversarial Networks\") listed a handful of architectural guidelines that made convolutional GANs train reliably, and they remain a template: - Replace pooling by strided convolutions in the discriminator and transposed convolutions (fractionally strided) in the generator, so the networks learn their own up- and down-sampling. - Use batch normalization in both networks (except the generator's output layer and the discriminator's input layer). - Remove fully connected hidden layers in deeper architectures. - Generator: ReLU activations, tanh at the output (images scaled to ). Discriminator: LeakyReLU (slope 0.2). - Optimizer: Adam with learning rate and (the default makes training oscillate). - Initialize weights from . The generator for images projects to a tensor and upsamples with four stride-2 transposed convolutions ( ) while halving channels ( ). The paper also showed that the latent space is meaningful: vector arithmetic on faces (the average code of \"smiling woman\" minus \"neutral woman\" plus \"neutral man\" decodes to a smiling man), and that interpolating yields smooth transitions rather than fading, evidence that the generator was not memorizing. For MNIST we use a variant in the lab below."},{"p":"week-12.html","w":"Week 12","t":"Generative Models I: Autoencoders, VAEs, and GANs","h":"Conditional GANs and image-to-image translation","a":"conditional","x":"Conditional GANs { cgan} Unconditional GANs give you a random sample. A conditional GAN (Mirza and Osindero, 2014) feeds a label or other conditioning signal to both networks, modelling : For class labels the usual implementation concatenates a one-hot (or learned embedding) of to for and to the input (or a feature map) for . Better-performing variants use projection discriminators (Miyato and Koyama, ICLR 2018), conditional batch normalization and class-conditional spectral normalization, as in BigGAN (Brock, Donahue and Simonyan, ICLR 2019), which scaled class-conditional GANs to ImageNet at and introduced the truncation trick : sample from a truncated normal and trade diversity for fidelity at test time. pix2pix (Isola, Zhu, Zhou and Efros, CVPR 2017) conditions on an image (edges to photo, day to night, labels to street scene): the generator is a U-Net, the discriminator judges local patches (\"PatchGAN\"), and the loss adds an term between the output and the paired target, so the adversarial loss only has to supply sharp high-frequency detail. CycleGAN: unpaired translation { cyclegan} Paired data is rare (nobody has a photograph of the same scene as a horse and as a zebra). CycleGAN (Zhu, Park, Isola and Efros, ICCV 2017, \"Unpaired Image-to-Image Translation using Cycle-Consistent Adversarial Networks\") learns two mappings and between two unpaired image collections, with two discriminators and . An adversarial loss on each mapping makes outputs look like the target domain, but that alone is under-constrained (any mapping that produces plausible zebras would do). The key idea is cycle consistency : translating and then translating back should reproduce the input. CycleGAN. Two generators translate between domains and two discriminators judge realism in each. The cycl"},{"p":"week-12.html","w":"Week 12","t":"Generative Models I: Autoencoders, VAEs, and GANs","h":"StyleGAN","a":"stylegan","x":"ProGAN (Karras, Aila, Laine and Lehtinen, ICLR 2018, \"Progressive Growing of GANs\") trained a GAN by starting at and adding layers that raise the resolution during training, which stabilized the generation of faces. StyleGAN (Karras, Laine and Aila, CVPR 2019, \"A Style-Based Generator Architecture for Generative Adversarial Networks\") rethought the generator : 1. A mapping network (an 8-layer MLP) transforms the noise into an intermediate latent . must follow a fixed distribution (a Gaussian), but the data distribution of factors such as \"age\" or \"hair length\" is not Gaussian; is free to be warped to make factors more linear and less entangled. 2. The synthesis network starts from a learned constant tensor (not from ). At each resolution, is turned by a learned affine layer into a per-channel scale and bias that modulate the feature maps, originally by adaptive instance normalization (AdaIN) and in StyleGAN2 by a weight demodulation that removes the \"droplet\" artefacts AdaIN created (Karras et al., CVPR 2020). 3. Noise inputs (per-pixel Gaussian noise, scaled by learned per-channel weights) are added after each convolution to generate stochastic detail such as hair strands and pores that need not be encoded in . The StyleGAN generator. A mapping MLP turns into , which controls every block through learned affine styles; noise adds stochastic detail. Using for coarse blocks and for fine blocks mixes attributes of two faces. Because each block receives its own style, style mixing works: use for the coarse blocks (pose, face shape, hair style) and for the fine blocks (colour, micro-texture), and you obtain a face that combines the two. A truncation trick in (pulling toward its mean) trades diversity for quality. Later versions fixed artefacts (StyleGAN2), removed aliasing ("},{"p":"week-12.html","w":"Week 12","t":"Generative Models I: Autoencoders, VAEs, and GANs","h":"Evaluating generative models","a":"evaluation","x":"Likelihood-based models report bits per dimension or negative log-likelihood on held-out data. For implicit models there is no likelihood, and for GANs even a VAE-style bound does not help. Evaluation therefore uses sample-based metrics, and no single number captures the three things you want: fidelity (samples look real), diversity (they cover the data) and novelty (they are not copies of the training set). Human evaluation remains the gold standard and is expensive. Inception Score { inception-score} The Inception Score (Salimans, Goodfellow, Zaremba, Cheung, Radford and Chen, NeurIPS 2016, \"Improved Techniques for Training GANs\") feeds generated images to an ImageNet-trained Inception network and looks at the class posterior . A good generator should produce images that are recognizable (each has low entropy) and diverse (the marginal has high entropy). Both are captured by It ranges from 1 (all outputs the same, or unrecognizable) to the number of classes (1000 for ImageNet; confident and perfectly spread). Weaknesses: it never looks at real data, so a generator can obtain a high score while missing the real distribution; it depends on the ImageNet classes (meaningless for faces or medical images); it is blind to intra-class diversity (a generator that produces one perfect image per class gets the maximum); and it is sensitive to the network implementation and image resizing. Frechet Inception Distance { fid} The FID (Heusel, Ramsauer, Unterthiner, Nessler and Hochreiter, NeurIPS 2017, in the paper that also proposed the two time-scale update rule for GANs) compares real and generated images in the feature space of the Inception network (the 2048-dimensional pooling layer). Fit a Gaussian to the real features and to the generated features and compute the Frechet (2-"},{"p":"week-12.html","w":"Week 12","t":"Generative Models I: Autoencoders, VAEs, and GANs","h":"Lab: a VAE and a DCGAN on MNIST","a":"lab","x":"In this lab you will train both models on MNIST and compare them. Everything runs in a few minutes on a free Colab GPU (the VAE is fast even on a CPU). The goals: (1) a working VAE, with reconstruction, sampling and latent interpolation ; (2) a working DCGAN; (3) a side-by-side comparison of samples and interpolations. Part 1: data and the VAE { lab-vae} As a rough guide, a convolutional VAE like this one typically ends up with a negative ELBO of a little over 100 nats per image on MNIST (a rule of thumb, not a benchmark; it varies with the seed and the latent size). Always check two things: the reconstruction and the KL separately, and the number of active latent dimensions. Part 2: sampling, reconstruction and latent interpolation { lab-interp} Look at the interpolation: a good VAE morphs the 3 into the 8 smoothly through plausible digits, since the decoder has been trained on neighbourhoods and the latent space is filled. Compare it with linear interpolation of the pixels (a cross-fade of two superimposed digits) and with a plain autoencoder's code space, where intermediate points often decode to garbage. Then train again with and compare samples, the KL, and the number of active units. Part 3: the DCGAN { lab-dcgan} Typical behaviour: the discriminator loss hovers around 1.0 to 1.4 (about when it cannot tell real from fake), the generator loss around 0.7 to 2, and digits appear after 2 to 3 epochs. Neither loss decreases monotonically: that is normal , so judge by the saved image grids from the fixed noise, not by the curves. Part 4: latent interpolation in the GAN, and comparison { lab-compare} To compare the two models, produce 64 samples each and look at them side by side: the VAE's digits are smooth and slightly blurry but cover every class, while the DCGAN's ar"},{"p":"week-12.html","w":"Week 12","t":"Generative Models I: Autoencoders, VAEs, and GANs","h":"Common mistakes and debugging tips","a":"mistakes","x":"Symptom Likely cause Fix --- --- --- VAE samples are blurry, KL near 0 for every latent Posterior collapse (KL weight too high relative to the reconstruction scale, e.g. averaging the pixel loss but summing the KL) Sum the reconstruction over pixels, KL warm-up, free bits, lower ; log KL per dimension VAE reconstructions are sharp but samples from the prior are garbage KL too weak ( ) so the aggregate posterior does not match the prior Increase , or fit a second-stage prior (a GMM or a small flow) on the codes nan loss in a VAE exp(logvar) overflow, or a log of 0 in a probability decoder Clamp logvar to , use binary cross entropy with logits , lower the learning rate Reconstruction loss does not decrease with a Gaussian decoder Pixel scaling mismatched with the decoder variance Scale data to or and set the variance (equivalently ) deliberately GAN: discriminator loss goes to 0, generator loss rises The discriminator is overpowering the generator (saturation) Use the non-saturating loss, smaller discriminator or learning rate, add noise/augmentation to the discriminator's inputs, spectral norm, label smoothing (0.9 for real) GAN: all samples look the same (mode collapse) The generator exploits the discriminator's per-sample judgement Minibatch standard deviation, WGAN-GP, unrolling, larger and more diverse batches, lower learning rate GAN: samples are noise after 50 epochs, losses are flat near 0.69 The discriminator is too weak, or a bug (not detaching, wrong labels, tanh missing) Verify can classify real vs fake noise on its own; check data range matches the generator's output range RuntimeError: Trying to backward through the graph a second time The fake batch is reused in both steps without detach() Use fake.detach() in the D step; compute the G loss from D(fake) aft"},{"p":"week-12.html","w":"Week 12","t":"Generative Models I: Autoencoders, VAEs, and GANs","h":"Exercises","a":"exercises","x":"Exercise 1: KL by hand Compute for , , and . Which of or is penalized more, and in which direction is the variance penalty asymmetric? Solution Use . : . : . : . : . Shrinking the variance to costs less than doubling the standard deviation costs, because the term grows slowly while grows quickly: too-large variance is penalized more than too-small by the same factor . A mean of 1 costs nats. Exercise 2: ELBO as a bound (derivation) Show that and deduce that if the encoder family can represent the true posterior exactly, then maximizing the ELBO over makes the bound tight. Why can the gap not be evaluated in a real VAE? Solution Write , take and the expectation under (the left side does not depend on ): . For fixed the left side is constant, so maximizing over minimizes the KL; if can equal the true posterior the KL is 0 and . The gap needs , which contains the intractable marginal we are trying to avoid computing. Exercise 3: reparameterization (conceptual and short coding) (a) Why can't we backpropagate through written as a sampling operation? (b) Show that and for . (c) Check numerically, with PyTorch or NumPy, that the reparameterized gradient of with respect to matches the analytic value . Solution (a) A sample is a draw from a distribution that depends on the parameters, but the draw itself is a random number: the map from the parameters to the value has no defined derivative, so the chain rule has nothing to differentiate. (b) Directly from with independent of . (c) , so and . Estimate with samples: Both estimates agree with the analytic values to about three decimals. Exercise 4: where does the KL weight hide? A VAE for 28x28 images uses a Gaussian decoder and the loss (plain squared error, KL weight 1). Show that this equals the negative ELBO (up to a constant) "},{"p":"week-12.html","w":"Week 12","t":"Generative Models I: Autoencoders, VAEs, and GANs","h":"Key takeaways","a":"key-takeaways","x":"Key takeaways - Generative models differ in what they let you compute: autoregressive models and flows give exact likelihoods, VAEs give a bound, GANs give only samples, and the choice trades off sample quality, coverage and speed. - Autoencoders learn a code by reconstruction through a bottleneck (or noise, or sparsity); a linear autoencoder spans the PCA subspace. A plain autoencoder has no prior over codes, so it is not a generative model. - The ELBO is , a lower bound whose gap is . The KL to a standard normal is . - The reparameterization trick, , moves the randomness out of the computation graph and makes the expectation differentiable with a low-variance gradient. - weights rate against distortion. Large or a strong decoder leads to posterior collapse; remedies are KL warm-up, free bits and weaker decoders. VQ-VAE uses discrete codes, a straight-through estimator and a separate prior, and avoids the KL altogether. - For a fixed generator the optimal discriminator is , and the minimax value is . The non-saturating generator loss avoids vanishing gradients early on. - GAN training is a game and may cycle; mode collapse, vanishing gradients and sensitivity to hyperparameters are typical. WGAN replaces JS with the Wasserstein distance, enforced via 1-Lipschitz critics (gradient penalty or spectral normalization). - DCGAN's recipe (strided convolutions, batch norm, LeakyReLU, Adam with ) is the template for stable convolutional GANs; conditional GANs, pix2pix and CycleGAN add control, and StyleGAN adds a mapping network and per-layer styles. - Evaluate with FID (fidelity and diversity, protocol-dependent), Inception Score (classifier-dependent, blind to real data), and precision and recall (which separate fidelity from coverage), and always look at samples. - Assignme"},{"p":"week-12.html","w":"Week 12","t":"Generative Models I: Autoencoders, VAEs, and GANs","h":"Further reading","a":"further-reading","x":"Essential - Kingma and Welling, \"Auto-Encoding Variational Bayes\", ICLR 2014 (arXiv:1312.6114). Essential. - Goodfellow et al., \"Generative Adversarial Nets\", NeurIPS 2014 (arXiv:1406.2661). Essential. - Arjovsky, Chintala and Bottou, \"Wasserstein GAN\", ICML 2017 (arXiv:1701.07875). Essential. Autoencoders and VAEs - Doersch, \"Tutorial on Variational Autoencoders\", 2016 (arXiv:1606.05908). - Kingma and Welling, \"An Introduction to Variational Autoencoders\", Foundations and Trends in Machine Learning, 2019 (arXiv:1906.02691). - Rezende, Mohamed and Wierstra, \"Stochastic Backpropagation and Approximate Inference in Deep Generative Models\", ICML 2014 (arXiv:1401.4082). - Vincent, Larochelle, Bengio and Manzagol, \"Extracting and Composing Robust Features with Denoising Autoencoders\", ICML 2008. - Higgins et al., \"beta-VAE: Learning Basic Visual Concepts with a Constrained Variational Framework\", ICLR 2017. - Bowman et al., \"Generating Sentences from a Continuous Space\", CoNLL 2016 (arXiv:1511.06349). - Alemi et al., \"Fixing a Broken ELBO\", ICML 2018 (arXiv:1711.00464). - van den Oord, Vinyals and Kavukcuoglu, \"Neural Discrete Representation Learning\" (VQ-VAE), NeurIPS 2017 (arXiv:1711.00937). - Esser, Rombach and Ommer, \"Taming Transformers for High-Resolution Image Synthesis\" (VQGAN), CVPR 2021 (arXiv:2012.09841). GANs - Goodfellow, \"NIPS 2016 Tutorial: Generative Adversarial Networks\", 2016 (arXiv:1701.00160). - Radford, Metz and Chintala, \"Unsupervised Representation Learning with Deep Convolutional Generative Adversarial Networks\" (DCGAN), ICLR 2016 (arXiv:1511.06434). - Arjovsky and Bottou, \"Towards Principled Methods for Training Generative Adversarial Networks\", ICLR 2017 (arXiv:1701.04862). - Gulrajani et al., \"Improved Training of Wasserstein GANs\", NeurIPS 2017 (a"},{"p":"week-13.html","w":"Week 13","t":"Generative Models II: Diffusion, Flows, and Autoregressive Models","h":"Generative Models II: Diffusion, Flows, and Autoregressive Models","a":"","x":"Learning objectives By the end of this week you should be able to: - Write the DDPM forward process, derive the closed form , and explain why it makes training cheap. - Derive the reverse-process posterior , the variational bound, and the simplified -prediction loss, and say what the simplification throws away. - Compare linear and cosine noise schedules through and the signal-to-noise ratio, and convert between -, -, and -parameterizations. - Define the score function, derive denoising score matching, and show that -prediction is score estimation up to a scale. - State the forward SDE, the reverse-time SDE and the probability-flow ODE, and explain how DDPM and DDIM are discretizations of them. - Derive classifier guidance and classifier-free guidance and explain the quality/diversity trade-off controlled by the guidance scale. - Explain latent diffusion, cross-attention conditioning, and the architectural differences between U-Net and DiT denoisers. - Describe normalizing flows (change of variables, coupling layers) and autoregressive models (PixelCNN, WaveNet, Transformers over tokens), and place all five generative families in one comparison. - Discuss deepfakes, provenance (C2PA), watermarking and other risks of generative media with technical precision. - Train a class-conditional DDPM on MNIST and run sampling-step and guidance experiments. Week 12 ended with a tension. VAEs train stably and cover the data, but their samples are blurry; GANs make sharp samples but are hard to train and can drop modes. Between roughly 2020 and 2022 a third family closed most of that gap and then took over image, audio and video generation: diffusion models . The idea is almost embarrassingly simple. Take a data point and add a little Gaussian noise, again and again, until nothing b"},{"p":"week-13.html","w":"Week 13","t":"Generative Models II: Diffusion, Flows, and Autoregressive Models","h":"The diffusion idea in one picture","a":"idea","x":"Diffusion as a Markov chain. The forward process gradually adds noise and has no parameters; the reverse process is learned and removes noise one step at a time. The dashed arc is the closed-form shortcut . Two Markov chains, one fixed and one learned: - The forward (noising) process for turns a data point into a nearly-pure-Gaussian . It is designed by hand and has no learnable parameters . - The reverse (denoising) process goes from back to . A neural network parameterizes it. If the forward steps are small, the true reverse step is also (close to) a Gaussian, which is why a Gaussian with a learned mean is a reasonable model of it, not just a convenient one. The model was introduced by Sohl-Dickstein, Weiss, Maheswaranathan and Ganguli in 2015 (inspired by non-equilibrium thermodynamics), then sat nearly unnoticed until Ho, Jain and Abbeel (2020) showed with a simple loss, a U-Net and a careful schedule that it could rival GANs on CIFAR-10 and 256x256 LSUN images. In parallel, Song and Ermon (2019) developed noise-conditional score networks from the score-matching tradition. Song et al. (2021) then showed the two lines are one theory. Two roads to the same place The thermodynamic road: Sohl-Dickstein et al. (2015) proposed learning to reverse a diffusion process. The score-matching road: Hyvarinen (2005) introduced score matching, Vincent (2011) connected it to denoising autoencoders, and Song and Ermon (2019) trained a network on many noise levels and sampled with Langevin dynamics. Ho et al. (2020) unified the practicalities (a simple -prediction loss, a U-Net backbone), and Song et al. (2021) framed both with stochastic differential equations. Modern systems (Stable Diffusion, Imagen, DALL-E 2 and 3, video models) are descendants of this merged line. Keep both voca"},{"p":"week-13.html","w":"Week 13","t":"Generative Models II: Diffusion, Flows, and Autoregressive Models","h":"The forward process and its closed form","a":"forward","x":"One step of noising { forward-step} Fix a variance schedule (small numbers, typically from to in the original paper). The forward step is Intuition: shrink the signal slightly (by ) and add a little fresh noise (variance ). The shrinking matters. If you only added noise the variance would grow without bound; with the shrink factor, the process is variance preserving : if has unit variance per coordinate, so does , because . Eventually the stationary distribution is , whatever the data looked like. Write and . The closed-form noising distribution { closed-form} Derivation: jumping straight to step Substitute the one-step recursion into itself: The sum of independent zero-mean Gaussians is a zero-mean Gaussian whose variance is the sum of the variances: . So the noise term equals for a fresh . Repeating the argument down to (a one-line induction) gives things to appreciate: 1. Training is cheap. To get a training example at a random step you do not simulate steps; you draw and apply one formula. This is what makes diffusion training a plain supervised regression. 2. is the whole story. The \"signal\" weight is and the \"noise\" weight is . Their squares sum to one (variance preserving). At you see almost clean data; at almost pure noise. Many papers drop entirely and define a model directly through . 3. Signal-to-noise ratio. is the ratio of signal power to noise power. It decreases monotonically in . Its logarithm will turn out to be the natural \"time\" variable (later). The NumPy code below builds the original linear schedule, checks the closed form against explicitly simulating steps, and prints how fast the signal dies. Forward diffusion explorer: noise a 2-D point cloud, compare schedules 520"},{"p":"week-13.html","w":"Week 13","t":"Generative Models II: Diffusion, Flows, and Autoregressive Models","h":"The reverse process and its posterior","a":"reverse","x":"Sampling needs the reverse conditionals . Applying Bayes' rule to them requires the marginal over all possible data , which is exactly the intractable thing we want to learn. Two facts rescue us. Fact 1 (small steps). When is small, the true reverse step is approximately Gaussian (Feller, 1949, for diffusion processes; this is what makes the whole construction legitimate). So we model . Fact 2 (conditioning on makes it exact). If we also condition on the clean data point, the reverse step is tractable and exactly Gaussian. We will use it as the training target. Derivation: the posterior By Bayes and the Markov property, Both factors on the right are Gaussians in , so the product is Gaussian. Collect the exponent (all terms in , per coordinate): Matching to gives the variance and mean (The simplification of uses .) Now substitute from the closed form; after algebra the coefficient collapses and where is the noise that was used to produce from . the boxed formula as a recipe: to step backwards, take , subtract the (scaled) noise that was added, and rescale. The only thing the network needs to supply is that noise . This is the origin of -prediction . Let us check the posterior coefficients numerically before building on them."},{"p":"week-13.html","w":"Week 13","t":"Generative Models II: Diffusion, Flows, and Autoregressive Models","h":"The variational bound and the simplified loss","a":"elbo","x":"Diffusion is a latent-variable model whose latents have the same dimension as the data. The inference (\"encoder\") distribution is the fixed forward process . Exactly as for the VAE of Week 12, the negative log-likelihood is bounded by the ELBO. Derivation: the diffusion ELBO Let with . Jensen's inequality gives Insert the factorizations, and rewrite each forward factor with Bayes' rule using for . The marginal ratios telescope, leaving - has no parameters; it is tiny when . - is a reconstruction (decoder) term for the last step. - Each is a KL between two Gaussians : the exact posterior and the model . we fix the model variance to (Ho et al. use or ; the two behave nearly the same), the KL between equal-covariance Gaussians is a squared distance between means (compare the closed-form KL in Week 12): Now choose the parameterization of to mirror , with the network predicting the noise: The terms cancel inside , leaving only the difference in the noise predictions. This is a weighted sum of denoising errors, with a -dependent weight . Ho et al. found that dropping the weights and sampling uniformly works better in practice for sample quality: What the simplification costs The true ELBO weights are large at small (low noise) and small at large . down-weights the easy, low-noise steps relative to the ELBO, which frees capacity for the harder high-noise denoising tasks that determine global structure. The price is that is not a bound on the likelihood: models trained with it have noticeably worse log-likelihoods (in bits per dimension) than models trained with the true bound, even though the samples look better. Nichol and Dhariwal (2021) recover good likelihoods by also learning the variances and adding a small weight on the true bound (\"hybrid loss\"); Kingma et al. (2021, V"},{"p":"week-13.html","w":"Week 13","t":"Generative Models II: Diffusion, Flows, and Autoregressive Models","h":"Noise schedules","a":"schedules","x":"The schedule decides how the finite budget of steps is spent across noise levels. Two schedules dominate the literature. Linear (Ho et al., 2020): increases linearly from to with . This was tuned for images. Cosine (Nichol and Dhariwal, 2021): define directly, and clip each implied to at most to avoid singularities near . The offset keeps from being too tiny at the start. A cleaner way to compare schedules is the log-SNR . Any schedule is a monotone curve ; what the model sees at a given noise level does not depend on itself, only on . Equal spacing in translates into a particular density of training samples along the axis . Practical consequences: - Resolution matters. At higher resolution neighboring pixels are redundant, so the same noise variance destroys less information; high-resolution models need a schedule shifted toward more noise (a lower SNR at every ). Hoogeboom et al. (2023, \"simple diffusion\") and Stable Diffusion 3 both rescale the schedule with image size. Treat a schedule tuned for 32x32 as a starting point, not a law. - Zero terminal SNR. Both schedules leave a small leftover signal at ( ; for the cosine schedule it is clipped to avoid exactly zero). At sampling time the model is then started from pure noise that does not match what it saw at , which can cause a bias toward medium brightness in generated images. Lin et al. (2024) analyze this and recommend enforcing together with -prediction. - Schedules are part of the model. Changing the schedule at sampling time without retraining is possible only through careful re-timing (see Karras et al. below), because the network was trained for a specific mapping from to noise level. - Karras et al. (2022, \"EDM\") argue for abandoning discrete steps and designing the noise level , the loss weighting, the netw"},{"p":"week-13.html","w":"Week 13","t":"Generative Models II: Diffusion, Flows, and Autoregressive Models","h":"Score matching and the denoising view","a":"score","x":"The score function { score-def} The score of a density is the gradient of its log-density with respect to the data , not the parameters: It is a vector field that points uphill on the log-density landscape; for a Gaussian it is , an arrow toward the mean whose length grows with distance. Two properties make scores attractive for generative modeling. First, the score does not depend on the normalizing constant: , so you can model an unnormalized density without computing . Second, given a score you can sample by following it with noise ( Langevin dynamics ): which under mild conditions converges to samples from as and . The score field of a Gaussian mixture at two noise levels. At small noise the arrows point sharply toward the nearest mode and vanish between modes (where the estimate is hard); at large noise the blobs merge and the field is smooth and global. Why one noise level is not enough { multi-noise} Estimating directly has two problems on real data. The data lie near a low-dimensional manifold, so the density is essentially zero (and its score undefined) off the manifold; and in the low-density regions between modes there are almost no training samples, so the score estimate there is poor, and that is exactly where Langevin chains start. Song and Ermon (2019) fixed both by perturbing the data with Gaussian noise at many scales and learning one network conditioned on the noise level. Heavy noise fills space with probability mass and gives the network a signal everywhere; light noise restores the fine structure. Sampling then goes from the largest to the smallest noise level ( annealed Langevin dynamics ). This is already the structure of diffusion. Denoising score matching { dsm} We cannot regress on directly because we do not know it. Vincent (2011) showed that "},{"p":"week-13.html","w":"Week 13","t":"Generative Models II: Diffusion, Flows, and Autoregressive Models","h":"The SDE view and the probability-flow ODE","a":"sde","x":"The discrete chain with is a time discretization of a continuous-time process. Song et al. (2021) took the limit . Forward SDE { forward-sde} A stochastic differential equation describes a process with a drift and a noise term driven by Brownian motion : The DDPM chain is the Euler-Maruyama discretization of the variance-preserving (VP) SDE where is the continuous-time analogue of (so ). Its marginals are exactly the Gaussian form we derived, with . The older score-network line (NCSN) corresponds to the variance-exploding (VE) SDE , with no shrinking drift and noise that grows without bound. Reverse-time SDE { reverse-sde} A theorem of Anderson (1982) says that the time-reversal of this process is again a diffusion, with the same noise scale but a drift corrected by the score: where time runs backward from to and is a Brownian motion in reverse time. This is the entire mathematical content of diffusion sampling : knowing the score of every marginal gives you a generative process. A network trained by denoising score matching supplies , and any SDE solver produces samples. The DDPM ancestral sampler is one particular discretization of the reverse VP-SDE. The probability-flow ODE { pf-ode} Remarkably, the same family of marginals is produced by a deterministic process: The reason, in one line, is that the Fokker-Planck equation (which governs how the density evolves) only depends on the combination of drift and diffusion, and the term can be moved between the \"drift\" and \"diffusion\" columns without changing . The ODE has no randomness, so: - Each noise sample maps to exactly one image (a smooth, invertible map): you can encode an image to noise by integrating forward. This gives meaningful latent interpolation and image editing (SDEdit, inversion). - You can use fast, hig"},{"p":"week-13.html","w":"Week 13","t":"Generative Models II: Diffusion, Flows, and Autoregressive Models","h":"DDIM and fast sampling","a":"ddim","x":"Sampling with 1000 sequential network calls is the practical bottleneck: an image takes seconds to minutes. Song, Meng and Ermon (2021, DDIM , \"Denoising Diffusion Implicit Models\") observed that the DDPM training objective depends only on the marginals , not on the joint Markov structure. Therefore many different (non-Markovian) forward processes share the same marginals and the same trained network, and some of them admit deterministic, much shorter reverse chains. The DDIM update Given the network output at a step , form the clean estimate . For any earlier step (not necessarily ) the DDIM update is with . Read it as: go to the predicted clean image, then re-noise it to level using a mix of the predicted noise direction and fresh noise. - recovers DDPM ancestral sampling (when ). - gives : a deterministic map, an Euler discretization of the probability-flow ODE written in the variable versus . - Any decreasing subsequence can be used: or network evaluations instead of 1000. code below implements both samplers for a one-dimensional two-Gaussian mixture where the optimal noise predictor is known in closed form, so any error you see comes from the sampler , not from a learned network. It produces the numbers in the next chart. The chart shows the main trend of the field: a 20-50 step deterministic sampler already reaches an error within a factor of a few of the 1000-step floor. In real image models the picture is similar, and later solvers (DPM-Solver, Lu et al. 2022; the EDM Heun sampler, Karras et al. 2022; UniPC) push good samples down to 10-20 evaluations by using higher-order ODE solvers, because the probability-flow trajectory is smooth. Beyond solvers: distillation { distillation} Solver improvements reduce the number of steps to roughly 10-20. To go lower, you c"},{"p":"week-13.html","w":"Week 13","t":"Generative Models II: Diffusion, Flows, and Autoregressive Models","h":"Diffusion as a hierarchical VAE","a":"vae-link","x":"Left: a standard VAE with one bottlenecked latent and a learned encoder. Right: diffusion as a hierarchical VAE whose latents are noisy copies of the data and whose encoder is fixed. The ELBO we derived above is formally the ELBO of a Markovian hierarchical VAE : the latents are , the encoder is the forward process, the decoder chain is . Comparing with Week 12: Standard VAE Diffusion model --- --- --- Latent dimension much smaller than the data the same as the data, copies Encoder learned fixed, Gaussian noising, no parameters Decoder one network call network calls, each a small denoising step Posterior collapse a real risk impossible: the latents are fixed noisy copies of the input KL term per-sample KL to the prior, trades off with reconstruction sum of KLs between Gaussians, each a denoising regression Blurriness mean-prediction of one big step is blurry each step is tiny, so predicting the mean is accurate This explains why diffusion succeeds where a one-step Gaussian decoder fails. The blurriness of a VAE comes from asking a unimodal Gaussian decoder to capture a multimodal . In diffusion each step really is nearly Gaussian (a small perturbation of ), so the Gaussian assumption is nearly true, and multimodality is resolved gradually across steps. It also explains why the objective is stable: with the encoder frozen, there is no adversary and no joint optimization of encoder and decoder. The cost is sampling speed: instead of one decoder pass you need many. A recurring theme is to combine the two: a VAE compresses images into a short, perceptually meaningful code, and diffusion models that code, which is exactly latent diffusion (below)."},{"p":"week-13.html","w":"Week 13","t":"Generative Models II: Diffusion, Flows, and Autoregressive Models","h":"Conditioning and guidance","a":"guidance","x":"An unconditional sampler is a curiosity; useful systems let you say what to generate: a class label, a text prompt, a sketch, a low-resolution image. Mathematically we want . The score decomposes by Bayes' rule: The first term is the unconditional score we already have; the second pushes the sample toward regions where the condition is likely. Classifier guidance { classifier-guidance} Dhariwal and Nichol (2021) trained a separate noisy-image classifier (a classifier trained on images corrupted at every noise level) and added its gradient to the sampler, scaled by a guidance scale : With this samples from the true conditional. With it samples from the sharpened distribution , which concentrates on points the classifier is very sure about. Their \"ADM-G\" model, with guidance, outperformed the best GANs of the time on ImageNet (FID), the paper's title (\"Diffusion Models Beat GANs on Image Synthesis\") being a landmark. The cost: you need to train and run a second network on noisy images, the classifier gradient can be adversarial (the sample may fool the classifier rather than look like class ), and it only works for conditions you can classify. Classifier-free guidance { cfg} Ho and Salimans (2022) removed the classifier. The trick is to train one network on both the conditional and the unconditional task by randomly replacing the condition with a null token (typically 10-20% of the time), so that and are two heads of one set of weights. Then the classifier gradient is implicit . Derivation: classifier-free guidance Rearrange Bayes' rule for the score: . Using , with and , Plug into the classifier-guided score and convert back to a noise prediction: The effective density being sampled is . -free guidance at one sampling step: two forward passes of the same network, one wit"},{"p":"week-13.html","w":"Week 13","t":"Generative Models II: Diffusion, Flows, and Autoregressive Models","h":"Latent diffusion","a":"latent-diffusion","x":"Running a denoiser at full pixel resolution is expensive: a image has 786,432 values and the network must be evaluated dozens of times. Rombach et al. (2022, Latent Diffusion Models , the basis of Stable Diffusion) observed that most pixel bits encode imperceptible high-frequency detail. Their recipe has two stages: 1. Perceptual compression. Train an autoencoder (a VAE with a small KL penalty and/or a VQ layer, plus an LPIPS perceptual loss and a patch GAN loss; see Week 12) that maps an image to a latent with spatial downsampling factor and decodes it back with . 2. Latent diffusion. Train the diffusion model on instead of (the loss is the same , with ). Generation: sample , denoise to , decode . Latent diffusion. A frozen text encoder produces tokens that condition the denoiser through cross-attention; denoising runs in the compressed latent space, and a VAE decoder maps the final latent back to pixels. The saving is large: pixel values versus latent values, a reduction, and self-attention cost (quadratic in positions) falls by a factor of between pixel positions and latent positions. The autoencoder is trained once and reused. Two caveats: the autoencoder's reconstruction ceiling caps the quality (small text and fine facial detail are often lost; newer systems use 16-channel latents), and the latent scale must be normalized to roughly unit variance (Stable Diffusion multiplies latents by a fixed constant, 0.18215 for version 1) so that the noise schedule is meaningful. Why not just make the VAE strong and skip diffusion? A VAE's decoder cannot model the global structure of a scene in one Gaussian step (previous section); diffusion handles global structure with a series of small steps. Latent diffusion splits the labor: the autoencoder removes perceptually irrelevant"},{"p":"week-13.html","w":"Week 13","t":"Generative Models II: Diffusion, Flows, and Autoregressive Models","h":"Backbones: U-Net and DiT","a":"backbones","x":"The network must map a tensor to a tensor of the same shape, using and as side information. Two families are common. The U-Net { unet} A diffusion U-Net: a convolutional encoder-decoder with skip connections, ResBlocks conditioned on the timestep embedding, self-attention at low resolution, and cross-attention to text tokens. The U-Net (Ronneberger et al., 2015, for biomedical segmentation) pairs a downsampling path that builds a multi-scale representation with an upsampling path that restores resolution, joined by skip connections that carry fine detail across. Diffusion U-Nets (Ho et al., 2020, building on PixelCNN++ and Wide-ResNet blocks) add: - Time conditioning. (or -SNR) is mapped through a sinusoidal embedding (as in Transformer positional encodings, Week 9) and an MLP, then added into every residual block (or used for FiLM scale-and-shift). - GroupNorm and SiLU activations throughout, rather than BatchNorm, because the per-sample noise level varies and batch statistics would leak across samples. - Self-attention at the low-resolution stages (for example 16x16 and 8x8) to capture global structure, and cross-attention blocks for text. - Output of the same shape as the input , plus optional variance channels. U-Nets have a strong inductive bias (locality, multi-scale), are efficient at resolutions up to 64x64 in latent space, and remain the backbone of many deployed systems. Diffusion Transformers (DiT) { dit} A DiT block: latent patches become tokens, and the timestep and class embedding modulate each block through adaptive LayerNorm. Zero-initialized gates make every block start as an identity map. Peebles and Xie (2023, \"Scalable Diffusion Models with Transformers\") replaced the U-Net by a plain Vision Transformer (Week 11) on latent patches. The latent is cut "},{"p":"week-13.html","w":"Week 13","t":"Generative Models II: Diffusion, Flows, and Autoregressive Models","h":"Normalizing flows","a":"flows","x":"Diffusion's probability-flow ODE is one instance of a broader idea: build a generative model by transforming a simple density through an invertible map and track how density changes. This is the normalizing flow family, and unlike VAEs and GANs, flows give the exact log-likelihood. Change of variables { change-of-variables} Let (typically ) and let with invertible and differentiable, . Then The derivation is one line from probability conservation, : a map that stretches space must lower the density by the same factor. A deep flow composes maps , and the log-determinants add up. Training maximizes the exact log-likelihood of the data; sampling draws and applies . For a general Jacobian the determinant costs and the inverse is not available, so a flow layer must be designed so that both are cheap. Invertibility plus an easy determinant is the design constraint that limits flows' architectural freedom: you cannot use arbitrary networks or bottlenecks (the dimension must be preserved). Coupling layers { coupling} An affine coupling layer: half of the coordinates pass through unchanged and parameterize an elementwise scale and shift of the other half. The Jacobian is triangular, so its log-determinant is a sum; the inverse needs no matrix inversion. The influential trick (NICE, Dinh et al. 2015; RealNVP , Dinh et al. 2017) splits and sets The functions can be arbitrary neural networks , because they are only ever evaluated in the forward direction. The Jacobian is block-triangular, and the inverse is with . Stacking layers that alternate which half is transformed (checkerboard or channel masks for images) lets every coordinate be transformed. Glow (Kingma and Dhariwal, 2018) added invertible convolutions and actnorm, and produced high-resolution faces. Here is a compact coup"},{"p":"week-13.html","w":"Week 13","t":"Generative Models II: Diffusion, Flows, and Autoregressive Models","h":"Autoregressive models","a":"autoregressive","x":"The other exact-likelihood family writes the data distribution as a product of conditionals by the chain rule of probability, and trains by maximizing , i.e. ordinary cross-entropy (or a mixture likelihood for continuous values), with teacher forcing : all conditionals can be computed in one parallel pass if the architecture enforces causality. Sampling is sequential: draw , then , and so on. You have already met the dominant instance: the GPT-style language models of Week 10. The question for images and audio is how to impose an ordering and a causal architecture. PixelRNN and PixelCNN { pixelcnn} van den Oord, Kalchbrenner and Kavukcuoglu (2016, PixelRNN/PixelCNN) order pixels in raster scan and model each pixel's three colour channels as a 256-way softmax conditioned on all previous pixels. A masked convolution zeroes the kernel weights that would look at the current pixel (mask type A, first layer) or at future pixels (mask type B, subsequent layers), so a standard convolutional stack computes all conditionals in parallel during training. Later versions (Gated PixelCNN, PixelCNN++) improved the receptive field (the plain stack has a blind spot to the upper right) and used a discretized logistic mixture output. WaveNet and audio { wavenet} For audio the sequence is a waveform at 16-24 kHz, so a model needs huge receptive fields. WaveNet (van den Oord et al., 2016) uses stacks of dilated causal convolutions with dilation repeated in blocks, so the receptive field grows exponentially with depth, gated activations, and a 256-way softmax over -law-quantized samples. It set the quality bar for text-to-speech but was painfully slow to sample (one network pass per audio sample); fast variants (Parallel WaveNet via distillation, WaveRNN, GAN vocoders) followed, and modern sp"},{"p":"week-13.html","w":"Week 13","t":"Generative Models II: Diffusion, Flows, and Autoregressive Models","h":"Comparing the generative families","a":"comparison","x":"You now have all the major families. The table compares them on the properties that decide which one to use. Ratings reflect the typical state of the art around 2023-2025 and are a guide, not a law: each family has been pushed beyond its stereotype by clever engineering. VAE GAN Normalizing flow Autoregressive Diffusion / flow matching --- --- --- --- --- --- Training objective ELBO (reconstruction + KL) minimax adversarial exact log-likelihood exact log-likelihood (cross-entropy) denoising regression (variational bound or reweighted) Exact likelihood no (lower bound) no yes yes bound; exact via ODE (expensive) Latent code yes, low-dim, learned encoder yes (no encoder) yes, same dimension, exact inverse none (tokens only) noise, deterministic via ODE Sample quality blurry in pixel space sharp, fast moderate high (with tokens and scale) state of the art Mode coverage good prone to collapse good good good Sampling cost one pass one pass one pass sequential passes (or tokens) 10-1000 passes (fewer with distillation) Training stability stable unstable stable stable stable Typical failure blur, posterior collapse mode collapse, instability poor fine detail, rigid architecture slow sampling, ordering artifacts slow sampling, high-guidance artifacts Main uses today compression, latent spaces for other models real-time synthesis, super-resolution, vocoders, distillation targets density estimation, scientific sampling language, code, audio tokens, multimodal LLMs images, video, audio, 3D, molecules, robotics policies A few observations for choosing in practice. If you need likelihoods (compression, anomaly detection, scientific models), prefer autoregressive models or flows. If you need real-time synthesis, prefer a GAN or a distilled diffusion model. If you want the best images"},{"p":"week-13.html","w":"Week 13","t":"Generative Models II: Diffusion, Flows, and Autoregressive Models","h":"Applications","a":"applications","x":"Diffusion-style generators are now general-purpose density learners for continuous signals, and a short survey shows how the ideas of this week recombine. - Images. Text-to-image systems (DALL-E 2, Imagen, Stable Diffusion, Midjourney, and many others) combine a text encoder, latent diffusion and classifier-free guidance. Conditioning variants cover inpainting and outpainting, super-resolution (cascaded or latent), image editing by inversion and re-sampling (SDEdit, prompt-to-prompt), and fine-grained control (ControlNet, IP-Adapter-style image prompts, LoRA personalization such as DreamBooth). - Video. Video diffusion models add a time axis to the latent (3-D convolutions, temporal attention, or spacetime patches in a DiT), and are trained on enormous caption/video corpora; quality is limited by temporal consistency and the cost of long sequences, and many systems generate short clips and extend them. - Audio and music. Diffusion operates on spectrograms or on neural-codec latents (text-to-music, text-to-audio, speech synthesis); autoregressive token models remain strong competitors, especially for speech. - Science and engineering. Diffusion on 3-D coordinates and rotation groups for protein structure and molecule generation, conditional weather and trajectory generation, and diffusion policies for robot control (learning a distribution over action sequences). These connect directly to the geometric ideas of Week 14. - Inverse problems. Because a diffusion model is a learned prior, one can sample from the posterior for a measurement (denoising, deblurring, MRI reconstruction) by adding the measurement likelihood gradient to the score: the same decomposition as classifier guidance, with in place of ."},{"p":"week-13.html","w":"Week 13","t":"Generative Models II: Diffusion, Flows, and Autoregressive Models","h":"Risks, provenance and watermarking","a":"risks","x":"High-quality, cheap generation changes the information environment, and responsible practitioners need to understand the technical facts as well as the ethics. What can go wrong { harms} - Deepfakes and impersonation. Synthetic faces, voices and video of real people enable fraud (voice cloning in phone scams), harassment (non-consensual intimate imagery, a major real-world harm that disproportionately affects women), and political disinformation. A related harm is the \"liar's dividend\": real evidence can be dismissed as fake. - Training data, copyright and consent. Models are trained on web-scale images and text, many protected by copyright or containing personal data. The legal status varies by jurisdiction and is actively litigated; it is unresolved at the time of writing. Carlini et al. (2023) demonstrated that diffusion models can memorize and regurgitate individual training images, particularly ones duplicated many times in the data, which makes deduplication and membership audits a technical necessity, not only a legal one. - Bias and representation. Models reproduce and can amplify stereotypes present in training captions (occupations, nationalities, skin tones). Guidance makes prototypical (hence stereotypical) outputs more likely: a diversity-versus-fidelity trade-off with social consequences. - Misuse of open weights. Releasing weights enables research, auditing and local use, and also removes the ability to enforce safety filters. Safety classifiers, prompt filters, concept erasure and fine-tuning to remove a concept are partial mitigations; fine-tuning can usually undo them for open models. - Environmental and labor costs. Training and serving cost energy, and data labeling and content moderation rely on human work that is often poorly paid. Detection, prove"},{"p":"week-13.html","w":"Week 13","t":"Generative Models II: Diffusion, Flows, and Autoregressive Models","h":"Lab: a class-conditional DDPM on MNIST","a":"lab","x":"You will build and train a small DDPM from scratch, conditioning on the digit label with classifier-free guidance, then run two experiments: number of sampling steps (DDPM vs DDIM) and guidance scale . A free Colab GPU trains it in 5-10 minutes (about 10 epochs); a CPU works but is slow. Fix a single device and seed at the top and keep every tensor on it. Part 1: the diffusion process { lab-process} Part 2: the network { lab-network} Part 3: training with condition dropout and an EMA { lab-train} The loss is a mean-squared error on unit-variance noise: at initialization (predicting zero) it equals about 1.0, and it should drop quickly to a few hundredths. Do not expect it to keep falling dramatically: the irreducible part (the noise at high that truly cannot be predicted) sets a floor, which is why sample quality, not loss, is the metric you must look at . Part 4: samplers (DDPM and DDIM) with guidance { lab-sample} Part 5: experiments { lab-experiments} Experiment A: steps. Compare DDIM at 5, 10, 20, 50 and 200 steps and DDPM at 1000 steps, with the same seed (so the same starting noise), at . Look at the grids first, then measure quality with the quick classifier below. Expect: with 5 steps images are blobby; at 20-50 DDIM steps digits are clearly legible; DDPM at 1000 steps is the quality reference but 20-50 times slower. Experiment B: guidance. Fix 50 DDIM steps and sweep . Expect (unconditional) to ignore the label, to follow it mostly, and to make digits bolder and more uniform in style (less diversity), with artifacts and saturation by . You should see class accuracy rise with (from chance, about 0.1, at ) and saturate near 1, while the diversity number falls: the same trade-off as the toy chart above. If accuracy at is already near 1, push the model harder by tr"},{"p":"week-13.html","w":"Week 13","t":"Generative Models II: Diffusion, Flows, and Autoregressive Models","h":"Common mistakes and debugging tips","a":"mistakes","x":"Symptom Likely cause Fix --- --- --- Samples are pure noise or grey blobs, loss barely moves from 1.0 data not scaled to , so the signal-to-noise assumptions break; or timestep not passed to the network normalize with mean 0.5, std 0.5; check t enters every block Loss is fine, samples are very over-saturated or burnt sampler uses wrong indices (off-by-one between 0-based arrays and in 1.. ), or a missing clamp of print ab[0] and ab[T-1] ; use the same index convention in training and sampling; clamp to the data range Samples bright or dim on average, never very dark or light non-zero terminal SNR: model trained with and sampled from pure noise zero-terminal-SNR rescaling plus -prediction; or at least a schedule with Guided samples ignore the label the null label was never used in training (no condition dropout), or sampling uses the wrong null index p uncond about 0.1; same NULL LABEL in train and sample; check Guided samples saturated, texture-less guidance scale too high; high-guidance artifacts lower , apply guidance only in mid-trajectory, rescale or threshold DDIM with few steps looks worse than expected timestep subsequence skipped the high-noise region, or eta nonzero with few steps include , space steps evenly (or in log-SNR), use for few steps Training spikes or diverges high learning rate, no gradient clipping, or a bad init lr about 1e-4 to 2e-4, clip grad norm to 1, zero-init the output layer, use an EMA for sampling EMA model gives worse samples than the raw model early on EMA decay too slow relative to the number of steps use decay=0.99 for short runs or warm up the decay; always sample from the EMA once it has seen enough steps Different results on every run despite a fixed seed sampling noise from the global RNG or non-deterministic ops pass a torch.Gene"},{"p":"week-13.html","w":"Week 13","t":"Generative Models II: Diffusion, Flows, and Autoregressive Models","h":"Exercises","a":"exercises","x":"Exercise 1: variance preservation and the closed form (a) Show that if has mean and variance per coordinate, then has mean and variance . (b) Use this to show that if the data have unit variance and zero mean, then every has zero mean and unit variance. (c) For data with variance and mean , find the mean and variance of in terms of . Solution (a) with independent: mean , variance . (b) With , : variance at every step, by induction. (c) By the closed form : mean , variance . This tends to as regardless of , but it only equals 1 at every step if . This is why data are normalized before diffusion and why latent-diffusion systems rescale their latents. Exercise 2: where is the signal-to-noise ratio equal to one? Using the NumPy code of the forward process, find the first step at which (i.e. ) for the linear schedule and for the cosine schedule with . What does the result say about how each schedule spends its steps? Solution For the linear schedule, np.argmax(ab < 0.5) + 1 returns ( ). For the cosine schedule the crossing is at , almost exactly the midpoint. So the linear schedule reaches equal signal and noise power after only a quarter of the steps and then spends the remaining three quarters (SNR from 1 down to ) on very noisy inputs, where the network has little to work with. The cosine schedule is much more evenly spread (see the log-SNR chart). This is the quantitative argument for the cosine schedule on small images. Exercise 3: the SNR-weighted view of the epsilon-loss Show that and , where and are the estimates implied by the same . Solution Both estimates satisfy . So , which gives . For , the error is . Substituting gives , so the squared error is because . Exercise 4: the optimal denoiser for Gaussian data (derivation) Let in one dimension. (a) Find the marginal"},{"p":"week-13.html","w":"Week 13","t":"Generative Models II: Diffusion, Flows, and Autoregressive Models","h":"Key takeaways","a":"key-takeaways","x":"Key takeaways - Diffusion has a fixed forward noising chain and a learned reverse chain. The closed form turns training into cheap regression at a random noise level. - The ELBO is a sum of KLs between Gaussians; with the -parameterization it reduces to weighted noise prediction, and dropping the weights ( ) improves samples but is no longer a likelihood bound. - -, - and -prediction are linear reparameterizations of each other; they differ in implicit loss weighting (SNR versus SNR versus 1) and numerical conditioning. -prediction behaves well at every noise level. - The network is a score estimator: . Denoising score matching gives the loss; the reverse SDE and the deterministic probability-flow ODE generate samples from it. - DDIM and higher-order ODE solvers reduce 1000 steps to 10-50 because the probability-flow trajectory is smooth; distillation and consistency training go lower by changing the model. - Classifier-free guidance, , samples from : higher fidelity and prompt adherence, lower diversity, artifacts at large . - Latent diffusion runs diffusion in a VAE latent space (48 times fewer values at ) with cross-attention to text tokens; U-Nets and DiTs are interchangeable denoisers, DiTs scale predictably. - Flows give exact likelihood through invertible layers with cheap Jacobians (coupling layers); autoregressive models factorize by the chain rule and now operate on learned tokens. Each family trades quality, likelihood, speed and stability differently. - Generative media bring real risks (deepfakes, memorization, bias). Provenance (C2PA), watermarking and detection are complementary and none is sufficient alone."},{"p":"week-13.html","w":"Week 13","t":"Generative Models II: Diffusion, Flows, and Autoregressive Models","h":"Further reading","a":"further-reading","x":"Essential - Ho, Jain and Abbeel, \"Denoising Diffusion Probabilistic Models\", NeurIPS 2020 (arXiv:2006.11239). The paper that made diffusion practical: read the forward process, the simplified loss and the U-Net. - Song, Sohl-Dickstein, Kingma, Kumar, Ermon and Poole, \"Score-Based Generative Modeling through Stochastic Differential Equations\", ICLR 2021 (arXiv:2011.13456). The SDE/ODE unification. - Rombach, Blattmann, Lorenz, Esser and Ommer, \"High-Resolution Image Synthesis with Latent Diffusion Models\", CVPR 2022 (arXiv:2112.10752). The basis of Stable Diffusion. - Ho and Salimans, \"Classifier-Free Diffusion Guidance\", NeurIPS 2021 Workshop on Deep Generative Models, 2021/2022 (arXiv:2207.12598). Foundations and theory - Sohl-Dickstein, Weiss, Maheswaranathan and Ganguli, \"Deep Unsupervised Learning using Nonequilibrium Thermodynamics\", ICML 2015 (arXiv:1503.03585). - Vincent, \"A Connection Between Score Matching and Denoising Autoencoders\", Neural Computation, 2011. - Song and Ermon, \"Generative Modeling by Estimating Gradients of the Data Distribution\", NeurIPS 2019 (arXiv:1907.05600). - Song, Meng and Ermon, \"Denoising Diffusion Implicit Models\", ICLR 2021 (arXiv:2010.02502). - Nichol and Dhariwal, \"Improved Denoising Diffusion Probabilistic Models\", ICML 2021 (arXiv:2102.09672): cosine schedule, learned variances. - Kingma, Salimans, Poole and Ho, \"Variational Diffusion Models\", NeurIPS 2021 (arXiv:2107.00630). - Karras, Aittala, Aila and Laine, \"Elucidating the Design Space of Diffusion-Based Generative Models\", NeurIPS 2022 (arXiv:2206.00364). - Dhariwal and Nichol, \"Diffusion Models Beat GANs on Image Synthesis\", NeurIPS 2021 (arXiv:2105.05233). - Salimans and Ho, \"Progressive Distillation for Fast Sampling of Diffusion Models\", ICLR 2022 (arXiv:2202.00512): -p"},{"p":"week-14.html","w":"Week 14","t":"Graph Neural Networks and Geometric Deep Learning","h":"Graph Neural Networks and Geometric Deep Learning","a":"","x":"Learning objectives By the end of this week you should be able to: - Describe how molecules, social networks, knowledge graphs and recommender systems are represented as graphs, and name the node-, edge- and graph-level tasks defined on them. - Build the adjacency, degree and Laplacian matrices of a graph, and explain what the Laplacian quadratic form and its eigenvectors measure. - State the message-passing framework (message, aggregate, update, readout) and prove that it is permutation equivariant. - Derive the GCN propagation rule from a first-order spectral filter and the renormalization trick, and compare it with GraphSAGE, GAT and GIN. - State precisely what the 1-Weisfeiler-Lehman (1-WL) test is, what message-passing GNNs can and cannot distinguish, and why GIN uses sum aggregation and an MLP. - Explain over-smoothing (with a Dirichlet-energy argument) and over-squashing (with a receptive-field argument), and list remedies: residual and initial connections, normalization, DropEdge, jumping knowledge, rewiring, and virtual nodes. - Choose a readout or pooling operator (sum, mean, max, attention, hierarchical) and say what each one can and cannot count. - Explain how graph Transformers use positional and structural encodings to inject graph structure into full attention. - State the geometric deep learning blueprint in terms of groups, invariance and equivariance, and identify the symmetry behind CNNs, GNNs, DeepSets and PointNet. - Implement PointNet and an E(n)-equivariant layer, and verify invariance and equivariance numerically. - Train GNNs with PyTorch Geometric for node classification on Cora and graph classification on a molecular dataset. you have built so far assumed a regular layout. A CNN (Week 5) needs pixels on a grid, an RNN or Transformer (Week 7, W"},{"p":"week-14.html","w":"Week 14","t":"Graph Neural Networks and Geometric Deep Learning","h":"Graph-structured data","a":"data","x":"A graph consists of a set of nodes (vertices) with and a set of edges . Each node may carry a feature vector , each edge may carry a feature vector , and the whole graph may carry a label or target. Edges can be undirected or directed, weighted or unweighted. A graph can also have several types of nodes and edges (a heterogeneous graph). Before touching any math, look at how four very different domains map onto this one abstraction. Molecules. Nodes are atoms, edges are covalent bonds. Node features encode the element (one-hot over C, N, O, ...), formal charge, hybridization or aromaticity; edge features encode the bond type (single, double, triple, aromatic). The typical task is graph-level regression or classification : predict a property of the whole molecule such as solubility, toxicity, binding affinity or an energy. A typical drug-like molecule has 20 to 50 heavy atoms, so the graphs are small, but there are millions of them. Molecular graphs are also sparse (an atom has at most about four bonds) and planar-ish , and the identity of a molecule is the graph itself, independent of how the atoms are numbered in a file. This is the setting where the invariance requirement is most obvious: a toxicity predictor that depends on atom numbering is simply wrong. Social and information networks. Nodes are users, papers or web pages; edges are friendships, citations or hyperlinks. These graphs can be enormous (billions of edges), have heavy-tailed degree distributions (a few hubs with millions of neighbours), and usually come with sparse labels. Typical tasks are node classification (which research area is this paper in? is this account a bot?) and link prediction (who will this user befriend?). Most of this graph is unlabeled, which is why semi-supervised learning, where the"},{"p":"week-14.html","w":"Week 14","t":"Graph Neural Networks and Geometric Deep Learning","h":"Graph basics: adjacency, degree and the Laplacian","a":"basics","x":"Number the nodes . The adjacency matrix has if there is an edge from to . For an undirected graph is symmetric. The degree of a node is its number of neighbours, , and the degree matrix is . Stack node features into , whose row is . Multiplying by is the fundamental graph operation. For a feature matrix , which is the sum of the features of the neighbours of node . One matrix product aggregates every neighbourhood simultaneously. Likewise is the neighbour mean , and counts walks of length : is the number of walks from to with exactly edges. So a -layer network that multiplies by at each layer mixes information within hops. Keep this in mind; it is the reason depth equals receptive-field radius in a GNN. The graph Laplacian The combinatorial Laplacian is and the symmetric normalized Laplacian is The Laplacian is the graph analogue of the second derivative, and its quadratic form measures how rough a signal is. Let assign one scalar to each node. Then (The middle step expands the square and uses ; the last sums each undirected edge once.) This Dirichlet energy is zero exactly when is constant on every connected component, and it is large when neighbouring nodes disagree. So the Laplacian measures smoothness over the graph . Three facts about its spectrum matter for GNNs: - is symmetric positive semi-definite, so it has real eigenvalues and orthonormal eigenvectors. - The multiplicity of the eigenvalue equals the number of connected components. The eigenvector for a connected graph is the constant vector. - Eigenvectors with small eigenvalues vary slowly over the graph (\"low frequency\") and those with large eigenvalues oscillate between neighbours (\"high frequency\"), exactly like sines and cosines of increasing frequency. The eigenvalues of lie in . The second eigenvector "},{"p":"week-14.html","w":"Week 14","t":"Graph Neural Networks and Geometric Deep Learning","h":"The message-passing framework","a":"mpnn","x":"Here is the idea of a GNN in one sentence: every node repeatedly collects information from its neighbours and updates its own representation . Let . One layer of message passing computes, for every node , The double braces denote a multiset (neighbours can repeat a value, and order is meaningless). Three ingredients: 1. MESSAGE turns a neighbour's state (optionally with the receiver's state and the edge features) into a message vector. It is often just a linear map . 2. AGGREGATE combines a variable number of messages into one fixed-size vector. It must be permutation invariant : sum, mean, max, or a learned but symmetric function. This is where the symmetry is enforced. 3. UPDATE merges the aggregated message with the node's own previous state, typically with an MLP and a nonlinearity. After layers, the final node embeddings feed a task head: a linear classifier for node classification, a dot-product or MLP over a pair of embeddings for link prediction, or a readout that pools all nodes for graph-level prediction, This template was named the message passing neural network (MPNN) by Gilmer, Schoenholz, Riley, Vinyals and Dahl (2017, ICML), who used it for predicting quantum-chemical properties of molecules, and it subsumes nearly every layer in the rest of this section. One round of message passing at node : neighbour states become messages, a permutation-invariant AGGREGATE reduces them to one vector, and UPDATE combines it with 's own state. Receptive fields: depth equals hops After one layer, depends on and its immediate neighbours. After two layers it depends on the 2-hop neighbourhood, because the neighbours' states already contained their neighbours. In general, is a function of exactly the nodes within distance of (the computation tree of depth rooted at ). Infor"},{"p":"week-14.html","w":"Week 14","t":"Graph Neural Networks and Geometric Deep Learning","h":"Graph convolutional networks (GCN)","a":"gcn","x":"The graph convolutional network of Kipf and Welling (2017, ICLR, \"Semi-supervised classification with graph convolutional networks\") is the simplest effective message-passing layer. In matrix form, one layer is Node by node, with , So GCN aggregates a weighted sum over the neighbours and the node itself , with weights that depend only on the degrees, and then applies one shared linear map and a nonlinearity. Three design choices need justification: why the self-loop, why this particular degree normalization, and where the formula comes from. Intuition for the normalization Plain sum aggregation has a scale problem: a node with 1000 neighbours receives a vector 1000 times larger than a node with 1 neighbour, so feature magnitude would track degree and training would be unstable. Mean aggregation fixes the scale (every row sums to one) but is asymmetric and treats a hub's message as important as a leaf's. The symmetric form down-weights messages from high-degree nodes and to high-degree nodes: the pair gets weight . It also has a clean spectral meaning, derived next. The self-loop ( ) lets a node retain its own signal. Without it a node's new state would be built only from its neighbours and its own features would be lost after a single layer. Spectral motivation From spectral graph convolution to the GCN layer Step 1: graph Fourier transform. Take the eigendecomposition . The columns of play the role of Fourier modes. The graph Fourier transform of a signal is . Step 2: spectral filtering. On a regular grid, convolution is pointwise multiplication in the Fourier domain. By analogy, filter a signal by scaling each frequency component with a learned function of the eigenvalue: Computing this needs the full eigendecomposition ( ), produces filters that are not localized in "},{"p":"week-14.html","w":"Week 14","t":"Graph Neural Networks and Geometric Deep Learning","h":"GraphSAGE: sampling and separate self weights","a":"sage","x":"GraphSAGE (Hamilton, Ying and Leskovec, 2017, NeurIPS, \"Inductive representation learning on large graphs\") addresses two practical limitations. Full-batch GCN needs the whole adjacency matrix in memory, and a hub node with neighbours makes the cost of a single node unbounded. The remedies are neighbour sampling and an explicit separation between a node and its neighbourhood. One layer is (the original paper writes this as a concatenation followed by one matrix, which is equivalent to two matrices summed), where is a fixed-size random sample of neighbours and AGG is a mean, a max-pool over per-neighbour MLP outputs, or an LSTM run over a random permutation of the sample (the LSTM is not permutation invariant, which the authors handle by random ordering). The embeddings are typically L2-normalized afterwards. Sampling bounds the computation. If each layer samples neighbours, the computation tree of one node has at most leaves regardless of the degrees, so mini-batches of target nodes have a predictable memory cost. At test time one can either sample again or use the full neighbourhood. Because the layer never refers to a global matrix, a trained model embeds nodes of a graph it has never seen (a new user, a new paper), which is the sense of inductive . Keeping separate from is also a real modeling improvement: a node's own features and its neighbourhood summary are different kinds of information, and the model can weight them differently (for example, when neighbours are noisy). GCN forces them through the same weights. PinSage (Ying et al., 2018, KDD) scaled this recipe to a graph of three billion nodes at Pinterest, replacing uniform sampling by importance sampling from random walks."},{"p":"week-14.html","w":"Week 14","t":"Graph Neural Networks and Geometric Deep Learning","h":"Graph attention networks (GAT)","a":"gat","x":"GCN weights every neighbour by a fixed function of the degrees. In many graphs some neighbours are more relevant than others, and the relevance depends on the features. Graph attention networks (Velickovic, Cucurull, Casanova, Romero, Lio and Bengio, 2018, ICLR) let the model learn the weights. This is the attention of Week 9 restricted to the graph neighbourhood: instead of attending over all tokens, node attends over . First apply a shared linear map, . For each edge compute an unnormalized score with a small learned vector and a LeakyReLU, The softmax runs over the neighbours of only, so the weights sum to one. As in Transformers, multi-head attention runs independent copies and concatenates (hidden layers) or averages (final layer). Two remarks that are often misunderstood: - Static attention. Because the score is (the concatenation splits the dot product), the ranking of neighbours is the same for every query node , since only the term differs between neighbours. This was pointed out by Brody, Alon and Yahav (2022, \"How attentive are graph attention networks?\"), who proposed GATv2 , , which makes the ranking depend on the query (\"dynamic attention\"). - Attention is still a convex combination. Weights are positive and sum to one, so a GAT neighbourhood summary is a weighted mean . It cannot count neighbours, which has consequences for expressive power below. Forgetting the self-loops in adj leaves isolated nodes with a row of all in the softmax, which produces nan s. This is one of the most common ways a hand-written GAT crashes."},{"p":"week-14.html","w":"Week 14","t":"Graph Neural Networks and Geometric Deep Learning","h":"Graph isomorphism networks (GIN)","a":"gin","x":"GCN, GraphSAGE-mean and GAT all aggregate with a mean-like operator. Xu, Hu, Leskovec and Jegelka (2019, ICLR, \"How powerful are graph neural networks?\") asked a sharper question: what is the most discriminative message-passing layer one can build? Their answer, the graph isomorphism network (GIN) , is with a learnable or fixed scalar (often ). Two choices distinguish it: the aggregator is a plain sum and the transformation is a full MLP (at least one hidden layer) instead of one linear map. The reason is developed in the next section: sum over a multiset can be made injective, mean and max cannot, and an MLP is needed to approximate the injective function that realizes it. The four layers side by side. All of them are permutation-equivariant message-passing layers; they differ in how neighbours are weighted and combined. Layer Aggregation weights Self handled by Sees degree? Typical use --- --- --- --- --- GCN fixed, self-loop only through weights homophilous node classification, small graphs GraphSAGE mean/max/LSTM over a sample separate no (mean) huge graphs, inductive, recommenders GAT learned softmax self-loop in the softmax no (convex combination) noisy neighbourhoods, edges of varying relevance GIN sum (weights all ) term yes (sum counts) graph classification, chemistry Sum aggregation is not free. It is scale-sensitive (large-degree nodes produce large activations), so in practice GIN is used with batch normalization inside the MLP, and on graphs with very heterogeneous degrees a mean can generalize better even though it is theoretically weaker. We now make \"theoretically weaker\" precise."},{"p":"week-14.html","w":"Week 14","t":"Graph Neural Networks and Geometric Deep Learning","h":"Expressive power and the Weisfeiler-Lehman test","a":"expressivity","x":"Which graphs can a GNN tell apart? This matters because if two non-isomorphic graphs (say, two molecules with different properties) always receive the same embedding, no amount of training can separate them. Expressive power is about the limits of the architecture , not of optimization. The 1-WL colour refinement algorithm The 1-dimensional Weisfeiler-Lehman test (Weisfeiler and Leman, 1968), also called colour refinement or naive vertex classification, is a classical heuristic for graph isomorphism. It works as follows. 1. Give every node an initial colour (a constant if the graph has no node features, otherwise a hash of the features). 2. Repeat: , where HASH is any injective map from (colour, multiset of colours) to new colours. 3. Stop when the partition of the nodes into colour classes no longer changes (at most rounds). 4. Compare the histograms of colours of the two graphs. If they differ at some round, the graphs are certainly non-isomorphic . If they agree forever, the test is inconclusive. The test is one-sided: \"different\" is a proof; \"same\" is not. The message-passing equation above is exactly this procedure with neural networks in place of the hash. That is the whole connection, and Xu et al. and, independently, Morris et al. (2019, AAAI, \"Weisfeiler and Leman go neural: higher-order graph neural networks\") turned it into theorems. The classical failure of 1-WL: a 6-cycle and two disjoint triangles. Every node in both graphs has degree 2, so refinement never splits the single colour class. The next demo runs colour refinement on two graphs simultaneously. Start with the hexagon versus two triangles, then try the pairs that 1-WL separates after one or two rounds, and observe the difference between \"histograms differ\" and \"stable partition with equal histogra"},{"p":"week-14.html","w":"Week 14","t":"Graph Neural Networks and Geometric Deep Learning","h":"Going deep: over-smoothing and over-squashing","a":"depth","x":"CNNs got dramatically better with depth (ResNet had 152 layers; Week 6). Message-passing GNNs usually do not: the best Cora models have two layers, and accuracy typically drops beyond three or four. Two distinct phenomena explain this, and it is worth keeping them apart because they have different causes and different cures. Over-smoothing Over-smoothing is the tendency of node representations to become indistinguishable as depth grows. Intuition: every layer replaces a node's feature by an average of its neighbourhood. Averaging repeatedly is diffusion, and diffusion on a connected graph converges to a constant. After enough layers, a node in one community and a node in another hold nearly the same vector, and a classifier on top cannot separate them. Li, Han and Wu (2018, AAAI, \"Deeper insights into graph convolutional networks for semi-supervised learning\") observed that the GCN layer is a form of Laplacian smoothing and that repeated smoothing makes features of different classes converge. Why repeated propagation converges Take the linear part of GCN layers, . The matrix is symmetric with eigenpairs ; for a connected graph with self-loops , and the top eigenvector is . Writing (a row vector of graph-Fourier coefficients), So every node ends up with the same direction , scaled by : only the degree survives. The rate is governed by (the spectral gap). To quantify it, define the degree-normalized Dirichlet energy . Then because the term has , and for we have . Smoothness measured by energy therefore vanishes geometrically in the depth. and Suzuki (2020, ICLR) extended this to GCNs with ReLU and weight matrices: under conditions on the singular values of the weights, the features approach an invariant subspace exponentially fast, so deep GCNs lose the information needed"},{"p":"week-14.html","w":"Week 14","t":"Graph Neural Networks and Geometric Deep Learning","h":"Pooling and readout","a":"pooling","x":"For graph-level prediction we need a single vector from the node embeddings , where varies from graph to graph. The readout must be permutation invariant and handle any . This is the same problem as DeepSets (Zaheer et al., 2017, NeurIPS), whose theorem says that a function on a set is invariant if and only if it can be written as for suitable (for countable inputs, and under suitable conditions). That is exactly \"per-node transformation, sum, then an MLP\". From node embeddings to a graph prediction: message-passing layers, then a permutation-invariant readout (flat or hierarchical), then an MLP head. Flat readouts. - Sum : . It counts: a graph with twice as many identical nodes has twice the output. This is the right choice for extensive properties such as molecular energy or number of rings, and it is what GIN uses (with the sum taken at every layer and the results concatenated). - Mean : . Invariant to graph size, so better for intensive properties (a ratio, a type), but it cannot tell a graph from two disjoint copies of itself. - Max : element-wise maximum over nodes. It detects whether a pattern occurs anywhere, and ignores how often. - Attention / Set2Set / gated readouts : a learned weighting of nodes. Set2Set (Vinyals, Bengio and Kudlur, 2016) runs an LSTM that repeatedly attends over the set; gated attention readouts (Li et al., 2016, Gated Graph Neural Networks) compute . Hierarchical pooling. Flat readouts discard structure in one step. CNNs instead pool gradually, shrinking resolution while increasing receptive field, and several graph methods mimic that by coarsening the graph : - DiffPool (Ying et al., 2018, NeurIPS) learns a soft assignment matrix of nodes to clusters with a GNN and a softmax, then forms a coarse graph , . It is differentiable but needs a"},{"p":"week-14.html","w":"Week 14","t":"Graph Neural Networks and Geometric Deep Learning","h":"Tasks: node, link and graph prediction","a":"tasks","x":"Node classification (and regression) The setup in the GCN paper is semi-supervised node classification on a single large graph: a few nodes have labels (for Cora, 20 per class, 140 in total out of 2,708 nodes), and the goal is to label the rest. The loss is cross-entropy computed on the labeled nodes only, , while forward passes use the whole graph. The reason it works is the homophily assumption: connected nodes tend to share labels, so the label information of a few nodes can be diffused to their neighbourhoods. Compare with the bar chart below: an MLP that ignores the edges reaches roughly 55% on Cora, the GCN reaches about 81.5%. About 26 points come from the structure alone. Two practical points about node classification. Splits : the standard Planetoid split (Yang, Cohen and Salakhutdinov, 2016) uses 20 labeled nodes per class for training, 500 for validation and 1,000 for test; results on it are sensitive to the split, and Shchur et al. (2018, \"Pitfalls of graph neural network evaluation\") showed that with random splits and tuned baselines the ranking of models changes considerably. Mini-batching : for graphs too large for full-batch training, sample the computation tree of each target node (GraphSAGE's neighbour sampling, or cluster-based sampling as in Cluster-GCN), so each step touches a bounded subgraph. Link prediction Link prediction asks whether an edge exists (or will exist). The standard recipe is an encoder-decoder : a GNN encoder produces node embeddings , and a decoder scores pairs, for example a dot product or an MLP on . The graph auto-encoder (GAE, Kipf and Welling, 2016) trains the encoder so that reconstructs the adjacency matrix. Training needs negative examples , because only positive edges are observed. Sample node pairs uniformly at random as"},{"p":"week-14.html","w":"Week 14","t":"Graph Neural Networks and Geometric Deep Learning","h":"Graph Transformers","a":"transformers","x":"Message passing is local. A Transformer (Week 9) lets every token attend to every other token in one layer, which removes both over-squashing (any two nodes are one hop apart) and the depth needed for long-range interactions. Applied naively to a graph, however, it treats nodes as a set and ignores the edges entirely: self-attention is permutation equivariant but knows nothing about who is connected to whom. A graph Transformer therefore has to inject the structure . There are three standard ways, usually combined. 1. Positional and structural encodings on nodes. Append to each node feature a vector describing where it sits in the graph: the first eigenvectors of the Laplacian (Dwivedi and Bresson, 2020; Kreuzer et al., 2021, \"SAN\"), random-walk return probabilities (RWSE), or degree and centrality. Laplacian eigenvectors generalize the sinusoidal position encodings of sequences: on a path graph they are exactly cosines. 2. Attention bias from structure. Add a learned bias to the attention logits that depends on the relationship between the two nodes: shortest-path distance, or edge features along the shortest path. Graphormer (Ying et al., 2021, NeurIPS) uses centrality (degree) encodings on nodes, spatial (shortest-path distance) encodings and edge encodings as biases, and won the OGB large-scale quantum-chemistry challenge. 3. Hybrid local plus global layers. GPS (Rampasek et al., 2022, NeurIPS, \"Recipe for a general, powerful, scalable graph transformer\") runs a message-passing layer and a global attention layer in parallel in each block and sums the outputs, with positional encodings fed in at the input. This keeps the local inductive bias that GNNs get cheaply, and adds global mixing. Laplacian positional encodings come with an annoying ambiguity : if is an eigenv"},{"p":"week-14.html","w":"Week 14","t":"Graph Neural Networks and Geometric Deep Learning","h":"Geometric deep learning: symmetry as a design principle","a":"gdl","x":"Step back from graphs. A CNN works because of translation symmetry : a cat shifted three pixels to the right is still a cat, and the same detector is useful at every position, so convolution shares weights across positions. A GNN works because of permutation symmetry : the same molecule with its atoms renumbered is the same molecule, so message passing treats every node identically and aggregates neighbours in an order-independent way. The programme of geometric deep learning (Bronstein, Bruna, Cohen and Velickovic, 2021, \"Geometric deep learning: grids, groups, graphs, geodesics, and gauges\") is to read both as instances of one recipe: identify the symmetry group of your domain, then restrict the network to functions that respect it . This shrinks the hypothesis space, improves sample efficiency and builds in generalization that data alone would need huge datasets to learn. Groups, actions, invariance and equivariance A group is a set of transformations that can be composed and inverted (it contains an identity, every element has an inverse, composition is associative). Examples: the integers under addition (shifts of a sequence), the permutation group (relabelling nodes), the rotation group , the group of rigid motions (rotations plus translations), the Euclidean group (adding reflections). A group acts on a space of signals: each gives a map (a shift of an image, a permutation of the rows of a feature matrix, a rotation of a set of 3-D points) with . Invariance and equivariance Let act on the input space by and on the output space by . A function is - -equivariant if for all and all : transform then compute equals compute then transform. - -invariant if for all . This is the special case where is trivial (the identity). and invariance as commuting diagrams: applying "},{"p":"week-14.html","w":"Week 14","t":"Graph Neural Networks and Geometric Deep Learning","h":"Point clouds and 3-D data","a":"pointcloud","x":"A point cloud is a set (optionally with colour, intensity or normals), as produced by LiDAR, depth cameras and photogrammetry. It is the native output of 3-D sensors and the main input of autonomous-driving perception and robotics. Point clouds break every assumption a CNN makes: they have no grid, density varies enormously (points are dense near the sensor and sparse far away), varies, and the points have no order . They are sets , so the symmetry group is , and for many tasks you also want the answer to be unchanged by a rigid motion of the whole scene. Three families of approaches existed before and alongside point-based networks: voxelization (rasterize into a 3-D occupancy grid and run a 3-D CNN, as in VoxNet and 3-D U-Nets; cubic memory growth limits resolution, though sparse convolutions fix much of it), multi-view methods (render the object from several viewpoints and apply a 2-D CNN, effective but hostile to scenes), and graph/point-based networks that consume points directly. PointNet PointNet (Qi, Su, Mo and Guibas, 2017, CVPR, \"PointNet: deep learning on point sets for 3D classification and segmentation\") was the first deep network to consume raw point sets. The architecture implements the DeepSets recipe from the previous section: where is a shared MLP applied to each point independently, the max is taken per feature channel across the points, and is an MLP on the resulting 1024-dimensional global descriptor. Because is symmetric, is permutation-invariant by construction. Qi et al. prove that such functions can approximate any continuous set function arbitrarily well (continuous with respect to the Hausdorff distance), given enough channels, and give an interpretation: the network selects a sparse set of critical points (those that win the max in some chann"},{"p":"week-14.html","w":"Week 14","t":"Graph Neural Networks and Geometric Deep Learning","h":"Equivariant networks: an overview","a":"equivariant","x":"Many 3-D problems have the symmetry that a rigid motion of the whole system changes nothing physically. The energy of a molecule is invariant under rotations and translations of its atoms, and the forces on its atoms are equivariant (they rotate with the molecule; indeed forces are minus the gradient of the energy, so an invariant energy gives equivariant forces automatically). Protein structure, material properties, particle simulations, and robotics policies share this structure. There are several ways to respect it, ordered roughly by how much machinery they need. 1. Augmentation and canonicalization. Feed randomly rotated copies, or align inputs to a canonical frame. Simple, approximate, and can be remarkably competitive when data is plentiful. 2. Invariant features only. Build the network from rotation-invariant quantities: interatomic distances (SchNet, Schutt et al., 2017, continuous-filter convolutions on distances), plus angles between bonds (DimeNet, Gasteiger, Groll and Guennemann, 2020) or dihedral angles. The network is exactly invariant, but purely scalar features can be incomplete: some distinct 3-D arrangements share all distances up to a given order. 3. Equivariant vector/tensor features. Keep, in addition to scalars, features that transform as vectors or higher-order tensors under rotation, and combine them only with operations that respect the group: Tensor Field Networks (Thomas et al., 2018), SE(3)-Transformers (Fuchs et al., 2020), and NequIP (Batzner et al., 2022, Nature Communications) for interatomic potentials. They use irreducible representations of (spherical harmonics), with Clebsch-Gordan tensor products as the equivariant way to multiply features; the e3nn library (Geiger and Smidt, 2022) implements them. These are the most expressive and "},{"p":"week-14.html","w":"Week 14","t":"Graph Neural Networks and Geometric Deep Learning","h":"Lab: GNNs with PyTorch Geometric on Cora and MUTAG","a":"lab","x":"PyTorch Geometric (PyG) is the standard library for graph deep learning. Its Data object holds a graph ( x node features, edge index of shape , y labels, optional edge attr ), DataLoader batches many graphs by concatenation as described above, and the torch geometric.nn module provides GCNConv , SAGEConv , GATConv , GINConv , pooling operators and much more. Install it in Colab with pip install torch geometric (recent versions of PyG need no extra compiled wheels for the layers used here). In this lab you will (1) train GCN and GAT on Cora, (2) probe depth and over-smoothing, (3) train a GIN on MUTAG with cross-validation, and (4) verify permutation equivariance and invariance on the trained models. Part 1: Cora node classification { lab-cora} Cora is a citation network: 2,708 papers (nodes) in seven topics, 5,278 undirected citation links (stored as 10,556 directed edges), and a 1,433-dimensional binary bag-of-words feature vector per paper. Only 140 nodes (20 per class) are labeled for training. The homophily of about 0.81 is the quantity that makes a low-pass filter (GCN) the right model here: four out of five edges connect papers of the same topic. Now a GCN and a GAT with the hyperparameters of the original papers (hidden size 16 with dropout 0.5 and weight decay for GCN; 8 heads of 8 units for GAT). Expect roughly 55 to 60% test accuracy for the MLP, about 80 to 82% for GCN and 81 to 83% for GAT, with a spread of one to two points across seeds (the published numbers are 81.5% and 83.0%, shown in the chart above). If your GCN lands at 70% or below, look at the first item in the debugging section. Part 2: depth, over-smoothing and a remedy { lab-depth} Now replace the two-layer GCN by an -layer one, and watch accuracy as grows. Add DropEdge via dropout edge and a re"},{"p":"week-14.html","w":"Week 14","t":"Graph Neural Networks and Geometric Deep Learning","h":"Common mistakes and debugging tips","a":"mistakes","x":"Symptom Likely cause Fix --- --- --- GCN on Cora gets about 70% or less, training loss is fine Row-normalized features were not used, dropout missing, or no weight decay; wrong split (training on all nodes) Use Planetoid masks, dropout 0.5, weight decay on the first layer, 200 epochs; optionally T.NormalizeFeatures() Test accuracy is much higher than expected Test labels or test nodes used in training or model selection; leakage via features Compute the loss only on train mask ; select epochs on val mask nan loss in a hand-written GAT Isolated nodes with no self-loop give an all softmax row Add self-loops before attention; use add self loops=True in GATConv Accuracy collapses for deeper models Over-smoothing plus optimization difficulty Residual or initial-residual connections, JK, DropEdge, or decouple propagation from transformation (APPNP, SGC) Embeddings of high-degree nodes explode with sum aggregation Unnormalized sum scales with degree Use mean or symmetric normalization, or normalization layers; clip degrees in PNA-style scalers Link prediction AUC near 1.0 in training, near 0.5 at test Supervision edges are also used for message passing (leakage) Separate message-passing edges from supervision edges ( RandomLinkSplit ) Graph classification accuracy has huge variance between runs Tiny dataset; single split; early stopping on test 10-fold CV, fixed protocol, report mean and std, compare with a simple baseline Model cannot distinguish graphs with identical degree sequences Constant node features and 1-WL limits Add structural features (degree, cycle counts, RWSE), or a more expressive architecture Mini-batch of graphs gives wrong results batch vector inconsistent with the nodes, or readout over the whole batch instead of per graph Pass batch to the pooling op; che"},{"p":"week-14.html","w":"Week 14","t":"Graph Neural Networks and Geometric Deep Learning","h":"Exercises","a":"exercises","x":"Exercise 1: Laplacian and smoothness (derivation) (a) Prove for an undirected graph with , and conclude that is positive semi-definite. (b) Show that the multiplicity of eigenvalue equals the number of connected components. (c) For the path graph on three nodes, compute the eigenvalues of . Solution (a) because node 's square appears once per incident edge. And . Subtracting gives , so every eigenvalue is nonnegative. (b) implies , hence across every edge, so is constant on each connected component; conversely every such vector is in the null space. The null space therefore has one dimension per component. (c) has eigenvalues with eigenvectors , , . The eigenvector for alternates in sign, the highest frequency. Exercise 2: One GCN layer by hand (coding or pencil) Take the path graph with scalar features and no learned weights. Compute and . Then explain why the output of node 1 is not simply the mean of . Solution With self-loops the degrees are . Then , , and , other entries zero. So . Node 1's output is , not the mean : the weights sum to , not to . Symmetric normalization is not an averaging operator: row sums differ from one (only the top eigenvalue equals one). A random-walk normalization would give exactly the mean . Exercise 3: What 1-WL cannot see (a) The triangular prism (two triangles joined by three parallel edges) and the complete bipartite graph are both 3-regular on 6 nodes. Show that 1-WL cannot distinguish them. (b) Name two properties on which they differ that a message-passing GNN therefore cannot detect. (c) Using the demo, find a pair of small graphs for which 1-WL succeeds after exactly one round. Solution (a) Start with a constant colouring. Every node has the same colour and exactly three neighbours of that colour, so after round 1 every node in b"},{"p":"week-14.html","w":"Week 14","t":"Graph Neural Networks and Geometric Deep Learning","h":"Key takeaways","a":"takeaways","x":"Key takeaways - A graph model must be permutation equivariant (node outputs) or invariant (graph outputs). Message passing, with a symmetric aggregation and shared weights, guarantees this by construction. - A -layer message-passing GNN is a function of each node's -hop computation tree. Depth equals receptive-field radius. - GCN is a first-order spectral low-pass filter, (eigenvalues in thanks to the renormalization trick). GraphSAGE samples neighbours and keeps separate self weights (inductive, scalable); GAT learns neighbour weights by attention but remains a weighted mean; GIN uses sum plus an MLP and is as strong as 1-WL. - Message-passing GNNs are at most as expressive as 1-WL and cannot count triangles, tell a 6-cycle from two triangles, or decide connectivity. Sum beats mean beats max on multisets. Beyond 1-WL you pay with higher-order tensors, extra structural features, randomness or subgraph methods. - Over-smoothing (features converge as energy decays like ) and over-squashing (an exponentially growing receptive field squeezed through a bottleneck) are different problems with different cures: residual or initial connections, normalization, DropEdge, jumping knowledge for the first; rewiring, virtual nodes, global attention for the second. - Readouts must be permutation invariant: sum for extensive, mean for intensive, max for presence, attention or hierarchical pooling when structure matters. Simple readouts are strong baselines. - Node tasks (semi-supervised, homophily), link prediction (negative sampling, avoid leakage), and graph tasks (batching, readout, cross-validation) each have their own evaluation traps. - Graph Transformers need positional and structural encodings (Laplacian eigenvectors, RWSE) or attention biases to know the graph; they trade struc"},{"p":"week-14.html","w":"Week 14","t":"Graph Neural Networks and Geometric Deep Learning","h":"Further reading","a":"reading","x":"Essential - Thomas N. Kipf and Max Welling. Semi-supervised classification with graph convolutional networks. ICLR 2017. arXiv:1609.02907. The GCN paper; short, clear, and the derivation of the renormalization trick. - Michael M. Bronstein, Joan Bruna, Taco Cohen and Petar Velickovic. Geometric deep learning: grids, groups, graphs, geodesics, and gauges. 2021. arXiv:2104.13478. The blueprint and the unifying view used in the second half of this week. - Keyulu Xu, Weihua Hu, Jure Leskovec and Stefanie Jegelka. How powerful are graph neural networks? ICLR 2019. arXiv:1810.00826. GIN and the WL connection. Models and framework - Justin Gilmer, Samuel S. Schoenholz, Patrick F. Riley, Oriol Vinyals and George E. Dahl. Neural message passing for quantum chemistry. ICML 2017. arXiv:1704.01212. - William L. Hamilton, Rex Ying and Jure Leskovec. Inductive representation learning on large graphs. NeurIPS 2017. arXiv:1706.02216. GraphSAGE. - Petar Velickovic, Guillem Cucurull, Arantxa Casanova, Adriana Romero, Pietro Lio and Yoshua Bengio. Graph attention networks. ICLR 2018. arXiv:1710.10903. - Michael Schlichtkrull, Thomas N. Kipf, Peter Bloem, Rianne van den Berg, Ivan Titov and Max Welling. Modeling relational data with graph convolutional networks. ESWC 2018. arXiv:1703.06103. R-GCN. - Michael Defferrard, Xavier Bresson and Pierre Vandergheynst. Convolutional neural networks on graphs with fast localized spectral filtering. NeurIPS 2016. arXiv:1606.09375. Expressivity and depth - Christopher Morris et al. Weisfeiler and Leman go neural: higher-order graph neural networks. AAAI 2019. arXiv:1810.02244. - Qimai Li, Zhichao Han and Xiao-Ming Wu. Deeper insights into graph convolutional networks for semi-supervised learning. AAAI 2018. arXiv:1801.07606. - Kenta Oono and Taiji Suzu"},{"p":"week-15.html","w":"Week 15","t":"Deep Reinforcement Learning","h":"Deep Reinforcement Learning","a":"","x":"Learning objectives By the end of this week you should be able to: - Formalize a sequential decision problem as a Markov decision process and define return, policy, state-value and action-value functions. - Derive the Bellman expectation and optimality equations and use them for policy evaluation, policy iteration and value iteration. - Contrast Monte Carlo and temporal-difference estimates in terms of bias and variance, and explain why Q-learning is off-policy while SARSA is on-policy. - Explain the deadly triad (function approximation, bootstrapping, off-policy data) and how DQN's replay buffer and target network mitigate it. - Describe Double DQN, Dueling DQN and prioritized experience replay and the specific failure each one repairs. - Derive the policy gradient theorem, REINFORCE, the baseline trick and generalized advantage estimation (GAE), and explain the bias-variance role of . - Explain A2C/A3C, TRPO's trust region, and PPO's clipped objective, and predict how the clip changes the gradient. - Compare DDPG, TD3 and SAC for continuous control and write the maximum-entropy objective. - Summarize exploration strategies, model-based RL and world models, and offline RL with its distribution-shift problem. - Describe the RLHF pipeline (supervised fine-tuning, reward model, PPO with a KL penalty) and how DPO and GRPO relate to it. - Implement DQN from scratch in PyTorch for CartPole and train PPO with Stable-Baselines3. in the previous weeks learned from a fixed dataset of labelled examples or from raw data. Reinforcement learning (RL) changes the problem. There is no teacher that says what the right output is. An agent takes actions in a world, the world responds with a new situation and a scalar reward , and the agent must discover, by trial and error, how to act so"},{"p":"week-15.html","w":"Week 15","t":"Deep Reinforcement Learning","h":"Markov decision processes","a":"mdp","x":"The standard model of a sequential decision problem is the Markov decision process (MDP) , a tuple : - is the set of states and the set of actions (both can be finite, as in a board game, or continuous, as in joint torques). - is the transition probability : the chance that taking action in state leads to state . - (or ) is the reward function ; denotes the random reward received after the action at time . - is the discount factor , explained below. The defining assumption is the Markov property : the next state and reward depend on the history only through the current state and action, . The state is a sufficient statistic of the past. This is a statement about how you define the state: if a single Atari frame does not reveal the velocity of the ball, it is not Markov, but a stack of four frames nearly is, and that is exactly why DQN feeds four frames to its network. When the observation is not Markov, the problem is a partially observable MDP (POMDP), and the agent needs memory (recurrent networks or attention over history, as in Week 7 and Week 9). A policy is the agent's rule for acting: a distribution over actions for each state (a deterministic policy is the special case with all mass on one action, written ). Following from a start state generates a trajectory . The goal is to find the policy that maximizes the expected sum of rewards. Returns and discounting The return from time is the discounted sum of future rewards: A reward steps in the future is worth of an immediate one. Discounting does three jobs. It keeps the sum finite for infinitely long tasks when rewards are bounded ( ). It encodes a preference for sooner rewards, and it reduces variance, because distant rewards, which are the most uncertain, count for less. Its effective horizon is about steps: wit"},{"p":"week-15.html","w":"Week 15","t":"Deep Reinforcement Learning","h":"Dynamic programming: planning with a known model","a":"dp","x":"Suppose, for now, you know the MDP exactly: and are given. Then finding a good policy is a planning problem, solved by dynamic programming (DP) . DP matters for deep RL even though you rarely know the model, because model-free methods are best understood as sampled, approximate versions of DP updates. Policy evaluation. To compute iteratively, turn the Bellman expectation equation into an update rule: start from any and repeatedly set This is also a -contraction, so . Policy improvement. Given , form the greedy policy . The policy improvement theorem says that is at least as good as : because acting greedily for one step and then following is no worse than following throughout, and the argument can be repeated at every subsequent step, everywhere. If the improvement is zero the Bellman optimality equation holds and is optimal. Policy iteration alternates full evaluation and greedy improvement; in a finite MDP it terminates in a finite number of iterations at an optimal policy, typically in surprisingly few. Value iteration collapses the two steps by using the optimality equation as an update: Each sweep costs . Generalized policy iteration (GPI) is Sutton and Barto's name for the unifying picture: an evaluation process pushes the value function toward , an improvement process pushes the policy toward greedy with respect to the value function, and the two chase each other to the optimum. Almost every algorithm below fits this pattern, with the exact steps replaced by sampled, partial ones. Run it and you will see values that decay smoothly with distance from the goal, with the greedy policy routing around the pit (not along the cheapest straight line) because of the slippery dynamics: this is the Bellman equation planning for noise. Gridworld: value iteration and Q-learn"},{"p":"week-15.html","w":"Week 15","t":"Deep Reinforcement Learning","h":"Monte Carlo and temporal-difference learning","a":"mc-td","x":"Without a model, the agent has only sampled transitions. Two basic ways to turn samples into value estimates differ in what they use as the learning target. Monte Carlo (MC) prediction The definition suggests the plain statistical approach: run complete episodes, compute the realized return from each visit to , and average. In incremental form, with a step size , The target is an unbiased sample of , but it has high variance , since it sums many random rewards, actions and transitions. MC also needs episodes to finish before any update, so it cannot learn online in continuing tasks. Temporal-difference (TD) learning TD learning replaces the unknown tail of the return with the current estimate. Using and approximating gives TD(0) : The quantity is the TD error : the difference between a one-step lookahead and the current prediction. The target is biased (it relies on a possibly wrong ), but it has low variance (only one random reward and one random transition) and it can update after every single step. This is bootstrapping : updating an estimate from another estimate. Bootstrapping is the source of most of RL's efficiency and, as we will see, most of its instability. Backup diagrams. Dynamic programming averages over all actions and successor states using the model. Monte Carlo follows one sampled path to the end of the episode. TD(0) takes a single sampled step and bootstraps from the current value estimate. Method Needs model Update after Target Bias Variance --- --- --- --- --- --- Dynamic programming yes each sweep full expectation over one step + bootstraps none (exact) Monte Carlo no end of episode unbiased high TD(0) no every step bootstraps low -step TD no after steps in between in between The -step return interpolates, and the -return averages all of them with "},{"p":"week-15.html","w":"Week 15","t":"Deep Reinforcement Learning","h":"Function approximation and the deadly triad","a":"triad","x":"Tables do not scale. An Atari frame is RGB pixels; the number of distinct states is astronomically large and the agent almost never sees the same state twice. We must generalize : represent by a parametric function , such as a neural network, so that experience in one state informs predictions in similar ones. The natural idea is to treat the TD target as a label and do gradient descent on the squared error. For a transition the semi-gradient Q-learning update is It is called a semi -gradient because the target contains too, but we treat it as a constant when differentiating (in PyTorch: compute the target under torch.no grad() or call .detach() ). The true gradient of the squared Bellman error (the \"residual gradient\") also pushes the target toward the prediction, converges more reliably in theory, and in practice learns far slower and to worse solutions, so nobody uses it for deep RL. Unlike supervised learning, this can diverge. Sutton and Barto identify the deadly triad : instability and divergence become possible when three ingredients are combined. 1. Function approximation : generalization means that updating one state changes the values of many others, including the very states whose values we bootstrap from. 2. Bootstrapping : targets depend on the current estimate, so errors feed back on themselves instead of being anchored by true returns. 3. Off-policy training : the data distribution does not match the distribution of the policy being evaluated, so there is no guarantee that the states where errors grow are the states that get corrected. Any two of the three are fine. Tabular Q-learning (no approximation) converges. On-policy TD with linear approximation converges. Monte Carlo (no bootstrapping) with off-policy data is well behaved. Baird's counterexample ("},{"p":"week-15.html","w":"Week 15","t":"Deep Reinforcement Learning","h":"Deep Q-networks (DQN)","a":"dqn","x":"Before 2013 it was widely believed that combining Q-learning with a neural network was too unstable to be useful, despite isolated successes such as Tesauro's TD-Gammon (1992) in backgammon. DQN (Mnih et al., 2013 workshop paper; Nature , 2015, \"Human-level control through deep reinforcement learning\") showed that a single convolutional network with a fixed architecture and hyper-parameters could learn to play dozens of Atari 2600 games from raw pixels and the score alone, reaching human-level performance on many of them. Its network takes a stack of the last four preprocessed frames (grayscale, ) and outputs one Q-value per discrete action, so a single forward pass scores every action; this is cheaper than feeding pairs and makes the trivial. DQN architecture and training loop. The agent acts epsilon-greedily with the online network and stores transitions in a replay buffer. Each gradient step samples a random minibatch, computes targets with a periodically-updated frozen copy of the network, and regresses the online network's Q-value for the taken action toward those targets. Experience replay A raw stream of experience is a poor training set: consecutive transitions are strongly correlated (almost identical frames), and the data distribution shifts as the policy changes, so gradient descent on the most recent transitions chases its own tail and can forget earlier behaviour. Experience replay (Lin, 1992) stores the last transitions in a buffer ( in DQN) and trains on minibatches sampled uniformly at random. This (i) decorrelates the minibatch, making it closer to the i.i.d. assumption of SGD, (ii) reuses each transition many times, making learning far more sample-efficient, and (iii) averages over a long history of behaviours, smoothing the data distribution. It also "},{"p":"week-15.html","w":"Week 15","t":"Deep Reinforcement Learning","h":"Improving DQN: Double, Dueling and prioritized replay","a":"dqn-extensions","x":"DQN has well-understood weaknesses. Three of the most useful repairs each address one specific failure and are cheap to add. Double DQN: fixing overestimation The target takes a maximum over noisy estimates. Even if each estimate is unbiased, the expected maximum of noisy values is larger than the maximum of the expected values: (Jensen's inequality for the convex max function). With a network full of approximation error, Q-learning is therefore systematically optimistic, and the optimism is then bootstrapped and propagated. The same network both selects the action that looks best and evaluates it, so selection noise and evaluation noise are positively correlated: an action is picked precisely because its error happens to be positive. Double Q-learning (van Hasselt, 2010) decouples the two roles. Double DQN (van Hasselt, Guez and Silver, 2016) does it with the machinery already at hand: select the action with the online network, evaluate it with the target network: The change is one line of code, costs nothing, and gave large improvements on several Atari games where vanilla DQN's value estimates were wildly too high. It is not an unbiased estimator (it can underestimate slightly), but the bias is much smaller and less harmful. Dueling DQN: separate what the state is worth from what the action adds In many states the choice of action barely matters (the car is not near an obstacle; the pole is nearly upright), yet a standard Q-network must learn separately for every action. The dueling architecture (Wang et al., 2016) shares the convolutional or MLP trunk and then splits into two heads: a scalar value stream and an advantage stream with one output per action, recombined as The decomposition alone is not identifiable (add a constant to and subtract it from every and is u"},{"p":"week-15.html","w":"Week 15","t":"Deep Reinforcement Learning","h":"Policy-gradient methods","a":"policy-gradient","x":"Value-based methods learn and derive a policy by maximizing over actions. That has three limits. The is awkward or expensive when actions are continuous. The greedy policy is deterministic, but some problems (rock-paper-scissors, partially observed environments) need a stochastic optimal policy. And a small change in can cause an abrupt change in the greedy action, which is part of what makes the triad dangerous. Policy-gradient methods parameterize the policy directly, (a softmax over action logits for discrete actions, a Gaussian with learned mean and standard deviation for continuous ones) and ascend the gradient of expected return, The challenge: depends on through the distribution of trajectories, including the unknown environment dynamics, and sampling an action is not a differentiable operation. The policy gradient theorem shows how to get a gradient anyway, with no derivative of the dynamics needed. The policy gradient theorem Derivation: the log-derivative trick and the policy gradient Write the probability of a trajectory as . Then using the identity (the log-derivative trick , also called the score-function or likelihood-ratio estimator). Now take the log of the trajectory probability: Only the middle term depends on ; the initial-state distribution and the dynamics drop out of the gradient. Hence Causality. The action at time cannot influence rewards received before . For , , because conditioned on the history up to the reward is fixed and . Dropping these terms gives the lower-variance form, the policy gradient theorem : (up to a factor on each term which practical implementations drop; the omitted factor changes the gradient to one that is not the true gradient of the discounted objective but works well in practice). The general form is for any whose expec"},{"p":"week-15.html","w":"Week 15","t":"Deep Reinforcement Learning","h":"Actor-critic methods","a":"actor-critic","x":"REINFORCE with a baseline still uses a full Monte Carlo return as the weight, so its variance grows with the episode length and it cannot update until an episode ends. Actor-critic methods apply the TD idea from earlier to the policy-gradient weight. They keep two function approximators: an actor and a critic . Replace the Monte Carlo return by a one-step bootstrapped estimate, , the TD error. Then the actor's update is and the critic is trained by regression to the bootstrapped target, (target held constant). The critic's TD error is a sample of the advantage: when . We have traded the unbiased, high-variance MC weight for a low-variance, biased (because is imperfect) TD weight. The two networks usually share a trunk and have separate output heads, with a combined loss with typical coefficients and . The entropy bonus discourages the policy from collapsing to a deterministic one too early and is the simplest on-policy exploration device. Actor-critic data flow. The actor chooses actions; the critic scores states. The TD error (an advantage estimate) is the learning signal for the actor, and its square is the learning signal for the critic. A2C and A3C Training an actor-critic from a single stream of consecutive transitions gives strongly correlated updates; with no replay buffer (the method is on-policy, so old data is invalid) we need a different decorrelation device. A3C (Asynchronous Advantage Actor-Critic, Mnih et al., 2016) runs many CPU workers, each with its own copy of the environment and a local copy of the network. Each worker collects a short rollout (for example 5 steps), computes -step advantages, and pushes its gradients asynchronously to a shared parameter server. Because workers explore different parts of the state space at different times, the gradient"},{"p":"week-15.html","w":"Week 15","t":"Deep Reinforcement Learning","h":"TRPO and PPO: safe policy updates","a":"ppo","x":"Policy-gradient methods have a problem that supervised learning does not. A step in parameter space of fixed size can change the policy a lot or a little depending on where you are, and a bad step ruins the data : the next batch is collected by the new, worse policy, so recovery is hard and a performance collapse can be permanent. We want to take the largest step that is guaranteed to be safe , and the right notion of \"size\" is measured in policy space (how much the action distribution changes), not parameter space. The surrogate objective and the trust region The starting point is an exact identity (Kakade and Langford, 2002) relating the performance of any two policies: The new policy is better exactly when, on states visited by the new policy, its actions have positive advantage under the old policy. The trouble is the expectation over 's state distribution, which we cannot sample before we have . If we approximate the state distribution by the old one (valid when the policies are close) and fix the action distribution with importance sampling, we get the surrogate objective At the ratio is 1 and the gradient of equals the ordinary policy gradient, so is a first-order-accurate local model of the true objective. It stops being accurate once the policies drift apart, which is why we need to constrain the step. TRPO (Trust Region Policy Optimization, Schulman, Levine, Abbeel, Jordan and Moritz, 2015) maximizes the surrogate subject to a bound on the average KL divergence between the old and new policies: with . The paper derives a lower bound showing that, with a KL penalty, each such step guarantees monotonic improvement; the practical algorithm uses the constraint form with a larger step. Expanding to first order and the KL to second order around gives a quadratic-con"},{"p":"week-15.html","w":"Week 15","t":"Deep Reinforcement Learning","h":"Continuous control: DDPG, TD3 and SAC","a":"continuous","x":"Robot joints, steering angles and trading sizes are continuous. DQN's would require solving an optimization problem at every step. Policy-gradient methods handle continuous actions naturally with a Gaussian policy, but PPO is on-policy and therefore sample-hungry, a real cost when each sample is a physical robot second. The methods in this section are off-policy actor-critic algorithms: they keep a replay buffer like DQN, learn a Q-function that takes the action as an input , and train an actor to output actions that maximize it. DDPG Deep Deterministic Policy Gradient (Lillicrap et al., 2016) builds on the deterministic policy gradient theorem (Silver et al., 2014). The actor is a deterministic function and the critic is trained with a DQN-style Bellman loss, with target networks for both: The of Q-learning is replaced by evaluating the target actor's action. The actor ascends the critic: by the chain rule, In code you simply backpropagate -q(s, mu(s)).mean() into the actor's parameters, with the critic's parameters excluded from that optimizer step. Because the policy is deterministic, exploration is added externally (Gaussian or Ornstein-Uhlenbeck noise on the action), and targets are updated by Polyak averaging. DDPG is sample-efficient but notoriously brittle: it is sensitive to hyper-parameters, and the critic's overestimation errors are exploited by the actor, which learns to climb narrow peaks of the approximate Q-function that do not exist in reality. TD3 Twin Delayed DDPG (Fujimoto, van Hoof and Meger, 2018) adds three fixes that target that exact failure. 1. Clipped double-Q learning. Learn two critics and use the smaller of the two target values, . Taking the minimum counters the upward bias of a single estimate, as in the Double DQN analysis. 2. Delayed pol"},{"p":"week-15.html","w":"Week 15","t":"Deep Reinforcement Learning","h":"Exploration strategies","a":"exploration","x":"Exploration is the part of RL with no analogue in supervised learning, and the part where deep RL is still weakest. Strategies form a ladder of increasing sophistication. - Undirected noise. -greedy for discrete actions, Gaussian or Ornstein-Uhlenbeck noise for continuous ones, and the entropy bonuses of A2C, PPO and SAC. These work when rewards are dense and the task requires only local variations. They fail when reaching any reward requires a long, specific sequence of actions: a random policy has an exponentially small chance of producing it. - Optimism in the face of uncertainty. Prefer actions or states whose value is uncertain. UCB for bandits is the template; in deep RL, ensembles of Q-networks (bootstrapped DQN, Osband et al., 2016) provide uncertainty by disagreement and enable deep exploration , where one sampled network's policy is followed for a whole episode (like Thompson sampling). - Intrinsic motivation and curiosity. Add a bonus reward for novelty: count-based bonuses (extended to high dimensions with pseudo-counts from density models, Bellemare et al., 2016), or prediction-error bonuses. In the Intrinsic Curiosity Module (Pathak et al., 2017) the bonus is the error of a learnt forward model in a learnt feature space; in Random Network Distillation (Burda et al., 2019) it is the error of a predictor network trying to match a fixed random network's output on the state, which is high for states rarely visited. RND was the first method to exceed average human performance on the notoriously hard-exploration Atari game Montezuma's Revenge without demonstrations. A caveat is the noisy TV problem : a purely random source of observations never becomes predictable, and a prediction-error agent can be trapped watching it. - Goal-conditioned and structured explora"},{"p":"week-15.html","w":"Week 15","t":"Deep Reinforcement Learning","h":"Model-based RL and world models","a":"model-based","x":"Everything so far is model-free : the agent never builds an explicit predictor of what happens next, and it pays for that with sample complexity (DQN needs on the order of frames per game). Model-based RL learns a model and from experience and then uses it to improve the policy with little or no further environment interaction. Having a model offers two routes. Planning at decision time. Use the model to search for good actions from the current state. Model predictive control (MPC) samples candidate action sequences, rolls them out in the model for a short horizon, and executes the first action of the best sequence, then replans. PETS (Chua et al., 2018) pairs this with an ensemble of probabilistic neural-network dynamics models so that the planner knows where the model is uncertain. AlphaZero (Silver et al., 2017/2018) plans with Monte Carlo tree search using the known rules of Go, chess and shogi, guided by a policy-value network trained on the search's own outputs; MuZero (Schrittwieser et al., 2020) removes the need for known rules by learning a latent model that predicts only quantities useful for planning (reward, value and policy), and matched AlphaZero in board games while also working on Atari. Learning from imagined data. Use the model as a simulator to generate extra training experience for a model-free learner. Sutton's Dyna (1990) does this in the tabular case: after each real step, apply the Q-learning update to a few simulated transitions from the model as well. In the deep setting, MBPO (Janner et al., 2019) trains a SAC agent on short model rollouts branched from real states, since long rollouts compound model error. World models. Ha and Schmidhuber's World Models (2018) trained a VAE to compress frames into latent codes, an RNN to predict the next late"},{"p":"week-15.html","w":"Week 15","t":"Deep Reinforcement Learning","h":"Offline reinforcement learning","a":"offline","x":"Offline (batch) RL learns a policy from a fixed dataset of transitions collected by some other behaviour policy, with no environment interaction during training. It matters when exploration is expensive or dangerous (healthcare, autonomous driving, industrial control), and it promises to bring to RL the data-scaling recipe that works in supervised learning. It is also much harder than it looks. The core problem is distribution shift (Levine, Kumar, Tucker and Fu, 2020, tutorial). An off-policy algorithm such as DQN or SAC queries at actions chosen by the learnt policy, including actions that never appear in the dataset. There the Q-function has no data to constrain it, and function approximators extrapolate arbitrarily; the or the actor then seeks out precisely the actions with the highest erroneous values. In online RL the agent tries the action and finds out it was bad. Offline, no correction ever arrives and errors compound through bootstrapping. Naively running DQN or SAC on a fixed dataset typically fails even when the dataset contains expert data. Offline methods therefore keep the learnt policy close to the data , or keep values pessimistic away from it: - Policy constraints (BCQ, Fujimoto et al., 2019; BEAR, Kumar et al., 2019; TD3+BC, Fujimoto and Gu, 2021): restrict or regularize the policy toward the behaviour policy. TD3+BC literally adds a behaviour-cloning term to the TD3 actor loss. - Conservative value learning (CQL, Kumar et al., 2020): add a penalty that pushes down Q-values on out-of-distribution actions while keeping them up on dataset actions, so the learnt Q lower-bounds the true value. - In-sample methods (IQL, Kostrikov et al., 2022): learn values using only actions that appear in the dataset, via expectile regression, and extract a policy by adv"},{"p":"week-15.html","w":"Week 15","t":"Deep Reinforcement Learning","h":"Reinforcement learning and language models","a":"llm-rl","x":"The most consequential application of deep RL today is not a game or a robot but post-training of large language models (Week 10). A pretrained LM is trained to imitate text, which is not the same as being helpful, honest or harmless, or being correct. Reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF) steers the model with a learnt reward signal instead. Casting text generation as an MDP Treat the LM as the policy . The state is the prompt plus the tokens generated so far, the action is the next token (a discrete action space of 30k to 200k tokens), and the transition is deterministic (append the token). An episode is one full response ending at an end-of-sequence token. Reward is typically given only at the end of the response (a sparse, delayed reward, so credit assignment across hundreds of tokens is the main difficulty) from a reward model , though some implementations add a per-token KL penalty. The RLHF pipeline (as in InstructGPT, Ouyang et al., 2022). Stage 1: supervised fine-tuning (SFT) on demonstrations. Stage 2: train a reward model on human preference comparisons. Stage 3: optimize the policy against the reward model with PPO, with a KL penalty to the SFT model. Stage 1: supervised fine-tuning Fine-tune the pretrained model by ordinary cross-entropy on a set of high-quality prompt-response demonstrations written by humans. This gives the RL stage a sensible starting policy (so the early samples are not gibberish) and is the \"demonstrations for exploration\" idea above. Call the result (the reference or SFT policy). Stage 2: the reward model Writing a numeric reward function for \"a good answer\" is impossible, but people can compare two answers reliably. Collect prompts with pairs of responses , where was preferred. Train a reward model (usually the same LM wi"},{"p":"week-15.html","w":"Week 15","t":"Deep Reinforcement Learning","h":"Lab: DQN on CartPole, then PPO with Stable-Baselines3","a":"lab","x":"In this lab you build a complete DQN from scratch in PyTorch (about 100 lines), train it on Gymnasium's CartPole-v1 , then solve the same task with PPO from the Stable-Baselines3 library and compare. In CartPole the state is four numbers (cart position, cart velocity, pole angle, pole angular velocity), there are two actions (push left or right), the reward is +1 per step, and an episode ends when the pole tilts more than about 12 degrees, the cart leaves the track, or after 500 steps. Return 500 is a perfect score. Install with pip install torch gymnasium stable-baselines3 (Colab has most of this). Step 1: the replay buffer and Q-network Step 2: the training loop Note the two details that most often go wrong: the done flag used for bootstrapping must be terminated (a true terminal state), not truncated (time limit), and targets are computed without gradient. On a typical run, the mean return climbs from about 20 (random behaviour) to several hundred within 20,000 to 30,000 steps, though DQN on CartPole is noisy and often oscillates (it may reach 500, collapse, and recover). Different seeds will differ a lot, so run at least three and plot the mean. This variability is a typical feature of deep RL, not a bug in your code. Step 3: evaluate the greedy policy and inspect Q-values The last lines show a useful debugging habit: compute the maximum possible value analytically and check that your learnt Q-values respect it. If they greatly exceed it, you have an overestimation or target bug. Step 4: PPO with Stable-Baselines3 With verbose=1 , SB3 prints a table at every update including approx kl , clip fraction , entropy loss , explained variance and value loss . Learn to read these: explained variance near 1 means the critic predicts returns well, clip fraction shows what sha"},{"p":"week-15.html","w":"Week 15","t":"Deep Reinforcement Learning","h":"Common mistakes and debugging tips","a":"mistakes","x":"Deep RL bugs are especially hard because the failure symptom (\"the return does not go up\") fits dozens of causes, and the training signal is noisy. Check cheap, deterministic things first. Symptom Likely cause Fix --- --- --- Q-values explode or grow without bound Missing target network, or targets not detached; learning rate too high; no reward scaling Use torch.no grad() for targets, soft target updates, Huber loss, gradient clipping, smaller LR Q-values far above the maximum possible return Overestimation (max over noisy values), or bootstrapping through terminal states Double DQN or twin critics; multiply bootstrap by (1 - terminated) Agent learns nothing; return flat at the random level Exploration too weak, reward never seen, or learning never started ( learning starts larger than steps) Check the minimum number of steps to reach a reward, raise entropy bonus or epsilon, verify the buffer is filling Return rises then collapses (policy gradient) Updates too large, no advantage normalization, learning rate too high Smaller LR, lower clip range, fewer epochs, watch approx kl ; early-stop an update when KL is large Value loss large and critic fails to fit Returns on very different scales Normalize observations and rewards (running statistics), use value clipping or a Huber loss Time-limit episodes treated as failures Using done = terminated or truncated in the bootstrap Bootstrap through truncation: use only terminated for the (1 - done) mask Policy collapses to deterministic too early Entropy too low, or log std initialized small Entropy bonus, larger initial std, SAC auto-temperature Works for one seed, not for the next High variance of RL Run 5 to 10 seeds, report mean and a confidence interval, never a best-of-seeds curve Off-by-one in returns or GAE Ordering erro"},{"p":"week-15.html","w":"Week 15","t":"Deep Reinforcement Learning","h":"Exercises","a":"exercises","x":"Exercise 1: Bellman by hand A deterministic chain has states under the only available policy, with rewards for leaving and for leaving . With , compute and (a) from the definition of the return, and (b) by solving the Bellman expectation equations. Then state what changes at and why this is still well defined. Solution (a) From : , so . From : , so . (b) The equations are (terminal value is zero) and , which give the same numbers. At : , ; the sum is finite because the episode has finite length, so the undiscounted return is well defined. Exercise 2: Q-learning versus SARSA update In state the current estimates are and . You have , observe , move to , and the -greedy behaviour policy happens to choose the exploratory action there. With and , compute the updated under SARSA and under Q-learning. Which one would be unchanged if the behaviour policy had picked ? Solution Q-learning target: , so . SARSA target uses the action actually chosen, : , so . If had been picked, both targets equal and the two updates agree (0.73). Q-learning's target never depends on the exploratory choice, which is exactly what \"off-policy\" means here. Exercise 3: Reading the PPO clip Let . For and for , evaluate and say whether the gradient with respect to is nonzero, for . Which cases correspond to \"the update made the action's probability change in the wrong direction\", and what does PPO do in them? Solution : : , gradient (unclipped branch is smaller). : , gradient . : , gradient (clipped, no further incentive). : : , gradient (clipped, already decreased enough). : , gradient . : , gradient (unclipped, strongly pushes back). Wrong-direction cases are (a good action became less likely) and (a bad action became more likely). In both the selects the unclipped term, so the full gradient remains an"},{"p":"week-15.html","w":"Week 15","t":"Deep Reinforcement Learning","h":"Key takeaways","a":"takeaways","x":"Key takeaways - An MDP, with the Markov property, a policy, a discount, and value functions , is the common language. The Bellman equations express a value as immediate reward plus discounted successor value, and the optimality equations replace the policy average by a max. - Dynamic programming needs a model; Monte Carlo is unbiased but high-variance; TD bootstraps for low variance at the price of bias. Q-learning is off-policy (learns ), SARSA is on-policy. - Function approximation, bootstrapping and off-policy data together (the deadly triad) can diverge. DQN's replay buffer, target network and Huber loss are practical antidotes; Double DQN fixes max-overestimation, Dueling separates and , prioritized replay samples by TD error. - The policy gradient theorem needs no model; baselines (best: ) cut variance without bias; actor-critic bootstraps; GAE trades bias for variance. - PPO's clipped objective approximates TRPO's trust region cheaply and is the default on-policy algorithm; watch KL and clip fraction. SAC and TD3 are the standard off-policy methods for continuous control, SAC adding a maximum-entropy objective. - Exploration is the hard part: entropy and for easy tasks, curiosity or count bonuses for sparse ones. Model-based RL buys sample efficiency at the price of model error; offline RL must contend with distribution shift via conservatism or in-sample learning. - RLHF = SFT, then a Bradley-Terry reward model, then PPO with a KL anchor to the reference policy. DPO optimizes the same KL-regularized objective with a supervised loss on preference pairs; GRPO drops the critic in favour of group-relative baselines. - RL results are noisy: use several seeds, log diagnostics, and test on trivial environments first."},{"p":"week-15.html","w":"Week 15","t":"Deep Reinforcement Learning","h":"Further reading","a":"reading","x":"Essential papers and books are marked with a star. - (Essential) Sutton and Barto, Reinforcement Learning: An Introduction , 2nd edition, MIT Press, 2018 (free online from the authors). Chapters 3, 6 and 13 correspond to this week. - (Essential) Mnih et al., \"Human-level control through deep reinforcement learning,\" Nature , 2015 (DQN; earlier version: \"Playing Atari with Deep Reinforcement Learning,\" arXiv:1312.5602, 2013). - (Essential) Schulman, Wolski, Dhariwal, Radford and Klimov, \"Proximal Policy Optimization Algorithms,\" arXiv:1707.06347, 2017. - (Essential) Haarnoja, Zhou, Abbeel and Levine, \"Soft Actor-Critic: Off-Policy Maximum Entropy Deep Reinforcement Learning with a Stochastic Actor,\" ICML 2018 (arXiv:1801.01290). - Williams, \"Simple statistical gradient-following algorithms for connectionist reinforcement learning,\" Machine Learning , 1992 (REINFORCE). - van Hasselt, Guez and Silver, \"Deep Reinforcement Learning with Double Q-learning,\" AAAI 2016; Wang et al., \"Dueling Network Architectures for Deep Reinforcement Learning,\" ICML 2016; Schaul et al., \"Prioritized Experience Replay,\" ICLR 2016; Hessel et al., \"Rainbow: Combining Improvements in Deep Reinforcement Learning,\" AAAI 2018. - Mnih et al., \"Asynchronous Methods for Deep Reinforcement Learning,\" ICML 2016 (A3C). - Schulman, Levine, Abbeel, Jordan and Moritz, \"Trust Region Policy Optimization,\" ICML 2015; Schulman, Moritz, Levine, Jordan and Abbeel, \"High-Dimensional Continuous Control Using Generalized Advantage Estimation,\" ICLR 2016. - Lillicrap et al., \"Continuous control with deep reinforcement learning,\" ICLR 2016 (DDPG); Fujimoto, van Hoof and Meger, \"Addressing Function Approximation Error in Actor-Critic Methods,\" ICML 2018 (TD3). - Engstrom et al., \"Implementation Matters in Deep Policy Gr"},{"p":"week-16.html","w":"Week 16","t":"Efficient and Scalable Deep Learning","h":"Efficient and Scalable Deep Learning","a":"","x":"Learning objectives By the end of this week you should be able to: - Describe the GPU memory hierarchy and explain why most deep-learning operations are limited by memory traffic rather than arithmetic. - Compute the arithmetic intensity of an operation, place it on a roofline, and decide whether it is memory-bound or compute-bound. - Profile a PyTorch training loop with torch.profiler , read the result, and separate data-loading, launch-overhead, memory-bound and compute-bound bottlenecks. - Explain FP16, BF16 and TF32, why FP16 needs loss scaling and BF16 usually does not, and train with torch.autocast . - Use gradient accumulation to emulate large batches, and gradient checkpointing to trade compute for memory, deriving the optimum. - Account for training memory (parameters, gradients, optimizer states, activations) and predict what ZeRO stages 1 to 3 and FSDP save. - Explain data, tensor, pipeline and hybrid parallelism, ring all-reduce cost, and the pipeline bubble fraction. - Derive online softmax and the tiled FlashAttention algorithm, and explain why it is faster although it does not reduce FLOPs. - Explain KV caching, its memory cost, and the idea behind speculative decoding. - Apply and analyze pruning, post-training and quantization-aware quantization, distillation with the Hinton loss, and low-rank factorization. - Describe sparse Mixture-of-Experts routing and the load-balancing loss, and give an overview of neural architecture search. - Export and accelerate a model with torch.compile , TorchScript and ONNX, and reason about serving and edge constraints. the past fifteen weeks the question was \"what model and what loss\". This week the question is \"what does it cost, and how do I make it cheaper without making it worse\". The question has become unavoidable."},{"p":"week-16.html","w":"Week 16","t":"Efficient and Scalable Deep Learning","h":"Hardware: how a GPU actually works","a":"hardware","x":"Throughput processors and the memory hierarchy A CPU is built to run one thread of irregular code quickly: big caches, branch prediction, out-of-order execution. A GPU is built to run thousands of nearly identical threads at once and to hide the latency of memory behind sheer parallelism. An NVIDIA A100, a typical 2020-era training GPU, has 108 streaming multiprocessors (SMs) . Each SM contains 64 FP32 cores, four tensor cores (units that multiply small matrix tiles, for example , in a single instruction) and a register file. Threads are grouped into warps of 32 that execute the same instruction in lockstep, and warps are grouped into thread blocks that are scheduled onto SMs. The tensor cores are where deep learning lives: on the A100 they deliver 312 TFLOP/s of dense BF16 or FP16 arithmetic, against about 19.5 TFLOP/s for ordinary FP32 cores. That 16-fold gap is a major reason mixed precision matters. Arithmetic is only half the story. Data must reach the cores, and the memory system is a hierarchy: The memory hierarchy of an A100-class GPU. Each level down is roughly an order of magnitude larger and slower. Figures are rounded vendor-spec values (A100 80 GB: about 2.0 TB/s HBM; the 19 TB/s SRAM figure is the aggregate on-chip bandwidth quoted in the FlashAttention paper). - Registers are private to each thread and effectively free to access, but scarce (256 KB per SM). - Shared memory / L1 is a programmer-managed scratchpad on each SM, up to 164 KB, with bandwidth an order of magnitude above HBM. Fast kernels load a tile of data here once and reuse it many times. We call this level SRAM below. - L2 cache (40 MB on A100) is shared across SMs. - HBM (high-bandwidth memory) , also called global or device memory, is the 40 or 80 GB where your tensors live. Its bandwidth "},{"p":"week-16.html","w":"Week 16","t":"Efficient and Scalable Deep Learning","h":"Arithmetic intensity and the roofline model","a":"roofline","x":"Two ways to be slow Any kernel needs to do floating-point operations and to move bytes between main memory and the chip. If the device can perform FLOP/s and move bytes/s, the kernel cannot finish faster than the larger of the two times: Dividing by and defining the arithmetic intensity (FLOP per byte moved), the best achievable throughput is Plotted against on log-log axes this is the roofline model (Williams, Waterman and Patterson, 2009): a sloped line of slope (the \"memory roof\") that meets a flat line at height (the \"compute roof\") at the ridge point . An operation left of the ridge is memory-bound : speeding up the arithmetic does nothing, only moving fewer bytes helps. An operation right of the ridge is compute-bound : only faster math (tensor cores, lower precision) helps. The roofline model. Throughput is limited by memory bandwidth on the sloped part and by peak compute on the flat part. The ridge point is the intensity an operation needs before the arithmetic units, not memory, become the bottleneck. For the A100 with BF16 tensor cores, TFLOP/s and TB/s, so FLOP/byte. For the H100 SXM, TFLOP/s (dense BF16) and TB/s, so . The ridge has moved right with each generation, because compute grows faster than bandwidth: more and more operations fall on the memory-bound side. Intensity of the operations you use Arithmetic intensity of a matrix multiply Multiply by into , with bytes per element. The work is (one multiply and one add per term). The minimum traffic reads and and writes : . So Square case. With : . In FP16 ( ) this is . To pass the A100 ridge of 153 you need . A FP16 matmul has and runs at only TFLOP/s at best, while a one is compute-bound. Matrix-vector case ( , the situation when generating one token at a time). The term (the weight matrix) dominates th"},{"p":"week-16.html","w":"Week 16","t":"Efficient and Scalable Deep Learning","h":"Profiling and bottleneck analysis","a":"profiling","x":"\"Premature optimization is the root of all evil\" applies doubly to deep learning, where intuition about performance is usually wrong. Measure first. The workflow is: (1) get an end-to-end number (samples per second, step time, GPU utilization); (2) find where the time goes; (3) classify the bottleneck; (4) fix the biggest one; (5) re-measure. Measuring correctly GPU kernels run asynchronously : when Python calls y = model(x) , PyTorch enqueues kernels and returns immediately. If you time with time.time() you measure how long it took to enqueue . You must call torch.cuda.synchronize() before reading the clock (or use CUDA events). You should also warm up (the first iterations include CUDA context creation, cuDNN autotuning and memory allocation), run many iterations, and report a median rather than a single value. Run this on a GPU and you will see achieved TFLOP/s rise steeply with and saturate, exactly the roofline's prediction. (On an A100 the case typically reaches only a fraction of the compute roof because it is both memory-bound and too small to occupy 108 SMs.) The PyTorch profiler torch.profiler records every CPU-side operator and CUDA kernel with timestamps. The usual recipe wraps a few training steps with a schedule that skips start-up noise: Open the trace with TensorBoard ( pip install torch tb profiler , tensorboard --logdir prof ) or load the generated JSON in chrome://tracing / Perfetto. In the timeline, look at the GPU stream row. Gaps between kernels mean the GPU is idle waiting for the CPU. In the operator table, sort by total CUDA time and check the top five rows. (Recent PyTorch versions are renaming cuda time total to device time total ; if the key is rejected, pick the nearest column name in the printed table header.) Diagnosing the bottleneck A GP"},{"p":"week-16.html","w":"Week 16","t":"Efficient and Scalable Deep Learning","h":"Mixed-precision training","a":"mixed-precision","x":"Number formats Floating-point numbers store a sign, an exponent (which sets the range ) and a mantissa (which sets the precision ). The formats you will meet: Format Bits Exponent Mantissa Max finite Smallest normal Unit roundoff --- --- --- --- --- --- --- FP32 32 8 23 TF32 (tensor-core math, stored as FP32) 19 used 8 10 FP16 16 5 10 65,504 (subnormals down to ) BF16 16 8 7 Unit roundoff is the worst-case relative rounding error of one operation, where is the number of mantissa bits. FP16 has three more bits of precision than BF16 (about three decimal digits versus two) but only a 5-bit exponent, so its dynamic range is narrow: values above 65,504 overflow to infinity and values below roughly flush to zero. BF16 trades precision for range: it can represent anything FP32 can, in coarser steps. TF32 is an A100 feature: matmuls take FP32 inputs, round them to 10 mantissa bits internally and accumulate in FP32; you enable it with torch.backends.cuda.matmul.allow tf32 = True (or torch.set float32 matmul precision(\"high\") ) and need no other code change. FP8 formats (E4M3 and E5M2) on H100-class hardware extend the same story one step further. The recipe of Micicevicius et al. Simply casting a network to FP16 fails for two reasons. The update problem : weights are updated by small increments , and with a 10-bit mantissa an increment smaller than about is rounded away entirely. A weight of 1.0 cannot change by less than 0.0005. The range problem : gradients are often tiny (activation gradients of are common), below FP16's representable range, so they underflow to zero and learning silently stalls. The recipe in Mixed Precision Training (Micicevicius et al., ICLR 2018) has three parts: 1. FP32 master weights. Keep an FP32 copy of the parameters. The forward and backward passes"},{"p":"week-16.html","w":"Week 16","t":"Efficient and Scalable Deep Learning","h":"Gradient accumulation","a":"accumulation","x":"Memory limits the micro-batch size, but optimization often wants a larger batch (large-batch training stabilizes gradient estimates, and some recipes such as LLM pretraining prescribe hundreds of thousands of tokens per step). Gradient accumulation simulates a large batch by summing gradients over several micro-batches before one optimizer step. The justification is the linearity of the gradient. For a loss that is a mean over examples, split into micro-batches of size : PyTorch's backward() adds into .grad , so calling it times and dividing each loss by yields exactly the large-batch gradient (up to floating-point reordering). The weights are not changed in between, so the result is mathematically identical to one big batch for models whose forward pass is independent across examples. Three caveats. (1) Batch normalization computes statistics per micro-batch, so accumulation changes the model's behavior (use GroupNorm/LayerNorm, or accept the difference). (2) Per-example weighting : if the loss is a sum over tokens and micro-batches contain different numbers of non-padding tokens, dividing by is wrong; normalize by the total token count of the full batch. (3) Distributed training : with DDP every backward() triggers an all-reduce, which is wasteful for the first micro-batches; wrap them in with model.no sync(): and synchronize only on the last. The learning rate should be tuned for the effective batch size ; the linear scaling rule (Goyal et al., 2017) with a warm-up is the usual starting point (Week 3)."},{"p":"week-16.html","w":"Week 16","t":"Efficient and Scalable Deep Learning","h":"Gradient checkpointing","a":"checkpointing","x":"Where training memory goes Backpropagation needs the intermediate activations of the forward pass, so they are kept until the backward pass reaches them. For a network of layers with activations of size each, activation memory is , which grows with batch size, sequence length and depth. Parameters, gradients and optimizer states do not depend on batch size; activations do, and for transformers with long sequences they dominate. Gradient checkpointing (also called activation recomputation or rematerialization; Chen, Xu, Zhang and Guestrin, 2016) stores only a subset of the activations and recomputes the others during the backward pass. Gradient checkpointing with layers split into segments. Only the segment boundaries (green) are kept after the forward pass. During backward, each segment is re-run once to regenerate its internal activations (faded), which are used and then freed. Deriving the square-root trade-off Memory and compute of checkpointing a chain Consider a chain of layers, each with the same activation size (one unit) and the same forward cost . Split it into equal segments of layers. In the forward pass we keep only the segment inputs (the checkpoints) and discard everything else. During backward, we process segments from last to first. For each segment, we (1) re-run its forward pass from the checkpoint, storing its internal activations, (2) backpropagate through it, (3) free those activations. Memory. At peak we hold all checkpoints plus the activations of one segment: Optimum. Differentiating, gives and . For layers that is 16 units instead of 64. For , about 63 instead of 1000. Compute. Every layer's forward pass is executed twice (once originally and once during recomputation), except layers in the last segment whose activations could be kept. The extra"},{"p":"week-16.html","w":"Week 16","t":"Efficient and Scalable Deep Learning","h":"Distributed training","a":"distributed","x":"When a model or the desired throughput exceeds one device, we spread the work. There are four basic axes of parallelism, and large runs combine them. Strategy What is split What is replicated Communication Main use --- --- --- --- --- Data parallel (DP/DDP) the batch the whole model all-reduce of gradients, once per step scale throughput when the model fits one GPU Sharded data parallel (ZeRO, FSDP) the batch, plus optimizer states, gradients, parameters nothing (at stage 3) reduce-scatter and all-gather model too big to replicate, no model code changes Tensor (intra-layer) parallel each weight matrix activations (partly) all-reduce inside every layer very large layers, needs fast NVLink Pipeline parallel groups of layers (stages) nothing point-to-point activations between stages very deep models across nodes Data parallelism and all-reduce In data parallelism every one of workers holds a full copy of the model and processes a different slice of the batch. Each computes a local gradient ; the workers then compute the average (an all-reduce ) and each applies the same optimizer update. Because all workers start from the same weights, use the same average gradient, and run deterministic optimizers, the replicas stay identical forever. The result equals single-device training on the combined batch, which makes data parallelism the safest way to scale. Left: one step of data-parallel training. Right: the ring all-reduce topology. The gradient is split into chunks; each GPU sends one chunk to its neighbour in reduce-scatter steps, then all-gather steps. Cost of ring all-reduce Let the gradient have bytes and let each GPU have a link of bandwidth to its ring neighbours. Split the gradient into chunks of bytes. Reduce-scatter: in each of steps every GPU sends one chunk to its "},{"p":"week-16.html","w":"Week 16","t":"Efficient and Scalable Deep Learning","h":"ZeRO and FSDP: sharding the redundancy","a":"zero","x":"Memory accounting for mixed-precision Adam In plain data parallelism every GPU stores the full model state, which is wasteful. Consider a model with parameters trained with Adam and mixed precision. Per parameter you store: - FP16/BF16 parameters: 2 bytes - FP16/BF16 gradients: 2 bytes - Optimizer states in FP32: a master copy of the parameters (4 bytes), Adam's first moment (4) and second moment (4): 12 bytes, called in the ZeRO paper. Total bytes, before activations. For B that is 120 GB . The optimizer states alone are 75% of it. This is a lot of memory that is identical on every GPU. ZeRO (Zero Redundancy Optimizer; Rajbhandari, Rasley, Ruwase and He, SC 2020) removes the redundancy by partitioning model states over the data-parallel ranks, in three cumulative stages: - Stage 1 ( ): shard the optimizer states. Each rank owns of them and updates the corresponding of the parameters, then an all-gather distributes the updated parameters. Memory: . - Stage 2 ( ): also shard the gradients. Gradients are reduce-scattered so that each rank receives only the (averaged) shard it owns. Memory: . - Stage 3 ( ): also shard the parameters. Each rank stores only of the parameters. Before a layer runs, its full parameters are all-gathered ; after use they are freed. Memory: . Per-GPU model-state memory under DDP and ZeRO stages 1 to 3 for mixed-precision Adam ( ). Each stage removes one more replicated component. Communication cost Baseline DDP all-reduces gradients: elements per step in ring terms (reduce-scatter + all-gather ). ZeRO-1 and ZeRO-2 use a reduce-scatter of gradients ( ) and an all-gather of updated parameters ( ): the same as DDP, so memory savings are free in volume. ZeRO-3 additionally all-gathers parameters in the forward pass ( ) and again in the backward pass ("},{"p":"week-16.html","w":"Week 16","t":"Efficient and Scalable Deep Learning","h":"Efficient attention: FlashAttention","a":"flashattention","x":"Why standard attention is slow Recall single-head attention for a sequence of length with head dimension (Week 9): , row-wise, . The textbook implementation runs three kernels. It computes and writes it to HBM ( numbers), reads it back to compute softmax and writes ( more), and reads again for the product with . At , one head's score matrix has 67 million entries, 134 MB in FP16, for a computation whose inputs are only M numbers. The matmuls have high intensity, but softmax and the surrounding data movement are memory-bound and the intermediate dominates both time and memory. The key observation of FlashAttention (Dao, Fu, Ermon, Rudra and Ré, NeurIPS 2022) is that attention is IO-bound , not FLOP-bound, and that the exact same result can be computed tile by tile in SRAM without ever materializing or in HBM. The obstacle is softmax, which needs the whole row to normalize. The solution is the online softmax . Online softmax Online (streaming) softmax For a row of scores the numerically stable softmax is with and . Naively this needs one pass for , one for , one to normalize. Milakov and Gimelshein (2018) observed that and can be maintained in a single streaming pass . Suppose after seeing the first scores we have and . A new score arrives. Set . The old sum was computed relative to , so rescale it to the new reference: Check: , so . The same rescaling works when scores arrive in blocks. Including the value-weighted sum. What attention actually needs is . Define the unnormalized accumulator . Exactly as for , and at the end . So a single pass over keys and values, carrying only the running triple per query row, gives the exact attention output. The tiled algorithm FlashAttention applies this recursion to blocks. Partition into row blocks (of rows) and into blocks (of rows"},{"p":"week-16.html","w":"Week 16","t":"Efficient and Scalable Deep Learning","h":"KV caching and speculative decoding","a":"kv-cache","x":"Autoregressive decoding and the KV cache A decoder-only language model generates one token at a time. Computing the attention output for token requires the keys and values of all tokens . Recomputing them at every step would cost projections per token, overall. Since keys and values of earlier tokens never change (causal masking), we cache them. At each step we compute only the query, key and value of the newest token, append to the cache, and attend over the cache. Per-token compute becomes for the attention itself and for everything else. The cache costs memory. For one token, each layer stores a key and a value for each KV head: For a Llama-2-7B-like configuration (32 layers, 32 heads of dimension 128, FP16, ) that is bytes, 0.5 MB per token . A 4096-token context costs 2 GiB per sequence, and 32 concurrent requests cost 64 GiB, more than the 13 GB of FP16 weights. At long context and large batch the KV cache, not the weights, is the memory bottleneck, and reading it every step makes decoding memory-bound. The main remedies reduce the number of cached values or manage them better. Multi-query attention (Shazeer, 2019) shares one key/value head across all query heads; grouped-query attention (Ainslie et al., 2023) shares a KV head among a group of query heads, a middle ground adopted by many open models. PagedAttention (Kwon et al., SOSP 2023; the basis of the vLLM server) stores the cache in fixed-size blocks like virtual-memory pages, removing the fragmentation that comes from reserving a maximum-length buffer per request. Cache quantization (int8/int4 keys and values) and sliding-window attention reduce it further. Speculative decoding Decoding is memory-bound: producing one token reads all the weights (13 GB for a 7B model in FP16) and uses almost none of the GPU'"},{"p":"week-16.html","w":"Week 16","t":"Efficient and Scalable Deep Learning","h":"Model compression","a":"compression","x":"Compression reduces the cost of a trained model: fewer parameters, fewer bits per parameter, or a cheaper architecture with similar behavior. The four classical families are pruning, quantization, distillation and low-rank factorization. They are largely orthogonal and often stacked (Han, Mao and Dally's Deep Compression , ICLR 2016, combined pruning, quantization and Huffman coding to shrink AlexNet 35x and VGG-16 49x without accuracy loss on ImageNet). A key practical lesson, which applies to everything below, is that a reduction in parameter count or theoretical FLOPs is not a reduction in latency unless the hardware and kernels can exploit it. Pruning Pruning removes weights (or larger structures) that contribute little. The earliest work (LeCun, Denker and Solla's Optimal Brain Damage, 1989) estimated the loss increase from deleting each weight with a second-order Taylor expansion. The simplest modern criterion is magnitude pruning : remove the weights with the smallest , on the assumption that small weights matter least. Han et al. (2015) showed that the loop train, prune, fine-tune, repeat (\"iterative pruning\") can remove 80 to 90% of the weights of many convolutional networks with little loss. - Unstructured pruning zeroes individual weights anywhere. It reaches the highest sparsity for a given accuracy, but the result is an irregular sparse matrix, and dense GPU kernels gain nothing from it: a matrix with 90% zeros takes the same time as a dense one unless special sparse kernels are used (which usually pay off only above ~90% sparsity). NVIDIA's Ampere GPUs support a hardware-accelerated 2:4 structured sparsity pattern (exactly two non-zeros in every four consecutive weights), which can give up to about 2x matmul speed-up at 50% sparsity. - Structured pruning r"},{"p":"week-16.html","w":"Week 16","t":"Efficient and Scalable Deep Learning","h":"Mixture-of-Experts","a":"moe","x":"The scaling laws of Week 10 say that capacity (parameters) and compute are tied together in a dense model: doubling the parameters doubles the FLOPs per token. Sparse Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) breaks this link. Replace the transformer's feed-forward block with parallel feed-forward networks (the experts ) and a small router that sends each token to only of them (typically or 2). Parameters grow -fold, but FLOPs per token grow only with . The idea of gating among experts goes back to Jacobs, Jordan, Nowlan and Hinton (1991); Shazeer et al. (2017) made it work at scale with a sparsely-gated layer in LSTMs, and GShard (Lepikhin et al., 2020), the Switch Transformer (Fedus, Zoph and Shazeer, 2021/JMLR 2022) and Mixtral 8x7B (Jiang et al., 2024) established it for transformers. A sparse MoE layer. The router scores each token against experts, keeps the top , and combines their outputs with the renormalized gate weights. Experts not selected cost no FLOPs for this token. Routing For a token representation the router has a weight matrix and computes probabilities . The top- indices are selected, the gates are renormalized over them (in some variants not), and the layer output is Top-1 routing (Switch) is simplest and cheapest; top-2 gives smoother gradients. Experts are spread over devices ( expert parallelism ): tokens are shuffled to the device holding their expert with an all-to-all communication, processed, and shuffled back. Because the number of tokens per expert varies with the routing decisions, implementations set a capacity per expert, ; tokens beyond capacity are dropped (they pass through the residual connection unchanged) or rerouted. Load balancing Left alone, routers collapse: a few experts get better, receive more tokens, get better still, and the rest are never"},{"p":"week-16.html","w":"Week 16","t":"Efficient and Scalable Deep Learning","h":"Neural architecture search (overview)","a":"nas","x":"Neural architecture search (NAS) automates the design of the network. A NAS method has three components: a search space (the set of candidate architectures, such as cell structures built from operations like 3x3 convolution, depthwise convolution, skip connection or pooling), a search strategy (how to explore it) and a performance estimation strategy (how to score a candidate cheaply). - Reinforcement learning and evolution. Zoph and Le (ICLR 2017) used an RNN controller trained with REINFORCE to emit architectures, rewarded by validation accuracy; it needed hundreds of GPUs for days, but found cells that transferred to larger datasets (NASNet). Evolutionary approaches (AmoebaNet; Real et al., 2019) mutate and select architectures. - Differentiable NAS. DARTS (Liu, Simonyan and Yang, ICLR 2019) relaxes the discrete choice of operation into a softmax-weighted mixture, , and trains architecture parameters by gradient descent alongside the weights, cutting the cost to a few GPU-days. It is known to be unstable (collapse toward parameter-free operations such as skip connections). - Weight sharing and one-shot methods. ENAS (Pham et al., 2018) and Once-for-All (Cai et al., ICLR 2020) train one large \"supernet\" whose sub-networks share weights, then pull out specialized sub-networks for different latency targets without retraining. - Hardware-aware NAS puts measured latency or energy on the target device into the objective (MnasNet, Tan et al., 2019; MobileNetV3; EfficientNet's compound scaling, Week 6). NAS has produced strong, efficient vision models, but its limits are worth knowing. Random search in a well-designed search space is often a surprisingly strong baseline, results are sensitive to training-pipeline details, and for large language models the field has converged"},{"p":"week-16.html","w":"Week 16","t":"Efficient and Scalable Deep Learning","h":"Deployment: compilers, export formats and serving","a":"deployment","x":"torch.compile: capture, fuse, generate PyTorch's default eager mode runs one operator at a time, which is flexible but launches many small kernels and cannot fuse across operators. torch.compile (PyTorch 2.x) traces your Python code into a graph with TorchDynamo (a bytecode-level tracer that falls back to eager on code it cannot capture, inserting \"graph breaks\"), lowers it with TorchInductor , which fuses memory-bound operators and generates Triton kernels on GPUs (C++/OpenMP on CPUs), and caches the result. A single line enables it: Gains typically range from 1.2x to 2x for transformer blocks, more for models dominated by elementwise operations. Pitfalls: the first call is slow; changing input shapes triggers recompilation (mark dynamic dimensions or pad to buckets); data-dependent control flow and .item() calls cause graph breaks; and for training, compile the model before wrapping it in DDP/FSDP only if your version documents that order (follow the current docs). For small models with tiny kernels, mode=\"reduce-overhead\" uses CUDA graphs to replay a whole sequence of kernels with one launch, removing the CPU launch overhead that dominates there. TorchScript, torch.export and ONNX To run a model without a Python interpreter (in a C++ server, on a phone, in a browser) you need a serialized, framework-neutral representation. - TorchScript ( torch.jit.trace and torch.jit.script ) was PyTorch's first answer. Tracing runs the model once on an example input and records the operators executed; it is exact for straight-line code but silently bakes in the path taken by Python control flow. Scripting compiles the Python source to a restricted IR and handles loops and branches, but supports only a subset of Python. TorchScript is in maintenance mode; new work goes through torch"},{"p":"week-16.html","w":"Week 16","t":"Efficient and Scalable Deep Learning","h":"Assignment 4 and the project progress report","a":"deliverables","x":"Two deliverables fall due at the end of this week: Assignment 4 (the Week 16 row of the assessment table in the syllabus) and the 2-page project progress report . Both reward the same habits, and this week's material is exactly what the efficiency-study option is about. What a strong Assignment 4 submission contains , whichever of the three options you chose (generative modelling, efficiency study, GNN/RL task): - A clearly stated question and a baseline that is trained properly before you try anything fancy (tuned learning rate, fixed seed, reported variance). An improvement over a weak baseline means nothing. - Controlled comparisons : change one thing at a time, keep the rest of the pipeline identical, and report the mean and spread over at least 3 seeds for anything where noise could be mistaken for signal. - For an efficiency study specifically: accuracy and a cost axis (latency, throughput, memory, model size, energy) on a named piece of hardware, measured with warm-up, synchronization and medians (see bench above); a Pareto plot of accuracy against cost with the baseline on it; and ablations that separate the contribution of each technique (AMP, compile, quantization, distillation, pruning). State batch size, precision, input resolution and hardware in every table. Include at least one negative result or limitation (\"4-bit lost 3 points on the rare classes\", \"pruning to 90% gave no speed-up on this GPU\"), and explain it using the concepts of this week (roofline, kernel support, granularity). - A short error analysis or sanity check (what does the compressed model get wrong that the baseline got right?) and honest discussion of threats to validity. - Reproducibility: code that runs end to end, a README with the commands, pinned library versions, and the seeds. - T"},{"p":"week-16.html","w":"Week 16","t":"Efficient and Scalable Deep Learning","h":"Lab: profile a training loop, then apply AMP, quantization and distillation","a":"lab","x":"Goal. Starting from a deliberately naive CIFAR-10 training loop, (1) profile it and find the bottleneck, (2) speed it up with data-loading fixes, mixed precision and channels last , (3) shrink a model with distillation, (4) quantize its weights, and (5) report an accuracy/latency/size table. Run on a Colab GPU runtime (T4 is enough; with a T4, the BF16 path is unavailable, so the code selects FP16 with a GradScaler automatically). Epoch counts are small; increase them if you want better numbers. Step 1: data, models and measurement helpers Step 2: the naive loop, profiled What to look for. On a typical Colab T4 with a single worker, the profile will show the GPU idle for a large fraction of each step: the DataLoader (random crop, flip, ToTensor , Normalize on PIL images in the main process) is the bottleneck, not the convolutions. In the table, aten::cudnn convolution and aten::cudnn batch norm dominate GPU time, but the wall-clock step is much longer than their sum. Write down the images per second and the GPU utilization ( nvidia-smi dmon in another cell). Step 3: apply the fixes one at a time Expect each change to move throughput in the direction the theory predicts: workers and pinned memory fix the starving GPU; AMP puts the convolutions on tensor cores and halves the memory traffic of activations; channels last removes layout conversions around tensor-core convolutions. Record the exact numbers for your hardware. If AMP gives nothing, you are still input-bound or launch-bound: re-profile. Step 4: train the teacher and distill the student The student has about 1/16 of the teacher's convolution parameters. A distilled student typically gains a few tenths of a point to about two points of accuracy over the label-only student on CIFAR-10, with the gap widening for sma"},{"p":"week-16.html","w":"Week 16","t":"Efficient and Scalable Deep Learning","h":"Common mistakes and debugging tips","a":"mistakes","x":"Symptom Likely cause Fix --- --- --- GPU utilization 20 to 40%, throughput barely changes with a faster GPU input pipeline, CPU-side work, .item() or print every step, tiny kernels profile; add workers and pin memory ; log every N steps; larger batch; torch.compile Timing results are absurdly fast or vary wildly timed asynchronous CUDA launches without torch.cuda.synchronize() ; no warm-up; including compile time synchronize, warm up, take medians Loss becomes nan after enabling FP16 overflow in activations or gradients; unscaled clipping; GradScaler missing use BF16 if available; scaler.unscale before clipping; check the scale value; keep sensitive ops in FP32 AMP is slower than FP32 tiny matmuls, dimension sizes not multiples of 8, input-bound loop, many casts pad dimensions to multiples of 8 (or 64), larger batch, profile Gradient accumulation gives a different loss curve than the big batch loss not divided by the number of accumulation steps; BatchNorm statistics; unequal token counts scale the loss, use LayerNorm/GroupNorm, normalize by total tokens Out-of-memory at the first backward, not at the forward activations kept for backward; optimizer states allocated lazily at the first step() checkpointing, smaller micro-batch, ZeRO/FSDP; budget for optimizer states OOM only after many steps memory fragmentation; accumulating tensors that retain graphs ( total += loss instead of loss.item() ) detach before logging; torch.cuda.empty cache() is rarely the fix; set PYTORCH CUDA ALLOC CONF=expandable segments:True DDP hangs at start or at an all-reduce ranks run different code paths (conditionals on rank, uneven data so one rank finishes early); firewall; wrong MASTER ADDR identical control flow on all ranks; drop last=True ; find unused parameters only if truly needed (slo"},{"p":"week-16.html","w":"Week 16","t":"Efficient and Scalable Deep Learning","h":"Exercises","a":"exercises","x":"Exercise 1: Roofline classification An A100 has TFLOP/s (BF16) and TB/s. (a) Compute the ridge point. (b) For a BF16 matrix multiply with , , compute arithmetic intensity and decide whether it is memory- or compute-bound; give the best-case time. (c) By what factor does int8 weight-only quantization (activations stay BF16) speed up this operation in the memory-bound regime, approximately? Solution (a) FLOP/byte. (b) . Traffic with 2 bytes per element: weights elements, , ; total about elements, bytes. FLOP/B, about 10x below the ridge: memory-bound . Best-case time (the compute time would be only ). (c) The traffic is dominated by the weights; going from 2 to 1 byte per weight roughly halves (to about B), so about 1.98x faster , until the operation crosses the ridge. With 4-bit weights: about 3.9x. Exercise 2: Profiling diagnosis A training loop on an A100 reports 35% GPU utilization. The profiler trace shows short GPU kernels separated by gaps of roughly 5 ms, and a DataLoader. next call of about 6 ms at the start of each 12 ms step. Give two experiments that distinguish an input-bound loop from a launch-bound one, and list three concrete fixes for each. Solution Experiments: (1) Train repeatedly on one cached batch already on the GPU; if throughput rises sharply the input pipeline is the cause. (2) Double the batch size; if time per step barely changes, the GPU had slack and the problem is CPU launch overhead or input waiting; if it doubles, you were compute-bound. Also check whether the gaps occur between kernels inside the step (launch-bound) or only at the step start (input-bound). Here the 6 ms DataLoader wait points to input-bound. Fixes, input-bound: more workers, pin memory=True with non blocking=True copies, persistent workers / prefetch factor , move augmenta"},{"p":"week-16.html","w":"Week 16","t":"Efficient and Scalable Deep Learning","h":"Key takeaways","a":"takeaways","x":"Key takeaways - Data movement, not arithmetic, is usually the bottleneck. Roofline: attainable FLOP/s . Large matmuls are compute-bound; elementwise ops, normalization, softmax and token-by-token decoding are memory-bound. Fusion, FlashAttention and quantization all work by moving fewer bytes. - Measure before optimizing : synchronize, warm up, use medians, profile with torch.profiler , and classify the bottleneck (input, launch overhead, memory-bound, compute-bound, communication). Check GPU utilization first. - Mixed precision uses 16-bit compute with FP32 master weights and accumulation. FP16 needs dynamic loss scaling because of its narrow range; BF16 has FP32's range and usually needs none. - Gradient accumulation reproduces a large batch exactly (scale the loss; watch BatchNorm and token counts). Checkpointing stores boundaries and recomputes the rest: memory , minimized at , for about 33% more compute. - Distributed training : DDP averages gradients with a ring all-reduce whose per-GPU cost is about regardless of . Tensor parallelism splits matrices (needs NVLink); pipeline parallelism splits layers with bubble . ZeRO/FSDP shards optimizer states, gradients and parameters: bytes at stage 3 for 1.5x the communication. - FlashAttention computes exact attention tile by tile in SRAM using the online-softmax recursion, never writing the matrix to HBM: same FLOPs, far less memory traffic and memory. The KV cache makes decoding linear per token but costs bytes per token; GQA, paging and quantization cut it. Speculative decoding uses a draft model and exact rejection sampling to produce several tokens per target pass. - Compression: pruning (unstructured gives sparsity but rarely speed; structured gives smaller dense models), quantization (each bit is about 6 dB; scale g"},{"p":"week-16.html","w":"Week 16","t":"Efficient and Scalable Deep Learning","h":"Further reading","a":"further-reading","x":"Essential - Micikevicius, P., et al. Mixed Precision Training. ICLR 2018. arXiv:1710.03740. The FP32 master weights and loss-scaling recipe. - Rajbhandari, S., Rasley, J., Ruwase, O., He, Y. ZeRO: Memory Optimizations Toward Training Trillion Parameter Models. SC 2020. arXiv:1910.02054. - Dao, T., Fu, D. Y., Ermon, S., Rudra, A., Re, C. FlashAttention: Fast and Memory-Efficient Exact Attention with IO-Awareness. NeurIPS 2022. arXiv:2205.14135. Also important - Hinton, G., Vinyals, O., Dean, J. Distilling the Knowledge in a Neural Network. 2015. arXiv:1503.02531. - Han, S., Mao, H., Dally, W. J. Deep Compression: Compressing Deep Neural Networks with Pruning, Trained Quantization and Huffman Coding. ICLR 2016. arXiv:1510.00149. - Williams, S., Waterman, A., Patterson, D. Roofline: An Insightful Visual Performance Model for Multicore Architectures. Communications of the ACM, 2009. - Chen, T., Xu, B., Zhang, C., Guestrin, C. Training Deep Nets with Sublinear Memory Cost. 2016. arXiv:1604.06174. - Milakov, M., Gimelshein, N. Online normalizer calculation for softmax. 2018. arXiv:1805.02867. - Shoeybi, M., et al. Megatron-LM: Training Multi-Billion Parameter Language Models Using Model Parallelism. 2019. arXiv:1909.08053. - Narayanan, D., et al. Efficient Large-Scale Language Model Training on GPU Clusters Using Megatron-LM. SC 2021. arXiv:2104.04473. - Huang, Y., et al. GPipe: Efficient Training of Giant Neural Networks using Pipeline Parallelism. NeurIPS 2019. arXiv:1811.06965. - Zhao, Y., et al. PyTorch FSDP: Experiences on Scaling Fully Sharded Data Parallel. VLDB 2023. arXiv:2304.11277. - Korthikanti, V., et al. Reducing Activation Recomputation in Large Transformer Models. 2022. arXiv:2205.05198. - Jacob, B., et al. Quantization and Training of Neural Networks for Effi"},{"p":"week-17.html","w":"Week 17","t":"Trustworthy Deep Learning and Theory","h":"Trustworthy Deep Learning and Theory","a":"","x":"Learning objectives By the end of this week you should be able to: - Derive FGSM and PGD from a first-order model of the loss, explain the linearity account of adversarial vulnerability, and state precisely what a threat model is. - Implement adversarial training as a min-max problem, and explain why it costs more, why it trades off clean accuracy, and why certified defenses give weaker but provable guarantees. - Classify distribution shifts (covariate, label, concept) and implement max-softmax, energy and Mahalanobis out-of-distribution detectors. - Separate aleatoric from epistemic uncertainty, build deep ensembles and MC dropout, compute the expected calibration error, and fix miscalibration with temperature scaling. - State and prove the marginal coverage guarantee of split conformal prediction. - Derive Grad-CAM, SmoothGrad and Integrated Gradients (with its axioms), and explain why attention weights and probing accuracy are not explanations. - Describe circuits, superposition and sparse autoencoders in mechanistic interpretability. - Compute demographic parity and equalized odds gaps, explain why they cannot generally be satisfied together, and list mitigation strategies. - Define membership inference, -differential privacy and DP-SGD, and reason about the privacy-utility trade-off. - Explain what the randomization experiments, implicit regularization, the neural tangent kernel, the lottery ticket hypothesis, grokking and double descent each tell us (and do not tell us) about generalization. seventeen weeks the question was \"how do I get a network to minimize a loss on a dataset\". This week the question is \"should anyone rely on the result\". A classifier with 99% test accuracy can be fooled by a change to its input that no human notices. A medical model that is ac"},{"p":"week-17.html","w":"Week 17","t":"Trustworthy Deep Learning and Theory","h":"Adversarial examples","a":"adversarial","x":"The phenomenon In 2013, Szegedy and colleagues reported something startling: for a trained image classifier you can find a tiny perturbation , invisible to a person, such that is confidently wrong. Biggio et al. had demonstrated related evasion attacks against classical classifiers in the same period. The perturbed input is an adversarial example . It is not noise: random perturbations of the same size almost never change the prediction, yet a carefully chosen one succeeds on essentially every input of an undefended network. Szegedy et al., \"Intriguing properties of neural networks\" (2013/2014), found adversarial examples by box-constrained L-BFGS. Goodfellow, Shlens and Szegedy, \"Explaining and harnessing adversarial examples\" (ICLR 2015), proposed the one-step FGSM attack and the linearity hypothesis. Madry et al., \"Towards deep learning models resistant to adversarial attacks\" (ICLR 2018), framed robustness as a saddle-point problem and popularized PGD adversarial training. Athalye, Carlini and Wagner (ICML 2018) showed that most defenses published at ICLR 2018 relied on \"obfuscated gradients\" and could be broken, a lesson that still shapes how defenses are evaluated. Threat models A defense is meaningless without saying what attack it defends against. A threat model specifies four things. 1. Goal. Untargeted : make the prediction anything but the true class. Targeted : make it a chosen class . Or other goals such as raising the loss, or inducing a high-confidence error. 2. Capability (the perturbation set). Most of the literature uses an ball , usually (each pixel moves at most , typically on CIFAR-10), , or / (few pixels change). The ball is a convenient proxy for \"imperceptible\", not a faithful model of real attackers: rotations, patches, colour shifts, paraphrase"},{"p":"week-17.html","w":"Week 17","t":"Trustworthy Deep Learning and Theory","h":"Distribution shift and out-of-distribution detection","a":"shift","x":"What can change Every guarantee in supervised learning assumes the test data are drawn from the same distribution as the training data. Deployment violates this constantly. Write the joint distribution as . Different factorizations make different shifts easy to describe. Shift What changes What stays Example Standard fix --- --- --- --- --- Covariate shift A camera is replaced; photos are brighter Importance weighting , augmentation, domain adaptation Label (prior) shift A disease becomes more prevalent Re-estimate class priors (BBSE, EM of Saerens et al.), re-weight the posterior Concept shift maybe What counts as \"spam\" or \"creditworthy\" evolves Monitoring, retraining, continual learning Subpopulation shift mixture weights of groups within-group distributions A face model is trained mostly on one demographic Group-robust training (Group DRO), data collection Novel class / OOD input support of A cat appears to a digit classifier Detection, abstention Under covariate shift you can correct the loss: , provided the support of covers that of and the weights do not have exploding variance. Weights are typically estimated with a domain classifier : train a model to tell train from test inputs, then where is its output. Shift is not hypothetical. Benchmarks such as ImageNet-C (Hendrycks and Dietterich, 2019: 15 corruption types at 5 severities), ImageNet-A and ImageNet-R, and WILDS (Koh et al., 2021, with real shifts across hospitals, cameras and time) show that accuracy collapses under shifts that humans shrug off. A recurring empirical finding (Recht et al. 2019, Taori et al. 2020, Miller et al. 2021) is that in-distribution accuracy and out-of-distribution accuracy are strongly (often linearly, after a probit transform) correlated across many models: better models are bett"},{"p":"week-17.html","w":"Week 17","t":"Trustworthy Deep Learning and Theory","h":"Uncertainty estimation","a":"uncertainty","x":"Aleatoric and epistemic uncertainty A softmax output is a number between zero and one, but it hides two very different kinds of \"I don't know\". - Aleatoric uncertainty (from Latin alea , dice) is irreducible noise in the data-generating process: two identical-looking X-rays with different diagnoses, a blurry digit between a 4 and a 9, sensor noise. More data does not reduce it; it only lets you estimate it better. It can depend on the input ( heteroscedastic ): a self-driving system should be less sure at night than at noon. - Epistemic uncertainty (from episteme , knowledge) is uncertainty about the model : many functions fit the finite training set and they disagree away from it. More data in the right places reduces it. This is the uncertainty that matters for OOD inputs, active learning and exploration. Bayesian reasoning draws the line cleanly. With a posterior over parameters, the predictive distribution is For classification, the total predictive entropy decomposes (using the mutual information between and ): where . If every ensemble member says \"50-50\", the epistemic term is zero and the aleatoric term is . If half say \"100% A\" and half say \"100% B\", each member has zero entropy (aleatoric zero), the mean is 50-50, and the whole entropy is epistemic. The decomposition is the main reason ensembles are used for uncertainty rather than a single softmax. For regression one can use a network that outputs both a mean and a variance, trained with the Gaussian negative log-likelihood . The learned is aleatoric. (Kendall and Gal, 2017, discuss combining both kinds in vision.) Practical caution: jointly learning and can ignore hard examples by inflating ; a warm-up phase or -NLL (Seitzer et al., 2022) mitigates this. Deep ensembles Lakshminarayanan, Pritzel and Blundell "},{"p":"week-17.html","w":"Week 17","t":"Trustworthy Deep Learning and Theory","h":"Interpretability and explainability","a":"interpretability","x":"What we want from an explanation An explanation should help a person do something: debug a model, decide whether to trust a prediction, discover a spurious cue, satisfy a regulator, or learn something about the world. Keep two properties apart. Faithfulness asks whether the explanation reflects what the model actually computes. Plausibility asks whether it looks reasonable to a human. They are different, and visually pleasing maps can be unfaithful. Adebayo et al. (NeurIPS 2018, \"Sanity checks for saliency maps\") showed that several popular methods produce nearly the same map when the network's weights are randomized, meaning the map reflected image edges more than the model. Always run such sanity checks: randomize the weights (a faithful explanation must change), and measure deletion and insertion curves (remove the most important pixels first; a faithful map makes the confidence fall fast). Gradient saliency and its relatives The simplest attribution is the gradient of the class score with respect to the input, (Simonyan, Vedaldi and Zisserman, 2014, whose saliency map is the per-pixel maximum of the absolute value over colour channels). By the first-order Taylor expansion , it measures local sensitivity : how much a small change at each pixel changes the score. This is the same quantity that FGSM uses, and the maps look just as noisy for the same reason: the gradient of a ReLU network fluctuates rapidly (it is piecewise constant), so neighbouring pixels can have unrelated gradients. Related variants: Gradient times input ( ), guided backpropagation (Springenberg et al. 2015, which zeroes negative gradients at ReLUs and is notorious for acting like an edge detector independent of the weights) and DeconvNet . The first-order view is also a limitation: gradients are lo"},{"p":"week-17.html","w":"Week 17","t":"Trustworthy Deep Learning and Theory","h":"Fairness and bias audits","a":"fairness","x":"Where bias comes from A model is not biased in a vacuum; harm arises along the whole pipeline. Historical bias : the world is unequal and data record it. Representation bias : some groups are underrepresented in the data (Buolamwini and Gebru, 2018, \"Gender Shades\", found that commercial gender classifiers had far higher error on darker-skinned women than on lighter-skinned men). Measurement bias : the label is a flawed proxy (arrests for crime, cost for health need; Obermeyer et al., 2019, showed a healthcare risk algorithm underestimated the needs of Black patients because cost was the target). Aggregation bias : one model for groups with different relationships. Feedback loops : model decisions change the data collected next. Language models inherit stereotypes from text (Bolukbasi et al. 2016 on word embeddings; Caliskan et al. 2017). Removing the protected attribute rarely helps (\"fairness through unawareness\") because other features are proxies for it. Group fairness metrics Let be the true outcome, the decision (e.g. approve), and the protected attribute (group). The three standard group criteria differ in what they condition on. Criterion Requirement Meaning --- --- --- Demographic parity (statistical parity) Same selection rate in every group Equalized odds (Hardt, Price and Srebro, 2016) equal across for and Same TPR and FPR in every group Equal opportunity equal TPR only ( ) Qualified people are accepted equally often Predictive parity / calibration within groups (or ) equal across groups A score means the same thing in each group Demographic parity ignores the true outcome, so it can force unqualified acceptances if base rates differ, or be satisfied by a useless classifier. Equalized odds takes the labels as ground truth, which is only reasonable if the lab"},{"p":"week-17.html","w":"Week 17","t":"Trustworthy Deep Learning and Theory","h":"Privacy","a":"privacy","x":"What can leak A trained model is a compressed, lossy summary of its training data, and summaries leak. Three increasingly serious attacks illustrate this. - Membership inference (Shokri, Stronati, Song and Shmatikov, 2017): given a record and access to the model, decide whether the record was in the training set. The signal is the generalization gap: models are more confident, with lower loss, on points they trained on. The simplest attack thresholds the loss: predict \"member\" if . Shokri et al. trained shadow models to learn the decision rule; Carlini et al. (2022, LiRA) showed that comparing against per-example reference distributions, evaluated at low false-positive rates, is far more powerful than average-case accuracy suggests. Membership of a single patient in a \"cancer clinic\" training set is itself sensitive. - Model inversion and training-data extraction. Fredrikson et al. (2015) reconstructed faces from a face-recognition model's confidence outputs. Carlini et al. (2021) extracted hundreds of verbatim training sequences from GPT-2 by sampling and filtering, and later work showed larger language models memorize more and that duplicated data is memorized most. - Memorization is not always overfitting. Feldman (2020) argued that memorizing rare examples is necessary for optimal generalization on long-tailed data; a model can generalize well on average while memorizing individuals. Empirically, membership attacks succeed more for small training sets, many epochs, large models and overfit training procedures. Regularization, early stopping and more data reduce, but do not eliminate, risk, and they give no quantitative guarantee . Differential privacy does. Differential privacy: the definition Imagine two datasets and that differ in exactly one person's record ( nei"},{"p":"week-17.html","w":"Week 17","t":"Trustworthy Deep Learning and Theory","h":"Safety, red-teaming and responsible release","a":"safety","x":"Thinking about harm Safety in this setting covers technical failure modes (the model does something unintended), misuse (a person uses it to cause harm) and structural effects (concentration of power, labour, information ecosystems). A useful frame for any model is to enumerate who is affected, how they could be harmed, how likely and how severe the harm is, and what mitigations exist at each stage: data, training, evaluation, release, monitoring. Distinguish capability (what the model can do) from propensity (what it tends to do) and from deployment context (who can access it, with which guardrails). Model cards and documentation Mitchell et al. (FAT 2019, \"Model cards for model reporting\") proposed a short standard document that accompanies a model. Gebru et al. (2018/2021, \"Datasheets for datasets\") did the same for data. A good model card states: - Intended use and out-of-scope uses; users and contexts it was evaluated for. - Training data (sources, collection, licensing, known gaps) and preprocessing. - Evaluation : metrics disaggregated by relevant groups and conditions (not just a global average), with confidence intervals, and the evaluation data. - Ethical considerations and risks , known failure modes, and limitations . - Environment and compute , version, contact, and update policy. A card is useful only if it contains numbers a reader could be disappointed by. Treat it as part of the engineering process (written during development), not as post-hoc marketing. Red-teaming and evaluation Red-teaming is adversarial testing by people (and increasingly models) whose job is to find failures before users do. It complements benchmarks: benchmarks measure known failure modes on fixed data, red teams search for unknown ones, adaptively. Practices that have emerged: - "},{"p":"week-17.html","w":"Week 17","t":"Trustworthy Deep Learning and Theory","h":"Deep learning theory highlights","a":"theory","x":"Theory for deep learning is in the odd position that practice runs far ahead of it. What we have is a collection of partial explanations , mostly for simplified models (linear, two-layer, infinite-width), plus empirical phenomena that any complete theory must explain. This section picks the results that every practitioner should know. For each, ask: what is proved, under which assumptions, and what does it not explain? Generalization of overparameterized models Classical statistical learning theory bounds the gap between training and test error by the capacity of the hypothesis class: VC dimension, Rademacher complexity, or parameter count. A typical bound has the form . A modern network has more parameters than training points, can fit any labelling of the data, and so the bound is vacuous. Yet it generalizes. Something other than raw capacity must govern the outcome: the algorithm (SGD), the data , the architecture . The randomization experiments. Zhang, Bengio, Hardt, Recht and Vinyals (ICLR 2017, \"Understanding deep learning requires rethinking generalization\", later published in CACM as \"Understanding deep learning (still) requires rethinking generalization\") trained standard CNNs on CIFAR-10 and ImageNet with the labels replaced by random labels . The networks reached zero training error, though more slowly. They also fit images replaced by random noise. Explicit regularizers (weight decay, dropout, data augmentation) neither were necessary for generalization on real labels nor prevented fitting random ones. The lesson is that the hypothesis class (these architectures) contains functions that fit pure noise, so uniform capacity-based bounds over the class cannot explain why the one solution SGD finds on real data generalizes. Any explanation must be data- and algo"},{"p":"week-17.html","w":"Week 17","t":"Trustworthy Deep Learning and Theory","h":"Open problems and research directions","a":"open-problems","x":"Predictions about the future are unreliable, so this section lists open questions , each with enough context to find the literature. Treat it as a map of where a project could contribute. - Reasoning. Language models solve many reasoning benchmarks, particularly with chain-of-thought prompting (Wei et al., 2022) and training on verifiable rewards, and still fail in ways that look unlike human errors: sensitivity to irrelevant changes, poor out-of-distribution length generalization, inconsistent answers. Open questions: how much of the reasoning in a chain of thought causally drives the answer (faithfulness of explanations, Turpin et al. 2023), how to train and verify long multi-step reasoning without a reliable ground truth, and whether current architectures can represent the needed algorithms (transformers have known expressivity limits at fixed depth, Merrill and Sabharwal 2023). - Agents. Models that act in environments (browsing, coding, operating software) over many steps compound errors, are exposed to prompt injection from the content they read, and are hard to evaluate (long horizons, non-deterministic outcomes). Open: reliability over long horizons, safe tool use and sandboxing, measuring autonomy-related risk, credit assignment over very long trajectories, and cost. - Data efficiency. Humans learn language from orders of magnitude fewer tokens than large language models. Scaling laws (Kaplan et al. 2020; Hoffmann et al. 2022) describe how loss falls with data and compute, and the supply of high-quality text is finite. Directions: better data curation and curricula, synthetic data and its risks (model collapse when training on one's own outputs, Shumailov et al. 2024), self-supervised objectives that extract more per example, few-shot and in-context adaptation,"},{"p":"week-17.html","w":"Week 17","t":"Trustworthy Deep Learning and Theory","h":"Lab: attack, defend, explain and calibrate","a":"lab","x":"In this lab you will take one small CNN on MNIST through the whole chapter: train a standard model, break it with FGSM and PGD, harden it with adversarial training, explain predictions with Grad-CAM and saliency, and measure and repair its calibration. Everything runs in a few minutes on a Colab GPU (CPU is fine but slower). Run the cells in order; each part reuses the previous variables. We use MNIST so you can see everything; the same code works for CIFAR-10 with a bigger network and . Part 0: data and model Expected: clean test accuracy around 0.99 after three epochs. Part 1: attacks Reuse fgsm and pgd from the earlier code block (save them in attacks.py , or paste them into a cell), then sweep . You should see FGSM accuracy dropping steeply by to and PGD driving the standard model to near 0% by (PGD is always at least as strong as FGSM, up to the randomness of the start). A perturbation of on MNIST is large and visible; is nearly invisible. Part 2: adversarial training and evaluation Expect the adversarially trained model to keep roughly 90% or more of its accuracy under PGD (the exact numbers vary with seed and training length) at the price of a little clean accuracy. Sanity checks you should run yourself: (a) evaluate the adversarially trained model at and see that robustness is specific to the training radius; (b) check that PGD with more steps or restarts does not reduce accuracy much more (if it does, the evaluation was too weak); (c) try an attack with pgd l2 to see that training does not guarantee robustness. Part 3: explanations with Grad-CAM and saliency What to look for: the saliency map of the adversarially trained model is typically much cleaner and more digit-shaped than the standard model's noisy map (Tsipras et al. 2019 and Kaur et al. 2019 observed t"},{"p":"week-17.html","w":"Week 17","t":"Trustworthy Deep Learning and Theory","h":"Exercises","a":"exercises","x":"Exercise 1: FGSM for a linear model (derivation) For a logistic regression with label and cross-entropy loss, (a) compute , (b) give the FGSM adversarial example, and (c) show that the logit moves by exactly in the direction that hurts, when is correctly classified. Solution (a) with , . Then and . (b) . If then , so the step is ; if , and it is . (c) For : . The classifier flips when , i.e. for : the margin in units of the -norm of the weights decides robustness. Note: for the linear model FGSM is exactly optimal for the ball (the loss is monotone in the logit). Exercise 2: Dimension dependence (conceptual and coding) In the chart earlier, the same per-coordinate budget is more damaging in higher dimension. (a) Show that with having all entries of magnitude , the logit change under an attack is but under random sign noise it is about in standard deviation. (b) Write NumPy code to verify numerically for . Solution (a) The attack: . Random: with independent signs , so with mean 0 and variance , so the standard deviation is . The ratio of attack to noise effect is . (b) Exercise 3: PGD versus FGSM (what would happen if...) You evaluate a defended model and find FGSM accuracy 62% and PGD-20 accuracy 41% at the same . Then you find that a black-box transfer attack from an undefended surrogate gives 30% accuracy on it. What do you conclude, and what do you do next? Solution FGSM above PGD is normal (PGD is stronger). The red flag is that a transfer attack beats the white-box attack (30% < 41%): a gradient-based white-box attack should be at least as strong as one crafted on a different model. That points to gradient masking or an under-powered attack (too few steps, a badly chosen step size, a saturating loss, non-differentiable preprocessing). Next: use more steps and resta"},{"p":"week-17.html","w":"Week 17","t":"Trustworthy Deep Learning and Theory","h":"Common mistakes and debugging tips","a":"mistakes","x":"Symptom Likely cause Fix --- --- --- Adversarial attack does nothing (accuracy unchanged) Model in train() mode with dropout, attack gradient is zero because the logits saturate, or input not in requires grad model.eval() , use logits-based margin loss, x.requires grad (True) , check grad.abs().max() Adversarial examples exceed the budget Forgot projection, or clipping to happens before adding the perturbation Project onto -ball and clamp to the valid range after every step Epsilon is \"8/255\" but inputs were normalized by mean/std Perturbation is applied in normalized space Attack in pixel space, normalize inside the model (a first layer), or rescale by the std Robust accuracy 0% at small after \"adversarial training\" Catastrophic overfitting of single-step training, or training on perturbations of a stale model Use PGD, random start, early stopping on a PGD validation set \"Defense\" works against FGSM but not PGD, or black-box beats white-box Gradient masking Adaptive attacks, AutoAttack, check unbounded- attack reaches 0% Evaluating robustness on the same samples used for attack tuning Leaks into hyperparameters Hold out an attack-evaluation split ECE looks great but the model is useless Always predicting base rates, or too few samples per bin Report accuracy, NLL, Brier; use equal-mass bins; bootstrap Temperature fitted on the test set Optimistic calibration numbers Fit on a validation split; evaluate on test Calibration collapses after deployment Distribution shift Monitor ECE on a labelled sample; ensembles; recalibrate per domain MSP OOD detector flags everything or nothing Threshold chosen on one OOD set; detector given softmax of a different temperature Fix a threshold from ID data only (e.g. 95% TPR) and test on several OOD sets Saliency map looks nice but is unc"},{"p":"week-17.html","w":"Week 17","t":"Trustworthy Deep Learning and Theory","h":"Key takeaways","a":"takeaways","x":"Key takeaways - Adversarial examples come from maximizing the loss in a small perturbation set. FGSM is the exact first-order solution for ( ); PGD iterates it with projection and is the standard evaluation. High dimension plus near-linearity makes small perturbations powerful. - A threat model (goal, capability, knowledge, setting) is part of every robustness claim. Evaluate with adaptive attacks; gradient masking is the usual reason for a false sense of security. - Adversarial training (a min-max problem) is the strongest empirical defense, costing about compute and some clean accuracy. Certified defenses (IBP, Lipschitz, randomized smoothing) give provable but smaller radii. - Distribution shift (covariate, label, concept) is the norm; OOD detectors (MSP, energy, Mahalanobis) are useful but not universal. - Uncertainty: aleatoric (irreducible) versus epistemic (reducible, disagreement among models). Deep ensembles are the strongest simple method; MC dropout is a cheap approximation. Calibration is measured by reliability diagrams and ECE and often fixed with a single temperature, which does not change accuracy. - Conformal prediction wraps any model to give sets with marginal coverage under exchangeability, with a short proof; the guarantee is marginal, not conditional. - Explanations: gradients are local and noisy; SmoothGrad averages; Grad-CAM weights feature maps by pooled gradients; Integrated Gradients satisfies completeness and sensitivity but depends on the baseline. Attention is not an explanation, and a probe shows decodability, not use. Validate with sanity checks and interventions. Mechanistic interpretability seeks causal circuits; superposition and sparse autoencoders are central ideas. - Fairness criteria (demographic parity, equalized odds, predictive "},{"p":"week-17.html","w":"Week 17","t":"Trustworthy Deep Learning and Theory","h":"Further reading","a":"reading","x":"Essential (marked with a star) and supporting references. Check the venue or arXiv listing for the latest version. Robustness and adversarial examples - (Essential) Goodfellow, Shlens, Szegedy. Explaining and Harnessing Adversarial Examples. ICLR 2015 (arXiv:1412.6572). - Szegedy et al. Intriguing properties of neural networks. ICLR 2014 (arXiv:1312.6199). - (Essential) Madry, Makelov, Schmidt, Tsipras, Vladu. Towards Deep Learning Models Resistant to Adversarial Attacks. ICLR 2018 (arXiv:1706.06083). - Athalye, Carlini, Wagner. Obfuscated Gradients Give a False Sense of Security. ICML 2018. - Cohen, Rosenfeld, Kolter. Certified Adversarial Robustness via Randomized Smoothing. ICML 2019. - Ilyas et al. Adversarial Examples Are Not Bugs, They Are Features. NeurIPS 2019. - Croce and Hein. Reliable evaluation of adversarial robustness with an ensemble of diverse parameter-free attacks (AutoAttack). ICML 2020. - Zhang et al. Theoretically Principled Trade-off between Robustness and Accuracy (TRADES). ICML 2019. Shift and uncertainty - Hendrycks and Gimpel. A Baseline for Detecting Misclassified and Out-of-Distribution Examples in Neural Networks. ICLR 2017. - Liu, Wang, Owens, Li. Energy-based Out-of-distribution Detection. NeurIPS 2020. - Lee, Lee, Lee, Shin. A Simple Unified Framework for Detecting Out-of-Distribution Samples and Adversarial Attacks. NeurIPS 2018. - Lakshminarayanan, Pritzel, Blundell. Simple and Scalable Predictive Uncertainty Estimation using Deep Ensembles. NeurIPS 2017. - Gal and Ghahramani. Dropout as a Bayesian Approximation. ICML 2016. - (Essential) Guo, Pleiss, Sun, Weinberger. On Calibration of Modern Neural Networks. ICML 2017. - Ovadia et al. Can You Trust Your Model's Uncertainty? Evaluating Predictive Uncertainty Under Dataset Shift. NeurIPS "},{"p":"week-18.html","w":"Week 18","t":"Final Projects, Course Review, and Final Exam","h":"Final Projects, Course Review, and Final Exam","a":"","x":"Learning objectives By the end of this week you should be able to: - Structure and deliver a 12-minute project talk, and design slides that make exactly one claim each. - Report experimental results honestly: strong baselines, ablations, multiple seeds, confidence intervals, and failure cases. - Write a 6 to 8 page report in conference format, section by section, and check it against a rubric before you submit. - Make a project reproducible: seeds, configs, environment, data versioning, experiment tracking and a model card. - State the key idea and the key equations of every week of the course, and connect them into one picture. - Choose an architecture, optimizer, regularizer and generative model for a new problem, and debug a training run systematically. - Solve a cumulative exam that mixes derivations, conceptual questions and code, with emphasis on Weeks 9 to 17. - Read a research paper efficiently, reproduce one, and plan what to learn next. is the last week and it is a different kind of week. There is no new architecture and no new loss function. Instead you will do three things: show your own work to others, consolidate eighteen weeks of material into a form you can retrieve under time pressure, and decide where to go next. The week has two sessions plus the exam, following the syllabus. Session 1 is the project presentations (12 minutes plus questions per team). Session 2 is the cumulative review and a discussion of next steps. The final exam is cumulative and emphasizes Weeks 9 to 17. The final report (6 to 8 pages in conference format) and the code repository with a reproducibility README are due this week. The page follows the same order. Part I is the project guide. Part II is the course review. Part III is a full practice exam. Part IV is about what comes a"},{"p":"week-18.html","w":"Week 18","t":"Final Projects, Course Review, and Final Exam","h":"Project types and what \"good\" looks like","a":"project-types","x":"Your project is 30% of the course grade (proposal 5%, progress report 5%, final report and code 15%, presentation 5%). The milestones are in the figure below; most of the work should already exist as a running baseline since Week 16. The project timeline. Each milestone tested a different skill: scoping (proposal), feedback (Week 12), evidence that the baseline works (progress report), and communication (talk and report). Projects that worked well in practice fall into five families. None is better than another; what matters is that the question is sharp and the evidence matches the claim. Type Core question Typical deliverable Main risk --- --- --- --- Application Can deep learning solve problem X on data D better than a sensible baseline? A tuned pipeline, error analysis, comparison with a classical method Weak baselines, leakage, no error analysis Method study Does modification M improve model Z, and why? Controlled ablations, multiple seeds, a mechanism hypothesis Gains within seed noise, untuned baseline Reproduction Do the claims of paper P hold? Reimplementation, comparison table with the paper, discrepancy analysis Silent differences in data or evaluation Analysis or interpretability What does a trained model know or fail at? Probes, attributions, stress tests, calibration, robustness curves Over-interpreting a single visualization Systems and efficiency How fast, small or cheap can we make model Z at a given quality? Latency, memory and accuracy trade-off curves, profiling Measuring the wrong thing, ignoring warm-up and batch size A useful self-test: finish the sentence \"We show that , by measuring , against .\" If you cannot fill in all three blanks, the project is not yet a project. The first blank is the claim, the second is the metric and protocol, the third"},{"p":"week-18.html","w":"Week 18","t":"Final Projects, Course Review, and Final Exam","h":"The 12-minute talk","a":"talk","x":"Twelve minutes is short. The audience has not read your report, is mixed in background, and will remember about one thing. Plan the talk around that one thing. A suggested allocation of 12 minutes. Spend the largest share on results, and keep about eight to ten slides. Put anything the audience might ask about, but that does not fit, in backup slides. Problem (2 minutes). Say what the task is, give a concrete input and output example, and say why a reader should care. If the audience cannot picture an example input within 30 seconds, you have lost them. End the section with your claim in one sentence. Approach (2 minutes). Describe the baseline and what you changed, with one diagram. Put the architecture and training setup in a single line (\"ResNet-18, SGD with momentum, 30 epochs, cosine schedule\"). Mention what you did not build yourself (pretrained weights, a library), since honesty about provenance is part of the grade. Main results (4 minutes). This is the core. Each slide should state one finding as a headline sentence, show one figure or table, and show uncertainty. Start with the baseline. Show your method next to it, under the same budget and tuning effort. The comparison must be fair and say so. Analysis (2 minutes). An ablation that isolates which part of your method matters, and at least one failure case: examples the model gets wrong, a condition under which the gain vanishes, or a limit of the evaluation. Audiences trust speakers who show failures. Wrap-up (2 minutes). One sentence of takeaway, the limitations, and what you would do with more time. Do not end with \"Thank you, questions?\" on an empty slide; leave the summary or headline result visible during Q&A. Rehearse out loud, with a timer Almost every first rehearsal is 18 to 20 minutes. Cut slides ra"},{"p":"week-18.html","w":"Week 18","t":"Final Projects, Course Review, and Final Exam","h":"Presenting results honestly","a":"honest-results","x":"Most of this course has been about building models. The work of reporting them is about not fooling yourself, which is harder than it sounds, because training runs are noisy, the hyperparameter search space is huge, and you are biased toward the outcome you hoped for. Five habits protect you. Baselines { baselines} A result means nothing without something to compare to. Always include (i) a trivial baseline (majority class, last-value forecast, a linear model or logistic regression on features), (ii) a strong, well-tuned standard baseline, and (iii) your method. Apply the same tuning budget to all. A very common failure is to tune the new method for a week and run the baseline once with default settings; the gap you then report is mostly the difference in effort. Say in the report how many configurations were tried for each method. Ablations { ablations} If your method has components A, B and C, an ablation removes one at a time (and sometimes adds one at a time to the baseline) and measures the change. It answers \"which part is responsible?\" and it protects against the case where the gain comes from an unrelated detail, such as a longer training schedule or a larger batch. Keep everything else fixed, including the number of parameters or the compute budget when those matter. If a component does not help, say so: that is information. Seeds and error bars { seeds} A neural network's final accuracy depends on the random seed through the initialization, data order, dropout masks and augmentation. The standard deviation across seeds is often 0.2 to 1 point on small benchmarks, comparable to the gains reported by many method papers. So a single run cannot tell a real improvement from luck. The estimator. Suppose you train models with different seeds and obtain final metrics "},{"p":"week-18.html","w":"Week 18","t":"Final Projects, Course Review, and Final Exam","h":"The final report","a":"report","x":"The report is 6 to 8 pages (excluding references and appendix) in a standard conference format (the NeurIPS, ICML or ICLR LaTeX styles are all acceptable; use one consistently). Use a single column of text with figures that are legible when printed. Below is a section-by-section template with a checklist for each section. Abstract (about 150 words) { report-abstract} Four sentences do most of the work: the problem, the approach, the main result with a number, and the main takeaway. Check that: it names the task and dataset; it states a quantitative result with uncertainty; it makes no claim that the paper does not support. Introduction (about 1 page) { report-intro} Motivate the problem, state what is hard, summarize your approach, and list your contributions in two to four bullets. Check that: a reader outside your sub-area understands the task; each contribution is checkable against a section or figure; claims are scoped (\"on CIFAR-10 with ResNet-18\", not \"for vision\"). Related work (about 0.5 to 1 page) { report-related} Group prior work by idea, and say how yours differs. Do not list papers; relate them. Check that: the strongest prior method on your task is cited and, ideally, compared; every citation is real and was actually read; text is your own words. Method (1 to 1.5 pages) { report-method} Define the notation, give the model and loss, and describe the algorithm so that an informed reader could reimplement it. A diagram is usually worth more than a paragraph. Check that: every symbol is defined once; shapes and hyperparameters are stated; any non-obvious design choice has a reason. Experimental setup (about 1 page) { report-setup} Datasets (with sizes and splits), preprocessing and augmentation, baselines, metrics, hyperparameter search protocol and budget, nu"},{"p":"week-18.html","w":"Week 18","t":"Final Projects, Course Review, and Final Exam","h":"Reproducibility","a":"reproducibility","x":"A result is reproducible if someone else can obtain the same result from your code and data, and replicable if they get a consistent result from fresh data and code. For a course project, the goal is the first: a stranger (including you in six months) should be able to run one command and regenerate every table and figure in your report. Five things a run must record so that it can be reproduced: the code version, the configuration, the data version, the software and hardware environment, and the sources of randomness. Store them together with the outputs of every run. The checklist { repro-checklist} - Seeds. Set Python, NumPy and PyTorch seeds; seed the DataLoader workers and generator; record the seeds in the run record. Different seeds for different replicates, the same seed list for compared methods (paired comparison). - Determinism. Know which flags exist ( torch.use deterministic algorithms(True) , torch.backends.cudnn.benchmark=False ) and what they cost. Exact bitwise reproducibility across GPU types is often impossible; aim for statistical reproducibility, and report seed variance. - Configs. Put all hyperparameters in one config file (YAML or a dataclass), not in scattered constants or command-line habits. Save the resolved config with every run. - Environment. Pin versions ( requirements.txt with exact versions, or a conda lock file or a Docker image). Record Python, PyTorch, CUDA and GPU model. - Data versioning. Record the dataset name, version or checksum, and the exact train, validation and test split (store the indices or a split seed). If you preprocess, script it and store the script. Tools such as DVC or simply a hash in the run record work. - Code versioning. Run from a clean git commit and record the hash; if the tree is dirty, store the diff. - M"},{"p":"week-18.html","w":"Week 18","t":"Final Projects, Course Review, and Final Exam","h":"Grading rubric","a":"rubric","x":"The final report and presentation are graded on the criteria stated in the syllabus. The rubric below makes them concrete (100 points; the report and code carry 15% of the course, the presentation 5%). Criterion Points Full marks look like --- --- --- Problem significance and clarity 10 A sharp question, well motivated, scoped to what the evidence can support Technical correctness 20 Correct method and implementation; no leakage; sound evaluation protocol Experimental rigor 25 Strong, equally tuned baselines; ablations; at least 3 to 5 seeds; intervals; failure cases Analysis and insight 15 Explains why results look as they do; tests at least one hypothesis; honest about negatives Clarity of writing and figures 15 Conference-style report with the checklist above, readable figures, correct citations Reproducibility 10 README with exact commands, pinned environment, configs and seeds, data instructions, model card Presentation 5 (scaled separately) On time, one claim per slide, uncertainty visible, handles questions well Common deductions: results from a single seed presented as conclusive; baseline weaker than a standard one; tuning on the test set; claims in the abstract that the experiments do not show; missing citation for borrowed code; a repository that cannot be run from the README."},{"p":"week-18.html","w":"Week 18","t":"Final Projects, Course Review, and Final Exam","h":"Course review: how to study","a":"review-intro","x":"Part II: cumulative review of Weeks 1 to 17 The final exam is cumulative with emphasis on Weeks 9 to 17. The midterm review in Week 8 covers the first seven weeks in more depth; here each week gets a compact \"must know\" summary with its key equations and the one idea you should be able to explain to a colleague. Start by rating yourself. Exam readiness self-check 720 Use the result as a plan: spend your first study hours on the weeks with the highest priority score, then do the practice exam in Part III, then revisit what you missed."},{"p":"week-18.html","w":"Week 18","t":"Final Projects, Course Review, and Final Exam","h":"Week-by-week must-know summaries","a":"weekly-summaries","x":"Each entry is short on purpose. If any equation looks unfamiliar, follow the link. Week 1: Foundations { rev-w1} Week 1 set up the language: a model , a loss , data, and gradient descent. Know: - Supervised learning as empirical risk minimization, and maximum likelihood: minimizing cross-entropy equals maximizing the log-likelihood; mean squared error is Gaussian MLE. - Gradient descent: , with the minibatch gradient an unbiased estimate of the full gradient. - Information theory: , , and cross-entropy . - Tensor shapes and broadcasting, and the PyTorch loop: forward, loss, zero grad , backward , step . Week 2: Neural networks and backpropagation { rev-w2} Week 2: an MLP layer is . Know: - Softmax cross-entropy gradient w.r.t. the logits: . - Backprop: , . It is reverse-mode autodiff: cost about the same as one forward pass. - Universal approximation: one hidden layer suffices in principle; depth gives efficiency. Why nonlinearity is needed, and the properties of ReLU, GELU, sigmoid and tanh. Week 3: Optimization { rev-w3} Week 3: momentum ; Adam with , , bias correction , update . AdamW decouples weight decay from the adaptive scaling. Know: - Condition number and the GD rate per step on a quadratic; why momentum helps. - Initialization: Xavier , He ; goal is to keep activation and gradient variance constant across layers. - BatchNorm with learned ; train and eval modes differ; LayerNorm normalizes across features. - Warmup plus cosine decay as the default schedule. Week 4: Regularization and generalization { rev-w4} Week 4: the bias-variance decomposition . L2 is a Gaussian prior (MAP), L1 a Laplace prior (sparsity). Inverted dropout scales by at train time. Know double descent, label smoothing, early stopping, augmentation, hyperparameter search (random beats grid), "},{"p":"week-18.html","w":"Week 18","t":"Final Projects, Course Review, and Final Exam","h":"Threads that connect the weeks","a":"threads","x":"Exam questions in the last third of the course often combine weeks. Five connecting ideas to keep in mind: 1. Everything is maximum likelihood or a close relative. Cross-entropy (classification, language modeling), MSE (regression, diffusion noise prediction), ELBO (VAEs and diffusion), the policy-gradient objective and InfoNCE are all expected log-probability objectives or bounds on them. 2. Inductive bias versus data. CNNs, RNNs, GNNs and Transformers hard-wire different symmetries (translation, time, permutation, none). Less bias needs more data or pretraining; more bias generalizes better from little data but can be limiting. 3. Gradient flow. Residual connections, normalization, initialization, gating and gradient clipping all keep products of Jacobians well behaved. Many \"model does not train\" problems are gradient-flow problems. 4. Resources. Memory and bandwidth, not FLOPs, are frequently the real limits (roofline, KV cache, FlashAttention). Scaling laws tell you how to split a compute budget. 5. Evaluation under uncertainty. Seeds, calibration, shift and subgroup analysis answer the question \"does this result and this model deserve trust?\"."},{"p":"week-18.html","w":"Week 18","t":"Final Projects, Course Review, and Final Exam","h":"Decision guide: what to use when","a":"decision-guide","x":"These are defaults, not laws; they encode what the field has learned to try first. A starting-point guide for the model family by data type. The first model you try should be the simplest strong baseline for that data; change one thing at a time. Which architecture { decide-architecture} Situation Default Why, and when to deviate --- --- --- Images, small data Pretrained CNN (ResNet, ConvNeXt, EfficientNet), fine-tune Strong locality prior, cheap; deviate to ViT when pretraining data is large Images, large data or multimodal ViT or hierarchical transformer; CLIP-style encoders Scales better; patch size controls cost Dense prediction U-Net or a transformer decoder; DETR or Mask R-CNN for detection Skip connections keep resolution Text understanding Pretrained encoder (BERT-like) fine-tuned, or an LLM with prompting Decoder-only models are often best at scale Text generation, assistants Decoder-only Transformer, instruction tuning, RLHF or DPO LoRA or QLoRA if GPU memory is limited Long sequences Efficient attention (FlashAttention, windowed, state space models) Quadratic cost dominates beyond a few thousand tokens Time series Start with a linear or gradient-boosting baseline, then a TCN or Transformer Deep models do not always win Graphs GCN or GraphSAGE baseline, GAT or GIN when edges or expressivity matter Watch over-smoothing beyond 3 or 4 layers Tabular Gradient boosting first; MLP with embeddings as a comparison Deep nets win mostly with large data or mixed modalities Decision making PPO for on-policy, SAC or TD3 for continuous control, DQN variants for discrete Offline data: conservative methods; or consider supervised imitation first Which optimizer and schedule { decide-optimizer} - Default: AdamW (learning rate to for small nets, to for fine-tuning pretrained Tr"},{"p":"week-18.html","w":"Week 18","t":"Final Projects, Course Review, and Final Exam","h":"Debugging checklist","a":"debugging","x":"When a run fails, work from simple to complex. Almost all failures are bugs in data or code, not in the idea. 1. Overfit one batch. Take 8 to 32 examples and train to near-zero loss. If you cannot, there is a bug in the model, loss, labels or optimizer step. 2. Check the initial loss. For -class classification with random initialization it should be about (for example for 10 classes). Far off means a wrong final layer scale, a missing softmax or wrong labels. 3. Inspect data. Visualize inputs after preprocessing and augmentation, with their labels. Check the label range, class balance, train-test leakage, and normalization statistics. 4. Check shapes and dimensions. Silent broadcasting bugs are the most common ones (a loss of shape minus gives ). 5. Check modes and devices. model.train() versus model.eval() (dropout, BatchNorm), torch.no grad() at inference, gradients actually non-zero and not NaN . 6. Learning rate first. Sweep it over several orders of magnitude; most \"it does not learn\" problems are a bad learning rate. Plot the loss curve per step. 7. Track gradient and update norms, and the ratio of update to weight norm (about is a healthy order of magnitude as a rule of thumb). 8. Loss goes to NaN: lower the learning rate, add warmup and gradient clipping, check for division by zero or , use a numerically stable loss ( cross entropy on logits, not log(softmax) ). 9. Train good, validation bad: overfitting (augmentation, weight decay, more data) or a train-eval mismatch such as BatchNorm statistics or leakage in the split. 10. Both bad: underfitting, a bug, or an optimization problem. Increase capacity, train longer, fix the schedule. 11. Compare against a trivial baseline and a known-good reference implementation on the same data. 12. Change one thing at a time, "},{"p":"week-18.html","w":"Week 18","t":"Final Projects, Course Review, and Final Exam","h":"Practice final exam","a":"practice-exam","x":"Part III: a cumulative practice final Forty-three questions, mixing derivations, conceptual questions, numeric calculations and coding. About two thirds touch Weeks 9 to 17, matching the exam emphasis. A realistic target is two to three hours for the whole set; the real exam will be shorter. Work on paper first, then open the solution. Questions are grouped by topic and each is tagged with its week. Marking yourself For each question award yourself full credit only if your reasoning, not just your final answer, matches the solution. If you could only do it after reading the solution, count it as a miss and revisit the linked week. Section A: Foundations (Weeks 1 to 4) { exam-a} Question 1 (Week 2): softmax cross-entropy gradient For logits , and a one-hot label , derive for . Solution where is the true class. Then . The gradient is the prediction minus the target; it is bounded in and vanishes only when the prediction matches the label, which is why softmax with cross-entropy does not suffer the saturation of sigmoid with squared error. Question 2 (Weeks 2 and 4): initial loss and overfitting one batch You train a 10-class classifier. At step 0 the loss is 14.7. After 500 steps on the full dataset it is 2.30 and stays there. (a) What would you expect the step-0 loss to be and what does 14.7 suggest? (b) What does a plateau at 2.30 mean? (c) Name the first experiment you run. Solution (a) A randomly initialized network with small outputs predicts nearly uniform probabilities, so the loss is . A value of 14.7 means large logits (scale too big, bad initialization, or an unnormalized input with huge magnitude) making the model confidently wrong. (b) 2.30 means the model has collapsed to predicting the uniform distribution (or the class prior): either it is not learning at a"},{"p":"week-18.html","w":"Week 18","t":"Final Projects, Course Review, and Final Exam","h":"Exercises","a":"exercises","x":"The practice exam above is the main exercise set for this week. These additional exercises focus on the project and on the cross-cutting skills. Exercise 1: audit your own project (conceptual) Fill in the sentence \"We show that , by measuring , against .\" for your project. Then list the three weakest points of your evidence and what experiment would address each. Solution There is no single answer; a good response is specific. Example: \"We show that cosine decay with warmup improves a small ViT on CIFAR-10 by about 1 point (claim), by measuring top-1 test accuracy over 5 seeds with a 95% interval (metric), against the same ViT with a constant learning rate and a ResNet-18 baseline (baselines).\" Typical weak points and fixes: only 3 seeds (run 5 to 10); the baseline learning rate was not tuned (give it the same search budget); the hyperparameters were chosen by looking at test accuracy (re-select on a validation split and evaluate once on test); no failure analysis (inspect and slice the 50 worst examples). Exercise 2: sample size for a target interval (derivation) Your per-seed standard deviation is about points. Using the normal approximation (multiplier 1.96), how many seeds do you need for a 95% confidence interval of half-width at most points? Is that the number you need to detect a true gap of 0.3 points between two methods? Solution Half-width , so (using the multiplier for such gives about 17). For detecting a difference between two independent methods, the standard error of the gap is , so to have the interval half-width of the gap below the gap itself ( ) you need , i.e. seeds per method. A reliable detection (power 80%) needs roughly a factor of 2 more than that threshold-type requirement. This is why small effects are expensive to establish and why paired des"},{"p":"week-18.html","w":"Week 18","t":"Final Projects, Course Review, and Final Exam","h":"Common mistakes and debugging tips","a":"mistakes","x":"Mistakes that cost points on the final project and in the exam: - Single-seed claims. Symptom: \"B beats A by 0.3\". Fix: at least 3 to 5 seeds, intervals, and cautious wording. - Baseline neglect. Symptom: huge gains over a default-hyperparameter baseline. Fix: tune the baseline with the same budget; include a simple non-deep baseline. - Test-set contamination. Symptoms: validation and test curves that are suspiciously close, or results that vanish on fresh data. Fix: freeze the test set, look at it once, check for duplicates across splits. - Cherry-picked figures. Qualitative examples that are best-case. Fix: random, uncurated examples and failure cases. - An over-long talk. Symptom: the last three slides are rushed. Fix: rehearse with a timer and cut content. - Unexplained numbers. A table with no stated metric, split or seed count. Fix: captions that say what, on which data, over how many runs. - A README that does not run. Symptom: missing data download step, unpinned versions. Fix: a clean-clone test on another machine or a fresh Colab session. - Exam: skipping units and shapes. Write the shape of every tensor and the units of every quantity; most calculation errors show up there. - Exam: derivations without definitions. State what every symbol means; partial credit depends on it. - Exam: memorizing without understanding. Many questions change one assumption (for example, \"what if the layer used a sigmoid instead of ReLU?\"); practice asking that question of every result."},{"p":"week-18.html","w":"Week 18","t":"Final Projects, Course Review, and Final Exam","h":"Key takeaways","a":"takeaways","x":"Key takeaways - A project talk makes one claim, spends most of its time on evidence, and shows uncertainty and failures. Twelve minutes means eight to ten slides. - A fair comparison needs a strong baseline with equal tuning effort, ablations that isolate each component, and several seeds. Intervals shrink only like ; a single seed can pick the wrong winner a third of the time for a 0.3-point effect. - Report mean and interval over seeds with the multiplier , say what the error bar is, and consider paired analyses and test-set sampling error separately. - Reproducibility means recording code, config, data, environment and randomness together for every run, and shipping a README that regenerates every number in the report. - The course in one line: a loss (usually a log-likelihood or bound), a parameterized function with the right inductive bias, an optimizer, data, and the scientific discipline to know whether it worked. - Debug from simple to complex: overfit one batch, check the initial loss, inspect data, then tune. - To study for the exam, retrieve rather than reread: derive, compute and code from a blank page, and fix what you miss. Part IV: next steps"},{"p":"week-18.html","w":"Week 18","t":"Final Projects, Course Review, and Final Exam","h":"How to read research papers","a":"reading-papers","x":"By now you have read and presented papers (see How to read and present a paper). The skill that separates efficient readers from exhausted ones is multi-pass reading , which follows Keshav's \"How to Read a Paper\" (2007). Three passes over a paper. Most papers deserve only the first pass; a few deserve the second; very few deserve the third. During pass 2 keep a short written summary in a fixed template; it forces understanding and makes the paper searchable later: 1. Problem. What is being solved, and why is it hard? 2. Key idea. One or two sentences, in your own words. 3. Method. The model, loss and training procedure, with notation. 4. Evidence. Which experiments support which claims? What are the baselines, the number of seeds, and the effect size relative to noise? 5. Limitations. Stated by the authors and found by you. 6. Connections. Which course weeks and other papers does it relate to? Questions to ask in the critique pass: Is the baseline strong and tuned? Is the compute budget matched? Does the ablation isolate the claimed cause? Is the improvement larger than seed noise? Does the benchmark measure what the title claims? Could the test set have been seen in pretraining? Are there negative results hidden in the appendix? What would falsify the central claim? Practical advice: read the abstract, the figures and the conclusion first; read the appendix for hyperparameters and extra results; follow the references of a paper you like backwards (what it builds on) and the citations forwards (what built on it, via Google Scholar or Semantic Scholar). Keep a reading log. Join or start a reading group: explaining a paper aloud exposes gaps better than rereading. Skim arXiv regularly (the daily lists in cs.LG, cs.CL, cs.CV), but prefer depth over breadth; a handful of pa"},{"p":"week-18.html","w":"Week 18","t":"Final Projects, Course Review, and Final Exam","h":"A reading list by topic","a":"reading-list","x":"The following papers are landmarks, chosen because they are readable and because they shaped the field. All are real and verifiable; years are the publication or first arXiv year. Foundations and optimization - Rumelhart, Hinton and Williams, \"Learning representations by back-propagating errors\", Nature , 1986. - Kingma and Ba, \"Adam: A method for stochastic optimization\", ICLR 2015 (arXiv:1412.6980). - Ioffe and Szegedy, \"Batch normalization: accelerating deep network training by reducing internal covariate shift\", ICML 2015. - Srivastava et al., \"Dropout: a simple way to prevent neural networks from overfitting\", JMLR 2014. - Zhang, Bengio, Hardt, Recht and Vinyals, \"Understanding deep learning requires rethinking generalization\", ICLR 2017. Vision - Krizhevsky, Sutskever and Hinton, \"ImageNet classification with deep convolutional neural networks\", NeurIPS 2012. - He, Zhang, Ren and Sun, \"Deep residual learning for image recognition\", CVPR 2016. - Dosovitskiy et al., \"An image is worth 16x16 words: transformers for image recognition at scale\", ICLR 2021. - He et al., \"Masked autoencoders are scalable vision learners\", CVPR 2022. - Radford et al., \"Learning transferable visual models from natural language supervision\" (CLIP), ICML 2021. Sequence models, Transformers and language models (the essential trio is marked) - Vaswani et al., \"Attention is all you need\", NeurIPS 2017. - Devlin, Chang, Lee and Toutanova, \"BERT: pre-training of deep bidirectional transformers for language understanding\", NAACL 2019. - Brown et al., \"Language models are few-shot learners\" (GPT-3), NeurIPS 2020. - Kaplan et al., \"Scaling laws for neural language models\", 2020 (arXiv:2001.08361). - Hoffmann et al., \"Training compute-optimal large language models\" (Chinchilla), 2022 (arXiv:2203.1555"},{"p":"week-18.html","w":"Week 18","t":"Final Projects, Course Review, and Final Exam","h":"Reproducing a paper","a":"reproducing","x":"Reproducing a result is the best exercise after this course. It forces you to confront every detail that papers omit. A workable process: 1. Choose well. Pick a paper with a small-scale core result (a figure you can regenerate in hours on one GPU), public code or at least a clear description, and a clear success criterion. Avoid papers whose headline result needs thousands of GPU-hours. 2. Define success first. Write down which numbers or curves you expect and the tolerance (relative to seed variance) before you run anything. 3. Start from the simplest working version and verify each component against a known value: shapes, parameter counts (the paper often states them), the initial loss, and a one-batch overfit. 4. Match the details that matter: data splits and preprocessing, initialization, optimizer settings, schedule, batch size, evaluation protocol and metric implementation. Differences here explain most failed reproductions. 5. Use reference code as a check, not a copy. Read the official implementation after your own attempt to find discrepancies, and cite it. Course policy requires disclosure of external code. 6. Log everything with the harness from this page, run several seeds, and keep a \"differences from the paper\" list. 7. Report honestly, including partial success. Discrepancies, such as a metric that moves by 0.5 points because of a data-loading detail, are valuable to the community. A useful ladder of difficulty: reproduce a figure from a course reading (a day), reproduce a small benchmark number (a week), reproduce an ablation from a recent paper (a few weeks), and extend it with one new experiment (a project)."},{"p":"week-18.html","w":"Week 18","t":"Final Projects, Course Review, and Final Exam","h":"Contributing to open source","a":"open-source","x":"Open-source contributions are an excellent way to learn how real systems are built and to become visible to future collaborators and employers. Start small and be patient. - Pick a library you use, such as PyTorch, Hugging Face Transformers and Datasets, timm, PyTorch Geometric, Stable-Baselines3, scikit-learn, or this course site. - Begin with documentation: fix typos, clarify an unclear example, add a missing docstring, or translate a page. These are welcome and teach you the workflow. - Look for issues labeled \"good first issue\" or \"help wanted,\" and comment before you start to avoid duplicated work. - Reproduce the bug first in a minimal script. A minimal, reproducible bug report is itself a contribution. - Follow the contribution guide: coding style, tests, commit message format, and the review process. Keep pull requests small and focused, include a test, and respond to review comments politely and quickly. - Add something you built: a model implementation with a verified result, a tutorial notebook, a benchmark script. Maintainers value correctness and clarity over novelty. - Release your project code properly: a license, a README that works, version pins, and a citation entry."},{"p":"week-18.html","w":"Week 18","t":"Final Projects, Course Review, and Final Exam","h":"Career paths","a":"careers","x":"Deep learning work has several overlapping roles. They emphasize different skills; you can move between them, and many people do. Path Typical work Skills to emphasize How to prepare --- --- --- --- Research (academic or industrial lab) Posing new questions, designing experiments, writing papers Strong math, rigorous experimentation, writing, depth in one area Reproduce and extend papers, a thesis or project with an advisor, workshops, then conference submissions Applied ML or data science Turning a business problem into a model with measurable impact Problem framing, data understanding, baselines, evaluation, communication Projects on real, messy data; Kaggle-style competitions as practice; domain knowledge ML engineering and MLOps Training and serving models at scale, pipelines, monitoring Software engineering, systems, GPUs, distributed training, testing Strong coding, profiling and deployment practice (Week 16), contributing to open-source infrastructure Specialist roles Safety and evaluation, interpretability, data and annotation quality, product and policy Domain-specific rigor, statistics, ethics (Week 17) Projects that stress-test models; reading and writing evaluations and audits General advice: build a small public portfolio of two or three well-documented projects (code, a short write-up, and results with honest error bars), and be able to explain every choice in them. Learn to communicate with non-specialists. Stay curious about the data: most real-world gains come from better data, evaluation and problem framing, rather than a new architecture. Be wary of hype in either direction, and keep checking claims, including your own, against evidence."},{"p":"week-18.html","w":"Week 18","t":"Final Projects, Course Review, and Final Exam","h":"Further courses and resources","a":"resources","x":"- Courses: Stanford CS231n (convolutional networks for visual recognition), CS224n (NLP with deep learning), CS224W (machine learning with graphs), CS236 (deep generative models); Berkeley CS285 (deep reinforcement learning); MIT 6.S191 (introduction to deep learning); fast.ai Practical Deep Learning; Andrej Karpathy's \"Neural Networks: Zero to Hero\" video series; the Hugging Face courses and documentation. - Fundamentals worth strengthening: probability and statistics (Murphy; Bishop's Pattern Recognition and Machine Learning ), optimization (Boyd and Vandenberghe's Convex Optimization ), linear algebra (Strang), and information theory (Cover and Thomas). - Where papers appear: NeurIPS, ICML and ICLR for general machine learning; CVPR, ICCV and ECCV for vision; ACL and EMNLP for language; plus arXiv and OpenReview (which also hosts peer reviews, an excellent way to learn how papers are judged). - Practice: build things end to end; enter a competition with a time limit; write a blog post explaining one paper; teach this course's material to someone else. - Keep your toolchain current: the specific libraries will change over the years, but the habits in this course (small experiments, baselines, seeds, logs, honesty) will not."},{"p":"week-18.html","w":"Week 18","t":"Final Projects, Course Review, and Final Exam","h":"Further reading","a":"further-reading","x":"The reading list above is the reading list for the week. If you read only three things: Keshav (2007) on reading papers , Henderson et al. (2018) on reproducibility and the effect of seeds in deep RL , and Karpathy's recipe for training neural networks (2019)."},{"p":"about.html","w":"Page","t":"About and contributing","h":"About and contributing","a":"","x":"What this course is { what} This is a free, open-source, 18-week deep learning course at the master's level. It is designed so that a motivated student can learn the material from the pages alone: every week moves from intuition, to mathematics, to code, to pitfalls, with figures, interactive demos, worked derivations, exercises with solutions, labs, and a reading list. It assumes calculus, linear algebra, probability, and programming experience in Python. The course runs from foundations (tensors, backpropagation, optimization, regularization), through core architectures (convolutional, recurrent, attention-based), pretrained and generative models (language models, vision-language models, VAEs, GANs, diffusion), to advanced topics (graph networks, reinforcement learning, efficiency, trustworthy deep learning) and a final project. Start from the syllabus, which has the weekly schedule, grading and policies, and use the glossary as a quick reference. License { license} - Text and figures (the content in content/ and figures/ ) are licensed under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International license (CC BY 4.0). You may share and adapt them, including commercially, as long as you give appropriate credit and indicate changes. - Code (everything in demos/ , src/ , build.js , and the code examples in the pages) is licensed under the MIT license. Papers, datasets, and software mentioned in the course belong to their respective authors and are covered by their own licenses. Build the site locally { build} The site is a static site generated by a small Node.js script. You need Node.js (a current long-term-support version) and npm. Then open site/index.html directly in your browser, or serve the folder so that all features work consistently: To check a single week while you"},{"p":"glossary.html","w":"Page","t":"Glossary","h":"Glossary","a":"","x":"Short definitions of the terms used throughout the course, in alphabetical order. Each entry links to the week where the idea is developed; use the page search to jump to a term. A Activation function (Week 2): A nonlinear function applied elementwise after a linear layer (ReLU, GELU, tanh, sigmoid); without it a stack of layers collapses to one linear map. Actor-critic (Week 15): A reinforcement-learning method that learns a policy (actor) and a value function (critic) together, using the critic to reduce the variance of the policy gradient. Adam (Week 3): An adaptive optimizer that keeps exponential moving averages of the gradient and its square and scales each coordinate's step by them, with bias correction. AdamW (Week 3): Adam with weight decay applied directly to the parameters instead of being added to the gradient, so decay is not rescaled by the adaptive denominator. Advantage (Week 15): The amount by which an action's value exceeds the average value of the state, ; the learning signal in modern policy-gradient methods. Adversarial example (Week 17): An input changed by a small, deliberately chosen perturbation that makes a model err while looking unchanged to a human. Adversarial training (Week 17): Training on adversarial examples generated on the fly, which solves a min-max problem and buys robustness at a cost in accuracy and compute. Aleatoric uncertainty (Week 17): Uncertainty from irreducible noise in the data; it remains no matter how much data you collect. Attention (Week 7): A mechanism that forms a weighted average of values, with weights given by a softmax of query-key similarities; first used in seq2seq translation and now the core of Transformers. Autoencoder (Week 12): A network that compresses its input to a low-dimensional code and reconstructs"},{"p":"syllabus.html","w":"Page","t":"Syllabus","h":"Syllabus","a":"","x":"Level: Master's (graduate) · Duration: 18 weeks · Format: 2 lectures (75 min each) + 1 lab/tutorial (2 hrs) per week · Credit hours: 3 (3+0) with lab component Course description This course takes students from the mathematical and algorithmic foundations of neural networks to the architectures and techniques that define modern deep learning: convolutional networks, sequence models, Transformers, large pretrained models, generative models (VAEs, GANs, diffusion), graph networks, and deep reinforcement learning. It also covers the practical side of training and deploying models at scale, along with robustness, interpretability, and responsible use. Students implement core ideas from scratch, reproduce results from research papers, and complete a capstone research-style project. Prerequisites - Linear algebra (matrices, eigendecomposition, SVD), multivariate calculus, probability and statistics - Programming in Python; familiarity with NumPy - An introductory machine learning course (regression, classification, overfitting, cross-validation) A short math refresher is built into Week 1 for students who need it. Learning outcomes By the end of the course, students will be able to: 1. Derive and implement forward and backward passes for feed-forward networks, and explain how automatic differentiation works. 2. Select and tune optimizers, initializations, normalization layers, and regularizers to train deep models reliably. 3. Design, train, and evaluate CNNs, RNNs, and Transformer models using PyTorch. 4. Apply transfer learning, fine-tuning, and parameter-efficient methods to pretrained models. 5. Explain and implement major generative frameworks (autoencoders, VAEs, GANs, diffusion models). 6. Apply graph neural networks and deep reinforcement learning to appropriate probl"}];